ding, lowest brain protein binding, the longest delay in onset activity, and the longest duration of action. The plasma DATE OF REVISION OF PACKAGE INSERT half-life for phenobarbital in adults ranges between 53 and 118 hours with a mean of 79 hours. 13 November 2020 The plasma half-life for p (less than 48 hours old) ranges between 60 to 180 hours with a mean of 110 hours. Manufactured by Amherst Laboratories, Inc. Metabolism: ali 4 UNILAB Pharma Campus, Barangay Mamplasan, Binafi, Laguna, Philippines are by the hepatic microsomal enzyme system, and the p are ed in the urine and, less commonly, in the feces. Marketing Authorization Holder: Elimination. PHILUSA CORPORATION 28 Shaw Boulevard, Pasig City Approximately 25% to 50% of a dose of phenobarbital is eliminated unchanged in the urine. The excretion of unmetabolized barbiturate is one feature that distinguishes the long-acting category from those belonging to other seen te which are almost entirely metabolized. The RPBOO of the as vy of g! acid,",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,Phenobarbital is used as an anti-epileptic agent to control tonic-clonic (grand mal) and partial (focal) seizures.,1,1,INDICATION(S),medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-29T18:42:34.943452+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X2619,Colchicine,Unbranded,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xp9l0_bXKAw7vpdXlxdSYkMPzZgOLHMJ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X2619_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1xp9l0_bXKAw7vpdXlxdSYkMPzZgOLHMJ_view_usp_drivesdk_0b532bb722.pdf,page_text/DR-X2619_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1xp9l0_bXKAw7vpdXlxdSYkMPzZgOLHMJ_view_usp_drivesdk_0b532bb722.json,9752f296ad7272bde088f2e75e89636ecd7455836be8775dc9994b4ae4500bdb,55122,1,ocr,,,,,"Colchicine is indicated for the treatment of gout. It is effective in relieving the pain of acute attacks, especially if therapy is begun early in the attack and in adequate dosage.",1,1,indications:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:42:39.324303+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X2710,Multivitamis + Iron,Terraferron,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1TzwbzgXAy926uhg5h1T5Z6d3r62TL7ba/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X2710_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1TzwbzgXAy926uhg5h1T5Z6d3r62TL7ba_view_usp_drivesdk_b268c96328.pdf,page_text/DR-X2710_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1TzwbzgXAy926uhg5h1T5Z6d3r62TL7ba_view_usp_drivesdk_b268c96328.json,fdb608081e3f3c33dda0ed5f17d4bacd0a953828c0fc5de3213dd565949ba9ec,459102,1,digital_text,"Rusty yellow powder, in scarlet cap/scarlet body, in EGC size “1”.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:42:42.080883+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X6084,Loperamide Hydrochloride,United Home Lormide,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1lktCgJHy_SyqGuV2LzOMzs6o4YKjpSMX/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X6084_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1lktCgJHy_SyqGuV2LzOMzs6o4YKjpSMX_view_usp_drivesdk_8864802aa7.pdf,page_text/DR-X6084_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1lktCgJHy_SyqGuV2LzOMzs6o4YKjpSMX_view_usp_drivesdk_8864802aa7.json,3611ce364b06719dfb0c4e9daa67e55fd37fff826e279a9d9a03c11715428e2b,233262,1,digital_text,"Loperamide (United Home ® Lormide®) Capsule has a dark green opaque cap and light green opaque body. WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE This medicine contains Loperamide, an antidiarrheal agent which slows intestinal movement and reduces fluid and salt loss in the intestines, resulting in improved stool consistency. STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE Please see formulation WHAT IS THE MEDICINE USED FOR? This medicine is used for the following conditions:  Control and symptomatic relief of: - acute nonspecific diarrhea - chronic diarrhea associated with inflammatory bowel disease  Reduction of number and volume of discharge in patients with ileostomies and colostomies HOW MUCH AND HOW OFTEN SHOULD YOU USE THIS MEDICINE? In addition to taking loperamide for diarrhea, you should replace lost fluid and electrolytes by drinking plenty of clear fluids and/or oral rehydration salts (ORS). Do not take more than the recommended dose of loperamide.  For Symptomatic Relief of Acute Diarrhea Adult and Children Over 12 Years: Take 2 capsules initially followed by 1 capsule after each loose bowel movement, or, as directed by a doctor. Maximum dose is 16 mg (8 capsules) per day. Discontinue use if there is no improvement after 2 days.  For Symptomatic Relief of Chronic Diarrhea Adult and Children Over 12 Years: Take 2 capsules initially followed by 1 capsule after each loose bowel movement. Then reduce loperamide dose to meet individual requirements. When the optimal daily dosage has been established, administer this amount as a single dose or in divided doses. Or, as directed by a doctor. Average Daily Maintenance Dose: Take 2 to 4 capsules/day. A dosage of 5 capsules (10 mg) is rarely exceeded. If no clinical improvement is observed after treatment with 8 capsules/day (16 mg/day) for at least 10 days, symptoms are unlikely to be controlled by further loperamide administration. WHEN SHOULD YOU NOT TAKE THIS MEDICINE?  When you are allergic to Loperamide or any ingredient in the product  If it is for a child under 12 years old  If you are pregnant or breastfeeding  If you are constipated and also in whom constipation should be avoided (e.g., diarrhea resulting from infections)  If your abdomen is swollen or you have abdominal pain  If you have acute dysentery which is characterized by blood in stools and high fever  If you have pseudomembranous colitis (diarrhea associated with antibiotics), active ulcerative colitis (inflammation of the colon), bacterial enterocolitis (inflammation of the digestive tract resulting from bacterial infection), meningitis, or a history of liver disease UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS Loperamide is generally well tolerated; however, dry mouth, abdominal pain, distention or discomfort, nausea, vomiting, fatigue, flatulence, constipation, epigastric pain, drowsiness and dizziness may occur. Hypersensitivity reactions such as pruritus (itch), rashes, urticaria (hives) and extremely rare cases of anaphylactic shock, angioedema (rapid swelling beneath the skin) and bullous eruptions (large skin blisters) including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, erythema multiforme, and toxic epidermal necrolysis have also been reported. Paralytic ileus (temporary absence of intestinal movement), and toxic megacolon (rapid widening of the colon accompanied by infection or inflammation), urinary retention, hypertonia (abnormal increase in muscle tension), loss/depressed level of consciousness, coordination abnormality, and miosis (excessive constriction of the pupil of the eye) may rarely occur. WHAT OTHER MEDICINE OR FOOD SHOULD BE AVOIDED WHILE TAKING THIS MEDICINE?  Concomitant administration with the following may result in increased Loperamide plasma levels: - Ritonavir (used to treat Human Immunodeficiency Virus) P300000021910 DR-X6084 Reg. IPOPHIL - Quinidine (used to treat abnormal heart rhythms) - Oral desmopressin (used to treat excessive urination) - Itraconazole or ketoconazole (used to treat fungal infections) - Gemfibrozil (used to lower cholesterol)  Medicines that slow down the action of the stomach and intestines (e.g., some medicines for depression and cold and allergy medications) as these may increase the constipating effect of Loperamide  Medicines that cause drowsiness since drowsiness may occur in the setting of diarrheal syndromes treated with loperamide Tell your doctor about all medicines you are taking including supplements and herbal medicines. WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU MISS A DOSE? If you miss a dose, just take the next dose if still needed for diarrhea. Do not double the dose. HOW SHOULD YOU KEEP THIS MEDICINE? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Keep the product out of sight and reach of children. SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS OF OVERDOSE Loperamide overdose may result in constipation, central nervous system (CNS) depression (e.g., near unconscious- ness, coordination abnormality, drowsiness, respiratory depression), urinary retention and paralytic ileus. Children and patients with liver problems may be more sensitive to CNS effects. In adults, reported overdosage within 24 hours resulted in nausea after 40 mg (20 capsules) and vomiting after 60 mg (30 capsules). WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU HAVE TAKEN MORE THAN THE RECOMMENDED DOSAGE? If you have taken more than the recommended dosage (i.e., more than 8 capsules per day), consult a doctor or a Poison Control Center right away. CARE THAT SHOULD BE TAKEN WHEN TAKING THIS MEDICINE Fluid and electrolyte depletion often occur in patients who have diarrhea thus, appropriate fluid and electrolyte intake is very important. The use of loperamide does not substitute the need for appropriate fluid and electrolyte therapy. Although loperamide stops diarrhea, it will not treat the cause of it. Whenever possible, the cause of diarrhea should also be treated. Patients with diarrhea should drink plenty of water, clear fluids, unsweetened juices or clear soups. Alcohol or milk should be avoided, as well as fruits, green vegetables, and spicy or fatty foods since they tend to worsen diarrhea. Loss/depressed level of consciousness, tiredness, dizziness, or drowsiness may occur when diarrhea is treated with loperamide. Therefore, it is advisable to use caution when driving a car or operating machinery. Patients with Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome (AIDS) who are treated with loperamide for diarrhea should stop therapy at the earliest signs of abdominal distention. There have been isolated reports of toxic megacolon in AIDS patients with infectious colitis from both viral and bacterial pathogens treated with loperamide. Loperamide should be used with special caution in young children because of the greater variability of response in this age group. Dehydration, particularly in younger children, may further influence the variability of response to loperamide. Do not use after the expiry date on the label. WHEN SHOULD YOU CONSULT YOUR DOCTOR? Consult your doctor before taking this medicine if you:  Are pregnant or breastfeeding  Are currently taking an anti-infective medicine or other medicines  Have a history of liver disease Stop use and consult your doctor if:  Any undesirable effect occurs or if symptoms worsen  Acute diarrhea persists for more than two days  Abdominal swelling or bulging develops, or if fever develops Dehydration may develop if too much fluid has been lost by the body due to diarrhea. Consult a doctor if any of the following conditions develop:  Decreased urination  Dizziness and lightheadedness  Dryness of mouth  Increased thirst  Wrinkled skin For suspected adverse drug reaction, seek medical attention immediately and report to the FDA at www.fda.gov.ph AND Unilab at (+632) 858-1000 or productsafety@unilab.com.ph. By reporting undesirable effects, you can help provide more information on the safety of this medicine. AVAILABILITY: Box of 100 tablets (in aluminum foil strip pack x 4s) DATE OF REVISION OF PATIENT INFORMATION LEAFLET (PIL): Jun 2018 DATE OF FIRST AUTHORIZATION: 24 May 2007 Manufactured by Amherst Laboratories, Inc. UNILAB Pharma Campus, Barangay Mamplasan, Biñan, Laguna, Philippines for UNILAB, Inc. No. 66 United Street, Mandaluyong City, Metro Manila, Philippines [Page 2]",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:42:44.943660+00:00
