PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X6630,,,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1s9WDJMQSKzo6jpdGaEwhnEmWFzbXll_g/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X6630_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1s9WDJMQSKzo6jpdGaEwhnEmWFzbXll_g_view_usp_drivesdk_44b212f226.pdf,page_text/DR-X6630_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1s9WDJMQSKzo6jpdGaEwhnEmWFzbXll_g_view_usp_drivesdk_44b212f226.json,7d4286fddc01c59b5a271a2157a33d2d61ddc147df8bad399c43ffe6d4e30f1a,1222882,1,ocr,,,,,"d and Ni Y sulfate (N cs 1 25) 5 mg / 250 mcg per g (0.5% w/w / 0.025% w/w) Hypersensitivity reactions to Neomycin have been reported. Allergic cross-reactions may occur which could prevent future use of kanamycin, paromomycin and streptomycin Cream i is suitable for treating a wide variety of local inflammatory, pruritic, and allergic disorders of the skin and mucosa. They are indicated for topical application in the treatment of topical bacterial infections caused by organisms sensitive to Neomycin and when the anti-inflammatory and/or anti-allergic action of Fluocinolone",1,1,Indications:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:43:13.419863+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X6796,Chloramphenicol (as Palmitate),Penachlor,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rDGKwB3kjqUxsbMUdFka0lngUfR2gqVx/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X6796_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1rDGKwB3kjqUxsbMUdFka0lngUfR2gqVx_view_usp_drivesdk_705f6759de.pdf,page_text/DR-X6796_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1rDGKwB3kjqUxsbMUdFka0lngUfR2gqVx_view_usp_drivesdk_705f6759de.json,316502757043a612e64aa696bfa6290c10c6a867df5f85ffc8a294d1d022a069,2243269,1,ocr,,,,,,,,,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-06-29T18:43:22.496463+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X7096,Naproxen Sodium,Flanax,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1qFsb-ftu2C4PgEbT99a9GghqAzXOo11x/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X7096_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1qFsb-ftu2C4PgEbT99a9GghqAzXOo11x_view_usp_drivesdk_6e6f23233e.pdf,page_text/DR-X7096_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1qFsb-ftu2C4PgEbT99a9GghqAzXOo11x_view_usp_drivesdk_6e6f23233e.json,9542627d9032e68437edb16ea7c06fbdc139933dedf7818237d3a95200851d0c,4059557,1,digital_text,"Naproxen Sodium (Flanax®) is an opaque film-coated, modified oval blue tablet with engraved “TAISHO” in one side and Flanax on the other side. 2. What is in the medicine? This product contains Naproxen Sodium which belongs to a group of medicines called non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (also known as NSAIDs). Like other NSAIDs, Naproxen Sodium works by changing the body’s chemical response to pain, swelling and fever resulting in relief of symptoms of inflammation (e.g., swelling, redness) and relief of pain and/or fever. 3. Strength of the Medicine? One tablet contains 275 mg of the active ingredient, Naproxen sodium (or the sodium salt of naproxen) – made from a combination of Naproxen 250 mg and 25 mg sodium (of low content, equating to a slice of wheat bread). 4. What is this Medicine used for? N aproxen sodium (Flanax®) can be used to relieve headache, toothache, muscular ache and backache, pain of menstrual cramps (i.e., period pain), minor pain of arthritis, minor aches and pains associated with the common cold, and fever. 5. How much and how often should you use this Medicine? Each dos e should be taken with water on full stomach after eating. Adults: 1 tablet every 12 hours while symptoms persist. Elderly (>65): 1 tablet per day and no more than 2 tablets per day. Take Naproxen only if with pain and stop when pain is gone. Do not give this drug to children under 12 years old, except under the advice and supervision of a doctor. 6. When should you not take this Medicine? Sto mach and intestinal pain should not be treated with Naproxen Sodium (Flanax®). Naproxen Sodium (Flanax®) should not be taken by patients with: a) history of disease of the stomach or of the intestines (ulcer), b) blood coagulation disorders or are receiving drug therapy that interferes with blood coagulation; c) kidney impairment; d) allergy to Naproxen; e) allergy to aspirin or other anti-inflammatory and pai n relieving dru gs, f) histo ry of Ca rdiovascular Ar rest, Myocardial In farction, Co ronary Ar tery By pass Gr aft, un controlled hypertension, Congestive Heart Failure II-IV. Naproxen Sodium (Flanax®) can cause allergic reactions in patients allergic to aspirin and to other anti-inflammatory or pain-relieving drugs. 7. Care that should be taken when taking this Medicine? A doctor should be consulted if the pain persists or gets worse. A doctor or a pharmacist should be consulted if: a) any other drugs are taken on a regular basis; b) heartburn, upset stomach or stomach pain occurs; c) even mild symptoms persist; d) you suffer from another illness or any unusual symptoms occur, e) you are allergic. Naproxen Sodium (Flanax®) should not be taken for more than 10 consecutive days, unless directed by a doctor. Do not exceed the indicated dosage and immediately consult a doctor in the case of an overdose. 8. Undesirable Effects of this Medicine This medicine can cause varying degrees of inconvenient effects in certain people. The most frequent adverse events are – abdominal discomfort, stomach discomfort and heartburn, headache, nausea, vomiting, peripheral edema, allergy, ringing in the ears, skin rash, urticaria, pruritus, and dizziness. In case of unusual reactions, stop treatment immediately and consult your doctor. 9. What other medicine or food should be avoided while using this Medicine? - When taking Naproxen, ask your doctor before using other medications for fever, swelling or flu-like symptoms because they may contain chemicals similar to Naproxen (such as Ibuprofen, Aspirin, Diclofenac, etc) - Avoid taking other NSAIDs such as Aspirin, Ibuprofen, Diclofenac or Ketoprofen as these may increase the risk for gastrointestinal bl eeding if taken together with Naproxen - Avo id taking alcohol since this can also increase the risk for gastrointestinal bleeding - Co-administration with anticoagulants or blood thinning agents such as warfarin or dicumarol or antiplatelet drugs may increase the ris k of gastrointestinal bleeding - For patients on corticosteroids, taking Naproxen or any other NSAID increases the chance of gastrointestinal bleeding. This may be du e to steroids reducing gastric mucus production. - Naproxen may reduce or blunt the effect of some antihypertensive drugs such as ACE Inhibitors (Captopril, Enalapril, Lisinopril, etc.) and beta-blockers (Propranolol, Carvedilol, Metroprolol, etc.) and Angiotensin Receptor Blockers (Losartan, Telmisartan) - Naproxen may reduce the effect of some diuretics like thiazides and furosemide - Taking Naproxen with Lithium may increase the levels of Lithium in the body which may increase the risk of toxicity - Taking Cholestyramine with Naproxen may affect the absorption of Naproxen - Taking Methotrexate with Naproxen may increase the risk for toxicity for methotrexate - Combining antidepressants such as selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRI) like Fluoxetine, Citalopram, Paroxetine, etc. with Naproxen can increase the risk of GIT bleeding 10. What should you do if you miss a dose? If you skip or miss a dose, take the next dose particularly if there is still pain and inflammation and continue with your regular dosing schedule of every 8 to 12 hours. Do not take a double dose to make up for a missed one. 11. Signs and Symptoms of Overdose D rowsiness, heart burn, indigestion, nausea or vomiting 12. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? Should a patient ing est a la rge quantity of Naproxen Sodium (Flanax®) acc identally or pu rposefully, seek medical at tention immediately. The stomach may be emptied, and usu al suppo rtive measures em ployed. An imal studies indicate that the prompt administration of activated charcoal in adequate amounts would tend to reduce markedly the absorption of the drug. 13. How should you keep this Medicine? Naproxen Sodium (Flanax®) should be kept out of reach of children. Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Do not use Naproxen Sodium (Flanax®) after the expiry date shown on the carton or blister strip. 14. When should you consult your doctor? Fo r further information, consult your doctor or pharmacist who has detailed scientific information. 15. What are the commercially available forms of packaging? N aproxen Sodium (Flanax®) 275 mg tablet – Packs of 100 and 500 tablets in blister strips. 16. ADR Reporting Statement Fo r suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph. Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. 17. Registration Number DR-X7096 18. Date of Renewal of Authorization 09 January 2026 19. Date of Revision February 2026 20. Name and Address of Manufacturer, Marketing Authorization Holder, and Distributior Manufactured by: Interphil Laboratories, Inc. Canlubang Industrial Estate, Bo. Pittland, Cabuyao, Laguna For Taisho Pharmaceuticals (Philippines), Inc. 8 /F Clipp Center Bldg., 11 th Ave. cor. 39th St., Bonifacio Global City, Taguig City Under Authority of: Distributed by: Zuellig Pharma Corporation Km. 14 West Service Rd., South Superhighway cor. Edison Ave., Sun Valley, Para ñaque, Metro Manila 140 mm 275 mm Revised / 02-02-24 / Gener [Page 2]",1,1,1. Description of the Product,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:43:28.019913+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X7696,PARACETAMOL,CLOCEPHEN,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/17rPnCHShfkeFJkrX_0NDAQjkEOe9dv1n/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X7696_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_17rPnCHShfkeFJkrX_0NDAQjkEOe9dv1n_view_usp_drivesdk_eb56d5e52e.pdf,page_text/DR-X7696_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_17rPnCHShfkeFJkrX_0NDAQjkEOe9dv1n_view_usp_drivesdk_eb56d5e52e.json,72b09856198255cc7e0cdea1b3a089258e4bee021b575e573b568e82c3853b41,477019,1,digital_text,"White, round, ﬂat, beveled edge, uncoated tablet, bisected on one side, plain on the other. WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE? Paracetamol STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE 500 mg WHAT IS THIS MEDICINE USED FOR? For eﬀective relief of headaches, muscle and joint pains, toothaches, for lowering of fever, and for relief of pains and discomfort due to colds or inﬂuenza. HOW MUCH AND HOW OFTEN SHOULD YOU USE THIS MEDICINE? Adults: 1 to 2 tablets 3 to 4 times a day. WHEN SHOULD YOU NOT TAKE THIS MEDICINE? Do not take Paracetamol (Clocephen) if you have an allergy to paracetamol or if you are taking other paracetamol containing medicines. Check with your doctor or pharmacist before taking paracetamol if you have liver problems or kidney problems. CARE THAT SHOULD BE TAKEN WHEN TAKING THIS MEDICINE? Do not take Paracetamol (Clocephen) for fever more than 3 days unless directed by the physician. Do not use more than 5 days (in children) and 10 days (in adults) unless directed by the physician. UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS OF THIS MEDICINE The most common undesirable eﬀects of Paracetamol (Clocephen) include nausea, vomiting and constipation. WHAT OTHER MEDICINE OR FOOD SHOULD BE AVOIDED WHILE TAKING THIS MEDICINE? Do not use any other over-the-counter cold, cough, allergy or pain medications which contain paracetamol. Daily alcohol use, especially when combined with paracetamol, may damage the liver. Avoid alcohol. WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU MISS A DOSE? If you are using the medication regularly, take the missed dose as soon as you remember. If it is almost time for your next regularly scheduled dose, skip the missed dose and use your next dose as directed. Do not take extra medicine to make up for a missed dose. SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS OF OVERDOSE The ﬁrst signs of Paracetamol (Clocephen) overdose are loss of appetite, sweating, and confusion or weakness. Later symptoms may include pain in upper stomach, dark urine, and yellowing of skin or the whites of your eyes. WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU HAVE TAKEN MORE THAN THE RECOMMENDED DOSAGE? Get medical help right away if you have taken too much Paracetamol (Clocephen), even if you feel well. HOW SHOULD YOU KEEP THIS MEDICINE? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Keep out of reach and sight of children. WHEN SHOULD YOU CONSULT YOUR DOCTOR? Check with your doctor before taking paracetamol if you have liver or kidney problems.",1,1,DESCRIPTION OF THE PRODUCT,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:43:30.869364+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X8065,Baclofen,Lioresal,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1cwWseMZdu2LNl5KS7XsVvliexg2BEqY3/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X8065_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1cwWseMZdu2LNl5KS7XsVvliexg2BEqY3_view_usp_drivesdk_4cd8973e25.pdf,page_text/DR-X8065_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1cwWseMZdu2LNl5KS7XsVvliexg2BEqY3_view_usp_drivesdk_4cd8973e25.json,bb6913d498a9d84cde05f8e0096ba5448fc90c48f7cc4eb27c2d06d36d1e27e6,184669,11,digital_text,Pharmaceutical forms Each scored tablet contains 10 mg of baclofen. Active substance Baclofen. Excipients Silica colloidal anhydrous; cellulose microcrystalline; magnesium stearate; povidone; wheat starch. Pharmaceutical formulations may vary between countries.,1,1,DESCRIPTION AND COMPOSITION,"Adults Treatment of spasticity of the skeletal muscles in multiple sclerosis. Treatment of spastic conditions occurring in spinal-cord diseases of infectious, degenerative, traumatic, neoplastic, or unknown origin: e.g. spastic spinal paralysis, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, syringomyelia, transverse myelitis, traumatic paraplegia or paraparesis, and compression of the spinal cord; muscle spasm of cerebral origin, as well as following cerebrovascular accidents or in the presence of neoplastic or degenerative brain disease. Pediatric population (below 18 years) Baclofen (Lioresal®) is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of spasticity of cerebral origin, especially due to infantile cerebral palsy, as well as following cerebrovascular accidents or in the presence of neoplastic or degenerative brain disease. Baclofen [Page 2] Baclofen (Lioresal®) is also indicated for the symptomatic treatment of muscle spasms occurring in spinal cord diseases of infectious, degenerative, traumatic, neoplastic, or unknown origin such as multiple sclerosis, spa stic spinal paralysis, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, syringomyelia, transverse myelitis, traumatic paraplegia or paraparesis, and compression of the spinal cord.",1,2,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T18:43:34.353979+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X8475,Benzylpenicillin Sodium,None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/18FutyWdSWp6qnfzEfm6rT8C_A0N9zdtf/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X8475_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18FutyWdSWp6qnfzEfm6rT8C_A0N9zdtf_view_usp_drivesdk_2804abbe0e.pdf,page_text/DR-X8475_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18FutyWdSWp6qnfzEfm6rT8C_A0N9zdtf_view_usp_drivesdk_2804abbe0e.json,95e9659d47fecccc4ba1deab7e70141367e8c9716f3f81463862b63871477b47,833049,2,digital_text,"Benzylpenicillin (as sodium) 1,000,000 Units (600 mg) is a white crystalline powder and odorless or practically so. It is supplied in a clear and colorless glass vial with rubber stopper and green aluminum seal. Benzylpenicillin (as sodium) 5,000,000 Units (3 g) is a white crystalline powder and odorless or practically so. It is supplied in a glass vial with rubber stopper and aluminum seal. Penicillin is an antibiotic substance of remarkable potency against a variety of pathogens. Penicillin G, from which all other penicillins have been separated, is supplied as the sodium salt in crystalline form. Its potency is expressed in terms of units, 60 mg of crystalline Penicillin G sodium being equivalent to 100,000 units. As defined in the USP, 1 unit represents the antibiotic activity of 0.6 microgram of C H N NaO S and approximately equivalent to the earlier Oxford unit.16 17 2 4 PHARMACODYNAMICS It exerts its killing action on growing and dividing bacteria by inhibiting bacterial cell-wall synthesis, although the mechanisms involved are still not precisely understood. Bacterial cell walls are held rigid and protected against osmotic rupture by peptidoglycan. Benzylpenicillin inhibits the final cross-linking stage of peptidoglycan production by binding to and inactivating transpeptidases, penicillin-binding proteins on the inner surface of the bacterial cell membrane. However, it is now realised that other earlier stages in cell-wall synthesis can also be inhibited. Other mechanisms involved include bacterial lysis by the inactivation of endogenous inhibitors of bacterial autolysins. Its action is inhibited by penicillinase and other beta-lactamases that are produced during the growth of certain micro-organisms. Many Gram-negative organisms are intrinsically resistant by virtue of the inability of benzylpenicillin to penetrate their outer membranes. Intrinsic resistance can also be due to structural differences in the target penicillin-binding proteins. Spectrum of activity The following pathogenic organisms are usually sensitive to benzylpenicillin: • Gram-positive aerobes and anaerobes including Bacillus anthracis, Clostridium perfringens, Clostridium tetani, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae, Listeria monocytogenes, Peptostreptococcus spp., non-beta- lactamase-producing staphylococci, and streptococci including Streptococcus agalactiae (group B), Str. pneumoniae (pneumococci), Str. pyogenes (group A), and some viridans streptococci; enterococci are relatively insensitive. • Gram-negative cocci including Neisseria meningitidis (meningococci) and Neisseria gonorrhoeae (gonococci), although beta- lactamase-producing strains are common. • Gram-negative bacilli including Pasteurella multocida, Streptobacillus moniliformis, and Spirillum minus (or minor); most Gram- negative bacilli, including Pseudomonas spp. and Enterobacteriaceae, are insensitive although some strains of Proteus mirabilis and Escherichia coli may be inhibited by high concentrations of benzylpenicillin. • Gram-negative anaerobes including Prevotella (non-fragilis Bacteroides) and Fusobacterium spp. • Other organisms including Actinomyces and the spirochaetes, Borrelia, Leptospira, and Treponema spp. • Mycobacteria, fungi, mycoplasmas, and rickettsias are not sensitive. PHARMACOKINETICS Absorption Benzylpenicillin rapidly appears in the blood after intramuscular injection of water-soluble salts, and maximum concentrations are usually reached in 15 to 30 minutes; peak plasma concentrations of about 12 micrograms/ mL have been reported after single doses of 600 mg. When given orally, benzylpenicillin is inactivated fairly rapidly by gastric acid and only up to about 30% is absorbed, mainly from the duodenum; maximum plasma-penicillin concentrations usually occur in about 1 hour. In order to attain plasma-penicillin concentrations after oral use similar to those after intramuscular injection, up to 5 times as much benzylpenicillin may be necessary. Absorption varies greatly in different individuals and is better in patients with reduced gastric acid production, including neonates and the elderly. Food decreases the absorption of benzylpenicillin and oral doses are best given at least half an hour before or 2 to 3 hours after a meal. Distribution Benzylpenicillin is widely distributed at varying concentrations in body tissues and fluids. It appears in pleural, pericardial, peritoneal, and synovial fluids, but in the absence of inflammation diffuses only to a small extent into abscess cavities, avascular areas, the eye, the middle ear, and the CSF. Inflamed tissue is, however, more readily penetrated and, for example, in meningitis higher concentrations of benzylpenicillin are achieved in the CSF. Active transport out of the CSF is reduced by probenecid. In patients with uraemia, other organic acids may accumulate in the CSF and compete with benzylpenicillin for active transport; toxic concentrations of benzylpenicillin sufficient to cause convulsions can result. Benzylpenicillin diffuses across the placenta into the fetal circulation, and small amounts appear in breast milk. The plasma half-life is about 30 minutes, although it may be longer in neonates and the elderly because of reduced renal function. In renal impairment the half-life may be increased to about 10 hours. Approximately 60% is reported to be bound to plasma protein. Metabolism Benzylpenicillin is metabolised to a limited extent and the penicilloic acid derivative has been recovered in the urine. Excretion Benzylpenicillin is rapidly excreted in the urine, principally by tubular secretion and about 20% of a dose given by mouth appears unchanged in the urine; about 60 to 90% of a dose of aqueous benzylpenicillin given intramuscularly appears in the urine, mainly within the first hour. Significant concentrations are achieved in bile, but in patients with normal renal function only small amounts are excreted via the bile. Benzylpenicillin is removed by haemodialysis. Renal tubular secretion is inhibited by probenecid, which sometimes given to increase plasma-penicillin concentrations.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Penicillin is useful chiefly in the treatment of staphylococci, streptococci, and pneumococci infections, clostridial infections (but only conjunction with antitoxin and appropriate surgery); syphilis; anthrax; gonococci infections, meningococci infections (as an adjunct to sulfonamide therapy); erysipeloid; as an adjunct to treatment to Vincent’s infection (along with appropriate dental procedures); leptospirosis; relapsing fever; diphtheria (but only in conjunction with antitoxin); susceptible actinomycotic infections (in conjunction with other therapy); Streptobacillus moniliformis and Spirillum minus infections. Penicillin is also useful prophylactically in major and minor surgery to prevent secondary infection. This is especially important in tonsillectomies and tooth extractions in patients with rheumatic fever or rheumatic or congenital heart disease. Penicillin is of doubtful value against mixed infections wherein Gram-negative pathogens predominate, and is not effective against Gram-negative bacilliary infections, virus infections, protozoan infections, most fungal infections, nonspecific inflammatory conditions, tuberculosis, and neoplastic diseases. DIRECTION FOR RECONSTITUTION Dissolve the penicillin aseptically in a suitable sterile, pyrogen-free solvent (Water for Injection USP, Sterile Isotonic Sodium Chloride Solution for Parenteral Use USP or 5% Dextrose Injection USP). The volume of solvent to be added can be calculated by dividing the total units in the vial by the number of units per mL desired on the final solutions. Intermittent intramuscular injection is usually preferred. The resulting reconstituted product is clear solution essentially free from particles of foreign matter that can be observed on visual inspection.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T18:43:37.562327+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X885,Furosemide,FRUSEMA,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CQO3C7nNUXdBr3F_IYc0mZ_U3SOLOo3V/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X885_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1CQO3C7nNUXdBr3F_IYc0mZ_U3SOLOo3V_view_usp_drivesdk_7dc0d8df17.pdf,page_text/DR-X885_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1CQO3C7nNUXdBr3F_IYc0mZ_U3SOLOo3V_view_usp_drivesdk_7dc0d8df17.json,55a4766c0025f3b07a768d706428660b0fe64aa4949a4675a263936b79dadf8d,6212906,2,digital_text,"Furosemide (Frusema ®) 10 mg/mL (20 mg/2 mL) Solution for Injection is a clear, colorless to slightly yellow, sterile, nonpyrogenic solution in 2 mL amber ampule. CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY PHARMACODYNAMICS Furosemide inhibits primarily the absorption of sodium and chloride not only in the proximal and distal tubules but also in the loop of Henle. The high degree of efficacy is largely due to the unique site of action. The action on the distal tubule is independent of any inhibitory effect on carbonic anhydrase and aldosterone. Furosemide diuresis results in enhanced excretion of sodium, chloride, potassium, hydrogen, calcium, magnesium, ammonia, bicarbonate, and possibly phosphate. Urinary pH usually decreases after furosemide administration; however, increased bicarbonate excretion in some patients may temporarily increase urinary pH. Low doses of furosemide promote uric acid retention while large intravenous (IV) doses may cause temporary uricosuria. Maximum diuresis and electrolyte loss is greater with furosemide than with the thiazide diuretics. The effectiveness of furosemide, like the thiazide diuretics, is independent of the acid-base balance of the patient. The diuretic response to furosemide is similar after oral or IV administration in patients with normal renal function. However, in uremic patients, diuresis and urinary excretion of sodium and potassium are greater after IV administration than after similar oral furosemide doses. As with other diuretics, furosemide’s blood pressure lowering effect may result from decreased plasma volume. However, furosemide produces only mild decreases in the supine systolic blood pressure and in the erect systolic and diastolic blood pressures when administered alone in the recommended oral dosage. Furosemide appears to have less effect on carbohydrate metabolism and blood glucose concentrations than do the thiazides; however, furosemide may cause increases in blood glucose, glycosuria and alterations in glucose tolerance possibly as a result of hypokalemia. After IV administration of furosemide, diuresis occurs within 5 minutes, peaks within 20 to 60 minutes and persists for about 2 hours. After intramuscular (IM) administration, onset of diuresis occurs later than after IV administration since peak plasma concentrations are attained only after 30 minutes. The diuretic response may be prolonged in patients with severely impaired renal function. PHARMACOKINETICS Furosemide is extensively bound to plasma proteins, mainly to albumin. Plasma concentrations ranging from 1 to 400 mcg/mL are 91 to 99% bound in healthy individuals. The unbound fraction averages 2.3 to 4.1% at therapeutic concentrations. Recent evidence suggests that furosemide glucuronide is the only, or at least the major, bio-transformation product of furosemide in man. In patients with normal renal function, approximately 80% of an IV or IM dose is excreted in the urine within 24 hours. Urinary excretion is accomplished both by glomerular filtration and proximal tubular secretion, which accounts for roughly 66% of the ingested dose, the remainder being excreted in the feces. A small fraction is metabolized by cleavage of the side chain. The terminal half-life of furosemide is approximately two hours. Significantly more furosemide is excreted in urine after the IV injection than after the tablet or oral solution. Special Population: Renal/Hepatic Impairment: In patients with marked renal impairment without liver disease, hepatic and fecal clearance of furosemide is increased so that up to 98% of the drug is removed from the plasma within 24 hours. Where liver disease is present, biliary elimination is reduced up to 50%. Elderly: The elimination of furosemide is delayed in the elderly where a certain degree of renal impairment is present. Carbenoxolone, corticosteroids, prolonged use of laxatives or ingestion of licorice in large amounts: May predispose a patient to hypokalemia Chlorothiazide: Concurrent use of chlorothiazide and furosemide has been reported to decrease hypercalciuria and dissolve some calculi Ciclosporin: Increases risk of gouty arthritis secondary to furosemide-induced hyperuricemia and ciclosporin impairment of renal urate excretion Curare-type muscle relaxants: Furosemide may potentiate the effects of these drugs. Digitalis and medicines inducing QT intervals prolongation syndrome: May be potentiated by changes in electrolyte concentrations (e.g., hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia) due to furosemide Ethacrynic acid, cisplatin: Should not be used concomitantly because of the possibility of ototoxicity. Ganglionic or peripheral adrenergic blockers: Furosemide potentiates the effects of these drugs. Indomethacin: Reduces natriuretic effect and antihypertensive effect of furosemide by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis. Closely observe patients receiving indomethacin and furosemide. Lithium: Should not be given with diuretics since they reduce lithium’s renal clearance and add a high risk of lithium toxicity Methotrexate, probenecid, reboxetine and other medicines which undergo significant renal tubular secretion: Reduce the effects of furosemide. Conversely, furosemide may decrease renal elimination of these medicines. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs, e.g., aspirin): Reduce creatinine clearance in patients with chronic renal insufficiency.There were reports of patients who developed increased BUN, serum creatinine and serum potassium levels, and weight gain when furosemide was used in conjunction with NSAIDs. Norepinephrine: Furosemide may decrease arterial responsiveness; however, norepinephrine may still be used effectively. Phenytoin: Attenuates the effect of furosemide Risperidone: Exercise caution; the risks and benefits of the combination or cotreatment with furosemide or with other potent diuretics should be considered prior to the decision to use. There was no increased incidence of mortality among patients taking other diuretics as concomitant treatment with risperidone. Irrespective of treatment, dehydration was an overall risk factor for mortality and should therefore be avoided in elderly patients with dementia. Salicylates (high doses): Salicylate toxicity at lower doses because of competitive renal excretory site Succinylcholine: Furosemide may potentiate the action of succinylcholine Sucralfate: Reduces the natriuretic effect and antihypertensive effect of furosemide. The intake of furosemide and sucralfate should be separated by at least 2 hours. Theophylline: Furosemide potentiates the effects of theophylline Tubocurarine: Furosemide (dependent on the dose may potentiate or antagonize the skeletal muscle relaxing effect of tubocurarine Other antihypertensive drugs: Furosemide may add to or potentiate the therapeutic effect of other antihypertensive drug. Furosemide combined with angiotensin II converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors or angiotensin II receptor blockers may lead to severe hypotension and deterioration in renal function, including renal failure. STATEMENT ON USAGE FOR HIGH RISK GROUPS Pregnancy: Pregnancy Category C. Furosemide must not be given during pregnancy unless there are compelling medical reasons. Treatment during pregnancy requires monitoring of fetal growth. Thiazides, related diuretics and loop diuretics enter the fetal circulation and may cause electrolyte disturbances. There have been reports of neonatal thrombocytopenia associated with thiazides and related diuretics. Loop diuretics, like furosemide, are probably also associated with this risk. During the latter part of pregnancy, products of this type should only be given on sound indications and in the lowest effective dose. In pregnancy, furosemide must only be used in patients with a marked reduction in glomerular filtration. Lactation: Furosemide is excreted in breast milk. Thus, the mother should discontinue breastfeeding when furosemide therapy is indicated. Infants: A sustained diuretic effect is seen in the newborn, possibly due to immature tubular function. Renal calcifications (from barely visible on x-ray to staghorn) have occurred in some severely premature infants treated with intravenous furosemide for edema due to patent ductus arteriosus and hyaline membrane disease. Elderly: In general, dose selection for an elderly patient should be cautious, usually starting at the low end of the dosing range, reflecting the greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and of concomitant disease or other drug therapy. UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS Body as a whole: Excessive sweating, fever, headache, malaise Cardiovascular: Cardiac arrhythmias, circulatory collapse, disorders of cardiac rhythm, hypotension, ischemic complications in the elderly Dermatologic and hypersensitivity reactions: Allergic reaction, anaphylactic shock, angioedema, bullous pemphigoid, dermatitis, erythema multiforme, exacerbation or activation of systemic lupus erythematosus, exfoliative dermatitis, itching, necrotizing angiitis, photosensitivity, pruritus, purpura, rash, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, systemic vasculitis, toxic epidermal necrolysis, urticaria, transient pain at injection site after intramuscular injection Gastrointestinal: Anorexia, constipation, cramping, dehydration, diarrhea, dry mouth, gastrointestinal drainage, nausea, oral and gastric irritation, vomiting Genitourinary: Acute urinary retention with overflow incontinence in elderly men; decreased glomerular filtration rate (GFR), increased risk of persistence of patent ductus arteriosus, calcium depletion and nephrocalcinosis/nephrolitiasis in premature infants; interstitial nephritis; obstructed micturition (e.g., uretostenosis, hydronephrosis), urinary bladder spasm; urinary frequency; prostatic hyperplasia; transient increases in creatinine and blood urea nitrogen (BUN). Hematologic: Agranulocytosis (rare), anemia, aplastic anemia (rare), bone marrow depression (rare), eosinophilia, hemoconcentration, hemolytic anemia, hypovolemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, thrombophilia, thrombophlebitis Hepatobiliary: Distention of biliary tree which is already obstructed. hepatic encephalopathy in patients with hepatocellular insufficiency, increased liver transaminase and bile flow, intrahepatic cholestatic jaundice, jaundice, liver dysfunction, pancreatitis Metabolic: Fluid and electrolyte imbalance; glycosuria; gout; hyperglycemia; hyperuricemia; hypokalemia; hypomagnesemia; increased insulin requirements in diabetic patients; latent diabetes mellitus; aggravated pre-existing metabolic alkalosis (e.g., due to compensated liver cirrhosis); alterations in glucose tolerance possibly as a result of hypokalemia; Elevations in the following: blood glucose, serum cholesterol, triglyceride, total cholesterol, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol, very low density lipoprotein cholesterol, serum uric acid levels Musculoskeletal: Calf muscle spasm, flank and loin pain, muscle spasm, weakness Nervous: Apathy, confusion, dizziness, drowsiness, lightheadedness, neuromuscular irritability, paresthesia, restlessness, tetany, vertigo, xanthopsia Special senses: Blurred vision, visual impairment, deafness, reversible hearing impairment and tinnitus (rare), permanent tinnitus and impairment of hearing in patients with markedly reduced renal function or hypoproteinemia (e.g., in nephrotic syndrome) OVERDOSE AND TREATMENT Manifestations of furosemide overdosage include dehydration, blood volume reduction, hypotension or severe hypotension (progressing to shock), electrolyte imbalance, cardiac arrhythmias (including atrioventricular block and ventricular fibrillation), hypokalemia and hypochloremic alkalosis, acute renal failure, thrombosis, delirious states, flaccid paralysis, apathy, confusion and extensions of its diuretic action. Treatment is supportive and consists of replacement of excessive fluid and electrolyte losses. Gastric lavage may be useful if ingestion is recent. Serum electrolytes, carbon dioxide level and blood pressure must be frequently determined. Adequate drainage must be assured in patients with urinary bladder outlet obstruction such as prostatic hypertrophy. Hemodialysis does not accelerate furosemide elimination. ADVERSE DRUG REACTION REPORTING STATEMENT For suspected adverse drug reaction, seek medical attention immediately and report to the FDA at www.fda.gov .ph AND RiteMED at (+632) 726-0835 or productsafety@ritemed.com.ph. By reporting undesirable effects, you can help provide more information on the safety of this medicine. CAUTION Foods, Drugs, Devices, and Cosmetics Act prohibits dispensing without prescription. Keep the product out of reach and sight of children. Store at temperatures not exceeding 30 o",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Adults: Usual Initial Dose: 20 to 40 mg as a single dose, administered IM or IV • If needed, another dose may be administered in the same manner 2 hours later, or the dose may be increased by 20 mg, and given no sooner than 2 hours after the previous dose, until the desired diuretic effect has been achieved. • This individually determined effective single dose may then be given once or twice a day. Adults: Usual Initial Dose: 40 mg administered by slow IV • If satisfactory response does not occur within 1 hour, increase dose to 80 mg administered by slow IV Infants and Children: Usual Initial Dose: 1 mg/kg administered by slow IV or IM • For resistant forms of edema, the initial dose may be increased by 1 mg/kg not more often than every 2 hours until the desired effect has been achieved. • Adequate response is usually obtained with individual parenteral doses of 1 mg/kg, but occasionally individual doses of 2 mg/kg may be required. • Maximum individual parenteral dose is 6 mg/kg body weight; however, the potential risks associated with large parenteral doses of furosemide must be considered and patient must be monitored closely. • Total Maximum Daily Dose: 20 mg",2,2,INDICATIONS RECOMMENDED FUROSEMIDE IV / IM DOSE,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T18:43:42.269193+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X9017,Diphenhydramine Hydrochloride,None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1GmjnDR3cOPs5t6F_5wjIYjYIn6f0t_K5/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X9017_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1GmjnDR3cOPs5t6F_5wjIYjYIn6f0t_K5_view_usp_drivesdk_ec49e878bc.pdf,page_text/DR-X9017_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1GmjnDR3cOPs5t6F_5wjIYjYIn6f0t_K5_view_usp_drivesdk_ec49e878bc.json,b6424230ebdd4014bf4862aa0f818067bf19b778cfbec821458cc549d786a6f3,1430617,1,ocr,"Diphenhydramine Hydrochloride 50 mg/mL Solution for Injection is a clear, colorless solution.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Diphenhydramine may be employed parenterally in serum reactions, severe angioneurotic edema, acute urticaria, acute drug reactions, and other emergency or exceptional situations.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-29T18:43:49.216260+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-X9620,GUAIFENESIN,GUAIACOF,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/17MPWMe4QSs-6VAJKdhFmOUCA1iH-ciOP/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-X9620_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_17MPWMe4QSs-6VAJKdhFmOUCA1iH-ciOP_view_usp_drivesdk_8196fafa80.pdf,page_text/DR-X9620_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_17MPWMe4QSs-6VAJKdhFmOUCA1iH-ciOP_view_usp_drivesdk_8196fafa80.json,8832b10f9cfc2c9fcf74c0f3b7159085a1f437ed70604b1327fc4cf2d3c9fb63,257699,1,digital_text,"Guaifenesin (Guaiacof) 100 mg/5 mL is a clear reddish oral solution with strawberry flavor. What is in the Medicine? This medicine contains Guaifenesin , an expectorant that helps loosen and relieve congestion in the chest and thus making the phlegm easier to cough out and expel. Strength of the Medicine: Each 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) oral solution contains: Guaifenesin ……………………………………………………….. 100 mg What is this medicine used for? This medicine is used as an expectorant for productive cough. It is used to reduce chest congestion caused by the common colds, infections or allergies. How much and how often should you use this medicine? Adult: 200 mg to 400 mg every 4 hours. Children: To be given every 4 hours 6 to 12 years – 5 mL (1 tsp) to 10 mL (2 tsp) 2 to 6 years – 2.5 mL (1/2 tsp) to 5 mL (1 tsp) Or as prescribed by the physician. When should you NOT take this medicine? * If you are allergic to the product and to any of its ingredients. * If you are taking any other medicines to stop you coughing. * If you are wheezing or if you are having an asthma attack. * If the seal of this medicine is broken. *If you have medical conditions such as liver disease, kidney disease or phenylketonuria (PKU). Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: *Take this medicine exactly as directed on the label or as prescribed by your doctor. *Do not use this medicine in larger amounts or for more than recommended. *Ask your doctor before using any other cold or cough medicines when taking guaifenesin. *Always ask a doctor before giving this medicine to a child. *Drink extra fluids to help loosen the phlegm and lubricate your throat while you are taking this medicine. *Do not use this medicine after the expiry date on the label. * Do not drive, operate machinery or perform other hazardous activities until you know how guaifenesin affects you. *Ask your doctor if you are pregnant or breastfeeding before taking this medicine. *Take this medicine with food if it upsets your stomach. What are the undesirable effects of this medicine? *Severe allergic reactions which include : difficulty in breathing, swelling of the face, ,neck, tongue or throat *Dizziness, headache *Nausea, vomiting, stomach upset *Drowsiness *Skin Rash What other medicine or food should be avoided while taking this medicine? Tell your doctor about all medicines you are using and medicines you start or stop using. Other drugs may interact with guaifenesin, including prescription and over-the-counter drugs, vitamins and herbal products. What should you do if you miss a dose of this medicine? If you miss a dose, leave it out and take the next dose whenever it is due. After that, just carry on as before. Do not take double the dose. If you have any further questions about these oral syrup, ask your doctor or pharmacist. What are the signs and symptoms of overdose? Gastro-intestinal discomfort has occasionally been reported with Guaifenesin. Very large doses cause nausea and vomiting. What should you do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? Contact your doctor, or hospital immediately. Bring the container or label with you to help doctors with necessary information. How should you keep/ store this medicine? Keep out of the reach and sight of children. Do not use Ascorbic Acid capsule after the expiry date which is stated on the label. They should be stored at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Keep in original container to protect from light. Medicines should n ot be disposed of via wastewater or household waste. Ask your pharmacist how to dispose of medicines no longer required. These measures will help to protect the environment. When should you consult your doctor? *If the cough lasts for more than 7 days or returns back. *If symptoms still occur. * If you get any of the side effects * If this medicine is not having the same effect as before. Name and Address of the Manufacturer and Marketing Authorization Holder: St. Martin Pharm Lab Co. 55 Lakandula Street, Parang, Marikina City, Philippines Registration Number: DR-X9620 Date of First Authorization/ Renewal of the Authorization: 12/27/2012 / 12/27/2024 Date of Revision of Patient Information Leaflet: 9/16/2019 “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph” Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. GUAIFENESIN [Page 2]",1,1,Description of the Product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:43:52.072670+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY10007,,,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZvHSYOMEKVzQfkcit63OI_tlefQEpvJC/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY10007_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ZvHSYOMEKVzQfkcit63OI_tlefQEpvJC_view_usp_drivesdk_9dd693956f.pdf,page_text/DR-XY10007_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ZvHSYOMEKVzQfkcit63OI_tlefQEpvJC_view_usp_drivesdk_9dd693956f.json,4a71f8c071dc595e2524965153291a5670f06380995882533536ef7a3c8ca50d,697414,1,digital_text,"RiteMED® Diphenhydramine Hydrochloride 12.5 mg / 5 mL Elixir: Light yellow, clear syrup, orange- WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE? This medicine contains diphenhydramine HCl, an antihistamine with anticholinergic (drying) and sedative effects. Diphenhydramine blocks the action of histamine, a substance that causes allergic symptoms. Diphenhydramine belongs to a group of medicines called sedating antihistamines, which help you to sleep. It is effective in reducing sleep onset (i.e., time to fall asleep) and increasing the depth and quality of sleep. It also acts as a cough suppressant (antitussive) through its effect on the cough center in the brain. As an antiemetic, diphenhydramine acts by suppressing the vomiting center in the brain. STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:43:55.594857+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY10083,Mefenamic Acid,Mefenax,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1TKyewmlmbO4aVlfbguyVygogRkCzVPjC/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY10083_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1TKyewmlmbO4aVlfbguyVygogRkCzVPjC_view_usp_drivesdk_0dc197a69a.pdf,page_text/DR-XY10083_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1TKyewmlmbO4aVlfbguyVygogRkCzVPjC_view_usp_drivesdk_0dc197a69a.json,2e5a9cb39457e9c83cb09417e0fc1caff1bc8a7847c403db4ed17230744c7d35,462171,1,ocr,"Hypoglycemic agents: There have been reports of changes in the effects of oral Mefenamic Acid 500 mg Tablet is a white, oval, biconvex, film-coated tablet, bisected hypoglycemic agents in the presence of NSAIDs. Therefore, Mefenamic acid should be administered with caution in patients receiving insulin or oral hypoglycemic agents. on one side, plain on the other. Lithium: Mefenamic acid has produced an elevation of plasma lithium levels and a",1,1,DESCRIPTION:,"Gastric irritation is infrequent and may be minimized by taking medications with meals. For the relief of pain including muscular, arthritic conditions, traumatic, dental post-surgi- Diarrhea has been observed when Mefenamic acid was given in higher than cal and postpartum pain; headache, and fever. Also, for the relief of primary dys- recommended doses and/or long-term continuous therapy. Skin rash has occurred but menorrhea. disappeared on withdrawal of medication. Rarely, thrombocytopenia and reversible hemolytic anemia have been reported with Mefenamic acid.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-29T18:44:05.664385+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY10083,Mefenamic Acid,Mefenax,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ukEvqW1JtiivpKT6xon0l_pwAzHVH__A/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY10083_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ukEvqW1JtiivpKT6xon0l_pwAzHVH__A_view_usp_drivesdk_487cc0258b.pdf,page_text/DR-XY10083_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ukEvqW1JtiivpKT6xon0l_pwAzHVH__A_view_usp_drivesdk_487cc0258b.json,8715a9ce6a69a611b74d7037f43a4241a1b50456d419e53971cc8ec07244e8d9,1143480,2,digital_text,,1,1,DESCRIPTION:,,1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-06-29T18:44:09.745640+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY12411,Pilocarpine Hydrochloride,Isopto Carpine,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1gswILfHbsLC22v9okYSp35otT-tC0TAU/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY12411_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1gswILfHbsLC22v9okYSp35otT-tC0TAU_view_usp_drivesdk_04a7d8675c.pdf,page_text/DR-XY12411_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1gswILfHbsLC22v9okYSp35otT-tC0TAU_view_usp_drivesdk_04a7d8675c.json,3e04f12d2e6a0539dfbd2b9c48e579a828fa1d1a673be7ae7bf6c425cae78b9c,60867,4,digital_text,PILOCARPINE hydrochloride (ISOPTO* CARPINE) is a cholinergic prepared as a sterile topical ophthalmic solution.,1,1,DESCRIPTION:,"Pilocarpine hydrochlor ide is a miotic (parasympathomimetic) used to control intraocular pressure. It may be used in combination with other miotics, beta blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, sympathomimetics or hyperosmotic agents.",1,1,INDICATIONS AND USAGE:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T18:44:12.964143+00:00
