PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY18411,Dicycloverine Hydrochloride,Dicymed,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1pQUWhNIDK9eBCKil_n3IHI4C18EMngL0/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY18411_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1pQUWhNIDK9eBCKil_n3IHI4C18EMngL0_view_usp_drivesdk_76bbc14d9c.pdf,page_text/DR-XY18411_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1pQUWhNIDK9eBCKil_n3IHI4C18EMngL0_view_usp_drivesdk_76bbc14d9c.json,32f711fe4571ca03facc401700284c633a5ff7107544ef7dbd16f800a05f8897,102020,2,digital_text,"Dicycloverine (Dicymed) 10 mg Tablet is an off-white tablet, round, biconvex, bisected on one side and plain on the other side. Dicycloverine (Dicymed) 10 mg/5 mL Syrup is a clear, very light-yellow syrup.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,Dicycloverine is used to treat a certain type of intestinal problem called irritable bowel syndrome. It helps to reduce the symptoms of stomach and intestinal cramping.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T18:52:08.024606+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY18526,Tretinoin,Vesanoid,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1s9Y7tNWrCpymMDhqePRVH4XB9w_U6OqO/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY18526_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1s9Y7tNWrCpymMDhqePRVH4XB9w_U6OqO_view_usp_drivesdk_b6dc1a6348.pdf,page_text/DR-XY18526_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1s9Y7tNWrCpymMDhqePRVH4XB9w_U6OqO_view_usp_drivesdk_b6dc1a6348.json,350640128d56531b35db557bb4d9f2c776155f0d16a93a942b4f226475d048d3,684301,2,digital_text,"Tretinoin (Vesanoid) soft capsules are oval, bicoloured, orange-yellow and reddish-brown. 3. PHARMACODYNAMICS Tretinoin is a natural metabolite of retinol and belongs to the class of retinoids, comprising natural and synthetic analogues. Mechanism of action According to FAB (French-American-British) classification of haematological disease, acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL) is classified as M3 and M3v form of acute myeloid leukaemia",1,1,2. PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Tretinoin (Vesanoid) is indicated in combination with arsenic trioxide or chemotherapy for treatment of patients with acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL) which is newly diagnosed, relapsed or refractory to chemotherapy (see section 3 and 6). Treatment regimens Combination of tretinoin with chemotherapy or arsenic trioxide is known to be effective and to induce very high rates of haematologic remission in patients with genetically confirmed APL, i.e. patients whose blasts harbour the t(15;17) by karyotyping or FISH or the PML-RARa fusion as detected by PCR. Thus, genetic confirmation of diagnosis is mandatory. Combination treatment with arsenic trioxide has been shown an effective treatment option in patients with newly diagnosed low-to-intermediate risk APL. However, because APL is characterised by high risk of early haemorrhagic death, current recommendations dictate that early treatment with tretinoin is started as soon as possible upon morphologic suspicion only. For the selection of treatment strategy, the relapse risk – indicated by pre-therapeutic white blood cell count (WBC) and platelet count (Sanz score) with high-risk (WBC > 10 x 109/L), intermediate risk (WBC ≤ 10 x 109/L, platelet count ≤ 40 x 109/L), and low risk (WBC ≤ 10 x 10 9/L, platelet count > 40 x 109/L) – should be taken into consideration.",1,1,5. INDICATIONS AND USAGE,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T18:52:11.168452+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY18554,Ofloxacin,Qinolon,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ySEs0Fge7iEOTldDycTmnTyz2jpIi4JO/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY18554_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ySEs0Fge7iEOTldDycTmnTyz2jpIi4JO_view_usp_drivesdk_10047e55ec.pdf,page_text/DR-XY18554_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ySEs0Fge7iEOTldDycTmnTyz2jpIi4JO_view_usp_drivesdk_10047e55ec.json,4d0ca22af62c436f1eddac488e79b20229338de11b56467eaa36c3d43e9271a1,754767,2,digital_text,"200 mg Film-coated Tablet: Beige, standard biconvex film-coated tablet, bisected on one side and plain on the other side 400 mg Film-coated Tablet: Beige, round film-coated tablet, bisected on one side and plain on the other side CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY PHARMACODYNAMICS Ofloxacin is a synthetic broad-spectrum fluoroquinolone antimicrobial agent which inhibits DNA topoisomerase, more commonly referred to as the DNA gyrase. DNA gyrase is necessary for bacterial DNA replication and some aspects of transcription, repair, recombination, and transposition. Inhibition of DNA gyrase in susceptible microorganisms results in the inhibition of ATP-dependent negative supercoiling of DNA, inhibition of ATP-independent relaxation of supercoiled DNA, and promotion of double-stranded DNA breakage resulting in bacterial cell death. ANTIMICROBIAL SPECTRUM OF ACTIVITY Ofloxacin is active in vitro and in clinical infections against most strains of the following microorganisms: *As with other drugs in this class, some strains of Pseudomonas aeruginosa may develop resistance fairly rapidly during treatment with ofloxacin. Ofloxacin has been shown to be active in vitro against most strains of the following microorganisms; however, the clinical significance of these data is unknown: Many strains of other streptococcal species, Enterococcus species, and anaerobes are resistant to ofloxacin. It is suggested to carry out susceptibility tests. PHARMACOKINETICS After oral administration, maximum serum ofloxacin concentration is achieved within 0.5 to 2 hours with a bioavailability of 85 – 100%. Ofloxacin absorption after a single or multiple 200 mg or 400 mg dose is predictable and the amount of drug absorbed increases proportionately with increasing dose and generally is unaffected by age. Although food slightly decreases the rate and/or extent of absorption, this drug-food interaction is not considered to be of clinical significance. After oral administration of a single 200, 300, or 400 mg dose of ofloxacin in healthy fasting adults, peak serum concentrations (Cmax) average 1.5-2.7, 2.4-4.6, or 2.9-5.6 mcg/mL, respectively. Some accumulation occurs following multiple doses. Steady-state serum concentrations of the drug are achieved after 4 doses and are about 40% higher than concentrations achieved after single oral doses. The pharmacokinetics of ofloxacin after parenteral infusion are very similar to those observed after oral administration. After intravenous (IV) infusion of 200 mg ofloxacin Cmax is 2.3 ± 0.6 mcg/mL, and with 400 mg dose is 4.5 ± 0.8 mcg/mL. Ofloxacin is widely distributed into body tissues and fluids including bone, cartilage, bile, skin, sputum, bronchial secretions, pleural effusions, tonsils, saliva, gingival mucosa, nasal secretions, aqueous humor, tears, sweat, lung, blister fluid, pancreatic fluid, ascitic fluid, peritoneal fluid, gynecologic tissue, vaginal fluid, cervix, ovary, semen, prostatic fluid, and prostatic tissue. Ofloxacin concentrations in most tissues and fluids are generally 0.5 to 1.7 times higher than serum concentration. Ofloxacin also distributes into the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) with peak concentrations attained within 2 to 6 hours after oral administration depending on the degree of inflammation. Peak CSF concentrations, like in most tissues and fluids, are higher than the drug’s serum concentration. The apparent volume of distribution is 1 to 2.5 L/kg and is generally not affected by renal impairment. About 20 to 32% is protein-bound, mainly to albumin. Ofloxacin is primarily metabolized into desmethyl ofloxacin and ofloxacin N-oxide. After a single 100 mg to 600 mg oral ofloxacin dose, 65% to 90% is excreted unchanged in the urine within 48 hours while less than 5% is excreted as metabolites. About 4% to 8% is excreted in the feces. In healthy adults with normal renal function, ofloxacin’s elimination half-life is approximately 4 to 8 hours and renal clearance is about 133 to 200 mL/minute. Hemodialysis removes only small amounts of ofloxacin and its desmethyl metabolite. After a single 100 or 200 mg ofloxacin dose, about 10% to 21% is removed by hemodialysis while 10% of a single 200 mg ofloxacin dose is removed via peritoneal dialysis.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,For the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible microorganisms: • Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis • Community-acquired pneumonia • Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections • Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis • Acute pelvic inflammatory disease,1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T18:52:14.770413+00:00
