PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY21816,Dexpanthenol,Corneregel,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1tNN6GYlDvnsTA1uyIOmwka7OQJU1_0qU/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY21816_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1tNN6GYlDvnsTA1uyIOmwka7OQJU1_0qU_view_usp_drivesdk_84ca1235cd.pdf,page_text/DR-XY21816_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1tNN6GYlDvnsTA1uyIOmwka7OQJU1_0qU_view_usp_drivesdk_84ca1235cd.json,7acd8650498f701a0145f63b8dae9b5406f86a61ffc77002973d7da346930dcb,190735,2,digital_text,,,,,"Corneal erosions; supportive and post-treatment of all types of keratitis, as e.g. keratitis dendritica; scalds, burns; dystrophic corneal diseases; prevention and treatment of corneal damage in wearers of contact lenses. Pharmacodynamic properties Mechanism of Action Dexpanthenol/panthenol is the alcohol analogue of pantothenic acid and, due to its intermediate conversion, has equivalent bi ological efficacy to pantothenic acid. It is bound to the dextrorotatory D configuration. Pantothenic acid is a water-soluble vitamin, which is involved in numerous metabolic processes as coenzyme A. In vitro studies have detected a fibroblast proliferation. In rats with dexpanthenol deficiency, a trophic effect on the skin has been observed as a result of administering dexpanthenol. Dexpanthenol/panthenol can, when applied topically, compensate for increased pantothenic acid requirements in damaged skin or mucous membranes. Pharmacokinetic properties In pharmacokinetic studies with tritium-labeled panthenol, the substance was absorbed dermally.",1,1,Indications,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T18:57:17.897466+00:00
