PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY24776,Metronidazole,Ambidazol,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1zmNoEl1Q7WnXcmCsp0cVHvR89FRrQ1-t/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY24776_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1zmNoEl1Q7WnXcmCsp0cVHvR89FRrQ1-t_view_usp_drivesdk_17755af57d.pdf,page_text/DR-XY24776_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1zmNoEl1Q7WnXcmCsp0cVHvR89FRrQ1-t_view_usp_drivesdk_17755af57d.json,8f9d9440b30c1e0d2e2cb298a7fd0f4b60188e63a8c26fbf9775ef7d2fa2c94d,419489,1,digital_text,"250 mg Tablet - White to creamy white, round convex, size 10mm tablet. 125 mg / 5mL Suspension - Creamy white to dirty white suspension with orange flavor.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"For the treatment of asymptomatic and symptomatic trichomoniasis, infections due to susceptible anaerobic bacteria, intestinal and hepatic amoebiasis, giardiasis (Giardis intestinalis).",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:00:56.637842+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY24853,Tramadol Hydrochloride,Siverol,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HeZLftakW442DWo4z_rXOU2677zAvd82/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY24853_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1HeZLftakW442DWo4z_rXOU2677zAvd82_view_usp_drivesdk_d2a6770c99.pdf,page_text/DR-XY24853_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1HeZLftakW442DWo4z_rXOU2677zAvd82_view_usp_drivesdk_d2a6770c99.json,a9066bc62c047a4c8d04f9dcc4dfc716e6179c727b08bf59ed1770eff976a83a,493509,2,digital_text,"Siverol 50mg Capsule: Hard gelatin capsule with a green opaque cap and an ivory opaque body Siverol 50 mg/1 mL Solution for Injection: Clear, sterile, colorless solution, free from extraneous matter, filled in a 2 mL clear ampule with blue dot Siverol 100 mg/2 mL Solution for Injection: Clear, sterile, colorless solution, free from extraneous matter, filled in a 2 mL clear ampule with blue dot CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY PHARMACODYNAMICS Tramadol is a synthetic opioid analgesic that acts on the central nervous system. It is a pure non-selective agonist of the µ, δ, and  opioid receptors with a higher affinity for the µ receptors. Other mechanisms which contribute to its analgesic effect are inhibition of neuronal uptake of noradrenaline and enhancement of serotonin release. PHARMACOKINETICS After oral administration of a single 50 mg capsule, tramadol is rapidly and almost completely absorbed with a mean absorption delay of approximately 30 minutes. Food does not significantly affect the rate or extent of absorption. The absorption half-life is 23 ± 11 minutes. Tramadol has a high tissue affinity with a volume of distribution of 2 to 3 L/kg in young adults. The volume of distribution is reduced by about 25% in those over 75 years old. Plasma protein binding is about 20% and is independent of concentration up to 10 mcg/mL. Saturation of plasma protein binding occurs only at concentrations outside the clinically relevant range. After oral administration of two 50 mg capsules, the mean absolute bioavailability is 68 to 72%, while the mean absolute bioavailability of a single oral 100 mg dose is approximately 75%. Peak serum level (C ) of tramadol is reached approximately two hours after oral administration. C is approximately 280 ng/mL after oral max max administration (100 mg), while the mean serum concentration after intravenous (IV) administration is 410 ng/mL (1.46 times higher). After repeated oral administration of 50 mg and 100 mg tramadol capsules every six hours, steady state is reached 30 to 36 hours after the first administration and bioavailability is greater than 90%. After intramuscular (IM) injection of 50 mg tramadol, bioavailability is approximately 100% and peak serum level is attained after 45 minutes. The peak concentrations of the M1 metabolite (major metabolite) occur three hours after tramadol administration. Tramadol crosses the placental and blood-brain barriers. The mean ratio of serum tramadol in the umbilical veins compared to maternal veins was 0.83 for 40 women given tramadol during labor. The cumulative excretion in breastmilk within 16 hours post-dose was 100 mcg of tramadol (0.1% of the maternal dose) and 27 mcg (0.02% of the maternal dose) of M1 after a single 100 mg IV dose of tramadol. Tramadol is extensively metabolized in the liver. The major metabolic pathways appear to be N- and O-demethylation and glucuronidation or sulfation. Only M1 is pharmacologically active. Production of M1 is dependent on the cytochrome P (CYP) 2D6 isoenzyme of CYP450. Patients who metabolize drugs poorly via CYP2D6 may obtain reduced benefit from tramadol, due to reduced formation of M1. N-demethylation is catalyzed by the CYP3A4 isoenzyme of CYP450. The inhibition of one or both types of the isoenzymes CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 involved in the biotransformation of tramadol may affect the plasma concentrations of tramadol or its active metabolite. Tramadol and its metabolites are excreted mainly by the kidneys with a cumulative renal excretion (tramadol and metabolites) of approximately 95%. In young adults, approximately 15 to 19% of an administered dose is excreted in the urine as unchanged drug. Biliary excretion is of little importance. The half-life of tramadol is five to seven hours and the half-life of M1 is six to eight hours. Total clearance is approximately 430 to 610 mL/min. Special Populations: Renal Impairment: Elimination of tramadol and M1 is diminished in patients with renal impairment. In patients with renal failure (creatinine clearance < 5 mL/min), the half-life of tramadol was 11 hours and that of M1 was 17 hours. Hepatic Impairment: Elimination of tramadol and M1 is diminished in patients with hepatic impairment, the mean half-life of tramadol was 13 hours and the mean half-life of M1 was 19 hours. Elderly: In the elderly (>75 years old), tramadol's volume of distribution is decreased by 25% and clearance is decreased by 40%. As a result, tramadol's C and max total exposure are increased by 30% and 50%, respectively. However, the half-life of tramadol is only slightly prolonged by 15%.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,> Relief of moderate to severe pain,1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:00:59.800746+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY24854,Tramadol Hydrochloride,Siverol,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CRMu7_q8kByWYLxSsUOqK_m4EySDzB6v/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY24854_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1CRMu7_q8kByWYLxSsUOqK_m4EySDzB6v_view_usp_drivesdk_87cce6a3bc.pdf,page_text/DR-XY24854_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1CRMu7_q8kByWYLxSsUOqK_m4EySDzB6v_view_usp_drivesdk_87cce6a3bc.json,5a407da7d17e70fbcb4cc98b00acf665d1a681fa18f95b1a7d2e10bd17f68169,493512,2,digital_text,"Siverol 50mg Capsule: Hard gelatin capsule with a green opaque cap and an ivory opaque body Siverol 50 mg/1 mL Solution for Injection: Clear, sterile, colorless solution, free from extraneous matter, filled in a 2 mL clear ampule with blue dot Siverol 100 mg/2 mL Solution for Injection: Clear, sterile, colorless solution, free from extraneous matter, filled in a 2 mL clear ampule with blue dot CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY PHARMACODYNAMICS Tramadol is a synthetic opioid analgesic that acts on the central nervous system. It is a pure non-selective agonist of the µ, δ, and  opioid receptors with a higher affinity for the µ receptors. Other mechanisms which contribute to its analgesic effect are inhibition of neuronal uptake of noradrenaline and enhancement of serotonin release. PHARMACOKINETICS After oral administration of a single 50 mg capsule, tramadol is rapidly and almost completely absorbed with a mean absorption delay of approximately 30 minutes. Food does not significantly affect the rate or extent of absorption. The absorption half-life is 23 ± 11 minutes. Tramadol has a high tissue affinity with a volume of distribution of 2 to 3 L/kg in young adults. The volume of distribution is reduced by about 25% in those over 75 years old. Plasma protein binding is about 20% and is independent of concentration up to 10 mcg/mL. Saturation of plasma protein binding occurs only at concentrations outside the clinically relevant range. After oral administration of two 50 mg capsules, the mean absolute bioavailability is 68 to 72%, while the mean absolute bioavailability of a single oral 100 mg dose is approximately 75%. Peak serum level (C ) of tramadol is reached approximately two hours after oral administration. C is approximately 280 ng/mL after oral max max administration (100 mg), while the mean serum concentration after intravenous (IV) administration is 410 ng/mL (1.46 times higher). After repeated oral administration of 50 mg and 100 mg tramadol capsules every six hours, steady state is reached 30 to 36 hours after the first administration and bioavailability is greater than 90%. After intramuscular (IM) injection of 50 mg tramadol, bioavailability is approximately 100% and peak serum level is attained after 45 minutes. The peak concentrations of the M1 metabolite (major metabolite) occur three hours after tramadol administration. Tramadol crosses the placental and blood-brain barriers. The mean ratio of serum tramadol in the umbilical veins compared to maternal veins was 0.83 for 40 women given tramadol during labor. The cumulative excretion in breastmilk within 16 hours post-dose was 100 mcg of tramadol (0.1% of the maternal dose) and 27 mcg (0.02% of the maternal dose) of M1 after a single 100 mg IV dose of tramadol. Tramadol is extensively metabolized in the liver. The major metabolic pathways appear to be N- and O-demethylation and glucuronidation or sulfation. Only M1 is pharmacologically active. Production of M1 is dependent on the cytochrome P (CYP) 2D6 isoenzyme of CYP450. Patients who metabolize drugs poorly via CYP2D6 may obtain reduced benefit from tramadol, due to reduced formation of M1. N-demethylation is catalyzed by the CYP3A4 isoenzyme of CYP450. The inhibition of one or both types of the isoenzymes CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 involved in the biotransformation of tramadol may affect the plasma concentrations of tramadol or its active metabolite. Tramadol and its metabolites are excreted mainly by the kidneys with a cumulative renal excretion (tramadol and metabolites) of approximately 95%. In young adults, approximately 15 to 19% of an administered dose is excreted in the urine as unchanged drug. Biliary excretion is of little importance. The half-life of tramadol is five to seven hours and the half-life of M1 is six to eight hours. Total clearance is approximately 430 to 610 mL/min. Special Populations: Renal Impairment: Elimination of tramadol and M1 is diminished in patients with renal impairment. In patients with renal failure (creatinine clearance < 5 mL/min), the half-life of tramadol was 11 hours and that of M1 was 17 hours. Hepatic Impairment: Elimination of tramadol and M1 is diminished in patients with hepatic impairment, the mean half-life of tramadol was 13 hours and the mean half-life of M1 was 19 hours. Elderly: In the elderly (>75 years old), tramadol's volume of distribution is decreased by 25% and clearance is decreased by 40%. As a result, tramadol's C and max total exposure are increased by 30% and 50%, respectively. However, the half-life of tramadol is only slightly prolonged by 15%.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,> Relief of moderate to severe pain,1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:01:02.874761+00:00
