PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY25441,Paclitaxel,Anzatax,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1k-Dq1xttKcNw4PE2qZFf2rzI18Fje-wx/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY25441_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1k-Dq1xttKcNw4PE2qZFf2rzI18Fje-wx_view_usp_drivesdk_61c336402b.pdf,page_text/DR-XY25441_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1k-Dq1xttKcNw4PE2qZFf2rzI18Fje-wx_view_usp_drivesdk_61c336402b.json,f7f14b04e07b85bf298d9b9abc644683c1927ab136577750371f6e0d8f31a23e,347338,19,digital_text,"Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is an anticancer agent from the taxane class of drugs. Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is a white powder. Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is extremely hydrophobic, and is therefore formulated in PEG-35 castor oil and ethanol. Paclitaxel (Anzatax) has a pH of 6 to 7. It is a clear to pale yellow solution, free of visible particles. Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is described chemically as (2S,5R,7S,10R,13S)-10,20-bis(acetoxy)-2- benzoyloxy-1,7-dihydroxy-9-oxo-5,20-epoxytax-11-en-13-yl (3S)-3-benzoylamino-3-phenyl- D-lactate. The chemical structure of Paclitaxel is shown below: Molecular weight: 853.9 CAS number: 33069-62-4 Excipients with known effect: Ethanol",1,1,2.0 DESCRIPTION,"Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is indicated for the primary treatment of ovarian cancer in combination with a platinum agent. Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is indicated for the treatment of metastatic ovarian cancer and metastatic breast cancer, after failure of standard therapy. Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is indicated for the treatment of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is indicated for adjuvant treatment of node positive breast cancer administered sequentially to doxorubicin and cyclophosphamide. Paclitaxel (Anzatax) is indicated for the treatment of metastatic cancer of the breast, in combination with trastuzumab (Herceptin), in patients who have tumors that over-express HER-2 and who have not received previous chemotherapy for their metastatic disease.",2,2,4.1 Therapeutic Indications,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:01:44.711359+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY25482,Lercanidipine,Zanidip,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ckUKeLY5tG_-k-GuGkdhAQ5DlAATNuZL/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY25482_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ckUKeLY5tG_-k-GuGkdhAQ5DlAATNuZL_view_usp_drivesdk_6ec2bec788.pdf,page_text/DR-XY25482_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ckUKeLY5tG_-k-GuGkdhAQ5DlAATNuZL_view_usp_drivesdk_6ec2bec788.json,1d36e6c66d7e16770fa01ecaf529c65b57b736e6095da2c613f94c1e989a30ce,503806,2,digital_text,"Film-coated tablet. Yellow, circular, biconvex tablets, scored on one side.",1,1,Pharmaceutical Form,Lercanidipine (Zanidip ®) is indicated in adults for the treatment of mild to moderate essential hypertension.,1,1,Therapeutic Indications,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:01:48.066584+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY25504,Vincristine Sulfate,Alcavixin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1W4862YdUalXiIzTxInpPUBbID7eR-Xlu/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY25504_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1W4862YdUalXiIzTxInpPUBbID7eR-Xlu_view_usp_drivesdk_3baf629e80.pdf,page_text/DR-XY25504_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1W4862YdUalXiIzTxInpPUBbID7eR-Xlu_view_usp_drivesdk_3baf629e80.json,0a9f2a861a678087f28a1e78dd6e5aa153f242156e1accb1c5d7a360786dc125,110568,2,digital_text,"Colorless to pale yellow, clear solution in amber vials. PHARMACODYNAMICS Vincristine is an antineoplastic drug with broad-spectrum anti-tumor activity in man. The drug may act by mitotic inhibition, causing an arrest of cell division in metaphase. The drug is relatively marrow-sparing and is thus suitable for use in combination with other cancer chemotherapeutic agents. PHARMACOKINETICS Vincristine is poorly absorbed orally. The clearance of the drug after rapid intravenous injection follows a triphasic decay pattern: a very rapid steep descent (alpha phase); a narrow-middle region (beta-phase) and a much longer terminal region (gamma phase). The terminal phase half-life of the drug varies from 15-155 hours. Dosing with the drug more frequently than once weekly is therefore probably unnecessary. Vincristine is primarily excreted by the biliary route. Patients with impaired hepatic or biliary function, as evidenced by a raised serum alkaline phosphatase, have been shown to have a significantly prolonged vincristine elimination half-life.",1,1,DESCRIPTION,"Vincristine is indicated in acute leukemia. Vincristine has also been shown to be useful in combination with other antineoplastic agents in Hodgkin’s disease, lymphatic sarcoma, reticulum cell granuloma, rhabdomyosarcoma, neuroblastoma, and Wilm’s tumor.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:01:50.861818+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY25799,Brinzolamide,Azopt,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1sGWVnc6n5wxhnqDfjzHZlnV559ncvRNY/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY25799_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1sGWVnc6n5wxhnqDfjzHZlnV559ncvRNY_view_usp_drivesdk_fb68a300e7.pdf,page_text/DR-XY25799_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1sGWVnc6n5wxhnqDfjzHZlnV559ncvRNY_view_usp_drivesdk_fb68a300e7.json,3abe41be25b02c37c070705f2bbafa220683bd5a63e206bb433817bda798813f,222424,9,digital_text,"Pharmaceutical form White to off-white Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension. Active substance One mL of the sterile ophthalmic suspension contains 10 mg of brinzolamide. Excipients Excipient with known effect: 1 mL of the Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension contains 0.1 mg of benzalkonium chloride. Other excipients: mannitol, carbomer 974P, sodium chloride, tyloxapol, disodium edetate, sodium hydroxide (to adjust pH) and/or hydrochloric acid, and purified water. Pharmaceutical formulations may vary between countries.",1,1,DESCRIPTION AND COMPOSITION,"Brinzolamide (Azopt®) Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor indicated to decrease elevated intraocular pressure (IOP) in adult patients with ocular hypertension or open-angle glaucoma, as monotherapy in adult patients unresponsive to beta-blockers or in adult patients in whom beta-blockers are contraindicated, or as adjunctive therapy to beta-blockers or prostaglandin analogues.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:01:53.752276+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY25799,Brinzolamide,Azopt,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1zKrhi5T6DzBPpo84uCEJM27A_HR__MzP/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY25799_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1zKrhi5T6DzBPpo84uCEJM27A_HR__MzP_view_usp_drivesdk_7068323546.pdf,page_text/DR-XY25799_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1zKrhi5T6DzBPpo84uCEJM27A_HR__MzP_view_usp_drivesdk_7068323546.json,4b7125dd1b5b2bc0e8816396155a28120b92e92a1168dfcef83c78091130b5c6,200836,9,digital_text,"Pharmaceutical form White to off-white Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension. Active substance Each mL of suspension contains: Brinzolamide.........................10 mg Excipients Excipient with known effect: 1 mL of the sterile ophthalmic suspension contains 0.1 mg of benzalkonium chloride. Other excipients: mannitol, carbomer 974P, tyloxapol, disodium edetate, sodium chloride, hydrochloric acid and/or sodium hydroxide (to adjust pH), and purified water. Pharmaceutical formulations may vary between countries.",1,1,DESCRIPTION AND COMPOSITION,"Brinzolamide (Azopt®) Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor indicated to decrease elevated intraocular pressure (IOP) in adult patients with ocular hypertension or open-angle glaucoma, as monotherapy in adult patients unresponsive to beta-blockers or in adult patients in whom beta-blockers are contraindicated, or as adjunctive therapy to beta-blockers or prostaglandin analogues.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:01:56.777728+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY25835,SALBUTAMOL (AS SULFATE),ASMACAIRE,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Z8cxyLc9P42-Xslr8HZi4Wr0pzvr-aql/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY25835_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Z8cxyLc9P42-Xslr8HZi4Wr0pzvr-aql_view_usp_drivesdk_33e5dec8b4.pdf,page_text/DR-XY25835_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Z8cxyLc9P42-Xslr8HZi4Wr0pzvr-aql_view_usp_drivesdk_33e5dec8b4.json,6fb71509ed97af8215e4b8a24fc995e6fbd52bfb7b0c788edab19b7764d4fdf5,1051594,2,digital_text,"Salbutamol (Asmacaire) 100 mcg/ actuation: White and homogenous suspension in aerosol PHARMACODYNAMICS Salbutamol is an adrenergic bronchodilator that works by stimulating beta-2-adrenegic receptors in the lungs to relax the bronchial smooth muscle, thus relieving bronchospasm, increasing vital capacity, reducing residual volume and reducing airway resistance. It thus ensures rapid bronchodilation in a few minutes that lasts for 4 to 6 hours. It is thought that this action is a consequence of the increase in 3',5'-cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cyclic AMP—3',5' or AMP-c) production brought about by activation of the enzyme adenylyl cyclase, which catalyses the conversion of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to AMP-c. The increase in AMP-c concentrations not only relaxes the bronchial smooth muscle, but also inhibits the release of mediators of immediate hypersensitivity from the cells, especially from mast cells. Clinical efficacy and safety Salbutamol has a greater effect on the respiratory tract than isoproterenol, but it has fewer cardiovascular effects due to its greater affinity for beta-2-adrenergic receptors. Its duration of action is longer than that of isoproterenol because it is not a substrate for the cellular uptake processes of catecholamines or catechol-O-methyltransferase. Salbutamol also causes vasodilation, which has a reflex chronotropic effect and general metabolic effects, including hypokalaemia PHARMACOKINETICS Absorption After treatment with inhaled salbutamol, only about 10% or less of the drug is deposited in the airways; the rest is swallowed. Salbutamol is well absorbed in the gastrointestinal tract. Distribution Due to its gradual absorption from the bronchi, systemic levels of salbutamol are low after inhalation of recommended doses. Peak plasma concentrations of salbutamol are reached after 2 to 4 hours. Metabolism There is significant first-pass metabolism of salbutamol, which takes place mainly in the gastrointestinal tract, conjugating to form an inactive sulphate ester. The results of animal studies show that salbutamol does not cross the blood-brain barrier. Elimination Systemic clearance of salbutamol is 30 litres/hour. Salbutamol is excreted in two forms: via the urine as unchanged drug and by metabo- lism through sulphate conjugation. The elimination half-life varies between 3 and 7 hours. Approximately 72% of the inhaled dose is excreted in the urine after 24 hours and consists of 28% unchanged drug and 44% metabolite.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"···Relief of bronchospasm associated with asthma, bronchitis or emphysema. ···Prophylaxis of bronchospasm induced by physical exercise or before exposure to a known and unavoidable allergenic stimulus. DOSAGE According to the doctor’s instructions. Do not exceed the recommended dosage. Adults: ···For immediate relief: one or two puffs ···In chronic or preventive treatment: two puffs, 3 to 4 times daily ···For relief of effort-induced bronchitis: two puffs before starting activity Children: Treatment with this drug should be supervised by an adult. ···For immediate relief or before effort: one puff ···In chronic or preventive treatment: one puff, 3 to 4 times daily. The dose may be increased to two puffs when necessary. This drug should be taken at fixed intervals, according to the doctor’s instructions. Treatment should be completed. In case of missed dose, you should take the dose immediately when you remember, but never take two doses together. DIRECTION FOR USE Check the expiry date before using the metered-dose inhaler. 1. Remove the cap from the mouthpiece (Fig. A) 2. Hold the inhaler vertically as illustrated. 3. Shake the inhaler for 20 seconds (Fig B). 4. Put the mouthpiece in your mouth. Press your lips tightly to the mouthpiece while exhaling fully (Fig. C). 5. Take a long deep breath through your mouth, at the same time push the aerosol canister as illustrated (Fig. D). 6. After inhaling, hold your breath for maximum penetration. 7. If necessary, wait a few seconds, then repeat the procedure (3, 4, 5 and 6). 8. Replace the cap after use. Clean regularly the plastic adapter. To clean, take out the metal container and wash the adapter in warm (not hot) soapy water. Rinse thoroughly, dry the adapter and reassemble the unit so that it is ready for use. Replace the cap upon reassembly.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:02:00.159784+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY26004,Bisoprolol Fumarate + Hydrochlorothiazide,Ziac,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZBQnba1gd-hHihnIxecqVphQ9eP3mht1/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY26004_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ZBQnba1gd-hHihnIxecqVphQ9eP3mht1_view_usp_drivesdk_8231914273.pdf,page_text/DR-XY26004_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ZBQnba1gd-hHihnIxecqVphQ9eP3mht1_view_usp_drivesdk_8231914273.json,6ebea65cb3c276353e8bbeaf3f18396dbbfdd211f385d014c8c8278592fa5d0c,182411,2,digital_text,"Ziac® 2.5 mg/6.25 mg film coated tablet is a yellow, round, biconvex, film coated tablet with an embossed heart on one side and 2.5 on the other side. Ziac® 5 mg/6.25 mg film coated tablet is a pastel-pink, round, biconvex, film-coated tablet with an embossed heart on one side and 5 on the other side. Ziac® 10 mg/6.25 mg film coated tablet is a white, round, biconvex, film-coated tablet with an embossed heart on one sideand 10 on the other side.",1,1,Product Description,Treatment of essential hypertension,1,1,Indication,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:02:03.230478+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY26031,Cefalexin,Unbranded,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LJIsydWAns1WkU398tyvJ6sfVKgjOeye/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY26031_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1LJIsydWAns1WkU398tyvJ6sfVKgjOeye_view_usp_drivesdk_c8803e7111.pdf,page_text/DR-XY26031_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1LJIsydWAns1WkU398tyvJ6sfVKgjOeye_view_usp_drivesdk_c8803e7111.json,9c703dfb3ca41ef4bfe5a442d4ce0d1b0292dfe5a23dffead41e9276fbcf2799,2020907,2,digital_text,"• RiteMED® Cefalexin 100 mg/mL Powder for Suspension (Oral Drops) and 125 mg/5 mL and 250 mg/5 mL Powder for Suspension: • RiteMED® Cefalexin 250 mg Capsule: creamy white to yellow ish gr anules in a har d gelatin capsule (No. 1) • RiteMED® Cefalexin 500 mg Capsule: creamy white to yellow ish gr anules in a har d gelatin capsule (No.1) with medium orange cap and body PHARMACODYNAMICS the bacter ial cell wall synthesis. It binds to specific penicillin- binding proteins responsible for the synthesis of peptidoglycan, a heter opolymer ic str uctur e that gives the cell w all its mechanical stability . The final stage o f peptidoglycan synthesis involves completion of the cross-linking of the terminal glycine residue of the pentaglycine bridge to the fourth residue of the pentapeptide. The transpeptidase enzyme that catalyzes this step is inhibited by cephalosporins. As a result the bacterial cell wall is weakened, the cell swells and then ruptures. ANTIMICROBIAL SPECTRUM OF ACTIVITY Cefalexin is active in vitro and in clinical infections against most strains of the following microorganisms: Cefalexin is inactive against methicillin-resistant staphylococci and most strains of enterococci (e.g., Enterococcus faecalis), Enterobacter spp., Morganella morganii, and Proteus vulgaris. It has no activity against Pseudomonas spp. or Acinetobacter calcoaceticus. Penicillin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae is usually cross-resistant to beta-lactam antibiotics. It is suggested to carry out susceptibility tests. PHARMACOKINETICS Cefalexin is acid stable and is rapidly and completely absorbed from the gastrointestinal (GI) tract. After single oral doses of cefalexin 250 mg and 500 mg in healthy, fasting adults with normal renal function, the mean peak serum concentrations (C max) are 9 and 18 mcg/mL, respectively, and are achieved within one hour of administration. Serum concentrations are still detectable after 6 hours. When cefalexin is administered with food, the peak serum concentrations are slightly lower and are attained later, although the total amount of drug absorbed remains unchanged. Absorption of cefalexin is delayed in young children and may be decreased up to 50% in neonates. Peak serum concentrations of the drug have been reported to occur within 3 hours in infants younger than 6 months old, within 2 hours in children 9-12 months old, and within 1 hour in older children. Cefalexin readily diffuses into tissues, including bone, joints and the pericardial as well as pleural cavities. It reaches and small quantities are found in breast milk. The serum half-life (t ½) of cefalexin is 0.5 to 1.2 hours in adults with normal renal function. In adults with creatinine clearances of 13.5, 9.2 and 4 mL/minute, the serum t½ were 7.7, 10.8, and 13.9, respectively. tubular secretion. About 70-90% of a single oral dose of cefalexin 250- or 500 mg is excreted within 8-12 hours in adults with normal renal function. Peak urine concentrations average about 2 mg/mL and occur 2 hours after a single 500 mg oral dose of cefalexin.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTIONS,"For the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible microorganisms: • Skin and skin structure infections • Bone and joint infections • Genitourinary tract infections, including acute prostatitis • Respiratory tract infections including acute and chronic bronchitis and infected bronchiectasis • Ear, nose and throat infections including otitis media, mastoiditis, sinusitis, follicular tonsillitis, and pharyngitis • Dental infections Note: Penicillin is the usual drug of choice in the treatment and prevention of streptococcal infections, including the prophylaxis of rheumatic fever. Cefalexin is generally effective in the eradication of streptococci from the nasophar- are not available at present.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:02:07.414894+00:00
