PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY27569,Cefaclor (as Monohydrate),None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/138_3dnP2p-yzKDM4INkDV_WgfFOkV8dp/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY27569_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_138_3dnP2p-yzKDM4INkDV_WgfFOkV8dp_view_usp_drivesdk_abce9a2ece.pdf,page_text/DR-XY27569_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_138_3dnP2p-yzKDM4INkDV_WgfFOkV8dp_view_usp_drivesdk_abce9a2ece.json,83bdaa01c2b5722d6b6924f7f50d70235c987ccc5a9fbf8f9b8f55e2cadedeee,145297,2,digital_text,"RiteMED ® Cefaclor 125 mg/5 mL and 250 mg/5mL powder for suspension and 50 mg/mL oral drops are off-white powder for reconstitution into red, melon-flavored suspensions. RiteMED ® Cefaclor 500 mg capsule has a purple opaque cap with white opaque body and white, free-flowing powder PHARMACODYNAMICS Cefaclor is a semi-synthetic, second generation cephalosporin which exerts its bactericidal activity by interfering with the bacterial cell wall synthesis. It binds to specific penicillin-binding proteins responsible for the synthesis of peptidoglycan, a heteropolymeric structure that gives the cell wall its mechanical stability. The final stage of peptidoglycan synthesis involves completion of the cross-linking of the terminal glycine residue of the pentaglycine bridge to the fourth residue of the pentapeptide. The transpeptidase enzyme that catalyzes this step is inhibited by cephalosporins. As a result the bacterial cell wall is weakened, the cell swells and then ruptures. ANTIMICROBIAL SPECTRUM OF ACTIVITY Cefaclor is active against the following microorganisms, both in vitro and in clinical infections: Aerobic Gram-Positive Aerobic Gram-negative Staphylococci, including coagulase-positive, coagulase-negative, and penicillinase-producing strains Streptococcus pneumoniae Streptococcus pyogenes (group A b -hemolytic streptococci) Cefaclor has also demonstrated in vitro activity against most strains of the following microorganisms; however, clinical significance is unknown. Aerobic Gram-negative Anaerobes Citrobacter diversus Moraxella catarrhalis Neisseria gonorrhoeae Pseudomonas spp., Acinetobacter calcoaceticus, and most strains of enterococci ( Enterococcus faecalis, group D streptococci), Enterobacter spp., indole-positive Proteus, Morganella morganii (formerly Proteus morganii ), Providencia rettgeri (formerly Proteus rettgeri), and Serratia spp. are resistant to cefaclor. It is suggested to carry out sensitivity tests. PHARMACOKINETICS Cefaclor is acid-stable and is well absorbed from the gastrointestinal (GI) tract. After administration of single oral doses of cefaclor 250 mg, 500 mg, and 1 g in healthy, fasting adults, the average peak serum concentrations were about 5 to 7, 13 to 15, and 23 to 25 mcg/mL, respectively, within 30 to 60 minutes. When taken with food, the peak concentration achieved is 50 to 75% of that observed when the medicine is given to fasting subjects and generally appears 45 to 60 minutes later. Cefaclor is widely distributed in the body with bactericidal concentrations achieved in middle ear fluid, sinus drainage and bronchial secretions. It crosses the placenta and low concentrations have been detected in breast milk. About 25% of cefaclor is bound to plasma proteins. There is no evidence of metabolism of cefaclor in humans. Cefaclor is excreted unchanged in urine. About 60 to 85% of a single oral dose is excreted within 8 hours in adults with normal renal function; the greater portion being excreted within the first two hours. During this 8-hour period, the average peak urine concentrations of cefaclor were 600 mcg/mL, 900 mcg/mL, and 1.9 mg/mL after a single 250 mg, 500 mg, or 1g-dose, respectively. Special Population: Renal Impairment: The serum half-life (t 1/2) of cefaclor is 0.6 to 1 hour in adults with normal renal function. In patients with reduced renal function, the serum half-life of cefaclor is slightly prolonged. In those with complete absence of renal function, the plasma half-life of the intact molecule is 2.3 to 2.8 hours. Excretion pathways in patients with markedly impaired renal function have not been determined. Hemodialysis shortens the half-life by 25% to 30%. Elderly: In elderly subjects, 65 years and older, with normal serum creatinine values, a higher peak plasma concentration and area under the curve are effects resulting from mildly diminished renal function and are not expected to have clinical significance. Therefore, dosage changes are not necessary in this population. Children: In one study in infants younger than 18 months old, peak serum concentrations of cefaclor ranged from 2 to 14 mcg/mL one hour after a single oral dose of 10 mg/kg body weight and 1.2 to 23 mcg/mL one hour after a single oral dose of 15 mg/kg body weight.",2,2,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"For the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible microorganisms: • Upper respiratory tract infections including otitis media, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, and sinusitis • Lower respiratory tract infections including pneumonia, acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis, and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis • Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections • Urinary tract infections, including pyelonephritis and cystitis",2,2,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:07:53.791453+00:00
