 or as directed by a What to do when you have taken more than the recommended doctor. May use a cotton bud to remove excess mucus. Use clean cloth to _ dosage? - ~ ff you have used more than the recommended t dosage, 66 Te CONSUIIE a doctor.” wipe the tip of the dropper after each use. Sodium.Chloride (Snif®) canbe used everyday by children, adults, and by pregnant or breastfeeding women. When should you consult a doctor? If your condition persists or worsens, consulta doctor. When should you not take this Medicine? Directions for Use: If you are allergic to sodium chloride or to any ingretent} in the product. While sitting or standing with the head slightly tilted, spray. the solution: into Care that should be taken when taking this Medicine? the nasal area until the desired effect is obtained. Do not share this product with others because of the possible increased. Availability risk of infection, Do not. use-_after the expiry date. on the. label. = ene ee ee ae -: 8. 65% Spray Solution - HDPE Bottle x 30 mL (Pox of 1 's) - 2.3% Spray Solution — HDPE Bottle x 30 mL (Box of 1's) | Undesirable éffects: This product does not contain any medication and can be used as often as needed without worry of any harmful side effects. However, if the inside of your nose is very dry and irritated, stinging may. occur. ‘This product may also induce transient snéezing. [Page 3]",2,2,conditions.,low,needs_human_review,2026-06-29T19:13:34.639137+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY29228,Ketorolac Trometamol,Kortezor,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1q9niWNUET1Z8oKEqnhawFBgFAGoiP26a/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY29228_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1q9niWNUET1Z8oKEqnhawFBgFAGoiP26a_view_usp_drivesdk_7001420bb8.pdf,page_text/DR-XY29228_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1q9niWNUET1Z8oKEqnhawFBgFAGoiP26a_view_usp_drivesdk_7001420bb8.json,a362d9a5eaea2d1133ab07607c89671fdd428f0cd7b4265850f197a4b876b8b7,554018,2,digital_text,"Ketorolac trometamol (Kortezor ) is a clear, slightly yellow, sterile, aqueous solution, free from extraneous matter and filled in a 2 mL clear ampule with blue dot. PHARMACODYNAMICS Ketorolac trometamol exhibits anti-inflammatory, analgesic and antipyretic activities by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX)-1 and COX-2 isoenzymes. These enzymes are found throughout the body and produce prostaglandins, which are important mediators of pain, fever, and adaptive and protective reactions in many organs and (inflamed) tissues. The greatest difference between large and small doses of ketorolac trometamol is in the duration of analgesia. PHARMACOKINETICS Following IV administration of 15 or 30 mg doses of ketorolac trometamol injection in healthy adults, time to peak plasma concentration was about 1 to 3 minutes, with peak plasma concentrations of approximately 2.5 or 4.7 mcg/mL, respectively. Ketorolac trometamol is rapidly and completely absorbed when administered by the IM route. The pharmacokinetics is linear following single and multiple dosing. After single IM ketorolac doses of 10 mg, 30 mg or 60 mg, peak plasma concentrations at 30 to 60 minutes are 0.7 to 1.4, 2.2 to 3, and 4 to 4.6 mcg/L, respectively. Ketorolac is not widely distributed following IM or IV administration. The apparent volume of distribution during the terminal elimination phase in healthy adults is approximately 0.15 to 0.3 L/kg and the volume of distribution at steady state (Vss) is about 0.11 to 0.33 L/kg. The drug also crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, with cerebrospinal fluid concentrations reported to be about 0.2% of concurrent plasma concentrations. Ketorolac is more than 99% protein bound. Approximately 40% of a ketorolac dose is largely metabolized in the liver. The drug undergoes hydroxylation to form - hydroxyketorolac. This hydroxy metabolite exhibits limited pharmacologic activity, having less than 20% or 1% of the anti- inflammatory or analgesic potency, respectively, of the parent drug. Ketorolac also undergoes conjugation with glucuronic acid. The drug is also metabolized to unidentified polar metabolites, which appear to be pharmacologically inactive probably because of their high polarity and rapid elimination. Ketorolac crosses the placenta and enters the fetal circulation to give fetal cord blood levels 11.6% (range 4 to 25%) of the corresponding maternal levels. There is very little excretion of ketorolac into breast milk, with peak milk ketorolac concentrations not exceeding 7.3 ng/mL after a single 10 mg oral dose, and the milk-to-plasma ratio was about 0.04. Following single IM or IV doses of ketorolac trometamol in healthy adults, plasma concentrations of ketorolac decline in a biphasic manner with a terminal elimination half-life of about 4 to 6 hours. In renally impaired patients, there is a reduction in clearance and an increase in terminal half-life (see table below). The drug undergoes hydroxylation in the liver to form p-hydroxyketorolac. This hydroxy metabolite exhibits limited pharmacologic activity, having less than 20% or 1% of the anti-inflammatory or analgesic potency, respectively, of the parent drug. Ketorolac also undergoes conjugation with glucuronic acid. The drug is also metabolized to unidentified polar metabolites, which appear to be pharmacologically inactive probably because of their high polarity and rapid elimination. The primary route of excretion of ketorolac trometamol and its metabolites (conjugates and the p-hydroxy metabolite) is in the urine (91%) within 2 days and the remainder (6%) being excreted in the feces within 3 days. In healthy adults, a single IV dose is excreted in urine as ketorolac (56 to 60%), ketorolac glucoronide (20 to 26%), p-hydroxyketorolac (11 to 12%), and unidentified polar metabolites (6 to 7%). The Influence of Age, Liver, and Kidney Function on the Clearance and Terminal Half-Life of Ketorolac in Adult Populations* *Estimated from 30 mg single IM doses of ketorolac trometamol",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION ,Short term (< 2 days) management of moderate to severe acute postoperative pain.,1,1,INDICATION,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:13:37.741301+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY29234,,,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eTz9MbHORXYp5FANYFnIl6bHoQmo6Pj6/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY29234_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1eTz9MbHORXYp5FANYFnIl6bHoQmo6Pj6_view_usp_drivesdk_6d85e34038.pdf,page_text/DR-XY29234_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1eTz9MbHORXYp5FANYFnIl6bHoQmo6Pj6_view_usp_drivesdk_6d85e34038.json,3ad4c0393558c3ba088e422ed549cb4ef5a570514ad89525d75657b715920410,237631,2,digital_text,"Solution for injection. Colorless, clear solution. Supplied in a 2.4 mL cartridge contained in a prefilled delivery device (pen) that delivers 20 mcg per dose. 5. CLINICAL PARTICULARS 5.1 Therapeutic indications Teriparatide (Forteo ®) is indicated in adults. Treatment of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and in men at increased risk of fracture (see section 6.1). In postmenopausal women, a significant reduction in the incidence of vertebral and non-vertebral fractures but not hip fractures has been demonstrated. Treatment of osteoporosis associated with sustained systemic glucocorticoid therapy in women and men at increased risk for fracture (see section 6.1). 5.2 Posology and method of administration Posology The recommended dose of Teriparatide (Forteo ®) is 20 micrograms administered once daily. The maximum total duration of treatment with Teriparatide (Forteo ®) should be 24 months (see Section 5.4). The 24-month course of Teriparatide (Forteo ®) should not be repeated over a patient’s lifetime. Patients should receive supplemental calcium and vitamin D supplements if dietary intake is inadequate. Following cessation of Teriparatide (Forteo®) therapy, patients may be continued on other osteoporosis therapies. Special populations Patients with renal impairment Teriparatide (Forteo ®) must not be used in patients with severe renal impairment (see section 5.3.). In patients with moderate renal impairment, Teriparatide (Forteo ®) should be used with caution. No special caution is required for patients with mild renal impairment. Patients with hepatic impairment No data are available in patients with impaired hepatic function (see section 6.3.). Therefore, Teriparatide (Forteo®) should be used with caution. Pediatric population and young adults with open epiphyses The safety and efficacy of Teriparatide (Forteo ®) in children and adolescents less than 18 years has not been established. Teriparatide (Forteo ®) should not be used in pediatric patients (less than 18 years), or young adults with open epiphyses. Elderly patients Dosage adjustment based on age is not required (See 6.2.) Method of administration Teriparatide (Forteo ®) should be administered once daily by subcutaneous injection in the thigh or abdomen. Patients must be trained to use the proper injection techniques (see section 7.6). A User Manual is also available to instruct patients on the correct use of the pen.",1,1,4. PHARMACEUTICAL FORM,"Teriparatide (Forteo ®) is indicated in adults. Treatment of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and in men at increased risk of fracture (see section 6.1). In postmenopausal women, a significant reduction in the incidence of vertebral and non-vertebral fractures but not hip fractures has been demonstrated. Treatment of osteoporosis associated with sustained systemic glucocorticoid therapy in women and men at increased risk for fracture (see section 6.1).",1,1,5.1 Therapeutic indications,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T19:13:40.677422+00:00
