PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY33551,Amphotericin B,Amphotret,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vLQ7Sfjy-KTDMMaqA9Vo2e3jIroe4-oh/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY33551_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1vLQ7Sfjy-KTDMMaqA9Vo2e3jIroe4-oh_view_usp_drivesdk_ef4b088586.pdf,page_text/DR-XY33551_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1vLQ7Sfjy-KTDMMaqA9Vo2e3jIroe4-oh_view_usp_drivesdk_ef4b088586.json,8317cfbc628a6c48859f0cd3d705972ff9b86124d07040861c8b0691ba77849d,128652,2,digital_text,"Amphotret (Amphotericin B for Injection) is a freeze dried prepa-ration of Amphotericin B for intravenous injection. Amphotret is a yellow coloured cake which is required to be reconstituted with sterile water for Injection before administration.Amphotericin B is a polyene macrolide antifungal antibiotic obtained from a strain of Streptomyces nodosus. Amphotericin B is insoluble in water at neutral pH and hence formulated as a colloidal dispersion using sodium deoxycholate as a solubiliser. Clinical Pharmacology: Clinical activity of Amphotericin B has been shown against Candida species. Crytococcus neoformans, Blastomyces dermatitidis, Histoplasma capsulatum, T orulopsis glabrata, Coccidioides immitis, Pracoccidioides braziliensis, Aspergillus species and species causing mucomycosis. Amphotericin B remains the antifungal of choice for progressive and potentially fatal infections with susceptible organisms. Depending on the concentration achieved in the body ﬂuids and the susceptibility of the fungus, Amphotericin B acts as either fungicidal or fungistatic.",1,1,Description:,"Amphotret should be administered primarily to patients with progressive, potentially life threatening fungal infections. Amphotret should not be administered to treat non-invasive fungal infections such as oral thrush, vaginal candidiasis and oesphageal candidiasis in patients having normal neutrophil counts. Amphotret is speciﬁcally indicated in the treatment of fungal infections susceptible to amphotericin B such as the one caused by Candida spp., Aspergillus spp., Crytococcus neoformans, Mucor spp., Rhodotorula spp., Absidia spp., Blastomyces dermatitidis.",1,1,Indications & Usage:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:05:30.979036+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY33558,Citicoline (as sodium),Cholinerv,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1yont1nuH7dNIOdZFhJkeknrZmLM8Fdst/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY33558_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1yont1nuH7dNIOdZFhJkeknrZmLM8Fdst_view_usp_drivesdk_cccea833fe.pdf,page_text/DR-XY33558_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1yont1nuH7dNIOdZFhJkeknrZmLM8Fdst_view_usp_drivesdk_cccea833fe.json,6fac2c189fc695746b2a3c3b8b6bc9302a162addb0df3c9d03007dc89a5a5745,3730688,1,ocr,"Citicoline Sodium (Cholinerv®) 500 mg Film-Coated Tablet: Purple, capsule-shaped, film-coated tablet Citicoline Sodium (Cholinerv®) 1 g Film-Coated Tablet: Purple, elliptical, film-coated tablet Citicoline Sodium (Cholinerv®) 100 mg/mL Adult Solution (Oral Drops): Clear, light purple-colored fruit-flavored liquid Citicoline Sodium (Cholinerv®) 125 mg/mL or 250 mg/mL Solution for Injection: Clear, colorless, sterile aqueous solution filled in 5 mL clear Type | glass ampules with a fill volume of 4 mL CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY PHARMACODYNAMICS Citicoline is a complex organic molecule that functions as an intermediate in the biosynthesis of cell membrane phospholipids. Citicoline is also known as CDP-choline or cytidine diphosphate choline (cytidine 5-'diphosphocholine). CDP-choline belongs to the group of biomolecules in living systems known as nucleotides that play important roles in cellular metabolism. The pharmacologic action of citicoline appears to involve mechanisms that extend beyond phospholipid metabolism. Citicoline metabolites - choline, methionine, betaine, and cytidine-derived nucleotides - enter a number of metabolic pathways. DXXXN Biochemical markers of cholinergic nerve transmission are known to be deficient in conditions characterized by degeneration of cholinergic neurons, such as Alzheimer's disease. Citicoline modestly improves cognitive function in Alzheimer's disease by serving as an acetylcholine precursor. The brain uses choline preferentially for acetylcholine synthesis, which can limit the amount of choline available for phosphatidylcholine production. Citicoline has been investigated as a therapy for stroke patients. Three mechanisms are postulated: (1) repair of the neuronal membrane via increased synthesis of phosphatidylcholine; (2) repair of damaged cholinergic neurons via potentiation of acetylcholine production; and (3) reduction of free fatty acid build-up at the site of stroke-induced nerve damage. Citicoline protects cholinergic neurons from autocannibalism, a process in which membrane phospholipids are catabolized to provide choline for the synthesis of acetylcholine. This occurs when choline supplies are depleted, necessitating sacrifice of membrane phospholipids to maintain neurotransmission. As an exogenous source of choline for acetylcholine production, citicoline thus spares membrane phospholipids (in particular, phosphatidylcholine) and prevents neuronal cell death. PHARMACOKINETICS Citicoline is a water-soluble compound with greater than 90% bioavailability. Pharmacokinetic studies in healthy adults have shown oral doses of citicoline to be rapidly absorbed, with less than 1% excreted in the feces. Plasma levels peak in a biphasic manner, at 1 hour after ingestion followed by a second larger peak at 24 hours post-dosing. Citicoline is metabolized in the gut wall and liver. The byproducts of exogenous citicoline formed by hydrolysis in the intestinal wall are choline and cytidine. After absorption, choline and cytidine are dispersed throughout the body, enter systemic circulation for utilization in various biosynthetic pathways and cross the blood-brain barrier for re-synthesis into citicoline in the brain. Pharmacokinetic studies using “C citicoline show citicoline elimination occurs mainly via respiratory CO, and urinary excretion, in two phases, mirroring the biphasic plasma peaks. The initial peak in plasma concentration is followed by a sharp decline, which then slows over the next 4 to 10 hours. In the second phase, an initially rapid decline after the 24-hour plasma peak is similarly followed by a slower elimination rate. The elimination half-life is 56 hours for CO, and 71 hours for urinary excretion.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTIONS,"* Acute and recovery phase of cerebral infarction (e.g., ischemia due to stroke) * Cognitive dysfunction due to degenerative (i.e., Alzheimer's disease) and cerebrovascular disease * Cerebral insufficiency (e.g., dizziness, memory loss, poor concentration, disorientation) due to head trauma or brain injury * Parkinson's disease",1,1,INDICATIONS,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T04:05:43.645152+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY33561,Ambroxol Hydrochloride,Broxitrol,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rzqbYIDARxGt4RZSOQGx-NgipRbMpp9v/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY33561_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1rzqbYIDARxGt4RZSOQGx-NgipRbMpp9v_view_usp_drivesdk_b8170a605c.pdf,page_text/DR-XY33561_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1rzqbYIDARxGt4RZSOQGx-NgipRbMpp9v_view_usp_drivesdk_b8170a605c.json,c3d305c92ba97d2328123cfcb251aab61c21f56c0ca790f7ddf9a3f2c6cd5f6e,460063,2,ocr,"Broxitrol Syrup is a clear straw, slightly viscous liquid with bitter-sweet taste cola flavor Broxitrol Syrup (oral drops) is a clear straw, slightly viscous liquid with bitter-sweet taste and banana flavor. WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE? This medicines contain Ambroxol , which stimulates fluid secretion in the respiratory tract and breaks down mucus resulting in the production of less thick and less sticky mucus/phlegm. Ambroxol improves mucocilliary clearance (mucus flow and transport) making phlegm easier to cough and expel. STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE See formulation above WHAT IS THE MEDICINE USED FOR? For the treatment of respiratory disorder associated with productive cough. HOW MUCH AND HOW OFTEN SHOULD YOU USE THE MEDICINE? This medication should be taken orally (by mouth). The recommendations for children are based on a total dose of 1.2 — 1.6 mg Ambroxol per kg bodyweight. The dose is best taken after meals. Ambroxol hydrochloride oral drops may be diluted to milk, juice or water. Ambroxol 15 mg/5 mL Syrup: Ambroxol 6 mg/mL Syrup (Oral drops): Children: Children: 5 to 10 years old: 5 mL 3 times a day 13 to 24 months: 1.25 mL 2 times a day 2 to 5 years old: 2.5 mL 3 times a day 7 to 12 months: 1 mL 2 times a day 1 to 2 years old: 2.5 mL 2 times a day Infant under 6 months: 0.5 mL 2 times a day For long-term treatment, the dose may be reduced or as prescribed by the physician. WHEN SHOULD YOU NOT TAKE THIS MEDICINE? If you are allergic to any ingredient of the product. UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS Ambroxol is generally well tolerated. Mild stomach disturbances such as pyrosis (heartburn), dyspepsia, diarrhea, nausea and vomiting may occur. Rash/urticaria, angioedema (rapid swelling of the skin), anaphylactic reactions (rare, life-threatening allergic reaction) and other allergic reactions have been reported. WHAT OTHER MEDICINE OR FOODS SHOULD BE AVOIDED WHILE TAKING THIS MEDICINE? There are no reports of adverse drug reactions with other medications. Administration of Ambroxo! with antibiotics (e.g. Amoxicillin, Cefuroxime, Erythromycin) leads to higher antibiotic concentration in bronchopulmonary secretions and in sputum, [Page 2] Tell your doctor about other medicines you are taking, and allergy. especially other medicines for cough, cold WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU MISS A DOSE? If you miss a dose, just take the next dose if still needed for the condition being treated, and the subsequent doses at the recommended time or schedule exceed recommended dose. (e.g every . or 12 hours). Do not HOW SHOULD YOU KEEP THE MEDICINE? * Keep the product out of reach and sight of children. * Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS OF OVERDOSE Symptoms of Ambroxol overdose include extensions of the diarrhea, dyspepsia, pyrosis, nausea, vomiting and skin rash es). common undesirable effects (e.g. WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU HAVE USED MORE THAN THE RECOMMENDED DOSAGE? If you have taken more than the recommended dosage, consult a doctor. CARE THAT SHOULD BE TAKEN WHEN TAKING THIS MEDICINE * Severe skin lesions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome and Lyell'e syndrome have been reported rarely with the administration of mucolytics such as Ambroxol. These may be due to the severity of the underlying disease or concomitant medication. If new skin or mucosal lesions occur, discontinue Ambroxol and consult a physician immediately. * Do not use after the expiry date on the label. WHEN SHOULD YOU CONSULT YOUR DOCTOR? If symptoms do not improve or new symptoms appear and if undesirable effects develops AVAILABILITY Syrup: Amber glass bottle x 60mL (Box of 1's) Drops: 15mL Amber bottle with medicine dropper (Box of 1's) DATE OF REVISION OF PATIENT INFORMATION LEAF LET (PIL): March 22, 2018 Ambroxol (Broxitrol) Syrup Oral Dro ps Registration no. DR-XY33561 Valid until July 10, 2024 Ambroxol (Broxitrol) Syrup Registrati on no. DR-XY33112 Valid until March 15, 2024 ADR Reporting Statement “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph Patient must seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction.” Manufactured by Manufactured for:",1,2,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T04:05:55.681529+00:00
