PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY36211,Cefaclor (as monohydrate),Cefazen,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DBDxuU6K7YGjP5pUyq2sduFJxtiQVsh8/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY36211_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1DBDxuU6K7YGjP5pUyq2sduFJxtiQVsh8_view_usp_drivesdk_3d56ce8e9b.pdf,page_text/DR-XY36211_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1DBDxuU6K7YGjP5pUyq2sduFJxtiQVsh8_view_usp_drivesdk_3d56ce8e9b.json,9d01216531eca5eec89ba65a5244fdebb79dd07451e7e987069c84d55f160d40,40377,2,digital_text,"Orange film -coated tablet, oval -shaped, biconvex plain on both sides.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Used in the treatment of susceptible infections including upper and lower respiratory -tract infections, skin infections and urinary tract infections.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:23:40.143875+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY36232,Norfloxacin,Uritracin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vCzrxGZsj8DE_BuI9r906kIizPISzht1/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY36232_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1vCzrxGZsj8DE_BuI9r906kIizPISzht1_view_usp_drivesdk_d9016bf565.pdf,page_text/DR-XY36232_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1vCzrxGZsj8DE_BuI9r906kIizPISzht1_view_usp_drivesdk_d9016bf565.json,03027839e7e9f6203149f5bf116289bda4a726cce823aec172af012e48b12194,235944,2,digital_text,"Norfloxacin (Uritracin®) 400 mg film-coated tablet is a light orange, elliptical, standard biconvex, film-coated tablet, scored on one side and plain on the other side. PHARMACODYNAMICS Norfloxacin is a synthetic broad-spectrum fluoroquinolone antimicrobial agent which inhibits deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) topoisomerase, more commonly referred to as the DNA gyrase. DNA gyrase is necessary for bacterial DNA replication and some aspects of transcription, repair, recombination, and transposition. Inhibition of DNA gyrase in susceptible microorganisms results in the inhibition of adenosine triphosphate (ATP)-dependent negative supercoiling of DNA, inhibition of ATP-independent relaxation of supercoiled DNA, and promotion of double stranded breakage resulting in bacterial cell-death. ANTIMICROBIAL SPECTRUM OF ACTIVITY Norfloxacin is active in vitro and in clinical infections against most strains of the following organisms: Norfloxacin demonstrated in vitro activity against strains of the following microorganisms; however, clinical significance is unknown: Norfloxacin is not generally active against obligate anaerobes. Cross resistance between norfloxacin and other quinolones may occur. It is suggested to carry out susceptibility tests. PHARMACOKINETICS Norfloxacin is rapidly but incompletely absorbed from the gastrointestinal (GI) tract after oral administration. In healthy, fasting adults, at least 30-50% of an oral norfloxacin dose is absorbed; peak plasma or serum concentrations are generally attained within 1 to 2 hours. Antacids containing magnesium and aluminum decrease the bioavailability of oral norfloxacin while food, especially dairy products, may decrease the rate and/or extent of norfloxacin absorption. After a single oral administration of norfloxacin 200 mg or 400 mg in healthy, fasting adults, mean peak plasma or serum norfloxacin concentrations (C max) were 0.75 to 1 mcg/mL, and 1.3 to 1.9 mcg/mL, respectively; plasma or serum norfloxacin concentrations were still detectable after 12 hours. After single oral administration of norfloxacin 800 mg, 1.2 g or 1.6 g in healthy, fasting adults, C max were 2.4, 3.2, or 3.9 mcg/mL, respectively. Multiple-dose studies show no drug accumulation in patients with normal renal function. Steady-state serum concentrations of the drug are achieved by the second day of treatment. After oral administration, norfloxacin is distributed into renal parenchyma, gallbladder, liver, prostatic tissue, testicles, seminal fluid, uterus, fallopian tubes, cervical and vaginal tissues, blister fluid, seminal fluid, tonsils, maxillary sinus mucosa, sputum, and bile. Norfloxacin concentrations in bile may be up to 10 times higher than concurrent serum concentrations. Norfloxacin crosses the placenta and is distributed into cord blood and amniotic fluid. It is not known whether the drug is distributed into human milk. Norfloxacin’s protein-binding is 10 to 15%. In adults with normal renal function. the effective plasma or serum half-life (t 1/2) of norfloxacin is 2.3 to 4 hours. In adults with renal impairment, the t 1/2 averaged 4.4, 6.6, or 7.6 hours in adults with creatinine clearances of 30-80, 10-29, or less than 10 mL/minute per 1.73 m2, respectively. There is limited date on the t1/2 of norfloxacin in patients with hepatic impairment. Norfloxacin is partially metabolized into six metabolites via modification of the piperazinyl group. Although some of these metabolites are microbiologically active, their activity is less than that of the parent drug. Norfloxacin is eliminated mainly through renal excretion. After oral administration of a single 400 mg dose of norfloxacin in adults with normal renal function, about 25-40% of the dose is excreted in urine as unchanged drug and 5-10% as metabolites within 24 to 48 hours; at least 30% is excreted in feces within 48 hours. The mean renal clearance of norfloxacin is 234-296 mL/minute in adults with normal renal function. A single 400 mg oral norfloxacin dose results in urinary concentration of 200 mcg/mL or greater two to three hours post-dose. After 12 hours post-dose, urinary concentrations are about 30 mcg/mL or greater. Norfloxacin is not removed by hemodialysis and it is not known whether the drug is removed by peritoneal dialysis.",2,2,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"• For the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible microorganisms. - Upper and lower, complicated and uncomplicated urinary tract infections, including cystitis, pyelitis, cystopyelitis, pyelonephritis, epididymitis, and urinary infections associated with urologic surgery, neurogenic bladder or nephrolithiasis - Prostatitis - Acute bacterial gastroenteritis • For the suppression of chronic, recurrent urinary tract infection in adults.",2,2,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:23:43.197999+00:00
