PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY37186,Fluticasone Propionate,Primair,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1U83Yv3Lbh-JAZymTFt69acKydr1XYBwK/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY37186_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1U83Yv3Lbh-JAZymTFt69acKydr1XYBwK_view_usp_drivesdk_1782e87762.pdf,page_text/DR-XY37186_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1U83Yv3Lbh-JAZymTFt69acKydr1XYBwK_view_usp_drivesdk_1782e87762.json,60ca722819c513bb62dc4ac0334ae35d9238b6bf76f3d6303fda0c0f53b974b8,600416,1,digital_text,"Fluticasone propionate 125 mcg per actuation HFA Metered-dose Inhaler is a white homogeneous suspension aerosol for inhalation, in propellant HFA-134a, supplied in a pressurized container. PHARMACODYNAMICS Fluticasone propionate is a synthetic, trifluorinated glucocorticoid with potent anti-inflammatory activity. It has a high affinity for glucocorticoid receptor with negligible activity at androsterone, progesterone, estrogen, or mineralocorticoid receptors. Inflammation is recognized as an important component in the pathogenesis of asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Glucocorticoids inhibit multiple cell types (e.g., mast cells, eosinophils, basophils, lymphocytes, macrophages, and neutrophils) and mediator (e.g., histamine, eicosanoids, leukotrienes, and cytokines) production or secretion involved in the inflammatory response. The anti-inflammatory actions of glucocorticoids contribute to their efficacy in asthma and COPD. PHARMACOKINETICS The activity of inhaled Fluticasone is due to the parent drug, Fluticasone propionate. Studies have demonstrated that Fluticasone propionate’s oral systemic bioavailability is negligible (<1%), primarily due to incomplete absorption and pre-systemic metabolism in the gut and liver. Following inhaled dosing in healthy volunteers, absolute systemic bioavailability of Fluticasone propionate varies between approximately 10-30% of the nominal dose depending on the inhalation device used. Systemic absorption of Fluticasone propionate occurs mainly through the lungs, and is initially rapid then prolonged. Fluticasone propionate is approximately 91% bound to human plasma proteins. Fluticasone propionate is rapidly metabolized in the liver by the cytochrome P-450 isoenzyme CYP3A4. The main metabolic product is the 17β-carboxylic acid derivative, which is produced by esterase-catalyzed hydrolysis and has no significant glucocorticoid or anti-inflammatory activity. This is then largely excreted in feces, with less than 5% being excreted in urine.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,• For the maintenance treatment of asthma as prophylactic therapy • For the symptomatic treatment of COPD Fluticasone propionate Inhaler is NOT indicated for the relief of acute bronchospasm,1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:31:02.464742+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY37203,Loteprednol etabonate,Lotemax,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BDPBg-GQazAChHqJB7mBvArUORrv6_Ri/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY37203_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1BDPBg-GQazAChHqJB7mBvArUORrv6_Ri_view_usp_drivesdk_94f4e65ece.pdf,page_text/DR-XY37203_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1BDPBg-GQazAChHqJB7mBvArUORrv6_Ri_view_usp_drivesdk_94f4e65ece.json,a3a2eec18152937156edc34dffb82baefb2388df30a30204511e5534c79c9fb2,1903356,2,ocr,"Loteprednol Etabonate (LOTEMAX”) is a milky white containing a sterile, topical corticosteroid for ophthalmic use. ‘0CH,CI C=O Loteprednol etabonate is represented by the following structural formula",1,1,DESCRIPTION,"Loteprednol Etabonate (LOTEMAX®) is indicated for the treatment of steroid responsive inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea and anterior segment of the globe such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, cyclitis, selected infective conjunctivitides, when the inherent hazard of steroid use is accepted to obtain an advisable in edema and i Loteprednol Etabonate (LOTEMAX®) is less effective than prednisolone acetate 1% in two 28-day controlled clinical studies in acute anterior uveitis, where 72% of patients treated with Loteprednol Etabonate (LOTEMAX®) experienced resolution of anterior chamber cells, compared to 87% of patients treated with prednisolone acetate 1%. The incidence of patients with clinically significant increases in 1OP (210 mmHg) was 1% with Loteprednol Etabonate (LOTEMAX®) and 6% with prednisolone acetate 1%. Loteprednol Etabonate (LOTEMAX®) should not be used in patients who require a more potent corticosteroid for this indication. Loteprednol Etabonate (LOTEMAX®) is also indicated for the treatment of post-operative inflammation following ocular surgery. For short-term treatment of dry eye syndrome (DES).",1,1,INDICATIONS AND USAGE,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T04:31:16.061085+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY37204,Amlodipine (as Besilate),Besylon,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OR7gi_4qkrEehWchka0qyc_ANI_S27zV/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY37204_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1OR7gi_4qkrEehWchka0qyc_ANI_S27zV_view_usp_drivesdk_09cef83527.pdf,page_text/DR-XY37204_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1OR7gi_4qkrEehWchka0qyc_ANI_S27zV_view_usp_drivesdk_09cef83527.json,fee49ec5ad323932157c7375c04df919148f774cee56bf87304e2bf5a42db3fc,137842,3,digital_text_and_ocr,"Besylon 5 mg is a white to off-white coated tablet, round, biconvex, plain on one side and bisected on the other side. Besylon 10mg is a white to off-white coated tablet, round, biconvex, plain on one side and bisected on the other side.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,Amlodipine is used in the management of hypertension and prophylaxis of angina pectoris.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T04:31:19.764060+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY37211,Cinnarizine,Unbranded,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1e_0O5MLhiC9DKu4Maj5PP4xxrgB-7XBK/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY37211_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1e_0O5MLhiC9DKu4Maj5PP4xxrgB-7XBK_view_usp_drivesdk_6e93e5ea04.pdf,page_text/DR-XY37211_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1e_0O5MLhiC9DKu4Maj5PP4xxrgB-7XBK_view_usp_drivesdk_6e93e5ea04.json,11db41f84a06535463b66f4c46ce430ed049663d5d0cdab4ec9c4c71bb46fd87,1295479,2,digital_text,"RiteMED® Cinnarizine 25 mg is a white, round, biconvex tablet, bisected on one side RiteMED® Cinnarizine 75 mg is a size #4 capsule with light green opaque cap and white opaque body PHARMACODYNAMICS Cinnarizine’s action in the treatment of peripheral vascular disease is due to its anti-vasoconstrictor properties, its action on blood hyperviscosity, and its anti-ischemic effect. Cinnarizine has been shown to be a non-competitive antagonist of the smooth muscle contractions caused by various vasoactive agents, including histamine. Anti-vasoconstriction is thought to be through a calcium blocker mechanism and is evident selectively in vascular smooth muscle. In addition to this direct calcium antagonism, cinnarizine decreases the contractile activity of vasoactive substances, such as norepinephrine and serotonin, by blocking receptor-operated calcium channels. Blockade of the cellular influx of calcium is tissue-selective, and results in anti-vasoconstrictor properties without effect on blood pressure and heart rate. Increased peripheral muscle blood flow may be mediated by prevention of calcium entry into ischemic erythrocytes, thereby preserving flexibility. Cinnarizine may further improve deficient microcirculation by increasing erythrocyte deformability and decreasing blood viscosity. Cellular resistance to hypoxia is increased. Cinnarizine inhibits stimulation of the vestibular system, which results in suppression of nystagmus and other autonomic disturbances. PHARMACOKINETICS After oral administration, absorption is relatively slow. Peak serum concentrations occur 1 to 3 hours after intake. The plasma protein binding of cinnarizine is 91%. Cinnarizine is extensively metabolized mainly via CYP2D6, but there is considerable inter-individual variation in the extent of metabolism. The reported elimination half-life for cinnarizine ranges from 4 to 24 hours. The elimination of metabolites is about 1/3 in the urine (unchanged as metabolites and glucuronide conjugates) and 2/3 in the feces. Plasma elimination half-life is about 3 to 4 hours. Hepatobiliary Disorders: Cholestatic jaundice Skin and Subcutaneous Tissue Disorders: Hyperhidrosis, lichenoid keratosis, lichens planus, subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus Musculoskeletal and Connective Tissue Disorders: Muscle rigidity General Disorders and Administration Site Conditions: Lethargy, fatigue Investigations: Weight increased OVERDOSE AND TREATMENT Acute cinnarizine overdoses have been reported with doses ranging from 90 to 2,250 mg. The signs and symptoms of cinnarizine overdose are mainly due to the anticholinergic (atropine-like) activity of the drug. The most commonly reported signs and symptoms include: alterations in consciousness ranging from somnolence to stupor and coma, vomiting, extrapyramidal symptoms, and hypotonia. In a small number of young children, seizures developed. Clinical consequences were not severe in most cases, but deaths have bee n reported after single and polydrug overdoses involving cinnarizine. There is no specific antidote. Treatment is symptomatic and supportive care. Activated charcoal should only be considered in patients presenting within one hour of taking a potentially toxic overdose (i.e., more than 15 mg/kg). STORAGE CONDITIONS Keep the product out of reach and sight of children. Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. AVAILABILITY RiteMED ® Cinnarizin e 25 mg Tablet RiteMED® Cinnarizin e 75 mg Capsule Pantone 2756 U COLOR GUIDE Spread size: 140 mm x 196 mm Folded size: 140 mm x 98 mm RM - CINNARIZINE Line Insert (Front Spread) V.3 ADVERSE DRUG REACTION REPORTING STATEMENT For suspected adverse drug reaction, seek medical attention immediately and report to the FDA at www. fda.gov.ph AND RiteMED at (+632) 8-726-0835 or e-mail productsafety@ritemed.com.ph. By rep orting undesirable effects, you can help ptovide more information on the safety of this medicine. CAUTION Foods, Drugs, Devices, and Cosmetics Act prohibits dispensing without prescription. Manufactured by UNILAB, Inc. No. 66 United Street, Mandaluyong City For RiteMED Phils., Inc. 56 EDSA, Mandaluyong, Metro Manila Date of First Authorization: December 18, 2009 Date of Revision: October 2021 25 mg Tablet : DR-XY37211 75 mg Capsule: DR-XY38677 Reg. IPOPHIL • F .A. Revision Date: 19 October 2021 , in Alu/PCTFE Blister Pack x 10’s (Box of 30’s) , in Alu/PCTFE Blister Pack x 10’s (Box of 30 capsules) [Page 2] WARNINGS AND PRECAUTIONS As with other antihistamines, cinnarizine may cause epigastric discomfort; taking it after meals may diminish gastric irritation. Cinnarizine is a vasodilator. While it has not been found to reduce blood pressure significantly, it should be used with caution in those with coronary artery disease or in patients with hypotension. In patients with Parkinson’s disease, cinnarizine should only be given if the advantages outweigh the possible risk of aggravating this disease. There have been rare reports of extrapyramidal symptoms after taking cinnarizine, sometimes associated with depressive feelings. Cinnarizine may cause somnolence, particularly at the start of treatment. Therefore, caution should be taken when alcohol or central nervous system depressants or tricyclic antidepressants are used concomitantly. Use of cinnarizine should be avoided in porphyria. Cinnarizine may cause drowsiness, particularly at the start of treatment; caution should be taken during activities such as driving or operating machinery. INTERACTIONS WITH OTHER MEDICAMENTS • Concomitant use of alcohol, CNS depressants or tricyclic antidepressants may potentiate the sedative effects of either these drugs or of cinnarizine. Alcoholic drink should be avoided. Diagnostic interference • Cinnarizine may prevent an otherwise positive reaction to dermal reactivity indicators if used within four days prior to testing because of its antihistamine effect. STATEMENT ON USAGE FOR HIGH RISK GROUPS PREGNANCY AND LACTATION Pregnancy The safety of cinnarizine in pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it is not recommended to administer cinnarizine during pregnancy. Lactation There are no data on the excretion of cinnarizine in breast milk. Therefore, it is not recommended in breastfeeding mothers. RENAL AND HEPATIC IMPAIRMENT Cinnarizine should be used with care in patients with hepatic or renal insufficiency. UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS Immune System Disorders: Hypersensitivity Psychiatric Disorders: Somnolence, hypersomnia Nervous System Disorders: Dyskinesia, extrapyramidal disorder, parkinsonism, tremor, headache Gastrointestinal Disorders: Nausea, stomach discomfort, vomiting, upper abdominal pain, dyspepsia, dry mouth",1,2,PRODUCT DESCRIPTIONS,"Cerebral circulatory disorders • Maintenance therapy for symptoms of cerebrovascular origin, including dizziness, tinnitus, vascular headache, unsociability and irritability disorders, loss of memory, and lack of concentration • Prophylaxis of migraine Disorders of balance • Maintenance therapy for symptoms of labyrinth disorders, including vertigo, dizziness, tinnitus, nystagmus, nausea and vomiting. Motion Sickness • Prophylaxis of motion sickness Peripheral circulatory disorders • Maintenance therapy for symptoms of peripheral circulatory disorders including Raynauds’ phenomenon, acrocyanosis, intermittent claudication, trophic disturbances, trophic and varicose ulcers, paresthesia, nocturnal cramps, and cold extremities",2,2,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:31:23.714607+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY37252,d-Alpha Tocopherol (Vitamin E),Elaia,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/19ITWF6D2OPzk7GZ8n9E8Gv19czEDTark/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY37252_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_19ITWF6D2OPzk7GZ8n9E8Gv19czEDTark_view_usp_drivesdk_417ffce799.pdf,page_text/DR-XY37252_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_19ITWF6D2OPzk7GZ8n9E8Gv19czEDTark_view_usp_drivesdk_417ffce799.json,99e91edc5f35fcfe5801239c435849ed34c3786f541e3f23ca4489d0cd290a97,540211,1,ocr,Clear amber liquid in size 1 hard gelatin capsule Natural,1,1,DESCRIPTION OF THE PRODUCT:,Do not take more than recommended dose. Do not use after the expiry date on the label.,1,1,medications.,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T04:31:32.098940+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY37275,Phenytoin (as sodium),Unbranded,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/10brT2fXOwZq_mFBKYVjmKb9RXIpWO9lt/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY37275_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10brT2fXOwZq_mFBKYVjmKb9RXIpWO9lt_view_usp_drivesdk_53778b96f6.pdf,page_text/DR-XY37275_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10brT2fXOwZq_mFBKYVjmKb9RXIpWO9lt_view_usp_drivesdk_53778b96f6.json,5861653f4be3f81d35b4dfe11d3572d22c5addacee15dd012b345f3dc7f65eaf,6296164,2,digital_text,"RiteMED ® 2 mL clear ampule with blue dot. CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY PHARMACODYNAMICS Phenytoin is a hydantoin-derivative anticonvulsant. It inhibits the spread of seizure activity in the motor cortex by blocking post-tetanic potentiation. The mechanism by w hich phenytoin exerts its anticonvulsant ef fect has not been fully explained, but possible contributory effects include: reduce post-tetanic potentiation; 2) post-synaptic action to enhance GABA-mediated inhibition and reduce excitatory synaptic transmission and; 3) pre-synaptic actions to reduce calcium entry and block release of neurotransmitter. Phenytoin sodium can be useful in ventricular arrhythmias, particularly those due to digitalis. The antiarrhythmic - tory period, and therefore shortens the QT interval and the duration of the action potential. PHARMACOKINETICS Phenytoin’s onset of action after an intravenous (IV) dose is 30 to 60 minutes and the effect persists up to 24 hours. Optimum control without clinical signs of toxicity occurs most often with plasma levels between 10 and 20 mcg/mL. In renal failure or hypoalbuminemia, 5 to 12 mcg/mL or even less may be therapeutic. Phenytoin demonstrates non-linear pharmacokinetics. Therefore, the half-life is affected by the degree of absorp- tion, saturation of metabolic pathways, dose and degree of metabolic enzyme induction. This results in considerable inter- and intra-patient variability in phenytoin pharmacokinetics. - toin also diffuses across the placenta, with fetal serum concentrations equal to those of the mother. Phenytoin is about 90% protein bound. As phenytoin is highly protein bound, free phenytoin levels may be altered in patients whose protein binding characteristics differ from normal. Protein binding may be lower in neonates and hyper- bilirubinemic infants; also altered in patients with hypoalbuminemia, uremia or acute trauma, and in pregnancy. Therapeutic concentrations of free (unbound) phenytoin, which are frequently monitored in patients with altered protein binding, usually fall in the range of 0.8 to 2 mcg/mL (3 to 8 micromoles/L). Phenytoin is metabolized in the liver; the major inactive metabolite is 5-(p-hydroxyphenyl)-5-phenylhydantoin (HPPH). The rate of metabolism is increased in younger children, pregnant women, in women during menstrua- tion, and in patients with acute trauma. The rate decreased with advancing age. Phenytoin may be metabolized slowly in a small number of individuals due to genetic polymorphism, which may cause isoenzyme mutations (e.g., CYP2C9/19), limited enzyme availability and lack of induction. Most of the drug is excreted in the bile as inactive metabolites which are then reabsorbed from the intestinal tract but more importantly by tubular secretion. Phenytoin plasma half-life is normally from 10 to 15 hours. The ap- parent half-life of the drug changes with dose and plasma concentration because phenytoin exhibits saturable or dose-dependent pharmacokinetics. At therapeutic concentrations, the enzyme system responsible for metabolizing phenytoin becomes saturated. Therefore, a constant amount of drug is metabolized, and small increases in dose may cause disproportionately large increases in plasma concentrations and apparent half-life, possibly causing unexpected toxicity.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"• For the contr ol of status epilepticus, tonic- clonic (gr and mal) , psychomotor seizur es, and the pr evention and treatment of seizures occurring during or after neurosurgery. • For the treatment of certain cardiac arrhythmias, particularly in those patients who do not respond to conventional antiarrhythmic agents or to cardioversion. It is the dr ug of choice in the tr eatment of arr hythmia secondar y to digitalis toxicity.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:31:41.443757+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY37290,LOSARTAN POTASSIUM,NEOSARTAN,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_Ow-0rlgJvKtEpDpoJG_ghfC7i1Q2ZLX/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY37290_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1_Ow-0rlgJvKtEpDpoJG_ghfC7i1Q2ZLX_view_usp_drivesdk_2167b1266b.pdf,page_text/DR-XY37290_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1_Ow-0rlgJvKtEpDpoJG_ghfC7i1Q2ZLX_view_usp_drivesdk_2167b1266b.json,9b87265a3813761a39a01dd7a53ccd2fff4df3aa23ee8b68f0765405edf5b9a4,719743,2,ocr,"Losartan potassium (Neosartan®) 50mg Tablet is a white to off-white, oblong, biconvex, film-coated tablet, plain on both sides. Losartan potassium (Neosartan®) 100mg Tablet is a white to off-white, oval, biconvex, film-coated, plain on both sides.",1,1,DESCRIPTION:,Losartan potassium is used in the management of hypertension and may have role in patients who develop cough with ACE inhibitors. Management of diabetic nephropathy.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T04:31:55.499735+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY37294,Brinzolamide + Timolol (as maleate),Azarga,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/18MKiKj9TFk5MPVNHena2bBvyDdvenuXJ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY37294_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18MKiKj9TFk5MPVNHena2bBvyDdvenuXJ_view_usp_drivesdk_fdb5e1fd56.pdf,page_text/DR-XY37294_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18MKiKj9TFk5MPVNHena2bBvyDdvenuXJ_view_usp_drivesdk_fdb5e1fd56.json,7b2a61a99a2faf53eb9d6e06a501d9f255b42f5ec3d30297ede1b9071be9ccd1,189760,14,digital_text,"Pharmaceutical form White to off white Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension. Active substances One mL of the Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension cont ains 10 mg of brinzolamide, and 6.8 mg timolol maleate corresponding to 5 mg of timolol. Excipients Excipient with known effect: 1 mL of the Ster ile Ophthalmic Suspension contains 0.1 mg of benzalkonium chloride. Other excipients: mannitol, carbomer 974P, sodium chloride, tyloxapol, disodium edetate, sodium hydroxide and/or hydrochloric acid (to adjust pH), and purified water Pharmaceutical formulations may vary between countries.",1,1,DESCRIPTION AND COMPOSITION,Brinzolamide + Timolol (as maleate) (Azarga®) Ster ile Ophthalmic Suspension is indicated for the reduction of intraocular pressure (IOP) in adult patients with open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension for whom monotherapy provides insufficient IOP reduction and when combination therapy is appropriate.,1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:31:58.736891+00:00
