PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY39650,Loratadine,None,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/191I6fzbjyChEBlNlxRYEqMaZaysMJr5t/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY39650_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_191I6fzbjyChEBlNlxRYEqMaZaysMJr5t_view_usp_drivesdk_345d6ce6d3.pdf,page_text/DR-XY39650_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_191I6fzbjyChEBlNlxRYEqMaZaysMJr5t_view_usp_drivesdk_345d6ce6d3.json,83b9d6dbb0f546544f97965856b05036031844307334c423f1c75c364c59d19f,434067,2,ocr,"Loratadine 10 mg Chewable Tablet is a green, mottled, round, biconvex, tutti-frutti flavored, uncoated tablet, plain on both sides. Loratadine 5 mg/5 mL Syrup is a clear, colorless to light orange- brown, cherry / menthol flavored syrup. WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE? These medicines contain Loratadine as active ingredient. Loratadine is a long-acting, non-sedating antihistamine with no significant antimuscarinic activity.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T04:49:50.704409+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY39651,Cefazolin,Stancef,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Bv4aq8fCAZXzax9wdzYxoYoAH7ns-s08/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY39651_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Bv4aq8fCAZXzax9wdzYxoYoAH7ns-s08_view_usp_drivesdk_4524d846c1.pdf,page_text/DR-XY39651_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Bv4aq8fCAZXzax9wdzYxoYoAH7ns-s08_view_usp_drivesdk_4524d846c1.json,eb4fbe881526c6acc47bd9193c9571d7144146beac66d40b3831859feaeec3a7,919252,1,digital_text,"Cefazolin 500 mg or 1 g injection is a sterile white to off-white, practically odorless, crystalline powder or white to off-white solid in colorless vial. When reconstituted, the powder or solid dissolves completely into a clear colorless solution. Each vial contains, cefazolin sodium equivalent to 500 mg or 1 g of cefazolin. The sodium content is approximately 24.2 mg per 500 mg or 48.4 mg per 1 g of cefazolin sodium. PHARMACODYNAMICS Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin which exerts its bactericidal activity by interfering with the bacterial cell wall synthesis. It binds to specific penicillin-binding proteins responsible for the synthesis of peptidoglycan, a heteropolymeric structure that gives the cell wall its mechanical stability. The final stage of peptidoglycan synthesis involves completion of the cross-linking of the terminal glycine residue of the pentaglycine bridge to the fourth residue of the pentapeptide. The transpeptidase enzyme that catalyzes this step is inhibited by cephalosporins. As a result the bacterial cell wall is weakened, the cell swells and then ruptures. ANTIMICROBIAL SPECTRUM OF ACTIVITY Cefazolin is active against the following microorganisms, both in vitro and in clinical infections: Staphylococcus aureus (including penicillinase-producing strains); Staphylococcus epidermidis; group A beta-hemolytic streptococci and other strains of streptococci (many strains of enterococci are resistant); Streptococcus pneumoniae; Escherichia coli; Proteus mirabilis; Klebsiella spp.; Enterobacter aerogenes; Haemophilus influenzae. Methicillin-resistant staphylococci are uniformly resistant to cefazolin. Most strains of indole positive Proteus (Proteus vulgaris), Enterobacter cloacae, Morganella morganii, and Providencia rettgeri are resistant. Serratia, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter calcoaceticus (formerly Mima and Herellea spp.) are almost uniformly resistant to cefazolin. PHARMACOKINETICS Cefazolin sodium is not appreciably absorbed from the GI tract and must be administered parenterally. After intramuscular (IM) administration of cefazolin sodium in healthy adults with normal renal function, the mean peak serum concentrations (C ) were 17 mcg/mL max after a single 250 mg dose, 30-44 mcg/mL after a single 500 mg dose, and 64-76 mcg/mL after a single 1 g dose. Peak serum cefazolin concentrations are attained within 1-2 hours. After a single 1 g IV dose in adults with normal renal function, mean serum cefazolin concentrations were 188 mcg/mL at 5 minutes, 74 mcg/mL at 1 hour, and 46 mcg/mL at 2 hours. In a study in adults with normal renal function, steady-state serum cefazolin concentrations were reached 3 hours after IV infusion of 3.5 mg/kg over 1 hour followed by 1.5 mg/kg over 2 hours. In a study in children, the mean C were 28 mcg/mL and 42 mcg/mL at 30 minutes after a max single cefazolin IM dose of 5-6.25 mg/kg and 10-12.5 mg/kg, respectively. Cefazolin is detectable in concentrations in excess of the minimum inhibitory concentration of many common pathogens in bile, urine, the tonsils, pleural, peritoneal, and synovial fluid and bone. It does not enter cerebrospinal fluid to any useful extent. Bile levels in patients without obstructive biliary disease can reach or exceed serum levels by up to 5 times; however, in patients with obstructive biliary disease, bile levels of cefazolin are considerably lower than serum levels (<1 mcg/mL). In synovial fluid, the level of cefazolin becomes comparable to that reached in serum at about 4 hours after drug administration. Levels in cord blood are equivalent to 40% of those found in maternal blood. Cefazolin is present in very low concentrations in the human milk. A 2 g IV dose may produce levels of 1.2-1.5 mg/L in breast milk after 3 hours, but it is estimated that the child would receive only 0.075% of the maternal dose per day. Cefazolin is not metabolized.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"> For the treatment of the following serious infections due to susceptible organisms: · Respiratory tract infections · Urinary tract infections · Skin and skin structure infections · Biliary tract infections · Bone and joint infections · Genital infections including prostatitis and epididymitis · Septicemia · Endocarditis > As perioperative prophylaxis to reduce the incidence of infection in patients undergoing contami nated or potenti ally contami nated surgical procedures (e.g., vaginal hysterectomy, and cholecystectomy in high-risk patients such as those > 70 years old, with acute cholecystitis, obstructive jaundice, or common bile duct stones), and in patients in whom infection would pose a serious risk (e.g., during open-heart surgery and prosthetic arthroplasty).",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:49:53.900184+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY3966,Fleicanide Acetate,Tambocor,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wkOdX-P6VSoDI3e6kq8jgXDzfCzeHcWA/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY3966_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1wkOdX-P6VSoDI3e6kq8jgXDzfCzeHcWA_view_usp_drivesdk_c534a8362c.pdf,page_text/DR-XY3966_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1wkOdX-P6VSoDI3e6kq8jgXDzfCzeHcWA_view_usp_drivesdk_c534a8362c.json,f262f70de0b0f8b0b4627568478d05ab667187b3bc112a0bbea021e7c8ce1716,344788,1,digital_text,"White, circular, biconvex tablets coded TR100 on one face with a breakline and plain on the other face. The tablets are packaged in Aluminum / PVC blisters packed",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Flecainide acetate (Tambocor) tablets are indicated for: a) Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardias, including atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia, atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia and other supraventricular tachycardias of unspeciﬁed mechanism. b) Paroxysmal atrial ﬁbrillation/ﬂutter. c) Symptomatic sustained ventricular tachycardia. d) Premature ventricular contractions and/ or non-sustained ventricular tachycardia which are causing disabling symptoms.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T04:49:57.251370+00:00
