PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY40119,Atorvastatin (As Calcium Propylene Glycol Solvate),Atorvast-Natrapharm,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1nD_OAYlspDNL80crF23KLfY7bdPiNrVx/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY40119_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1nD_OAYlspDNL80crF23KLfY7bdPiNrVx_view_usp_drivesdk_5601b98764.pdf,page_text/DR-XY40119_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1nD_OAYlspDNL80crF23KLfY7bdPiNrVx_view_usp_drivesdk_5601b98764.json,ed27f581070da5149f32651956a7da3e5adc4330f665fd2eb9b8f355c88be035,16373217,2,ocr,"Drug Substance Adolescent females should be counselled on appropriate contraceptive methods while on Atorvastatin therapy (see CONTRAINDICA- TIONS and PRECAUTIONS, Use in Pregnancy). Atorvastatin has not been studied in controlled clinical trials involving pre-pubertal Proper Name: Atorvastatin Calcium Propylene Glycol Solvate patients or patients younger than 10 years of age. Chemical Name: (8A,5A)-7-2-(4-Flurophenyl)-5-(1-methylethy!)-3-phenyl-4-[(phenylamino)carbonyl]-1H-pyrrole-1-yl}-3,5- Doses of atorvastatin up to 80 mg/day for 1 year have been evaluated in 8 pediatric patients with homozygous familial hypercholesterol- dihydroxy-1-heptanoic acid,calcium salt (2:1), propylene glycol solvate emia (see Clinical Studies - Heterozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia in pediatric patients). Molecular formula and molecular weight: CegH76CaF2N4012 and 1231.45 g/mol Structural Formula: Geriatric Use: Treatment experience in adults /0 years or older (N=221) with doses of atorvastatin up to 80 mg/day has demonstrated —— that the safety and effectiveness of atorvastatin in this population was similar to that of patients </0 years of age. Pharmacokinetic Cat evaluation of atorvastatin in subjects over the age of 65 years indicates an increased AUC. As a precautionary measure, the lowest H3C CH, = OH QOH oO dose should be administered initially (see DETAILED PHARMACOLOGY, Human Pharmacokinetics; REFERENCES). Elderly patients may be more susceptible to myopathy (see WARNINGS - Muscle Effects - Predisposing Factors for Myopathy/Rhabdo- Qu. aa myolysis). ADVERSE REACTIONS OH Adverse reactions with atorvastatin have usually been mild and transient. In the atorvastatin placebo-controlled clinical trial database of 16,066 (8/55 atorvastatin versus 7311 placebo) patients treated for a median period of 53 weeks, 5.2% of patients on atorvastatin ) © discontinue due to adverse reactions compared to 4.0% of the patients on placebo. Adverse experiences occurring at an incidence =1% in patients participating in placebo-controlled clinical studies of atorvastatin and reported to be possibly, probably or definitely drug related are shown in Table 1 below: ae Table 1: Associated Adverse Events Reported in 21 % of Patients in Placebo-Controlled Clinical Trials Description: Atorvastatin calcium propylene glycol solvate is a white to off-white crystalline powder that is practically insoluble in Atorvastatin % (n=8755) Placebo % (n=/311) Gastrointestinal disorders: aqueous solutions of pH 4 and below. Atorvastatin calcium propylene glycol solvate is very slightly soluble in distilled water, pH 7.4 phosphate buffer and acetonitrile, slightly soluble in ethanol, and freely soluble in methanol. Diarrhea 6.8 6.3 Dyspepsia 4.6 4.3",1,1,PHARMACEUTICAL INFORMATION,"Atorvastatin (atorvastatin calcium) is indicated as an adjunct to lifestyle changes, including diet, for the reduction of elevated total Nausea 4.0 3.9 cholesterol (total-C), LDL-C, triglycerides (TG) and apolipoprotein B (apo B), the Total-C/HDL-D ratio and for increasing HDL-C; in Constipation 3.9 43 Flatulence 1.2 1.0 hyperlipidemic and dyslipidemic conditions, including: General disorders and administration site conditions * Primary hypercholesterolemia (Type Ila); Asthenia 1.1 1.1 * Combined (mixed) hyperlipidemia (Type IIb), including familial combined hyperlipidemia,regardless of whether cholesterol or triglycerides are the lipid abnormality of concern; Infections and Infestations: * Dysbetalipoproteinemia (Type Ill); Nasopharyngitis 8.3 8.2 * Hypertriglyceridemia (Type IV); * Familial hypercholesterolemia (homozygous and heterozygous). For homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia, Metabolism and nutrition disorders: Liver function test abnormal” 4.1 2.0 Atorvastatin should be used as an adjunct to treatments such as LDL apheresis, or as monotherapy if such 1.9 1.8 treatments are not available. Blood creatine phosphokinase increased * An adjunct to diet to reduce total-C, LDL-C, and apo B levels in boys and postmenarchal girls, 10 to 1/ years of age with Hyperglycemia 9.9 9.9 heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia, if after an adequate trial of diet therapy the following findings are still present: Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: a. LDL-C remains =4.9 mmol/L (190 mg/dL) or Arthralgia 6.9 6.5 6.0 9.9 b. LDL-C remains 24.1 mmol/L (160 mg/dL) and: Pain in extremity ¢ there is a positive family history of premature cardiovascular disease or Musculoskeletal Pain 3.8 3.6 ‘two or more other CVD risk factors are present in the pediatric patient. Muscle spasms 3.6 3.0 Prior to initiating therapy with Atorvastatin, secondary causes should be excluded for elevations in plasma lipid levels (e.g. poorly Myalgia 3.5 3.1 controlled diabetes mellitus, hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, dysproteinemias, obstructive liver disease, and alcoholism), and a Joint swelling 1.3 1.2 lipid profile performed to measure total cholesterol, LDL-C, HDL-C, and TG. For patients with TG <4.52 mmol/L (<400 mg/dL), LDL-C Nervous system disorders can be estimated using the following equation: Headache 6.5 6./ LDL-C (mmol/L) = total-C - [(0.37 x (TG) + HDL-C)] Respiratory, thoracic and mediastinal disorders: LDL-C (mg/dL) = total-C - [(0.2 x (TG) + HDL-C)] 2.3 2.1 Pharyngolaryngeal pain For patients with TG levels >4.52 mmol/L (>400 mg/dL), this equation is less accurate and LDL-C concentrations should be measured Epistaxis 1.2 1.1 directly or by ultracentrifugation. “alanine aminotransferase increased, aspartate aminotransferase increased, blood bilirubin increased, hepatic enzyme increased, liver function test Patients with high or very high triglyceride levels, i.e. >2.2 mmol/L (200 mg/dL) or >5.6 mmol/L (500 mg/dL), respectively, may require abnormal and transaminases increased. triglyceride-lowering therapy (fenofibrate, bezafibrate or nicotinic acid) alone or in combination with Atorvastatin. The following additional adverse events were reported in placebo-controlled clinical trials during atorvastatin therapy: Muscle cramps, In general, combination therapy with fibrates must be undertaken cautiously and only after risk-benefit analysis (see myositis, muscle fatigue, myopathy, paresthesia, peripheral neuropathy, pancreatitis, hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice, cholestasis, anorexia, vomiting, abdominal discomfort, alopecia, pruritus, rash, urticaria, erectile dysfunction, nightmare, vision blurred, tinnitus,",1,1,INDICATIONS AND CLINICAL USE,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T04:56:23.883291+00:00
