sible for dehydrogenation and hydroxylation of aripiprazole, and N-dealkylation is catalyzed by CYP3A4. Aripiprazole is the predominant drug moiety in the systemic cir culation. At steady -state, dehydro-aripiprazole, the active metabolite, represents about 40% of aripiprazole AUC in plasma. Following a single oral dose of [ 14C]-labeled aripiprazole, approximately 25% and 55% of the administered radioactivity was recovered in the urine and feces, respectively. Less than 1% of unchanged aripiprazole was excreted in the urine and approximately 18% of the oral dose was recovered unchanged in the feces. Drug Interaction Studies Effects of other drugs on the exposures of aripip razole and dehydro -aripiprazole are summarized in Figure 1 and Figure 2, respectively. B ased on simulation, a 4.5 -fold increase in mean C max and AUC values at steady -state is expected when extensive metabolizers of CYP2D6 are administered with both strong CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 inhibitors. A 3 -fold increase in mean C max and AUC values at steady -state is expected in poor metabolizers of CYP2D6 administered with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. Figure 1: The effects of other drugs on aripiprazole pharmacokinetics [Page 54] Change Relative to Reference (without interacting drug) 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 CYP3A4 Inhibitor: ketoconazole AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax Other: valproate Gastric Acid Blockers: famotidine CYP2D6 Inhibitor: quinidine CYP3A4 Inducer: carbamazepine Effect of Other Drugs on Abilify PK Aripiprazole Fold Change and 90% CI lithium lorazepam Figure 2: The effects of other drugs on dehydro-aripiprazole pharmacokinetics Change Relative to Reference (without interacting drug) 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 CYP3A4 Inhibitor: ketoconazole AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax Other: valproate Gastric Acid Blockers: famotidine CYP2D6 Inhibitor: quinidine CYP3A4 Inducer: carbamazepine Effect of Other Drugs on Abilify PK Dehydro-Aripiprazole Fold Change and 90% CI lithium lorazepam The effects of Aripiprazole (Abilify®) on the exposures of other drugs are summarized in Figure 3. A population PK anal ysis in patients with majo r depressive disorder showed no substantial change in plasma concentrations of fluoxetine (20 or 40 mg/day), paroxetine CR (37.5 or 50 mg/day), or sertraline (100 or 150 mg/day) dosed to steady -state. The steady-state plasma concentrations of fluoxetine and norfluoxetine increased by about 18% and 36%, respectively, and concentrations of paroxe tine decreased by about 27%. The steady-state plasma concentrations of sertraline and desmethylsertraline were not substantially changed whe n these antidepressant the rapies were coadministered with aripiprazole. Figure 3: The effects of Aripiprazole (Abilify®) on pharmacokinetics of other drugs [Page 55] Change Relative to Reference (without interacting drug) 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax INR AUCCmax DM/DRP AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax Effect of Abilify on Other Drugs PK Fold Change and 90% CI Other valproate lithium UGT1A4 lamotrigine CYP2D6 dextropmethorphan CYP2C9, 2C19 warfarin omeprazole S-warfarin R-warfarin lorazepam venlafaxine O-desmethylvenlafaxine escitalopram venlafaxine Studies in Specific Populations Exposures of aripiprazole and dehydro-aripiprazole in specific populations are summarized in Figure 4 and Figure 5, respectively. In addition, in pediatric patients (10 to 17 years o f age) administered with Aripiprazole (Abilify ®) (20 mg to 30 mg), the body weight corrected aripiprazole clearance was similar to the adults. Figure 4: Effects of Intrinsic Factors on Aripiprazole Pharmacokinetics Change Relative to Reference 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 Gender female vs. male AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax AUCCmax Renal Impairment: Severe Age 18-64 vs. >65 years old Hepatic Impairment: mild vs. normal Special Populations PK Aripiprazole Fold Change and 90% CI moderate vs. normal severe vs. normal C