PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY47133,Etelcalcetide (as hydrochloride),Parsabiv,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/10myDT9wj9C5KVW64H9YlurL2qWFyuz4h/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY47133_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10myDT9wj9C5KVW64H9YlurL2qWFyuz4h_view_usp_drivesdk_f4f649be77.pdf,page_text/DR-XY47133_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10myDT9wj9C5KVW64H9YlurL2qWFyuz4h_view_usp_drivesdk_f4f649be77.json,b46885579fd65cfe0ce7ade40d3b9442f36944b7cbc1ddfd8e664414b5729544,2200459,6,ocr,"Dose adjustments based on serum calcium levels Solution for injection. Serum calcium should be measured within | week of initiation or dose adjustment of Parsabiv. Once the maintenance phase has been established for a patient, corrected serum calcium should be measured Clear colourless solution. approximately every 4 weeks. In the studies total serum calcium was measured using Roche modular analysers. The lower limit of the normal range for corrected serum calcium was 8.3 mg/dl. (2.08 mmol/L). Other laboratory assays may have different cut-offs for the lower limit of the normal 4 CLINICAL PARTICULARS range. 4.1 Therapeutic indications In the event that clinically meaningful decreases in corrected serum calcium levels below the lower limit of the normal range occur and/or symptoms of hypocalcaemia occur, the following management is recommended: Parsabiv is indicated for the treatment of secondary hyperparathyroidism (SHPT) in adult patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD) on haemodialysis therapy. Corrected serum calcium value 4.2 Posology and thod of ad ation or clinical symptoms of Recommendations | hypocalcaemia*: Posology . If clinically indicated: - start or increase calcium supplements, calcium- The recommended initial dose of etelcalcetide is 5 mg administered by bolus injection 3 times per <8.3 mg/dL (2.08 mmol/L) and containing phosphate binders, and/or vitamin D week. Corrected serum calcium should be at or above the lower limit of the normal range prior to > 7.5 mg/dL (1.88 mmol/L) sterols. administration of first dose of Parsabiv, a dose increase, or reinitiation after a dose stop (see also dose increase dialysate calcium concentration. adjustments based on serum calcium levels). Parsabiv should not be admi d more fi ly than consider reducing Parsabiv dose. 3 times per week. — [Page 2] Corrected serum calcium value 4.4 Special warnings and precautions for use Recommendations or clinical symptoms of hypocalcaemia*: Hypocalcaemia Stop Parsabiv until corrected serum calcium levels are Parsabiv treatment should not be initiated in patients if the corrected serum calcium is less than the > 8.3 mg/dL (2.08mmol/L) and symptoms of hypocalcaemia (if present) have resolved lower limit of the normal range (see section 4.3). If clinically indicated: - Potential manifestations of hypocalcaemia include paraesthesias, myalgias, muscle spasm and start or increase calcium supplements, calcium- seizures. containing phosphate binders, and/or vitamin D < 7.5 mg/dL (1.88 mmol/L) or sterols. symptoms of hypocalcaemia = increase dialysate calcium concentration. Since etelcalcetide lowers serum calcium, patients should be advised to seek medical attention if they experience symptoms of hypocalcaemia and should be monitored for the occurrence of hypocalcaemia Reinitiate Parsabiv at a dose 5 mg lower than the last administered dose. If patient's last administered dose was (see section 4.2). Serum calcium levels should be measured prior to initiating treatment, within 1 week 2.5 mg or 5 mg, reinitiate at 2.5 mg once corrected serum of initiation or dose adjustment of Parsabiv and every 4 weeks during treatment. If clinically meaningful decreases in corrected serum calcium levels occur, steps should be taken to increase serum calcium levels are > 8.3 mg/dL (2.08 mmol/L) and symptoms of hypocalcaemia (if present) have resolved. calcium levels (see section 4.2). * Total calcium was measured using Roche modular analyser. For albumin levels < 4.0 g/dL cCa (mg/dL) = Ventricular arrhythmia and OT prolongation secondary to hypocalcaemia Total Ca (mg/dL) + (4 - albumin[g/dL])*0.8 Switch from cinacalcet to Parsabiv Decreases in serum calcium can prolong the QT interval, potentially resulting in ventricular arrhythmia (see section 4.8). Serum calcium levels should be closely monitored in patients with congenital long QT syndrome, previous history of QT prolongation, family history of long QT Parsabiv should not be initiated in patients until 7 days after the last dose of cinacalcet and the corrected serum calcium is at or above the lower limit of the normal range (see section 5.1). syndrome or sudden cardiac death and other conditions that predispose to QT prolongation and ventricular arrhythmia while being treated with Parsabiv. Paediatric population Convulsions The safety and efficacy of etelcalcetide in children and adolescents less than 18 years has not yet been established. No data are available. The threshold for seizures may be lowered by significant reductions in serum calcium levels. Serum calcium levels should be closely monitored in patients with a history of a convulsion disorder while Elderly being treated with Parsabiv. Worsening heart failur Dosing recommendations for elderly patients are the same as for adult patients Method of administration Decreased myocardial performance, hypotension, and congestive heart failure (CHF ) may be associated with significant reductions in serum calcium levels. Serum calcium levels should be Parsabiv should not be diluted. monitored in patients with a history of congestive heart failure while being treated with Parsabiv (see section 4.2), which may be associated with reductions in serum calcium levels. Parenteral medicinal products should be inspected visually for particulate matter and change in colour prior to administration. Co-administration with other medicinal products Administer Parsabiv with caution in patients receiving any other medicinal products known to lower Parsabiv is administered into the venous line of the dialysis circuit at the end of the haemodialysis serum calcium. Closely monitor serum calcium (see section 4.5) treatment during rinse-back or intravenously after rinse-back. When given during rinse-back at least 150 mL of rinse-back volume should be administered after injection. If rinse-back is completed and Parsabiv was not administered, then it may be administered intravenously followed by at least 10 mL Patients receiving Parsabiv should not be given cinacalcet. Concurrent administration may result in severe hypocalcaemia. saline flush volume.",1,2,PHARMACEUTICAL FORM,"In the event that clinically meaningful decreases in corrected serum calcium levels below the lower limit of the normal range occur and/or symptoms of hypocalcaemia occur, the following management is recommended: Parsabiv is indicated for the treatment of secondary hyperparathyroidism (SHPT) in adult patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD) on haemodialysis therapy. Corrected serum calcium value 4.2 Posology and thod of ad ation or clinical symptoms of Recommendations | hypocalcaemia*: Posology . If clinically indicated: - start or increase calcium supplements, calcium- The recommended initial dose of etelcalcetide is 5 mg administered by bolus injection 3 times per <8.3 mg/dL (2.08 mmol/L) and containing phosphate binders, and/or vitamin D week. Corrected serum calcium should be at or above the lower limit of the normal range prior to > 7.5 mg/dL (1.88 mmol/L) sterols. administration of first dose of Parsabiv, a dose increase, or reinitiation after a dose stop (see also dose increase dialysate calcium concentration. adjustments based on serum calcium levels). Parsabiv should not be admi d more fi ly than consider reducing Parsabiv dose. 3 times per week. — [Page 2] Corrected serum calcium value",1,2,Therapeutic indications,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T08:19:19.802988+00:00
