PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY47136,Propofol,Nupovel,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1O0cf9q4JjFiw5n0g9bzLwz5qz0IFVfev/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY47136_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1O0cf9q4JjFiw5n0g9bzLwz5qz0IFVfev_view_usp_drivesdk_fa3e970be5.pdf,page_text/DR-XY47136_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1O0cf9q4JjFiw5n0g9bzLwz5qz0IFVfev_view_usp_drivesdk_fa3e970be5.json,59304dce7cdcfcb7e22942ff74c6aaa64aad99446be9af4c62f38e8d138a9825,920896,2,digital_text,Propofol (Nupovel®) 10 mg/mL (1% w/v) Emulsion for Injection (I.V./Infusion) is a white sterile emulsion in clear glass vial.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Propofol (Nupovel ®) 10 mg/mL (1% w/v) Emulsion for Injection (I.V./Infusion) is an IV general anesthetic and sedation drug that can be used as described in the table below. Table 1. Indications for Propofol (Nupovel®) 10 mg/mL (1% w/v) Emulsion for Injection (I.V./Infusion) Safety, effectiveness and dosing guidelines for propofol emulsion for injection have not been established for MAC Sedation in the pediatric population; therefore, it is not recommended for this use (see PRECAUTIONS, Pediatric Use). Propofol emulsion for injection is not recommended for induction of anesthesia below the age of 3 years or for maintenance of anesthesia below the age of 2 months because its safety and effectiveness have not been established in those populations. In the Intensive Care Unit (ICU), propofol emulsion for injection can be administered to intubated, mechanically ventilated adult patients to provide continuous sedation and control of stress responses only by persons skilled in the medical management of critically ill patients and trained in cardiovascular resuscitation and airway management. Propofol emulsion for injection is not indicated for use in Pediatric ICU sedation since the safety of this regimen has not been established (see PRECAUTIONS, Pediatric Use). Propofol emulsion for injection is not recommended for obstetrics, including Cesarean section deliveries. Propofol emulsion for injection crosses the placenta, and as with other general anesthetic agents, the administration of propofol emulsion for injection may be associated with neonatal depression (see PRECAUTIONS). Propofol emulsion for injection is not recommended for use in nursing mothers because propofol has been reported to be excreted in human milk, and the effects of oral absorption of small amounts of propofol are not known (see",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T08:23:58.070023+00:00
