PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DR-XY48857,Co-Amoxiclav,Spotclav Forte,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1y0hmAa7tZIfNsTQBOe4o-0IgeWBSlKlH/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DR-XY48857_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1y0hmAa7tZIfNsTQBOe4o-0IgeWBSlKlH_view_usp_drivesdk_80797f44e3.pdf,page_text/DR-XY48857_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1y0hmAa7tZIfNsTQBOe4o-0IgeWBSlKlH_view_usp_drivesdk_80797f44e3.json,dcf81943f199ef52f65dbfab7b8254c518ec3448dbcc0ad88f57170cedcfbe28,1009716,2,ocr,"Neither amoxicillin nor clavulanic acid is highly protein-bound; studies show that Co-amoxiclav (Spotclav Forte) 457 mg/5 mL suspension: After reconstitution, about 13% to 25% of total plasma drug concentration of each compound is each 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) suspension contains: Amoxicillin (as trihydrate) 400 protein-bound. Both Co-amoxiclav components readily cross the placenta. Only small amounts mg; Clavulanic acid (as potassium clavulanate) 57 mg. of amoxicillin and clavulanic acid are distributed in human milk.",1,1,Product Description,"Streptococcus pyogenes Co-amoxiclav (Spotclav Forte) is indicated for the treatment of the following Enterococcus faecalis bacterial infections due to susceptible organisms Aerobic Gram-negative Haemophilus influenzae e Upper respiratory tract infection (including ENT) e.g. tonsilitis, Microorganisms *Escherichia coli* sinusitis, otitis media. Moraxella catarrhalis * Lower respiratory tract infections e.g. acute and chronic bronchitis, Klebsiella spp. Proteus mirabilis * lobar bronchopneumonia Genito-urinary tract infection e.g. cystitis, urethritis, pyelonephritis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae* Eikenella corrodens* pelvic infections, chancroid, gonorrhea, other e.g. puerperal sepsis, Anaerobic Gram-positive Peptostreptococcus spp. septic abortion, intra-abdominal sepsis. Skin and soft tissue infections e.g. boils, abscesses, cellulitis, Microorganisms Anaerobic Gram-negative Bacteroides spp. “including animal bites, wound infection Microorganisms B. fragilis e Bone and joint infection e.g. osteomyelitis Fusobacterium spp. e Dental infections e.g. dentoalveolar abscess. *Including B-lactamase-producing and non-producing strains Dosage Adults and Children over 12 years Although most strains of Enterobacter species are resistant in vitro, clinical Adult and Children = 40kg Recommended doses: efficacy has been demonstrated with Co-amoxiclav (Spotclav Forte) in urinary e Standard dose: (for all tract infections caused by these organisms. indications) 875mg/125mg two times a day Pharmacokinetics: Co-amoxiclav is stable in the presence of acidic gastric Higher dose- (particularly for secretions and is well absorbed following oral administration. Peak serum infections such as otitis media, concentrations of amoxicillin and of clavulanic acid are generally attained within sinusitis, lower respiratory tract 1 to 2.5 hours after oral administration. infections, and urinary tract infections): 875mg/125mg In a study in children 2 to 5 years old with urinary tract infections who received a single oral dose of 125 mg of amoxicillin and 31.75 mg of clavulanic acid three times a day suspension, the mean serum concentrations (Cmax) of amoxicillin were 9.4, 9.7, and 6.5 g/mL and of clavulanic acid were 2.1, 4.4, and 2.5 pg/mL at 30, 60, and 90 minutes, respectively, after the dose. Children 12 years and below Children 6-12 years (18- 2.5 mL every 8 hours or 0.2 mL/kg daily in In a study in fasting children who received a single amoxicillin dose of 35 mg/kg 3 divided doses given as Co-amoxiclav oral suspension, concentrations of amoxicillin and of 40 kg) clavulanic acid in middle ear effusions averaged 3 and 0.5 g/mL, respectively, 2 hours after the dose. When a single 5 mL oral dose of Co-amoxiclav 457 mg/5 mL suspension was Dosage in Hepatic Impairment administered in healthy male and female subjects (fasted _ state), Dose with caution; monitor hepatic function at regular intervals. There is, as pharmacokinetic parameters reached were: Cmax 5.789 yg/mL, Tmax 1.164 yet, insufficient evidence on which to base dosage recommendation. [Page 2]",1,1,Indications,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T12:09:45.314472+00:00
