PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-10990,Ursodeoxycholic Acid,Bilecid,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/16jXDdQAQZhrERkqKlFU4wqT2wUZcKomY/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-10990_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_16jXDdQAQZhrERkqKlFU4wqT2wUZcKomY_view_usp_drivesdk_0a9d149031.pdf,page_text/DRP-10990_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_16jXDdQAQZhrERkqKlFU4wqT2wUZcKomY_view_usp_drivesdk_0a9d149031.json,60e2b47d9a9f8b1d4a5d71ac9e3a393430feef47c212268ade0ed39bc79e69b4,1433091,1,digital_text,White hard gelatin capsule size 0. The content – white or almost white powder.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Ursodeoxycholic acid (Bilecid) is indicated for the treatment of: - dissolution of cholesterol gallstones in the gall bladder (the gallstones must not show as shadows on X-ray images and should not exceed 15 mm in diameter; the function of the gall bladder must not be substantially limited despite the present gallstones); - primary biliary cholangitis (PBC, previously known as primary biliary cirrhosis) in patients without decompensated hepatic cirrhosis; - hepatobiliary disease associated with cystic fibrosis in children and adolescents aged 6-18 years.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T14:47:41.724330+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-10990-01,,,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1s9r8hJS1qCRDpQFIQnBDzgc3sYiBue7H/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-10990-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1s9r8hJS1qCRDpQFIQnBDzgc3sYiBue7H_view_usp_drivesdk_014beba827.pdf,page_text/DRP-10990-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1s9r8hJS1qCRDpQFIQnBDzgc3sYiBue7H_view_usp_drivesdk_014beba827.json,2fa62132bce0bf50c06dd00d83875d6e0580a69ba5fc4ab23c92d1c72dd52ac0,1432125,1,digital_text,Urosodeoxycholic Acid 250 mg is a white hard gelatin capsule size 0. The content – white or almost white powder.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Ursodeoxycholic Acid is indicated for the treatment of: - dissolution of cholesterol gallstones in the gall bladder (the gallstones must not show as shadows on X-ray images and should not exceed 15 mm in diameter; the function of the gall bladder must not be substantially limited despite the present gallstones); - primary biliary cholangitis (PBC, previously known as primary biliary cirrhosis) in patients without decompensated hepatic cirrhosis; - hepatobiliary disease associated with cystic fibrosis in children and adolescents aged 6-18 years.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T14:47:38.080773+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-10994,Azithromycin (as dihydrate),Azisaph-D,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UvzriLukZaRYq-Rd220yDMUtG_sNh6DE/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-10994_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1UvzriLukZaRYq-Rd220yDMUtG_sNh6DE_view_usp_drivesdk_3d9771ad2a.pdf,page_text/DRP-10994_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1UvzriLukZaRYq-Rd220yDMUtG_sNh6DE_view_usp_drivesdk_3d9771ad2a.json,d683c1cfd0f9a594d90bd925b8a60407ccbd7a07c6739607e24a951739a4793f,752562,1,digital_text,,,,,,,,,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-06-29T14:47:46.848269+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-11020-01,Prednisolone (as Sodium Phosphate),Predzone,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1yqb1g9XQUvxd7fUmp8goKd9qPxwP5tnp/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-11020-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1yqb1g9XQUvxd7fUmp8goKd9qPxwP5tnp_view_usp_drivesdk_512ff8dab5.pdf,page_text/DRP-11020-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1yqb1g9XQUvxd7fUmp8goKd9qPxwP5tnp_view_usp_drivesdk_512ff8dab5.json,e1a6718ead5f5ade0d7eaade73d89e4cd29f491bd614d407f976e6d5e01777bd,612282,1,digital_text,"Prednisolone 20 mg / 5 mL is a light pink syrup with vanilla and strawberry flavor and odor. PHARMACOLOGIC CATEGORY This medicine contains the active ingredient prednisolone, which belongs to a group of medicines called corticosteroids or “steroids”. These corticosteroids occur naturally in the body and help maintain health and well-being. Boosting your body with extra corticosteroids (such as prednisolone) is an effective way to treat various illness involving inflammation in the body. Prednisolone reduces this inflammation and lowering the body’s immune response, which could go on making your condition worse. PHARMACODYNAMICS Corticosteroids bind to the glucocorticoid receptor, inhibiting pro-inflammatory signals, and promoting anti-inflammatory signals. Prednisolone has a short duration of action as the half-life is 2-3 hours. Corticosteroids have a wide therapeutic window as patients may require doses that are multiples of what the body naturally produces. Patients taking corticosteroids should be counselled regarding the risk of hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis suppression and increased susceptibility to infections. PHARMACOKINETICS After oral intake, it exhibits rapid absorption and becomes almost completely (80%-100%) available. Peak plasma concentrations reach 1 to 2 hours after oral administration. Distribution of prednisolone is dependent on its protein-binding properties. Prednisolone binds reversibly to albumin as well as to a specific α1-glycoprotein named transcortin (corticosteroid-binding globulin). Transcortin has a higher affinity but a relatively low capacity for binding prednisolone and thus becomes saturated at therapeutic concentrations because of its relatively lower abundance in plasma. On the contrary, albumin possesses a lower affinity but a much higher capacity for binding prednisolone and does not appear to become saturated. To summarize, prednisolone shows higher protein binding (80%-90%) at low concentrations, but lower protein binding (60%-70%) at higher concentrations as transcortin becomes saturated.",1,1,DESCRIPTION OF THE PRODUCT,"Prednisolone is used to treat a variety of inflammatory diseases including severe asthma, rheumatoid arthritis, allergic reactions, severe skin conditions, and some blood disorders.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T14:47:49.870341+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-11026,Acetylcysteine,Fluzetrin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1EcGfl_PPNOKmcT7x4nwvTRRZ7G6FmQwx/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-11026_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1EcGfl_PPNOKmcT7x4nwvTRRZ7G6FmQwx_view_usp_drivesdk_12670e3800.pdf,page_text/DRP-11026_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1EcGfl_PPNOKmcT7x4nwvTRRZ7G6FmQwx_view_usp_drivesdk_12670e3800.json,778eccd38d095a66f359d046a5ce973978e0a81b08881ca782631dbececf1b52,544599,1,digital_text,"Effervescent tablet - White to light yellow, round, flat-faced tablets, embossed with “SYN” on one side. Pharmacodynamics: Pharmacotherapeutic group: Mucolytics Acetylcysteine exerts its mucolytic action through its free sulfhydryl group, which opens the disulfide bonds and lowers mucus viscosity. This action increases with increasing pH and is most significant at pH 7 to 9. The mucolytic action of acetylcysteine is not affected by the presence of DNA. N-acetylcysteine has been demonstrated to cause a decrease in sputum consistency, to facilitate easier expectoration, and to increase sputum volume. Bronchial mucus is composed of over 95% water; however, the physical characteristics of the mucus are due to glycoproteins. These glycoproteins bind to each other by way of disulfide bonds and give the mucus viscosity. N-acetylcysteine ruptures these disulfide bonds causing depolymerization and a rapid decrease in mucus viscosity. It also produces an irritative bronchorrheic effect on the mucosa, stimulating mucociliary clearance; this irritative effect may cause bronchospasm, thus acetylcysteine is not recommended in asthmatics. Pharmacokinetics: Absorption Absorption of acetylcysteine is rapid following oral administration, but the bioavailability is only 6-10% due to extensive first past metabolism. Oral bioavailability is similar for a single 600-mg dose and three 200-mg doses Peak plasma levels of acetylcysteine occur approximately one hour following oral administration. Distribution Acetylcysteine may be present in plasma as the parent compound or as various oxidised metabolites such as N-acetylcysteine, N,N-diacetylcysteine, and cysteine either free or bound to plasma proteins by labile disulfide bonds or as a fraction incorporated into protein peptide chains. Following a 100-mg oral dose, 48% was present in lung tissue. Metabolism Acetylcysteine undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver and intestinal wall. Acetylcysteine undergoes rapid deacetylation in vivo to yield cysteine or oxidation to yield diacetylcysteine. Following deacetylation in the liver, it enters the normal metabolic pathway of the amino acid cysteine. An appreciable elevation in total serum sulfhydryl concentration occurs. Elimination Renal Excretion: 22% to 30% Feces: 3% Total Body Clearance: 6.5 L/hr (healthy subjects) The mean terminal half-life is approximately 6 hours.",1,1,Product Description:,Reduce the viscosity of the secretion from respiratory tract.,1,1,Indication:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T14:47:52.827556+00:00
