following subcutaneous Storage Condition: administration sumatriptan is excreted into breast milk. Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Infant exposure can be minimised by avoiding breast- feeding for 12 hours after treatment. Keep out of reach of children. Caution: Adverse Drug Reactions: Foods, Drugs, Devices, and Cosmetics Act prohibits Adverse events are listed below by system organ class dispensing without prescription. and frequency. Frequencies are defined as: very common (= 1/10), common (= 1/100 to <1/100), uncommon For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph. Seek medical attention (2 1/1000 to <1/1000), rare (= 1/10,000 to <1/1000) and very rare (<1/10,000). immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. Nervous System Disorders Dosage Form and Packaging Availability (pack size): 50 mg Film-Coated Tablet Common: Dizziness, drowsiness, sensory Alu/Alu Blister Pack x 4’s (Box of 4’s) disturbance including paresthesia and Registration number: DRP-11252 hypoesthesia. Date of First Authorization: February 17, 2022 Vascular Disorders Date of Revision of Package Insert: July 15, 2024 Common: Transient increases in blood pressure arising soon after treatment, flushing. Respiratory, Thoracic, Mediastinal Disorders Common: | Dyspnea Gastrointestinal Disorders Common: Nausea and vomiting occurred in some patients but the relationship to sumatriptan is not clear. and C Tissue Disorders The following symptom is usually transient and may be intense and can affect any part of the body including the chest and throat: Manufactured by: Siam Bheasach Co., Ltd. Common: Sensations of heaviness. 123 Soi Chokechairuammitr, Vibhavadi-Rangsit Road, Chomphon, Chatuchak, Bangkok 10900 General Disorders and Connective Tissue Disorders and 9 Soi Chokechairuammitr 3, Vibhavadi-Rangsit The following symptoms are usually transient and Road, Dindang, Dindang, Bangkok 10400, Thailand. may be intense and can affect any part of the body including