PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-12540-02,Meropenem (as trihydrate),Emero 500,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/16sg4A8pR3sB8dtGG9PGT9KhlWo6FIFom/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-12540-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_16sg4A8pR3sB8dtGG9PGT9KhlWo6FIFom_view_usp_drivesdk_b43afc9b8c.pdf,page_text/DRP-12540-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_16sg4A8pR3sB8dtGG9PGT9KhlWo6FIFom_view_usp_drivesdk_b43afc9b8c.json,7242f4ccaee2d4f3d153cd9c0b9a648ad70a338190276c2dd039a7cba81a8b95,1298324,2,digital_text,"Meropenem is a s terile, pyrogen-free, synthetic, broad-spectrum, carbapenem a ntibiotic for intravenous administration. It is (4R, 5S, 6S)-3 [ [3S,5S) - 5 - (dimethylcarbamoyl)-pyrrolidin-3-yl] thiol-6( 1R)-1hydr oxyethyl]-4-methyl-7oxo-1-aza bicyc lo[3,2,0] hept-2-ene-2- ca rboxylic a cid trihydrate. Its empirical formula is C H N O S. 3H 0 with molecular weight 437.52.17 25 3 5 2 Meropenem is a white to pale yellow crystalline powder. The solution varies from colourless to yellow depending on the concentration. The pH of freshly constituted solution is between 7.3 and 8.3. Meropenem is soluble in 5 % monobasic potassium phosphate solution, sparingly soluble in water, very slightly soluble in hydrated ethanol, and practically insoluble in acetone or ether. When constituted as instructed, each 500mg Meropenem vial will deliver 500 mg of meropenem and 45.1 mg of sodium as sodium carbonate When constituted as instructed, each 1g Meropenem vial will deliver 1g of meropenem and 90.2mg of sodium as sodium carbonate (3.92mEq). CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY Mechanism of Action The bactericidal activity of meropenem results from the inhibition of cell wall synthesis. Meropenem readily penetrates the cell wall of most gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria to reach penicillin- binding protein (PBP) targets. Its strongest affinities are towards PBPs 1,2 and 4 of Staphylococci aureus. Bactericidal concentrations (defined as a 3 log 10 reduction in cell counts within 12 to 24 hours) are typically 1-2 times the bacteriostatic concentrations of meropenem, with the exception of Listeria monocytogenes, against which lethal activity is not observed. Meropenem has significant stability to hydrolysis by ß-lactamases of most categories, both penicillinases and cephalosporinases produced by gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, with exception of metallo ß-lactamases Pharmacokinetics and Drug Metabolism At the end of a 30-minute intravenous infusion of a single dose of Meropenem in normal volunteers, mean peak plasma concentrations are approximately 23/49 µg/ml for 500mg/1g dose respectively In subject with normal renal function, the elimination half-life of Meropenem is approximately 1 hour. Approximately 70% of the intravenously administered dose is recovered as unchanged meropenem in urine over 12 hours, after which little further urinary excretion is detectable. No accumulation of meropenem in plasma or urine was observed with regimens using 500mg/1g administered every 8/6",1,1,DESCRIPTION,"Meropenem is indicated as single agent therapy for the following infections when caused by susceptible strains of the designated microorganisms: • Intra-abdominal infections • Bacterial Meningitis (Paediatric patients>3 months only) • Pneumonia and nosocomial Pneumonia • Urinary Tract Infections • Gynecological infections such as endometritis and pelvic inflammatory disease. • Skin and skin structure infections • Septicemia • Empiric treatment for presumed infections in adult patients with febrile neutropenia, used as monotherapy or in combination with anti-viral or anti-fungal agents. Appropriate Cultures should usually be performed before initiating antimicrobial treatment in order to isolate and identify the organisms causing infection and determine their susceptibility the organisms causing infection and determine their susceptibility to Meropenem.",1,1,INDICATIONS AND USAGE,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T16:05:59.561846+00:00
