PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-12834,Diclofenac sodium (equivalent to Diclofenac diethylamine),Coxyn,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XQhf9zCiNVqLqJytyWBchnzzTCgfV3ci/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-12834_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1XQhf9zCiNVqLqJytyWBchnzzTCgfV3ci_view_usp_drivesdk_12ebbace63.pdf,page_text/DRP-12834_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1XQhf9zCiNVqLqJytyWBchnzzTCgfV3ci_view_usp_drivesdk_12ebbace63.json,3be2fa8f93532f975b8321b20e06bea793d26bbe127e9042aa9f1a8f9d86438d,453544,1,digital_text,"Clear, colorless smooth gel. WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE AND STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE? Each gram contains: Diclofenac Sodium (equivalent to Diclofenac diethylamine) BP ................. 10 mg (1% w/w) WHAT IS THIS MEDICINE USED FOR? COXYN Topical Gel contains the active ingredient diclofenac, which belongs to a group of medicines called Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAID). Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel has analgesic and anti-inflammatory properties and, due to the aqueous-alcoholic base, has a soothing and cooling effect. Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel is used for the external treatment of pain, inflammation and swelling such as: • Injuries of the tendons, ligaments, muscles and joints, e.g. sprains, bruises, strains or back pain after sports or accidents. • Localized forms of soft tissue rheumatism such as tendonitis (tennis elbow), shoulder-hand syndrome, bursitis, periarthropathy. • The symptomatic treatment of osteoarthritis of small and medium-sized joints located close to the skin such as the finger joints or knee joints. HOW MUCH AND HOW OFTEN SHOULD YOU USE THIS MEDICINE? Adults and adolescents aged 12 and over: Depending on the size of the painful or swollen area or surfaces to be treated, rub, with light massage, or with more pronounced massage in case of muscular pain, an amount of 2 - 4 g of Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel (cherry to walnut sized amount sufficient to treat an area of about 400 - 800 cm 2) 3 to 4 times a day. Wash hands after application, except for the treatment of finger arthritis. When used without medical prescription, patients should consult their doctor if the condition does not improve within 7 days, or if it gets worse. If symptoms worsen or if symptoms have not improved after one week, consult your doctor immediately. Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel should not be used longer than 2 weeks without medical prescription. Children under 12 Years: It is not recommended for use in children below 12 years. Elderly patients (over 65 years of age): The usual adult dosage of Diclofenac sodium. The duration of treatment depends on the indication and clinical response. Do not use Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel more than: • 2 weeks for muscle and joints injuries (e.g. sprains, stress, bruises) or tendonitis. • 3 weeks for arthritis pain. Longer treatment may be recommended by a doctor. When used without medical prescription, patients should consult their doctor if the condition does not improve withint 7 days, or if it gets worse. WHEN SHOULD YOU NOT TAKE THIS MEDICINE? Do not use Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel in cases of: • Hypersensitivity to diclofenac sodium or to any of the excipients contained in the medicine. • Patients who have attacks of asthma, urticaria, or acute rhinitis precipitated by acetylsali- cylic acid or other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). • Pregnancy during the last trimester. CARE THAT SHOULD BE TAKEN WHEN TAKING THIS MEDICINE? • Do not apply Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel on open wounds (e.g. abrasions, cuts) or on damaged skin (e.g., eczema, skin rash). Discontinue the treatment if a skin rash develops after applying the product. • Avoid applying on large areas of skin or for a longer than approved duration of use, except on medical prescription. The possibility of systemic adverse events from application of Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel cannot be excluded if the preparation is used on large areas of skin and over a prolonged period. • Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel is for external use only. Do not use it in the mouth. Do not swallow it. Wash your hands after use. Be careful not to get Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel in your eyes. In case of contact, rinse your eyes well with water. See doctor if any discomfort persists. • Do not swallow the product. If you accidentaly swallow the topical gel or if a child does, contact your doctor immediately. • A brace or wrap commonly used for injuries like sprains can be used but do not use under airtight (plastic) bandages. • Do not use Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel on a child under 12 years of age. • Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel may contain ingredients that might cause hypersensitivity reaction. UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS OF THIS MEDICINE Like all medicines, the use of Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel can cause the following side effects, although not everybody gets them. Infections and infestations Very rare: Rash pustular Immune System Disorders Very rare: Hypersenstivity (including urticaria), angiodema Respiratory, Thoracic and Mediastinal Disorders Very rare: Asthma Skin and Subcutaneous Tissue Disorders Common: Dermatitis (including contact dermatitis), rash, erythema, eczema, pruritis Rare: Dermatitis bullous Very rare: Photosensitivity reaction If any of the above side effects or any other side effects that are not described in this leaflet occur, you should stop the treatment immediately and consult your doctor or pharmacist. WHAT OTHER MEDICINE OR FOOD SHOULD BE AVOIDED WHILE TAKING THIS MEDICINE? Since systemic absorption of diclofenac sodium from topical application is very low, such interactions are very unlikely. WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU MISS A DOSE? If you miss your application of Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel at the correct time, apply it when you remember and then carry on as normal. Do not apply a double quantity to makeup for the forgotten application. If you have any further questions on the use of this product, consult with your doctor or pharmacist. SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS OF OVERDOSE The low systemic absorption of topical diclofenac makes the overdose very unlikely. However undesirable effects, similar to those observed following an overdose of diclofenac tablets, can be expected if Diclofenac sodium Topical Gel is inadvertently ingested (1 unit of 100 g contains the equivalent of 1 g diclofenac sodium). WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU HAVE TAKEN MORE THAN THE RECOMMENDED DOSAGE? There are no current studies indicating serious effect of diclofenac gel if applied topically more than the recommended dosage. In the event of accidental ingestion, resulting in significant systemic adverse effects, general therapeutic measures normally adopted to treat poisoning with non-steroidal anti-inflamma- tory medicines should be used. Gastric decontamination and the use of activated charcoal should be considered, especially within a short time of ingestion. HOW SHOULD YOU KEEP THIS MEDICINE? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Keep out of sight and reach of children. WHEN SHOULD YOU CONSULT YOUR DOCTOR? If symptoms do not improve or if you experience any side effects, consult your doctor. For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph. Patients should seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. REGISTRATION NUMBER: DRP-12834 DATE OF FIRST AUTHORIZATION: 12 October 2022 DATE OF REVISION OF PATIENT INFORMATION LEAFLET: Not Applicable",1,1,DESCRIPTION OF THE PRODUCT,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-29T16:17:21.202027+00:00
