PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-13048-10,Salbutamol (as sulfate) + Ipratropium bromide,Salbproduo,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1dmArGlShwjCmZPJHPIsXcwYGakMn7XuI/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-13048-10_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1dmArGlShwjCmZPJHPIsXcwYGakMn7XuI_view_usp_drivesdk_cb3ac70351.pdf,page_text/DRP-13048-10_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1dmArGlShwjCmZPJHPIsXcwYGakMn7XuI_view_usp_drivesdk_cb3ac70351.json,dffeb235e7367d7acf68cdcc2fcd8e147d11c118321745f5fb704a499c3ad75b,1487511,2,digital_text,A clear colourless solution.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,Management of reversible bronchospasm associated with obstructive airway diseases.,1,1,INDICATION:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T16:24:11.465152+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-13051,Sitagliptin (as phosphate) + Metformin Hydrochloride,KoztaTM Plus,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1qdKmeWE-uBa-eaSOO2fgHXtU7N22VVHt/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-13051_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1qdKmeWE-uBa-eaSOO2fgHXtU7N22VVHt_view_usp_drivesdk_b55b7348c3.pdf,page_text/DRP-13051_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1qdKmeWE-uBa-eaSOO2fgHXtU7N22VVHt_view_usp_drivesdk_b55b7348c3.json,bd6a4ceea2ced25b113164af7b35332b6adcb38818fcc6f1449eb105cfc8beaf,1696611,1,digital_text,"CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY Pharmacodynamics Sitagliptin + metformin HCl is a combination of two antihyperglycemic agents with complementary mechanisms of action to improve glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Sitagliptin Sitagliptin is a potent and highly selective inhibitor of dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4), an enzyme that inactivates glucagon-like peptide (GLP-1) and glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP), which are incretin hormones. GLP-1 and GIP stimulate insulin synthesis and release from pancreatic β-cells in a glucose-dependent manner (i.e., when glucose concentrations are normal or elevated) by intracellular signaling pathways involving cyclic 3′,5′-adenosine monophosphate (cAMP). GLP-1 also decreases glucagon secretion from pancreatic α-cells in a glucose-dependent manner, leading to reduced hepatic glucose production. The activity of GLP-1 and GIP is limited by the DPP-4 enzyme, which rapidly hydrolyzes the incretin hormones to produce inactive products. Sitagliptin prevents the hydrolysis of incretin hormones by DPP-4, thereby increasing plasma concentrations of the active forms of GLP-1 and GIP. By enhancing active incretin levels, sitagliptin increases insulin release and decreases glucagon levels in a glucose-dependent manner. Sitagliptin lowers hemoglobin A 1c (HbA1c) and lowers fasting and postprandial glucose concentrations in patients with type 2 diabetes. Sitagliptin inhibits DPP-4 selectively with no effect on DPP-8 or DDP-9 in vitro at concentrations approximating those from therapeutic dosage. Metformin HCl Metformin is a biguanide antidiabetic agent that reduces both basal and postprandial plasma glucose concentrations in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus by improving both peripheral and hepatic sensitivity to insulin. It does not stimulate insulin secretion and therefore does not produce hypoglycemia when used alone. Fasting insulin levels and day-long insulin response remain the same or may even decrease with metformin therap y. Metformin may act via three mechanisms: it reduces hepatic glucose production by inhibiting gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis; increases insulin sensitivity in the skeletal muscles and adipocytes, improving peripheral glucose uptake and utilization; delays intestinal glucose absorption. Metformin stimulates intracellular glycogen synthesis by acting on glycogen synthase. It also increases the transport capacity of all types of membrane glucose transporters. Metformin has demonstrated modest favorable effects on lipid metabolism in patients with type 2 diabetes. It lowers total cholesterol, mean fasting serum triglycerides and low density lipoprotein cholesterol levels; it has no adverse effects on other lipid levels. Pharmacokinetics Sitagliptin Sitagliptin is r apidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tr act with peak plasma concentrations reached within 1 to 4 hours after oral administr ation. Absolute bioavailability is approximately 87%. Area under the plasma high-fat meal had no effect on the pharmacokinetics of the drug. Sitagliptin may be administered with or without food. Sitagliptin is about 38% bound to plasma proteins; its mean volume of distribution at steady state is approximately 198 L. Sitagliptin is metabolized to a limited extent by cytochrome P450 (CYP) isoenzymes 3A4 and 2C8 to inactive metabolites; about 79% of sitagliptin is excreted in the urine unchanged. Sitagliptin is mainly eliminated via renal excretion and involves active tubular secretion; it is a substrate for organic anion transporter-3 and P-glycoprotein. Following administration of a single radiolabeled dose of sitagliptin, 87% of the dose was excreted in the urine (mainly as unchanged drug) and 13% was excreted in the feces. The apparent terminal half-life of sitagliptin is approximately 12.4 hours and renal clearance is approximately 350 mL/min. Metformin HCl Metformin HCl is slowly and incompletely absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract after oral administration. The absolute bioavailability of metformin HCl tablet under fasting conditions is approximately 50 to 60% with metformin doses of 500 mg to 1,500 mg. Single doses of metformin 500 mg to 1,500 mg show lack of dose proportionality with increasing doses which is due to decreased absorption rather than altered elimination. Me concentrations of metformin are generally <1 mcg/mL and are reached within 24 to 48 hours at usual clinical doses and dosing schedules. Re being cleared within 24 hours in patients with normal renal function. In the blood, the elimination half-life is about 17.6 hours. Special Populations Elderly ced by age. Elderly subjects (65 to 80 years old) had approximately 19% higher plasma concentrations of sitagliptin compared to younger subjects. The limited pharmacokinetic data of metformin in elderly subjects suggest that total plasma clearance of metformin is decreased, half-life is prolonged, and Cmax is increased, compared to healthy young subjects. It appears that the change in metformin pharmacokinetics with aging is primarily related to a change in renal function. Renal Impairment 2 to <45 mL/min/1.73 m 2 , and an approximately 4-fold increase was observed in patients with severe renal impairment, including patients with end-stage renal disease (ESRD) on hemodialysis, as compared with healthy subjects. The plasma and blood half-life of metformin is prolonged and the renal clearance decreased in proportion to the decrease in creatinine clearance in patients with decreased renal function (based on measured creatinine levels). Hepatic Impairment After administration of a single dose sitagliptin (100 mg) in patients with moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh score 7 to 9), mean AUC and C max of sitagliptin increased approximately 21% and 13%, respectively, compared No pharmacokinetic studies on metformin have been conducted in patients with hepatic impairment.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus • As initial combination therapy in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control • As an adjunct to diet and exer cise to improve glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus w ho have not achieved adequate glycemic contr ol with sitagliptin or metformin monotherapy; or who are already treated with a combination of sitagliptin and metformin as separate components • As triple therapy in combination w ith a sulfonylurea or a thiazolidinedione [a per oxisome pr oliferator-activated r eceptor gamma (PPARγ) agonist] in patients w ith type 2 diabetes mellitus as an adjunct to diet and exercise who have not achieved adequate glycemic control • In combination with insulin in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus as an adjunct to diet and exercise who have not achieved adequate glycemic control Limitations of Use: Sitagliptin + metformin HCl is not indicated for patients with type 1 diabetes mellitus or for the treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis.,1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T16:24:19.353812+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-13052,Lidocaine Hydrochloride,Blesanumb,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xo4sRqjwWBGTsUzvoJz8T-c1G5wXaFls/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-13052_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1xo4sRqjwWBGTsUzvoJz8T-c1G5wXaFls_view_usp_drivesdk_a8a8914f5c.pdf,page_text/DRP-13052_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1xo4sRqjwWBGTsUzvoJz8T-c1G5wXaFls_view_usp_drivesdk_a8a8914f5c.json,d9ff8bcbe6efb285902ff38734c58b046c3361897d3caaa251187e2ac1b09a21,634817,2,digital_text,"Lidocaine Hydrochloride Injection, BP is a clear colourless solution.",1,1,DESCRIPTION:,"Lidocaine Hydrochloride Injection, BP is indicated for production of local or regional anesthesia by infiltration techniques such as percuta neous injection and intravenous regional anesthesia by peripheral nerve block techniques such as brachial plexus and intercostal and by ce ntral neural techniques such as lumbar and caudal epidural blocks, when the accepted procedures for these techniques as described in sta ndard textbooks are observed.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-29T16:24:23.028733+00:00
