PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15090,Rabeprazole sodium,Rabefaz,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Mt3emH-o5BFyrsqa0fzs21kAnq2Fb9HS/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15090_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Mt3emH-o5BFyrsqa0fzs21kAnq2Fb9HS_view_usp_drivesdk_f526d5aad8.pdf,page_text/DRP-15090_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Mt3emH-o5BFyrsqa0fzs21kAnq2Fb9HS_view_usp_drivesdk_f526d5aad8.json,267e5816c26f0ab2d6adea26e43d9011f990c0390dc9564d06d112b09e94b297,1446563,2,digital_text,"A white to almost white powder, hygroscopic.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Rabeprazole IV is an alternative in patients for whom oral administration of Rabeprazole is not indicated. Rabeprazole Sodium is indicated in the treatment of: Ÿ Sequential-therapy (step-up) from oral rabeprazole, e.g. a patient previously on oral rabeprazole who is temporarily unable to take oral medication for any reason. Ÿ Active duodenal ulcer with bleeding or severe erosions. Ÿ Active gastric ulcer with bleeding or severe erosions. Ÿ Short-term treatment of erosive or ulcerative gastroesophageal reflux disease",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T12:56:52.697737+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-151,Cefalexin (As Monohydrate),Diacef,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1StPyWcJ1zW66IbX4AUdkkx7xEE2cjGhq/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-151_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1StPyWcJ1zW66IbX4AUdkkx7xEE2cjGhq_view_usp_drivesdk_8ccece34ce.pdf,page_text/DRP-151_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1StPyWcJ1zW66IbX4AUdkkx7xEE2cjGhq_view_usp_drivesdk_8ccece34ce.json,663cfb47a5215c95384d8477d91b1df07c5f8c30e1108922aeecef0265cf0a9a,2712033,1,ocr,,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"August 14, 2023 For the treatment of infections of the respiratory, gastrointestinal and urinary tract. Indicated also for otic, bone, skin and soft tissue, including penicillinase producing staphylococcus “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph.” aureus infection. Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction",1,1,INDICATION:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T12:57:02.149931+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15105,,,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UOtoQ88Uk5uklBSf2XwT6YOoYogTP6HL/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15105_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1UOtoQ88Uk5uklBSf2XwT6YOoYogTP6HL_view_usp_drivesdk_f049019342.pdf,page_text/DRP-15105_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1UOtoQ88Uk5uklBSf2XwT6YOoYogTP6HL_view_usp_drivesdk_f049019342.json,aae9cf3587562098655b4bf6bd404fbbdcac778b39e82c7fd5fe92988bcd3752,1979482,1,digital_text,"Doxofylline is a white to off-white, circular, flat faced beveled edge tablet with break line on one side and plain on the other side packed in Alu/Clear PVC-PVdC Blister Pack x 10’s (Box of 50’s).",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Doxofylline is indicated for the treatment of COPD, bronchial asthma and pulmonary disease with spastic bronchial component.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T12:57:05.619801+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15140,Rosuvastatin (as calcium) + Fenofibrate,Fenrus,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1yynlV3ooJ4NadYe01U-ffocd9SHmmNeX/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15140_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1yynlV3ooJ4NadYe01U-ffocd9SHmmNeX_view_usp_drivesdk_818ee4e5cf.pdf,page_text/DRP-15140_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1yynlV3ooJ4NadYe01U-ffocd9SHmmNeX_view_usp_drivesdk_818ee4e5cf.json,788175b2c70e0772efe18d491196c132db2162f755a1aa735a047956409d30c1,463433,2,digital_text,,,,,"Mixed Dyslipidemia: Rosuvastatin and Fenoﬁbrate is indicated as adjunctive therapy to diet to reduce elevated total-C, LDL-C, ApoB, and triglycerides and to increase HDL-C in adult patients with mixed dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Types II a and II b). Hypercholesterolemia: Rosuvastatin and Fenoﬁbrate is indicated as adjunctive therapy to diet to reduce elevated total-C, LDL-C, ApoB, and to increase HDL-C in adult patients with primary hypercholesterolemia. Hypertriglyceridemia: Rosuvastatin and Fenoﬁbrate is indicated as adjunctive therapy to diet for the treatment of adult patients with hypertriglyceridemia (Fredrickson Types IV and V hyperlipidemia). Lipid-altering agents should be used in addition to a diet restricted in saturated fat and cholesterol when response to diet and non-pharmacological interventions alone has been inadequate. (see National Cholesterol Education Program Treatment Guidelines, summarized in Table 1 below). *CHD = coronary heart disease **Almost all people with 0-1 risk factor have 10-year risk <10%; thus, 10-year risk assessment in people with 0- 1 risk factor is not necessary. After the LDL-C goal has been achieved, if the TG is still ≥200 mg/dL, non HDL-C (total-C minus HDL-C) becomes a secondary target of therapy. Non-HDL-C goals are set 30 mg/dL higher than LDL-C goals for each Table 1: NCEP guidelines for lipid management Risk category LDL goal (mg/dL) LDL level at which to consider drug therapy (mg/dL) CHD *or CHD risk equivalents (10-year risk >20%) <100 ≥130(100-129: drug optional) 2+ Risk Factors (10-year risk ≤20%) <130 10-year risk 10%-20%: ≥130 10-year risk <10%: ≥160 0-1 Risk Factor ** <160 (160-189: LDL-lowering drug optional) 20mg/160mgFilm-CoatedTablet LipidModifyingAgents (HMGCOAReductaseInhibitor/Fibrate) Fenrus Rosuvastatin(ascalcium)+Fenofibrate [Page 2] statistically signiﬁcant, higher age-adjusted all-cause mortality in the cloﬁbrate group compared with the placebo group (5.70% vs 3.96%, p=<0.01). Excess mortality was due to a 33% increase in noncardiovascular causes, including malignancy, post-cholecystectomy complications, and pancreatitis. This appeared to conﬁrm the higher risk of gallbladder disease seen in cloﬁbrate-treated patients studied in the Coronary Drug Project. The Helsinki Heart Study was a large (n=4,081) study of middle-aged men without a history of coronary artery disease. Subjects received either placebo or gemﬁbrozil for 5 years, with a 3.5-year open extension afterward. Total mortality was numerically higher in the gemﬁbrozil randomization group but did not achieve statistical signiﬁcance (p=0.19, 95% CI for relative risk G:P = 0.91-1.64). Although cancer deaths trended higher in the gemﬁbrozil group (p=0.11), cancers (excluding basal cell carcinoma) were diagnosed with equal frequency in both study groups. Due to the limited size of the study, the relative risk of death from any cause was not shown to be diﬀerent than that seen in the 9-year follow-up data from the WHO study (RR=1.29). A secondary prevention component of the Helsinki Heart Study enrolled middle-aged men excluded from the primary prevention study because of known or suspected CHD. Subjects received gemﬁbrozil or placebo for 5 years. Although cardiac deaths trended higher in the gemﬁbrozil group, this was not statistically signiﬁcant (HR: 2.2, 95% CI:0.94-5.05). The rate of gallbladder surgery was not statistically signiﬁcant between study groups, but did trend higher in the gemﬁbrozil group (1.9% vs 0.3%, p=0.07). Renal Impairment: Fenoﬁbrate should be initiated at a lower dose in patients having mild to moderately impaired renal function and it should be avoided in patients with severe renal impairment. As Rosuvastatin and Fenoﬁbrate contains 145 mg of fenoﬁbrate, it should be avoided in all renal impairment patients. Hepatic Impairment: Rosuvastatin is contraindicated in patients with active liver disease, which may include unexplained persistent elevations of hepatic transaminase levels. Chronic alcohol liver disease is known to increase rosuvastatin exposure; the use of fenoﬁbrate is not evaluated in subjectswith hepatic impairment. Rosuvastatin and Fenoﬁbrate should be used with caution in these patients.",1,2,INDICATIONS AND CLINICAL USES OF THE PRODUCT:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T12:57:08.708798+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15155,Fluconazole,Desfluzole 200,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FmbKSWeHUgPsNlDCR-Y8lq4bc00ylNis/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15155_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1FmbKSWeHUgPsNlDCR-Y8lq4bc00ylNis_view_usp_drivesdk_41b52f26b7.pdf,page_text/DRP-15155_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1FmbKSWeHUgPsNlDCR-Y8lq4bc00ylNis_view_usp_drivesdk_41b52f26b7.json,62a7dbe7ebbd163c8b1fdb2fc24d44453d6f34a1f82351b5b7924363aade92d1,820759,2,digital_text,"Black/Orange colored, size ""2"" hard gela�n capsule containing white to oﬀ-white colored powder. PHARMACODYNAMICS Pharmacotherapeutic group: An�myco�cs for systemic use, triazole deriva�ves. ATC code: J02AC01. Mechanism of Action: Fluconazole, a triazole an�fungal agent, is a potent and speciﬁc inhibitor of fungal sterol synthesis. Its primary mode of ac�on is the inhibi�on of fungal cytochrome P-450-mediated 14-alpha-lanosterol demethyla�on, an essen�al step in fungal ergosterol biosynthesis. The accumula�on of 14-alpha-methyl sterols correlates with the subsequent loss of ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane and may be responsible for the an�fungal ac�vity of ﬂuconazole. Fluconazole has been shown to be more selec�ve for fungal cytochrome P-450 enzymes than for various mammalian cytochrome P-450 enzyme systems. Fluconazole is highly speciﬁc for fungal cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes. Fluconazole 50 mg daily given up to 28 days has been shown not to aﬀect testosterone plasma concentra�ons in males or steroid concentra�ons in females of child-bearing age. Fluconazole 200 mg to 400 mg daily has no clinically signiﬁcant eﬀect on endogenous steroid levels or on adrenocor�cotropic hormone (ACTH) s�mulated response in healthy male volunteers. Interac�on studies with an�pyrine indicate that single or mul�ple doses of ﬂuconazole 50 mg do not aﬀect its metabolism. Pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic relationship: In animal studies, there is a correla�on between MIC values and eﬃcacy against experimental mycoses due to Candida spp. In clinical studies, there is an almost 1:1 linear rela�onship between the AUC and the dose of ﬂuconazole. There is also a direct though imperfect rela�onship between the AUC or dose and a successful clinical response of oral candidosis and to a lesser extent candidemia to treatment. Similarly cure is less likely for infec�ons caused by strains with a higher ﬂuconazole MIC. Microbiology: In vitro, ﬂuconazole displays an�fungal ac�vity against most clinically common Candida spp. (including C. albicans, C. parapsilosis , C. tropicalis). MICs for C. glabrata and C. guilliermondii tend to be higher, while C. krusei is intrinsically resistant to ﬂuconazole. The recently emerging species C. auris tends to be rela�vely resistant to ﬂuconazole. Fluconazole also exhibits ac�vity in vitro against Cryptococcus neoformans and Cryptococcus gattii as well as the endemic moulds Blastomyces dermatiditis, Coccidioides immitis , Histoplasma capsulatum and Paracoccidioides brasiliensis. Both orally and intravenously administered ﬂuconazole was ac�ve in a variety of animal fungal infec�on models. Ac�vity has been demonstrated against opportunis�c mycoses, such as infec�ons with Candida spp., including systemic candidiasis in immunocompromised animals; with C. neoformans, including intracranial infec�ons; with Microsporum spp.; and with Trichophyton spp. Fluconazole has also been shown to be ac�ve in animal models of endemic mycoses, including infec�ons with Blastomyces dermatitidis; with Coccidioides immitis , including intracranial infec�on; and with Histoplasma capsulatum in normal and immunosuppressed animals. Mechanisms of resistance: In usually suscep�ble species of Candida, the most commonly encountered mechanism of resistance involves the target enzymes of the azoles, which are responsible for the biosynthesis of ergosterol. Point muta�ons in the gene (ERG11) encoding for the target enzyme lead to an altered target with decreased aﬃnity for azoles. Overexpression of ERG11 results in the produc�on of high concentra�ons of the target enzyme, crea�ng the need for higher intracellular drug concentra�ons to inhibit all of the enzyme molecules in the cell. The second major mechanism of drug resistance involves ac�ve eﬄux of ﬂuconazole out of the cell through the ac�va�on of two types of mul�drug eﬄux transporters; the major facilitators (encoded by MDR genes) and those of the ATP-binding casse�e superfamily (encoded by CDR genes). Upregula�on of the MDR gene leads to ﬂuconazole resistance, whereas, upregula�on of CDR genes may lead to resistance to mul�ple azoles. Resistance in Candida glabrata usually includes upregula�on of CDR genes resul�ng in resistance to mul�ple azoles. For an isolate where the minimum inhibitory concentra�on (MIC) is 16 to 32 mg/L, the highest ﬂuconazole dose is recommended. There have been reports of cases of superinfec�on with Candida spp. other than C. albicans, which are o�en inherently not suscep�ble to ﬂuconazole (e.g., Candida krusei). Such cases may require alterna�ve an�fungal therapy. PHARMACOKINETICS The pharmacokine�c proper�es of ﬂuconazole are similar following administra�on by the intravenous or oral route. A�er oral administra�on, ﬂuconazole is well absorbed, and plasma levels (and systemic bioavailability) are over 90% of the levels achieved a�er intravenous administra�on. Oral absorp�on is not aﬀected by concomitant food intake. Peak plasma concentra�ons in the fas�ng state occur between 0.5 and 1.5 hours post-dose with a plasma elimina�on half-life of approximately 30 hours. Plasma concentra�ons are propor�onal to dose. Ninety percent steady-state levels are reached by Days 4 to 5 with mul�ple once-daily dosing. Administra�on of loading dose (on Day 1) of twice the usual daily dose enables plasma levels to approximate to 90% steady-state levels by Day 2. The apparent volume of distribu�on approximates to total body water. Plasma protein binding is low (11%-12%). Fluconazole achieves good penetra�on in all body ﬂuids studied. The levels of ﬂuconazole in saliva and sputum are similar to plasma levels. In pa�ents with fungal meningi�s, ﬂuconazole levels in the cerebrospinal ﬂuid (CSF) are approximately 80% the corresponding plasma levels. High skin concentra�ons of ﬂuconazole, above serum concentra�ons, are achieved in the stratum corneum, epidermis-dermis and eccrine sweat. Fluconazole accumulates in the stratum corneum. At a dose of 50 mg once daily, the concentra�on of ﬂuconazole a�er 12 days was 73 μg/g, and 7 days a�er cessa�on of treatment the concentra�on was s�ll 5.8 μg/g. At the 150 mg once-a-week dose, the concentra�on of ﬂuconazole in stratum corneum on Day 7 was 23.4 μg/g, and 7 days a�er the second dose was s�ll 7.1 μg/g. Concentra�on of ﬂuconazole in nails a�er 4 months of 150 mg once-a-week dosing was 4.05 μg/g in healthy and 1.8 μg/g in diseased nails; and, ﬂuconazole was s�ll measurable in nail samples 6 months a�er the end of therapy. The major route of excre�on is renal, with approximately 80% of the administered dose appearing in the urine as unchanged drug. Fluconazole clearance is propor�onal to crea�nine clearance. There is no evidence of circula�ng metabolites. Toxicology: Preclinical Safety Data: Carcinogenesis: Fluconazole showed no evidence of carcinogenic poten�al in mice and rats treated orally for 24 months at doses of 2.5, 5 or 10 mg/kg/day (approximately 2-7 �mes the recommended human dose). Male rats treated with 5 and 10 mg/kg/day had an increased incidence of hepatocellular adenomas. Mutagenesis: Fluconazole, with or without metabolic ac�va�on, was nega�ve in tests for mutagenicity in four strains of Salmonella typhimurium , and in the mouse lymphoma L5178Y system. Cytogene�c studies in vivo (murine bone marrow cells, following oral administra�on of ﬂuconazole) and in vitro (human lymphocytes exposed to ﬂuconazole at 1000 μg/mL) showed no evidence of chromosomal muta�ons. Impairment of Fertility: Fluconazole did not aﬀect the fer�lity of male or female rats treated orally with daily doses of 5 mg/kg, 10 mg/kg or 20 mg/kg or with parenteral doses of 5 mg/kg, 25 mg/kg or 75 mg/kg, although the onset of parturi�on was slightly delayed at 20 mg/kg orally. In an intravenous perinatal study in rats at 5 mg/kg, 20 mg/kg and 40 mg/kg, dystocia and prolonga�on of parturi�on were observed in a few dams at 20 mg/kg (approximately 5-15 �mes the recommended human dose) and 40 mg/kg, but not at 5 mg/kg. The disturbances in parturi�on were reﬂected by a slight increase in the number of s�ll-born pups and decrease of neonatal survival at these dose levels. The eﬀects on parturi�on in rats are consistent with the species-speciﬁc estrogen-lowering property produced by high doses of ﬂuconazole. Such a hormone change has not been observed in women treated with ﬂuconazole.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Therapy may be ins�tuted before the results of the cultures and other laboratory studies are known; however, once these results become available, an�-infec�ve therapy should be adjusted accordingly. Cryptococcosis, including cryptococcal meningi�s and infec�ons of other sites (e.g., pulmonary, cutaneous). Normal hosts and pa�ents with AIDS, organ transplants or other causes of immunosuppression may be treated. Fluconazole can be used as maintenance therapy to prevent relapse of cryptococcal disease in pa�ents with AIDS. Systemic candidiasis, including candidemia, disseminated candidiasis and other forms of invasive candidal infec�ons. These include infec�ons of the peritoneum, endocardium, eye, and pulmonary and urinary tracts. Pa�ents with malignancy, in intensive care units, receiving cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapy, or with other factors predisposing to candidal infec�on may be treated. Mucosal candidiasis. These include oropharyngeal, esophageal, non-invasive bronchopulmonary infec�ons, candiduria, mucocutaneous and chronic oral atrophic candidiasis (denture sore mouth). Normal hosts and pa�ents with compromised immune func�on may be treated. Preven�on of relapse of oropharyngeal candidiasis in pa�ents with AIDS. Genital candidiasis. Vaginal candidiasis, acute or recurrent, and prophylaxis to reduce the incidence of recurrent vaginal candidiasis (three or more episodes a year). Candidal balani�s. Preven�on of fungal infec�ons in pa�ents with malignancy who are predisposed to such infec�ons as a result of cytotoxic chemotherapy or radiotherapy. Dermatomycosis, including �nea pedis, �nea corporis, �nea cruris, �nea versicolor, �nea unguium (onychomycosis), and dermal Candida infec�ons. Deep endemic mycoses in immunocompetent pa�ents, coccidioidomycosis, paracoccidioidomycosis, sporotrichosis and histoplasmosis.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T12:57:12.451329+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15157,Cefoxitin (as sodium),None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1doBHq65oCy_vsWuztyKWXz5a3PO6SfR3/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15157_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1doBHq65oCy_vsWuztyKWXz5a3PO6SfR3_view_usp_drivesdk_feccdebaf6.pdf,page_text/DRP-15157_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1doBHq65oCy_vsWuztyKWXz5a3PO6SfR3_view_usp_drivesdk_feccdebaf6.json,8ee730a60de17645ec37f755c15823f7b7e02a12bd000d8bdb432f3c9d4ab01d,737901,1,ocr,"Cefoxitin for Injection is a white to off white sterile powder of cefoxitin for intravenous and intramuscular administration following reconstitution. . PHARMACOKINETICS Cefoxitin for Injection administered by the parenteral route is excreted virtually unchanged by the kidneys. The mean terminal serum half life is approximately one hour and co-administration of probenecid will slow tubular excretion and increase and prolong blood levels. Although cefoxitin sodium will penetrate into the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), concentrations are considerably lower than in the plasma. Following intravenous administration, the peak serum concentration of cefoxitin for injection following 1 g infused intravenously over 3 minutes was 110.5 micrograms/mL, when infused over 30 minutes was 72 micrograms/mL, and when infused over 120 minutes was 25 micrograms/mL. Following 2 g infused intravenously over 3 minutes, the peak serum concentration was 221 micrograms/mL. The serum half life was approximately 50 minutes, falling to less than 1 microgram/mL at 4 hours.",1,1,DESCRIPTION:,"Cefoxitin for Injection is indicated for the treatment of the following infections when due to susceptible organisms peritonitis and other intraabdominal and intra-pelvic infections, female genital tract infections, septicaemia, endocarditis, urinary tract infections, respiratory tract infections, bone and jointinfections, skin and skin structure infections. Cefoxitin for Injection has a high degree of stability against beta-lactamase and is therefore effective against beta- lactamase producing organisms resistant to penicillins or cephalosporins. It can also be used in mixed infections provided that the organisms are sensitive to it. Cefoxitin for Injection can be used as adjunctive therapy in the surgical treatment of infections including abscesses, infection complicating hollow viscous perforations, cutaneous infections and infections of serous surfaces whether caused by aerobes, mixed aerobes and anaerobes, or anaerobes. Cefoxitin for Injection is also indicated for the prevention of post operative infections associated with certain surgical procedures of the gastrointestinal, biliary and genital tracts.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T12:57:21.708088+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15158,Levocarnitine,Metacartin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xK_Aix1BA4cl1zUOBpYmmQC1kNEga6fc/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15158_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1xK_Aix1BA4cl1zUOBpYmmQC1kNEga6fc_view_usp_drivesdk_ba9bd6dd32.pdf,page_text/DRP-15158_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1xK_Aix1BA4cl1zUOBpYmmQC1kNEga6fc_view_usp_drivesdk_ba9bd6dd32.json,4273f8980153e15dff11a79939b155f992dfde04562f558322cf1df82cebee2e,872725,1,digital_text,"Clear, Colourless or light yellow solution.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Levocarnitine is indicated for: • Treatment of primary systemic carnitine deficiency • Acute and chronic treatment of patients with an inborn error of metabolism which results in a secondary carnitine deficiency, and • Prevention and treatment of carnitine deficiency in patients with end stage renal disease (ESRD) who are undergoing dialysis In some patients, particularly those presenting with cardiomyopathy, carnitine supplementation rapidly alleviated signs and symptoms. Treatment should include, in addition to carnitine, supportive and other therapy as indicated by the condition of the patient.",1,1,INDICATION:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T12:57:25.394737+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-1517,Paroxetine (as Hydrochloride),Unbranded,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oInXRtmdk0SMYjhov6wNW4t-Q9pg4R8R/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-1517_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1oInXRtmdk0SMYjhov6wNW4t-Q9pg4R8R_view_usp_drivesdk_f7a0c5c42e.pdf,page_text/DRP-1517_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1oInXRtmdk0SMYjhov6wNW4t-Q9pg4R8R_view_usp_drivesdk_f7a0c5c42e.json,da24f8521c97bd64cd485708e771c4399e6bb95c2c8709f5cebeea7016871e7b,3904113,2,digital_text,"Paroxetine hydrochloride is a pink, film-coated, capsule-shaped tablet, bisected on one side. PHARMACODYNAMICS Paroxetine is a potent and selective inhibitor of 5-hydroxytryptamine (5-HT, serotonin) uptake. Its efficacy, in the treatment of major depressive disorder (MDD), obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), panic disorder (PD), social anxiety disorder (social phobia), generalized anxiety disorder (GAD), and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), is presumed to be linked to potentiation of serotonergic activit in the central nervous system (CNS) resulting from inhibition of neuronal reuptake of serotonin. At clinically relevant doses in humans, paroxetine blocks the uptake of serotonin into human platelets. Paroxetine is chemically unrelated to the tricyclic, tetracyclic and other available antidepressants. In vitro studies in animals showed that paroxetine is a potent and highly selective inhibitor of neuronal serotonin reuptake and has only weak effects on norepinephrine and dopamine neuronal reuptake. In vitro radioligand binding studies indicate that, in contrast to tricyclic antidepressants, paroxetine has little affinity for muscarinic, α1, α2, β-adrenergic, dopamine (D2), 5-HT1, 5-HT2, and histamine (H1)-receptors. Antagonism of muscarinic, histaminergic, and α1 adrenergic receptors has been associated with various anticholinergic , sedative, and cardiovascular effects of other psychotropic drugs. PHARMACOKINETICS Paroxetine is well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract after oral administration. Food does not substantially affect the absorption of paroxetine. The mean elimination half-life (t 1/2) is approximately 21 hours after oral dosing of 30 mg tablets of paroxetine daily for 30 days. Steady state systemic levels of paroxetine are attained by 7 to 14 days after starting treatment and pharmacokinetics do not appear to change during long-term therapy. Paroxetine is distributed throughout the body including the CNS. Approximately 95% of the paroxetine present in the plasma is protein bound at therapeutic concentrations. Paroxetine is extensively metabolized after oral administration. The principal metabolites are polar and conjugated products of oxidation and methylation, which are readily cleared. Conjugates with glucuronic acid and sulfate predominate, and major metabolites have been isolated and identified. Data indicate that the metabolites have no more than 1/50 the potency of the parent compound at inhibiting serotonin uptake. Paroxetine metabolism is mediated in part by CYP2D6. Saturation of this enzyme at clinical doses appears to account for the nonlinearity of paroxetine kinetics with increasing dose and increasing duration of treatment. At steady state, when CYP2D6 is essentially saturated, paroxetine clearance is governed by alternate P450 isoenzymes which, unlike CYP2D6, are not saturable at clinical doses. Because of the involvement of CYP2D6 in the metabolic clearance of paroxetine, considerable variation can occur in the plasma concentrations achieved between individuals. However, no correlation has been found between paroxetine plasma concentrations and clinical effect (adverse experiences and efficacy). About 64% of the paroxetine dose is excreted in the urine; urinary excretion of unchanged paroxetine is generally less than 2% of dose. About 36% of the dose is excreted in the feces, probably via the bile; fecal excretion of unchanged paroxetine represents less than 1% of the dose. Thus, paroxetine is eliminated almost entirely by metabolism. Metabolite excretion is biphasic, being initially a result of first-pass metabolism and subsequently controlled by systemic elimination of paroxetine. Special Population: Renal impairment: In patients with mild to severe renal impairment, plasma levels of paroxetine tended to increase with deteriorating renal function. Paroxetine should be used with caution in these patients. Hepatic impairment: In subjects with severe hepatic dysfunction, the clearance of paroxetine is markedly reduced. As the elimination of paroxetine is dependent upon extensive hepatic metabolism, its use in patients with hepatic impairment should be undertaken with caution. Geriatric: In elderly subjects, increased steady-state plasma concentrations and prolongation of the elimination t1/2 were observed relative to younger adult controls. Elderly patients should, therefore, be initiated and maintained at the lowest daily dosage of paroxetine which is associated with clinical efficacy.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,Paroxetine is indicated for the treatment of: • Major depressive disorder • Obsessive-compulsive disorder • Panic disorder with or without agoraphobia • Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) • Post-traumatic stress disorder • Premenstrual dysphoric disorder • Generalized anxiety disorder,1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T12:57:31.262759+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15177,Amlodipine (as besylate) + Atorvastatin (as calcium),Amlotin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1v8zOi5w1oIBkYjA4yBtAbsza5kh4GdZt/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15177_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1v8zOi5w1oIBkYjA4yBtAbsza5kh4GdZt_view_usp_drivesdk_8b7b9a06b6.pdf,page_text/DRP-15177_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1v8zOi5w1oIBkYjA4yBtAbsza5kh4GdZt_view_usp_drivesdk_8b7b9a06b6.json,24e2284db10df4d0fc51974e8d462af2afe4b6cff2ee446e2782adb860e26076,11322531,1,digital_text,,,,,,,,,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-06-30T12:57:41.129771+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15178,Amlodipine (as besylate) + Atorvastatin (as calcium),Amlotin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1YbpzxaRGLy1oziKTlIYTyEI_Lwbk5vPQ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15178_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1YbpzxaRGLy1oziKTlIYTyEI_Lwbk5vPQ_view_usp_drivesdk_59a03f6201.pdf,page_text/DRP-15178_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1YbpzxaRGLy1oziKTlIYTyEI_Lwbk5vPQ_view_usp_drivesdk_59a03f6201.json,24e2284db10df4d0fc51974e8d462af2afe4b6cff2ee446e2782adb860e26076,11322531,1,digital_text,,,,,,,,,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-06-30T12:57:51.175018+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15179,Amlodipine (as besylate) + Atorvastatin (as calcium),Amlotin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1pAvCTC0fMWm2YimA2xoKFyepo3VHnuIf/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15179_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1pAvCTC0fMWm2YimA2xoKFyepo3VHnuIf_view_usp_drivesdk_71a08d2403.pdf,page_text/DRP-15179_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1pAvCTC0fMWm2YimA2xoKFyepo3VHnuIf_view_usp_drivesdk_71a08d2403.json,24e2284db10df4d0fc51974e8d462af2afe4b6cff2ee446e2782adb860e26076,11322531,1,digital_text,,,,,,,,,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-06-30T12:58:00.851103+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15202,Bupivacaine hydrochloride,Blesupican,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wuZN6DoWz-j5PyopqLJvGIq9sJCgXlTc/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15202_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1wuZN6DoWz-j5PyopqLJvGIq9sJCgXlTc_view_usp_drivesdk_91af459910.pdf,page_text/DRP-15202_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1wuZN6DoWz-j5PyopqLJvGIq9sJCgXlTc_view_usp_drivesdk_91af459910.json,729b8862c1618be495d2d2da43d428fc1166e68441ef987b85fadced24fc7202,2922495,1,digital_text,"Bupivacaine 0.5% Heavy is local anesthetic of the amide type given in sterile hyperbaric solution for subarachnoid injection (spinal block ). The solution has been made hyperbaric by addition of dextrose.Given as a spinal anesthetic, it has a rapid onset and a medium to long duration. The duration is dose dependent.",1,1,DESCRIPTION:,"Bupivacaine 0.5% Heavy is indicated for spinal (subarachnoid) anesthesia for surgical and obstetrical, procedures, abdominal, urological and lower limb surgery.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T12:58:05.021037+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15228-01,Acetylcysteine,Mucocystein,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Bt_3f0JqFwwIKK0anYbKpKyWR4a9BFkG/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15228-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Bt_3f0JqFwwIKK0anYbKpKyWR4a9BFkG_view_usp_drivesdk_5ad192eddb.pdf,page_text/DRP-15228-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Bt_3f0JqFwwIKK0anYbKpKyWR4a9BFkG_view_usp_drivesdk_5ad192eddb.json,6305a61dc1dae915ecdbcf39fc49f1450df886a9ae6c6c666d2cf08a1193e2ce,1053779,2,ocr,,1,1,"PRODUCT DESCRIPTION: White to Off white, round, beveled edge, flat tablet.","1 For the respiratory infections characterised by thick and viscous hypersecretions such as acute bronchitis, chronic bronchitis and its exacerbation, pulmonary emphysema, mucoviscidosis and bronchiectasis. For the prevention or lessening of hepatic injury which may occur following the ingestion of a potentially hepatotoxic quantity of acetaminophen. It is essential to initiate treatment as soon as possible after the overdose and, in any case, within 24 hours of Ingestion.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T12:58:15.753775+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15254,Azithromycin (as dihydrate),Azmyn IV,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UHRNCkcfNPBN_ih5HP_eBWPKRP3nwLWX/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15254_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1UHRNCkcfNPBN_ih5HP_eBWPKRP3nwLWX_view_usp_drivesdk_6ae2d4a830.pdf,page_text/DRP-15254_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1UHRNCkcfNPBN_ih5HP_eBWPKRP3nwLWX_view_usp_drivesdk_6ae2d4a830.json,8c0cf4aa3783df00b67f545d24f36bd5a3ce0454f9efc45e97ce6f443bc977bc,5099083,1,digital_text,A clear and colorless liquid.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Azithromycin IV is indicated for the treatment of community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) caused by susceptible organisms, including Legionella pneumophila, in patients who require initial IV therapy.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T12:58:20.896430+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-15266,Hydrocortisone (as sodium succinate),Dasocort,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1EoLuAGJOy4duAxlNfkgIYljRmNQENmqo/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-15266_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1EoLuAGJOy4duAxlNfkgIYljRmNQENmqo_view_usp_drivesdk_8df722ac43.pdf,page_text/DRP-15266_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1EoLuAGJOy4duAxlNfkgIYljRmNQENmqo_view_usp_drivesdk_8df722ac43.json,a195fde0804b83cea431381d3f4b56b2f5ca967cafdcbcf26fe7f11eb6141972,486448,2,digital_text,"White or almost white, hygroscopic powder.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"When oral therapy is not feasible, and the strength, dosage form, and route of administration of the drug reasonably lend the preparation to the treatment of the condition, the intravenous or intramuscular use of Hydrocortisone Sterile Powder is indicated as follows: Ÿ Allergic states: Control of severe or incapacitating allergic conditions intractable to adequate trials of conventional treatment in asthma, atopic dermatitis, contact dermatitis, drug hypersensitivity reactions, serum sickness, transfusion reactions. Ÿ Dermatologic diseases: Bullous dermatitis herpetiformis, exfoliative erythroderma, mycosis fungoides, pemphigus, severe erythema multiforme (Stevens-Johnson syndrome). Ÿ Endocrine disorders: Primary or secondary adrenocortical insuﬃciency (hydrocortisone or cortisone is the drug of choice; synthetic analogs may be used in conjunction with mineralocorticoids where applicable; in infancy, mineralocorticoid supplementation is of particular importance), congenital adrenal hyperplasia, hypercalcemia associated with cancer, nonsuppurative thyroiditis. Ÿ Gastrointestinal diseases: To tide the patient over a critical period of the disease in regional enteritis (systemic therapy) and ulcerative colitis. Ÿ Hematologic disorders: Acquired (autoimmune) hemolytic anemia, congenital (erythroid) hypoplastic anemia (Diamond Blackfan anemia), idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura in adults (intravenous administration only; intramuscular administration is contraindicated), pure red cell aplasia, select cases of secondary thrombocytopenia. Ÿ Miscellaneous: Trichinosis with neurologic or myocardial involvement, tuberculous meningitis with subarachnoid block or impending block when used concurrently with appropriate antituberculous chemotherapy. Ÿ Neoplastic diseases: For the palliative management of leukemias and lymphomas. Ÿ Nervous System: Cerebral edema associated with primary or metastatic brain tumor, or craniotomy. Ÿ Ophthalmic diseases: Sympathetic ophthalmia, uveitis and ocular inﬂammatory conditions unresponsive to topical corticosteroids. Ÿ Renal diseases: To induce diuresis or remission of proteinuria in idiopathic nephrotic syndrome, or that due to lupus erythematosus. Ÿ Respiratory diseases: Berylliosis, fulminating or disseminated pulmonary tuberculosis when used concurrently with appropriate antituberculosis chemotherapy, idiopathic eosinophilic pneumonias, symptomatic sarcoidosis. Ÿ Rheumatic disorders: As adjunctive therapy for short-term administration (to tide the patient over an acute episode or exacerbation) in acute gouty arthritis; acute rheumatic carditis; ankylosing spondylitis; psoriatic arthritis; rheumatoid arthritis, including juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (selected cases may require low-dose maintenance therapy). For the treatment of dermatomyositis, temporal arteritis, polymyositis, and systemic lupus erythematosus. Ÿ",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T12:58:24.211234+00:00
