4-6 hours but is about 6-7 hours in the 7. Not given to patients with coagulation hemorrhage disorders. elderly and 9-10 hours in patients with renal dysfunction. The major metabolic pathway is glucoronic acid conjugation, there is some parahydroxylation. About 90% of a dose is excreted in urine as unchanged drug and conjugated and hydroxylated metabolites; the DRUG INTERACTION remainder is excreted in the feces. 1. Binding of Warfarin to plasma proteins is only slightly reduced by Ketorolac trometamol (Torosenz). 2. At therapeutic concentrations of salicylate (300 g/mL), the binding of Ketorolac was reduced from approximately 99.2% to 97.5%",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"3. Therapeutic concentration of Digoxin, Warfarin, Ibuprofen, Naproxen, Piroxicam, Acetaminophen, Phenytoin and Tolbutamine did not 1. Indicated for the short term management of moderately severe, acute pain at the opioid level. alter Ketorolac trometamol protein binding. 4. Patients on therapeutic doses of anticoagulants (e.g., heparin or dicumarol derivatives) have an increased risk of bleeding 2. Post-operative pain after general, orthopedic, gynecological and dental operation. 3. Pediatrics (2 years of age and older): Severe post-operative pain that requires analgesia at the opioid level. complications if given Ketorolac trometamol concurrently. The concurrent use of Ketorolac trometamol and prophylactic low-dose Heparin (2500-5000 units q 12 h), Warfarin and Dextrans have not been studied extensively, but may also be associated with an",1,1,INDICATIONS,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T13:39:52.196508+00:00
