PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2445,Metformin hydrochloride,Metta SR,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1RrlTxh9_A8bnEPnRRZWv9mAfVWvyALTC/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2445_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1RrlTxh9_A8bnEPnRRZWv9mAfVWvyALTC_view_usp_drivesdk_3014287dda.pdf,page_text/DRP-2445_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1RrlTxh9_A8bnEPnRRZWv9mAfVWvyALTC_view_usp_drivesdk_3014287dda.json,eb55e98d9f052895f1c746eabc358e870639e07f9a780a527bc3b2e776c9791c,378344,1,digital_text,"Metformin Hydrochloride (Metta SR) 500 mg Extended-Release Tablet is a white to off-white colored, capsule shaped, biconvex tablets with ""Torrent logo"" debossed on one side and '500' on the other side.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"As monotherapy, it is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus. May be used concomitantly with sulfonylureas or insulin to improve glycemic control.",1,1,INDICATION:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T13:41:35.422128+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2450-01,Allopurinol,None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FcuJ-Y5trSNOiyE-V7Nju1sIljMr9fAh/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2450-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1FcuJ-Y5trSNOiyE-V7Nju1sIljMr9fAh_view_usp_drivesdk_7a8757348f.pdf,page_text/DRP-2450-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1FcuJ-Y5trSNOiyE-V7Nju1sIljMr9fAh_view_usp_drivesdk_7a8757348f.json,11a3c8b34277b40109cdff61d4e3497d30119b5c2a4f5c9e81e6e14b1bc021ab,9819613,2,ocr,"Tablet CLINICAL PARTICULARS heapeutic indications -All forms of hyperuricaemia not controllable by diet, including secondary hyperuricaemia of differing origin and clinical complications of hyperuricaemic states, particularly manifest gout, urate nephropathy and for the dissolution and prevention of uric acid stone The management of recurrent mixed calcium oxalate stones in concurrent hyperuricaemia, when fluid, dietary and similar measures have failed. Children and adolescents Secondary hyperuricaemia of differing origin Uric acid nephropathy during treatment of leukaemia Hereditary enzyme deficiency disorders, Lesch-Nyhan syndrome (partial or total hyp thi gt defi and adenine transferase deficiency Dosage and administration ‘loprio should be introduced at low dose e.g. 100 mg/day to reduce the risk of adverse reactions and increased only ifthe serum urate response is unsatisfactory. Extra caution should be exercised if renal function is poor (see “Renal impairment’) The following dose schedules are suggested 100 mg to 200 mg daily in mild conditions, 300 mg to 600 mg daily in moderately severe conditions, 700 mg to 900 mg daily in severe conditions. If dose on a mg/kg bodyweight basis is required, 2 mg to 10 mg/kg bodyweightiday should be used Paediatric population 100 mg tablets: Paediatric population =15 kg bodyweight 300 mg tablets: Paediatric population =45 kg bodyweight Children under 15 years 10 to 20 mgkkg bodyweight/day up to a maximum of 400 mg daily given as 3 divided doses Use in children is rarely indicated, except in malignant conditions (especially leukaemia) and certain enzyme disorders such as Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, Elderly In the absence of specific data, the lowest dose which produces satisfactory urate reduction should be used, Particular attention should be paid to advice in Renal impairment and Special warnings and precautions for use, Renal impairment Since allopurinol and its metabolites are excreted by the kidney, impaired renal function may lead to retention of the active substance and/or its metabolites with consequent prolongation of plasma half-lives. The following schedule may serve as guidance for dose adjustments at renal impairment: Creatinine clearance Daily dose >20 mL/min normal dose 10 to 20 mL/min 100 to 200 mg per da\ <10 mL/min 100 mg/day or longer dose intervals In severe renal insufficiency, it may be advisable to use less than 100 mg per day or to use single doses of 100 mg at longer intervals than one day. If facilities are available to monitor plasma oxipurinol concentrations, the dose should be adjusted to maintain plasma oxipurinol levels below 100 mol/L (15.2 mglL). Allopurinol and its metabolites are removed by renal dialysis. If dialysis is required two to three times a week, consideration should be given to an altemative dose schedule of 300-400 mg allopurinol immediately after each dialysis with none in the interim. Hepatic impairment Reduced doses should be used in patients with hepatic impairment. Periodic liver function tests are recommended during the early stages of therapy. ‘Treatment of high urate turnover conditions, e.g. neoplasia, Lesch-Nyhan syndrome: itis advisable to correct existing hyperuricaemia andlor hyperuricosuria with allopurinol before starting cytotoxic therapy. Its important to ensure adequate hydration to maintain optimum diuresis and to attempt alkalinisation of urine to increase solubilty of urinary urate/uric acid, Dose of allopurinol should be at the lower end of the recommended dose schedule. Ifurate nephropathy or other pathology has compromised renal function, the advice given in Renal impairment should be followed. These steps may reduce the risk of xanthine andlor oxipurinol deposition complicating the clinical situation (See Interactions with other medicinal products and Undesirable effects). Monitoring advice The dose should be adjusted by monitoring serum urate concentrations and urinary urate/uric acid levels at appropriate intervals. Method and duration of treatment Allopurinol may be taken orally once a day after a meal If the daily dose exceeds 300 mg and gastrointestinal intolerance is evident, a divided doses regimen may be appropriate.",1,1,PHARMACEUTICAL FORM,"-All forms of hyperuricaemia not controllable by diet, including secondary hyperuricaemia of differing origin and clinical complications of hyperuricaemic states, particularly manifest gout, urate nephropathy and for the dissolution and prevention of uric acid stone The management of recurrent mixed calcium oxalate stones in concurrent hyperuricaemia, when fluid, dietary and similar measures have failed. Children and adolescents Secondary hyperuricaemia of differing origin Uric acid nephropathy during treatment of leukaemia Hereditary enzyme deficiency disorders, Lesch-Nyhan syndrome (partial or total hyp thi gt defi and adenine transferase deficiency",1,1,heapeutic indications,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T13:42:05.817714+00:00
