PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2552,Enalapril Maleate,Stalpril-10,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Zt5MJwK_APSUPN-B6o5Bg_Y3FfptA5dI/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2552_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Zt5MJwK_APSUPN-B6o5Bg_Y3FfptA5dI_view_usp_drivesdk_3ffffc6788.pdf,page_text/DRP-2552_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Zt5MJwK_APSUPN-B6o5Bg_Y3FfptA5dI_view_usp_drivesdk_3ffffc6788.json,5118753006bd57fb723e1f655033e257c119fdc0693b935323f52eabd950abf1,557608,1,digital_text,Tablet [Page 2],1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Treatment of hypertension, heart failure and symptomatic left ventricular dysfunction.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T13:47:14.482007+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2558,CEFACLOR (AS MONOHYDRATE),XEZTRON,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1P6FkZlVZzasvSHgdbHY6FoSbjKwTbsEW/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2558_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1P6FkZlVZzasvSHgdbHY6FoSbjKwTbsEW_view_usp_drivesdk_8ab6706c3a.pdf,page_text/DRP-2558_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1P6FkZlVZzasvSHgdbHY6FoSbjKwTbsEW_view_usp_drivesdk_8ab6706c3a.json,c22edfb99ac539da513293db159afe61833e0b01cc00b1d2decbefe97eaeb315,445890,1,digital_text,"Cefaclor is a semisynthetic second generation cephalosporin and like other cephalosporins is an inhibitor of bacterial cell wall synthesis. It acylates membrane-bound transpeptidase enzymes thus preventing the cross- linking of peptidoglycan necessary for bacterial cell wall strength and rigidity. CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY Cefaclor is well absorbed after oral administration to fasting subjects. Total absorption is the same whether the drug is given with or without food; however, when it is taken with food, the peak concentration achieved is 50% to 75% of that observed when the drug is administered to fasting subjects and generally appears from three fourths to 1 hour later. Following administration of 250 mg, 500 mg, and 1 g doses to fasting subjects, average peak serum levels of approximately 7, 13, and 23 µg/mL respectively were obtained within 30 to 60 minutes. Approximately 60% to 85% of the drug is excreted unchanged in the urine within 8 hours, the greater portion being excreted within the first 2 hours. During this period, peak urine concentrations following the 250 mg, 500 mg and 1 g doses were approximately 600, 900, 1,900 µ/mL, respectively. The serum half-life in normal subjects is 0.6 to 0.9 hour. In patients with reduced renal function, the serum half-life of cefaclor is slightly prolonged. In those with complete absence of renal function, the plasma half-life of the intact molecule is 2.3 to 2.8 hours. Excretion pathways in patients with markedly impaired renal function have not been determined. Hemodialysis shortens the half-life by 25% to 30%. ANTIMICROBIAL ACTION Cefaclor is bactericidal and has antimicrobial activity similar to that of cefalexin but is reported to be more active against Gram-negative bacteria including Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, and Proteus mirabilis, and especially against Haemophilus influenzae. It is active against some beta-lactamase-producing strains of H. influenzae. It may be less resistant to staphylococcal penicillinase than cefalexin or cefradine and a marked inoculum effect has been reported in vitro. PHARMACOKINETICS Cefaclor is well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract. Oral doses of 250 mg, 500 mg, and 1 g produce peak plasma concentrations of about 7, 13, and 23 micrograms/mL respectively after 0.5 to 1 hour. The presence of food may delay the absorption of cefaclor, but the total amount absorbed is unchanged. A plasma half-life of 0.5 to 1 hour has been reported; it may be slightly prolonged in patients with renal impairment. About 25% is bound to plasma proteins. Cefaclor appears to be widely distributed in the body; it crosses the placenta and low concentrations have been detected in breast milk. It is rapidly excreted by the kidney; up to 85% of a dose appears unchanged in the urine within 8 hours, the greater part within 2 hours. High concentration of cefaclor are achieved in the urine within 8 hours of a dose; peak concentrations of 600, 900, and 1900 micrograms/mL have been reported after doses of 0.25, 0.5, and 1 g respectively. Probenecid delays excretion. Some cefaclor is removed by haemodialysis.",1,1,DESCRIPTION,"For the treatment of susceptible infections including upper and lower respiratory-tract infections, skin infections, and urinary-tract infections.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T13:47:17.925407+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-256,Cloxacillin (As Sodium),Dialox,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/15NWaz6rD-ujaHpYzuOYWvoyBDCmyVGWk/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-256_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_15NWaz6rD-ujaHpYzuOYWvoyBDCmyVGWk_view_usp_drivesdk_df877be3d8.pdf,page_text/DRP-256_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_15NWaz6rD-ujaHpYzuOYWvoyBDCmyVGWk_view_usp_drivesdk_df877be3d8.json,ce3290cd3b2cdcfcf8bdc5000521dd42cb800ea8975a19c7197ca27240d33d8b,969215,1,ocr,"Cloxacillin (DIALOX) is a fine white powder which becomes a yellow banana flavored suspension when reconstituted with water. Each 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) contains: Cloxacillin (as sodium) 125 mg FD & C Yellow #5 (Tartrazine) 400 mcg DIRECTION FOR RECONSTITUTION To make a 60 mL suspension loosen the powder by shaking and slowly add 50 mL of water. Shake well until the powder is evenly suspended. The reconstituted suspension is stable for 7 days at temperatures not exceeding 30°C and 14 days under refrigeration (2-8°C PHARMACODYNA Cloxacillin is a bactericidal antibiotic that binds to 1 or more of the penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs) which in turn inhibit the final transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis in bacterial cell wales thereby annbasg cer wat baci pzy murein ar while PHARMACOKINETICS wall assembly is arrest Cloxacillin sodium is incompletely absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract after oral administration and absorption is further reduced by the presence of food in the stomach, in fasting subjects in 1 to 2 hours. Absorption is more complete when given by intramuscular injection and peak plasma concentrations of about 15 ug per mL have been observed 30 minutes after a dose of 500 mg. Doubling the dose can double the plasma protein. Cloxacillin has been reported to have a plasma half-life of 0.5 to 1 hour. The half-life is prolonged in neonates. Cloxacillin crosses the placenta and is excreted in breast milk. There is little diffusion into the CSF except when the Cloxacillin is metabolized to a limited extent, and the unchan: meninges are inflamed. Therapeutic concentrations can be achieved in pleural and synovial fluids and in bone. ed drug and metabolites are excreted in the urine by glomerular filtration and renal tubular secretions. About 35% of an oral dose is excreted in the urine and up to 10% in the bile Plasma concentrations are enhanced if probenecid is given concomitantly. Reduced concentrations in patients with cystic fibrosis have been attributed to enhance nonrenal clearance of Cloxacillin",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Cloxacillin is an isoxazolyl penicillin and similar to Mucloxacillin in the treatment of infections due to staphylococci resistant to benzylpenicillin. Which includes bone and joint infections, endocarditis, peritonitis (associated with continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis), pneumonia, skin disorders (Including soft-tissue infections), surgical infections prophylaxis and toxic shock syndrome.",1,1,INDICATION,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T13:47:36.165207+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-256-02,Cloxacillin (As Sodium),Fulclox,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KTtt1OBZN81KB111hHTs9rpYlFGDhgre/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-256-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KTtt1OBZN81KB111hHTs9rpYlFGDhgre_view_usp_drivesdk_47e0990dc5.pdf,page_text/DRP-256-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KTtt1OBZN81KB111hHTs9rpYlFGDhgre_view_usp_drivesdk_47e0990dc5.json,cedee5ea3712525667cf71200c10ec0f61302168de77627d87613cde0f7aefa4,983034,1,ocr,"Cloxacillin (FULCLOX) is a fine white powder which becomes a yellow banana flavored suspension when reconstituted with water. Each 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) contains: Cloxacillin (as sodium) 125 mg FD & C Yellow #5 (Tartrazine) 400 mcg DIRECTION FOR RECONSTITUTION To make a 60 mL suspension loosen the powder by shaking and slowly add 50 mL of water. Shake well until the powder is evenly suspended. The reconstituted suspension is stable for 7 days at temperatures not exceeding 30°C and 14 days under refrigeration (2-8°C). Shake well before using PHARMACODYNA Cloxacillin is a bactericidal antibiotic that binds to 1 or more of the penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs) which in turn inhibit the final transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis in bacterial cell wales thereby NIRS cer wa baci wa pzy murein ar while PHARMACOKINETICS wall assembly is arrest Cloxacillin sodium is incompletely absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract after oral administration and absorption is further reduced by the presence of food in the stomach, in fasting subjects in 1 to 2 hours. Absorption is more complete when given by intramuscular injection and peak plasma concentrations of about 15 ug per mL have been observed 30 minutes after a dose of 500 mg. Doubling the dose can double the plasma protein. Cloxacillin has been reported to have a plasma half-life of 0.5 to 1 hour. The half-life is prolonged in neonates. Cloxacillin crosses the placenta and is excreted in breast milk. There is little diffusion into the CSF except when the Cloxacillin is metabolized to a limited extent, and the unchan: meninges are inflamed. Therapeutic concentrations can be achieved in pleural and synovial fluids and in bone. ed drug and metabolites are excreted in the urine by glomerular filtration and renal tubular secretions. About 35% of an oral dose is excreted in the urine and up to 10% in the bile Plasma concentrations are enhanced if probenecid is given concomitantly. Reduced concentrations in patients with cystic fibrosis have been attributed to enhance nonrenal clearance of Cloxacillin",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Cloxacillin is an isoxazolyl penicillin and similar to Mucloxacillin in the treatment of infections due to staphylococci resistant to benzylpenicillin. Which includes bone and joint infections, endocarditis, peritonitis (associated with continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis), pneumonia, skin disorders (Including soft-tissue infections), surgical infections prophylaxis and toxic shock syndrome.",1,1,INDICATION,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T13:47:26.786290+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2570,Losartan Potassium + Hydrochlorothiazide,Coxart,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/11uyXQ52etwxGx4aj8_7ER901viX5l_Yx/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2570_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_11uyXQ52etwxGx4aj8_7ER901viX5l_Yx_view_usp_drivesdk_fdd63f28ff.pdf,page_text/DRP-2570_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_11uyXQ52etwxGx4aj8_7ER901viX5l_Yx_view_usp_drivesdk_fdd63f28ff.json,3e2ac0c989d6801fc771fa246a3a594d7bd5dba92f79b36fdb22aae9892c1a41,430164,1,digital_text,"Losartan Potassium + Hydrochlorothiazide (Sydentan Plus) 50 mg / 12.5 mg is a yellow, 8.5 x 5 mm, oval-shaped Film-Coated tablet, scored on one side and plain on the other.",1,1,Product Description:,"Treatment of hypertension; for patients in whom combination therapy is appropriate. CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY Pharmacologic Action: Losartan is an angiotensin II receptor antagonist with antihypertensive activity due mainly to selective blockade of AT1 receptors and consequent reduced pressor effect of Angiotensin II. It is used in the management of hypertension. Hydrochlorothiazide is a moderately potent diuretic and exerts its diuretic effect by reducing the reabsorption of electrolytes from the renal tubules, thereby increasing the excretion of sodium and chloride ions, and consequently water. They act mainly at the beginning of distal tubules. The excretion of other electrolytes, notably potassium and magnesium, is also increased. The excretion of calcium is reduced. They also reduce carbonic-anhydrase activity so that the bicarbonate excretion is increased, but this effect is generally small compared with the effect on chloride excretion and does not appreciably alter pH of urine. They may also reduce glomerular filtration rate. Their antihypertensive effects are probably partly due to a reduction in peripheral resistance; they also enhance the effects of other antihypertensives. Pharmacokinetics: Losartan is readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract following oral administration, with an oral bioavailability of about 33%. It undergoes first-pass metabolism to form an active carboxylic acid metabolite E-3174 (EXP-3174), which has greater pharmacological activity than Losartan, and some inactive metabolites. Metabolism is primarily by cytochrome P450 isoenzymes CYP2C9 and CYP3A4. Peak plasma concentrations of Losartan and E-3174 occur about 1 hour and 3 to 4 hours, respectively, after an oral dose. Both Losartan and E-3174 are more than 98% bound to plasma proteins. Losartan is excreted in the urine, and in the feces via bile, as unchanged drug and metabolites. Following oral dosing, about 35% of the dose is excreted in the urine and about 60% in the feces. The terminal elimination half-lives of Losartan and E-3174 are about 1.5 to 2 hours and the metabolites are about 6 to 9 hours, respectively. Hydrochlorothiazide is fairly rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract. It is reported to have a bioavailability of about 65% to 70%. It has been estimated to have a plasma half-life of between about 5 and 15 hours and appears to be preferentially bound to red blood cells. It is excreted mainly unchanged in the urine. Hydrochlorothiazide crosses the placental barrier and is distributed into breast milk.",1,1,Indication:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T13:47:39.710919+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2586-03,Thiamine Mononitrate (Vitamin B1) + Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) + Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12),United Home Multi-B,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1fLoouBc1qCefSHciW3hv4g_SjDCQKElT/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2586-03_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1fLoouBc1qCefSHciW3hv4g_SjDCQKElT_view_usp_drivesdk_29e1b10af8.pdf,page_text/DRP-2586-03_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1fLoouBc1qCefSHciW3hv4g_SjDCQKElT_view_usp_drivesdk_29e1b10af8.json,c1a0bffe20fec83322f4391cb82673a1ff1a84185e82b964b9f51454d6cc2092,311153,1,ocr,,,,,,,,,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-06-30T13:47:47.673302+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2586-05,Thiamine Mononitrate (Vitamin B1) + Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) + Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12),None,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1yjLbEtAJnbsFBqgCmsps47vwzIGVbUSv/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2586-05_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1yjLbEtAJnbsFBqgCmsps47vwzIGVbUSv_view_usp_drivesdk_7edd7fa5a6.pdf,page_text/DRP-2586-05_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1yjLbEtAJnbsFBqgCmsps47vwzIGVbUSv_view_usp_drivesdk_7edd7fa5a6.json,18be2a3b99acb48cf309ae0f7a4527d92aef1ab904c49ad29ff0452cd649e866,133609,2,digital_text,"B-Complex is a pink, round, ﬂat beveled edge tablet, scored on one side and plain on the other side. WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE? This product contains B-complex vitamins (Vitamins B1, B6, and B12). These nutrients are required for the normal function of the nervous system and are used as adjunct in the management of neuromuscular pain. B-complex vitamins also help in the conversion of food to energy to support numerous normal body processes. B-complex vitamins have the following functions/ effects: VITAMINS Ÿ Involved in the production of acetylcoline, a neurotransmitter required in conveying signals between nerve cells Ÿ Assists in the conversion of carbohydrate to energy Ÿ Deﬁciency of Vitamin B1 results in beri-beri manifested as muscle weakness, loss of appetite and nerve degeneration (inability of nerves to function properly) Ÿ Required in the formation of neurotransmitters such as Ÿ serotonin, gamma amino butyric acid (GABA), dopamine, and epinephrine to facilitate normal nervous system function. Ÿ Assists in the breakdown and utilization of energy from amino acids and protein Ÿ Deﬁciency of Vitamin B6 is manifested as nausea, impaired immune function, skin disorders, mouth sores, weakness, mental depression, and anemia Ÿ Required for the synthesis of myelin. the white sheath that surrounds nerve ﬁbers Ÿ Essential in the breakdown and utilization of energy from fatty acids Ÿ Deﬁciency of Vitamin B12 is manifested as neuromuscular symptoms such as unsteadiness of the feet, poor muscle coordination and agitation Thiamine (B1) Pyridoxine (B6) Cyanocobalamin (B12) NUTRIENTS NEUROMUSCULAR AND OTHER FUNCTIONS STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE Please see formulation WHAT IS THE MEDICINE USED FOR? Ÿ To prevent and treat deﬁciencies of Vitamins B1, B5, B12 Ÿ As an adjunct in the management of neuromuscular pain respomsive to Vitamins B1, B6, B12 which may include neuralgia (nerve pain), neuritis (inﬂammation of the nerves), and neuropathies (disturbance in the function of the brain or spinal cord that may affect the nerves and muscles of the body) HOW MUCH AND HOW OFTEN SHOULD YOU USE THE MEDICINE? Orally, 1-2 tablets daily. Or as prescribed by a doctor. WHEN SHOULD YOU NOT TAKE THIS MEDICINE? If you are allergic to any component of the product. VITAMIN B-COMPLEX Each tablet contains: Thiamine Mononitrate (Vitamin B1),USP................................................................... 100mg Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6),USP................................................................. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12),USP......................................................................... 50mcg 5mg Vitamin [Page 2] UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS Vitamin B6 Ÿ Although pyridoxine has generally been considered relatively nontoxic, adverse neurologic effects have been reported following chronic administration of high Pyridoxine doses (e.g., 100 - 500 mg daily) and long term (i.e., 2 months or longer) megadose (usually 2 g or more daily) Pyridoxine use. WHAT OTHER MEDICINES OR FOOD SHOULD BE AVOIDED WHILE TAKING THIS MEDICINE? The amounts of nutrients in the product are not expected to interact with food or medicines. WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU MISS A DOSE? If you miss a dose, just take the next dose and the subsequent doses at the usual recommended schedule. i.e., once or twice daily. Do not double the dose. HOW SHOULD YOU KEEP THE MEDICINE? Ÿ Keep the product out of reach and sight of children. Ÿ Store at temperatures not exceeding 30 o",1,2,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T13:47:50.593134+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2586-07,,None,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wx5wz1GazexVnik7EwwVSGwV13CkiXAo/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2586-07_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1wx5wz1GazexVnik7EwwVSGwV13CkiXAo_view_usp_drivesdk_10e8d07ea6.pdf,page_text/DRP-2586-07_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1wx5wz1GazexVnik7EwwVSGwV13CkiXAo_view_usp_drivesdk_10e8d07ea6.json,bb94886767832c2a0a434f2819345a92a544aae6e5bf8e8f59e1d724e3514f3a,131177,2,digital_text,"B-Complex is a pink, round, ﬂat beveled edge tablet, scored on one side and plain on the other side. WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE? This product contains B-complex vitamins (Vitamins B1, B6, and B12). These nutrients are required for the normal function of the nervous system and are used as adjunct in the management of neuromuscular pain. B-complex vitamins also help in the conversion of food to energy to support numerous normal body processes. B-complex vitamins have the following functions/ effects: VITAMINS Ÿ Involved in the production of acetylcoline, a neurotransmitter required in conveying signals between nerve cells Ÿ Assists in the conversion of carbohydrate to energy Ÿ Deﬁciency of Vitamin B1 results in beri-beri manifested as muscle weakness, loss of appetite and nerve degeneration (inability of nerves to function properly) Ÿ Required in the formation of neurotransmitters such as Ÿ serotonin, gamma amino butyric acid (GABA), dopamine, and epinephrine to facilitate normal nervous system function. Ÿ Assists in the breakdown and utilization of energy from amino acids and protein Ÿ Deﬁciency of Vitamin B6 is manifested as nausea, impaired immune function, skin disorders, mouth sores, weakness, mental depression, and anemia Ÿ Required for the synthesis of myelin. the white sheath that surrounds nerve ﬁbers Ÿ Essential in the breakdown and utilization of energy from fatty acids Ÿ Deﬁciency of Vitamin B12 is manifested as neuromuscular symptoms such as unsteadiness of the feet, poor muscle coordination and agitation Thiamine (B1) Pyridoxine (B6) Cyanocobalamin (B12) NUTRIENTS NEUROMUSCULAR AND OTHER FUNCTIONS STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE Please see formulation WHAT IS THE MEDICINE USED FOR? Ÿ To prevent and treat deﬁciencies of Vitamins B1, B5, B12 Ÿ As an adjunct in the management of neuromuscular pain respomsive to Vitamins B1, B6, B12 which may include neuralgia (nerve pain), neuritis (inﬂammation of the nerves), and neuropathies (disturbance in the function of the brain or spinal cord that may affect the nerves and muscles of the body) HOW MUCH AND HOW OFTEN SHOULD YOU USE THE MEDICINE? Orally, 1-2 tablets daily. Or as prescribed by a doctor. WHEN SHOULD YOU NOT TAKE THIS MEDICINE? If you are allergic to any component of the product. VITAMIN B-COMPLEX Each tablet contains: Thiamine Mononitrate (Vitamin B1),USP................................................................... 100mg Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6),USP................................................................. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12),USP......................................................................... 50mcg 5mg Vitamin [Page 2] UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS Vitamin B6 Ÿ Although pyridoxine has generally been considered relatively nontoxic, adverse neurologic effects have been reported following chronic administration of high Pyridoxine doses (e.g., 100 - 500 mg daily) and long term (i.e., 2 months or longer) megadose (usually 2 g or more daily) Pyridoxine use. WHAT OTHER MEDICINES OR FOOD SHOULD BE A VOIDED WHILE TAKING THIS MEDICINE? The amounts of nutrients in the product are not expected to interact with food or medicines. WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU MISS A DOSE? If you miss a dose, just take the next dose and the subsequent doses at the usual recommended schedule. i.e., once or twice daily. Do not double the dose. HOW SHOULD YOU KEEP THE MEDICINE? Ÿ Keep the product out of reach and sight of children. Ÿ Store at temperatures not exceeding 30 o",1,2,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T13:47:53.228046+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2590-01,Multivitamins,Fulvit,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1EY1yd5u5gBqDfg8ehlTQyGgxuPJhvENd/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2590-01_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1EY1yd5u5gBqDfg8ehlTQyGgxuPJhvENd_view_usp_drivesdk_98206bdae6.pdf,page_text/DRP-2590-01_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1EY1yd5u5gBqDfg8ehlTQyGgxuPJhvENd_view_usp_drivesdk_98206bdae6.json,3dc2769fe06d9fd260fa736a73dc8d355f36e10f3ec6f97b69758cb95819b74f,677261,1,ocr,"Fulvit syrup is a dark orange to brown colored slightly viscous syrup with caramel orange flavor contains in 60mL/120mL amber round bottle. What is in the medicine? Multivitamins (Fulvit) syrup contains the required vitamins essential for the daily intake of vitamin deficient children and is fortified with growth nutrients to keep them active and strong day. Strength of the medicine: Each 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) contains: 636 IU Retinol Palmitate (Vitamin A), USP Cholecalciferol (Vitamin Ds) 400 IU dl-a-Tocopheryl Acetate (Vitamin E) 51U Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C) 35 mg Niacin 13 mg Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin Be). 900 mcg Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 700 mcg Folic Acid Riboflavin-5-Sodium Phosphate (Vitamin Bz). 700 meg 40 mcg Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 2 mcg What is this medicine used for? It is used for the prevention and treatment of vitamin deficiency due to poor absorption, restricted dietary intake and illness. And it is also used to provide nutritional balance necessary for proper physical growth and mental development. How much and how often should you use this medicine? Infants (6 months old): Start with 2.5 mL (1/2 teaspoon) Children (6 months old — 12 years old): 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) Adults: 10 mL (2 teaspoonfuls) To be taken once a day, or as prescribed by the physician. When should you not take this medicine? If you are allergic to any of the components in this product. Care that should be taken when taking this medicine 5 Do not take more than the recommended dose. = Do not use after the expiry date stated on the label. Undesirable effects of this medicine: Retinol Palmitate (Vitamin A) - Hypervitaminosis A due to intake of very high doses can cause fatigue, irritability, anorexia, loss of weight, vomiting, sedation, dizziness, confusion and diarrhea. Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) - Excessive intake may lead to hypercalcemia or hyperphosphatemia. dl-a-Tocopheryl Acetate (Vitamin E) - A higher incidence of necrotizing enterocolitis has been noted in premature infants weighing less than 1.5 kg treated with Vitamin E. Riboflavin-5-Sodium Phosphate (Vitamin B2) - Large doses may result to bright yellow discoloration in urine. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) - Allergic hypersensitivity reactions have occurred rarely that includes rash, itching, anaphylaxis. Nicotinic Acid - It has a vasodilating action that causes flushing, sensation of heat, faintness, pounding in the head. What other medicine or food should be avoided? Retinol Palmitate (Vitamin A) - Neomycin, Cholestyramine or Liquid paraffin may reduce the absorption of Vitamin A. Increase risk of hypervitamino- sis A if given with retinoids such as isotretinoin and tretinoin. Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) - Increased risk of hypercalcemia if Vitamin D is given with Thiazide diuretics, Calcium, Phosphate. dl-a-Tocopheryl Acetate (Vitamin E) - Cholestyramine, Colestipol, Orlistat may interfere with the absorption of Vitamin E. High doses of Vitamin E may increase the effects of anti-coagulants. Pyridoxine HCI (Vitamin B6) - Pyridoxine reduces the effects of Levodopa and Altretamine. Vitamin B6 decreases concentration of Phenobarbital and Phenytoin. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) - Neomycin, Aminosalicylic Acid, Histamine H2 Antagonist, Omeprazole, Colchicine reduce the absorption of Vitamin 5 Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C) - Omeprazole What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Signs and symptoms of overdose: The following symptoms might be seen in large doses: Retinol Palmitate (Vitamin A) - Intake in excess of 10,000 IU daily or weekly intakes in excess of 25,000 IU have resulted to hypervitaminosis A (fatigue, irritability, anorexia, loss of weight, vomiting, sedation, dizziness, confusion, diarrhea). Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) - Anorexia, lassitude, nausea, vomiting, constipation, diarrhea, polyuria, nocturia, sweating, headache, thirst, somnolence, vertigo. dl-a-Tocopheryl Acetate (Vitamin E) - Large doses may cause diarrhea, abdominal pain and disturbances, blurred vision, dizziness, fatigue and weakness. Pyridoxine HCI (Vitamin B6) - Long term use of large doses is associated with the development of severe peripheral neuropathies. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) - gastrointestinal disturbances, fever, chills, hot flushing, dizziness, malaise, acneform, eruptions, tremor. Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C) - Large doses may cause diarrhea, abdominal pain and disturbances. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Protect from direct sunlight. Keep the container tightly closed and out of reach and sight of children. When should you consult your doctor? If any undesirable effect occurs. “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph”. Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. Registration Number: DRP-2590-01 Date of Authorization: June 18, 2018 Date of Revision of Patient Information Leaflet: February 13, 20: Manufactured by the Marketing Authorization Holder: Distributed by: FULL HEALTH INTERNATIONAL PHARMA DIAMOND LABORATORIES, INC Pp DLI Bldg., #8 Feria Rd., Commonwealth Ave., % # 8 Feria Rd., Commonwealth Ave., Diliman, Quezon City Diliman, Quezon City [Page 2]",1,1,Description of the product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T13:48:02.113029+00:00
