PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2905,Oxaliplatin,Zildox 50,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DaJsHS-FWo4YlCCNMQJNhhjXEkot6P75/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2905_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1DaJsHS-FWo4YlCCNMQJNhhjXEkot6P75_view_usp_drivesdk_ce8ba3ea81.pdf,page_text/DRP-2905_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1DaJsHS-FWo4YlCCNMQJNhhjXEkot6P75_view_usp_drivesdk_ce8ba3ea81.json,d055be30ae06826be040e19738c8a234773594663874a33e5293c5214aaf46db,1048439,2,digital_text,"Oxaliplatin for Injection (Zildox) is an antineoplastic agent with the molecular formula C H N O Pt and the chemical name 8 14 2 4 cis-[(1R, 2R)-1,2-cyclohexanediamine-N, N'] [Oxalato (2-)- O,O'] platinum. Oxalipatin is an organoplatinum complex in which the platinum atom is complexed with 1,2- diaminocyclohexane (DACH) and with an oxalate ligand as a leaving group. Oxaliplatin for Injection (Zildox) is supplied in vials containing 50 mg or 100 mg of Oxaliplatin as a sterile, preservative-free lyophilized powder for IV Infusion. Lactose monohydrate is present as an inactive ingredient. CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY Mechanism of Action Oxaliplatin undergoes nonenzymatic conversion in physiologic solutions to active derivatives via displacement of the labile oxalate ligand. Several transient reactive species are formed, including monoaquo and diaquo DACH platinum, which covalently bind with macromolecules. Both inter- and intra-strand Pt-DNA cross-links are formed. Crosslinks are formed between the N7 positions of two adjacent guanines (GG), adjacent adenine-guanines (AG), and guanines separated by an intervening nucleotide (GNG). These crosslinks inhibit DNA replication and transcription. Cytotoxicity is cell-cycle nonspecic. Pharmacology In vivo studies have shown antitumor activity of oxaliplatin against colon carcinoma. In combination with 5-uorouracil (5-FU), oxaliplatin exhibits in vitro and in vivo antiproliferative activity greater than either compound alone in several tumor models [HT29 (colon), GR (mammary), and L1210 (leukemia)]. Pharmacokinetics The reactive oxaliplatin derivatives are present as a fraction of the unbound platinum in plasma ultraltrate. The decline of ultralterable platinum levels following oxaliplatin administration is triphasic, characterized by two relatively short distribution phases (t1/2a; 0.43 hours and t1/2b; 16.8 hours) and a long terminal elimination phase (t1/2g; 391 hours). Pharmacokinetic parameters obtained after a single 2-hour IV infusion of Oxaliplatin for Injection (Zildox), at a 2 dose of 85 mg/m expressed as ultralterable platinum were Cmax of 0.814 mg/mL and volume of distribution of 440 L. Interpatient and intrapatient variability in ultralterable platinum exposure (AUC0-48) assessed over 3 cycles was moderate to low (23% and 6%, respectively). A pharmacodynamic relationship between platinum ultraltrate levels and clinical safety and effectiveness has not been established. Distribution At the end of a 2-hour infusion of Oxaliplatin for Injection (Zildox), approximately 15% of the administered platinum is present in the systemic circulation. The remaining 85% is rapidly distributed into tissues or eliminated in the urine. In patients, plasma protein binding of platinum is irreversible and is greater than 90%. The main binding proteins are albumin and gamma-globulins. Platinum also binds irreversibly and accumulates (approximately 2-fold) in erythrocytes, where it appears to have no relevant activity. No platinum accumulation was observed in plasma ultraltrate 2 following 85 mg/m every two weeks. Metabolism Oxaliplatin undergoes rapid and extensive nonenzymatic biotransformation. There is no evidence of cytochrome P450-mediated metabolism in vitro. Up to 17 platinum-containing derivatives have been observed in plasma ultraltrate samples from patients, including several cytotoxic species (monochloro DACH platinum, dichloro DACH platinum, and monoaquo and diaquo DACH platinum) and a number of noncytotoxic, conjugated species. Elimination The major route of platinum elimination is renal excretion. At ve days after a single 2-hour infusion of Oxaliplatin for Injection (Zildox), urinary elimination accounted for about 54% of the platinum eliminated, with fecal excretion accounting for only about 2%. Platinum was cleared from plasma at a rate (10 – 17 L/h) that was similar to or exceeded the average human glomerular ltration rate (GFR; 7.5 L/h). There was no signicant effect of gender on the clearance of ultralterable platinum. The renal clearance of ultralterable platinum is signicantly correlated with GFR. Pharmacokinetics in Special Populations Renal Impairment The AUC0-48hr of platinum in the plasma ultraltrate increases as renal function decreases. The AUC0-48hr of platinum in patients with mild (creatinine clearance, CLcr 50 to 80 mL/min), moderate (CLcr30 to <50 mL/min) and severe renal (CLcr <30 mL/min) impairment is increased by about 60, 140 and 190%, respectively, compared to patients with normal renal function (CLcr >80 mL/min)].",1,1,DESCRIPTION,For the treatment of metastatic colorectal cancer in combination with 5-uorouracil and folinic acid.,1,1,INDICATION,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:25:19.778715+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2909,Clobetasol Propionate,Unbranded,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1357879E9zL461zrUzs5Z5UaOOTULngFS/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2909_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1357879E9zL461zrUzs5Z5UaOOTULngFS_view_usp_drivesdk_3a71773a2d.pdf,page_text/DRP-2909_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1357879E9zL461zrUzs5Z5UaOOTULngFS_view_usp_drivesdk_3a71773a2d.json,c2801c8728251cab98e3b8bd86c229866ae0267a8eeb6c8888cb20476360d209,1834833,1,ocr,White or off white homogenous cream.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Date of Second Renewal of Authorization: March 11, 2020 Clobetasol Propionate Cream is indicated for the short term use in the treatment of steroid Date of Revision of Package Insert: July 20, 2020 responsive dermatoses resistant to other less potent topical corticosteroids such as: seborrhoeic dermatitis, atopic dermatiti: lichen simplex chronicus, pruritus and psoriasis,",1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T14:25:34.829250+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-2909-02,Clobetasol Propionate,Unbranded,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1SV-jOBlea6oAMBaTvcprsBFuVaIxrK95/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-2909-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1SV-jOBlea6oAMBaTvcprsBFuVaIxrK95_view_usp_drivesdk_a000ff6205.pdf,page_text/DRP-2909-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1SV-jOBlea6oAMBaTvcprsBFuVaIxrK95_view_usp_drivesdk_a000ff6205.json,cc5284206ad0081e8626f4ab1313d28e330502dba402f328c76dfec50f8953df,457460,1,digital_text,White or off white homogenous cream.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Clobetasol is a very potent topical corticosteroid indicated for adults, elderly and children over 1 year for the short term treatment only of more resistant inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of steroid responsive dermatoses unresponsive to less potent corticosteroids. These include the following: - Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis) - Recalcitrant dermatoses - Lichen planus - Discoid lupus erythematosus - Other skin conditions which do not respond satisfactorily to less potent steroids.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:25:22.788654+00:00
