PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3283-02,Lamotrigine,Lamicdin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1sgR-Vr9Al2MClGNl5rVKXtfifTJx7-Ci/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3283-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1sgR-Vr9Al2MClGNl5rVKXtfifTJx7-Ci_view_usp_drivesdk_95f380e72c.pdf,page_text/DRP-3283-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1sgR-Vr9Al2MClGNl5rVKXtfifTJx7-Ci_view_usp_drivesdk_95f380e72c.json,8dd84a40336a8738e117dd43f3f12b8106b8d166c43f484eab024da076c1e243,593765,1,digital_text,"White, round, biconvex, uncoated tablets, scored on one side.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,For monotherapy and adjunctive treatment of partial seizures and primary and secondary generalized tonic-clonic seizures; seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:39:59.933234+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3289,Clozapine,Ziproc-100,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1y0n1OReoo_5TZFdYFs4g6XvjOhlVhjMZ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3289_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1y0n1OReoo_5TZFdYFs4g6XvjOhlVhjMZ_view_usp_drivesdk_aa5348ebd2.pdf,page_text/DRP-3289_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1y0n1OReoo_5TZFdYFs4g6XvjOhlVhjMZ_view_usp_drivesdk_aa5348ebd2.json,e471ed9bedc87909fad6d775aadea9bae1c00249fb988a6ca1683503b8dacb0e,294457,1,digital_text,"Clozapine (Ziproc-25) 25 mg Tablet and Clozapine (Ziproc-100) 100 mg Tablet are light yellow coloured, round, binconvex, uncoated tablet with bisecting line on one side. CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY Clozapine is classified as an atypical antipsychotic agent, which differs from conventional antipsychotics. It has a weak dopamine receptor blocking activity at D2 receptors, but has a D1 anti-adrenergic, anticholinergic, antihistaminergic and arousal reaction inhibiting effects. It has also been shown to possess antiserotonergic property. Clozapine produces rapid and marked sedation and exerts strong antipsychotic effects. It is effective in patients exhibiting both positive and negative schizophrenic symptoms. PHARMACOKINETICS Although clozapine is well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, its bioavailability is limited to about 50% by fisrt-pass metabolism. Peak plasma concentrations are achieved an average of about 2.5 hours after oral administration. Clozapine is about 95% bound to plasma proteins and has mean terminal elimination half-life of about 12 hours at a steady state. It is almost completely metabolized and routes of metabolism include N-demethylation, hydroxylation, and N-oxidation, the desmethyl metabolite (norclozapine) has limited activity. The metabolism of clozapine is mediated mainly by the cytochrome P450 isoenzyme CYP1A2. Metabolites and trace amounts of unchanged drug are excreted mainly in the urine and also in the feces. There is wide interindividual variation in plasma concentrations of clozapine and no simple correlation has been found between plasma concentrations and therapeutic effect.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Used for the treatment of schizophrenia; reserved for patients who fail to respond to, or who experience severe extrapyramidal side-effects with classical antipsychotics. Used for reducing the risk of recurrent suicidal behavior in those with schizophrenia or schizoaffective disorder who is at risk of suicidal behavior. Also used in the management of treatment-resistant psychoses associated with Parkinson's disease.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:40:06.421573+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3290-01,Sildenafil (As Citrate),None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/10L7dGm0Xqga82jtmQKpaujtAOCJP_A11/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3290-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10L7dGm0Xqga82jtmQKpaujtAOCJP_A11_view_usp_drivesdk_a13854d588.pdf,page_text/DRP-3290-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10L7dGm0Xqga82jtmQKpaujtAOCJP_A11_view_usp_drivesdk_a13854d588.json,58f8741db18167f29f255a6075f4aa00b47cadd10397b2ae0b1e1a838d6e44ef,7699065,3,digital_text,"RiteME~ Sildenafil 50 mg tablets Sildenaffl 50 mg tablets are lght blue round slightly dotted tablets, with cross t>reaki'lg notch on one side and embossment ·50· on tie other side. The tablet can be divided into equal quarters. Only illti(e of the whole tablet Of concomitant illake of two qua-ters is covered by posology. CLINICAL PARTICULARS lndicatio11s Siklenafil is indicated in adult men with erectile dysfunction, which is the inability to achieve or maintain a penile erection sufficient for satisfactory sexual performarw;:e. In order tor sildenafll to be effective, sexual stimulat,on is reqlired. Dosage and modt/route of administration Use in adults The recoovnended dose is 50 mg taken as needed approximately one hour before sexual activity. Based on efficacy and tolerability, the dose may be increased to 100 or decreased to 25 mg. The maximum recommended dose is 100 mg. The maximum rerommended dosing frequency is once per day. If sildenafil is taken with food, the onset cf activity may be delayed compared to the fasted state (see Pharmacokinetic properties). Useinefderly Dosage adjus1ments are not reQIJired in elderly patients. Use in patients with impaired renal function The dosing recommendations described in ""Use in aclllts"" apply to patients with mild to moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance =30~0 ml.Jmln). Since sild&Mfil clearance is reduced in patients wnh sevem rel'\8.I impairment (aeatinine chmance <30 mUmin) a 25 mg dosa should be considered. Based on efficacy and tolerab~ity, the dose may be increased stepwise to 50 m9, 75 mg, aOO 100 Ill!· Use in patients with impaired hepatic function Since sildenafil clearance is reduced in paHents with hepatic impairment (e.g. cirrhosis) a 25 mg des€ should be considered. Based oo effrcacy and tileration, tne dose may be increased to 50 mg, 75 llVil, and 100 mg. Use in children and edolesrenls Sildenafil is not indicated for individuals below 18 years of age. Use in patients using other medicines With the excepUon of ritonavir for which co-administration with sildenafil is not advised (see Special wamings and precautions for use) a starting dose of 25 mg should De considered in patients receiving concomitant treatment with CYP3M inhibitors (see Interactions wilh other medicinal products arid other k>rms of iriteraction). In order to minimise the potential of developirig postural, patients should be stabile on alpha-blocker therapy prior to initiating silderiafil treatment. In addition, inila1ion of sildenafil al a dose of 25 mg should be considerad (see Special warnings aOO pracautioos for use arid lriteractions with olher medicinal products arid other forms of interactiori). Contrairidications Hypersensitivity to lhe active substance or to any of the excil)ients. Consistent wilh its known effects on the nitric oxidefcyclic guanosine mono phosphate (cGMP) pathway (see Pharmacodynamic properties), sildenafi was shown to potentiate fie hypotensive etfecls of nitrates, and its co-adrrinist""a.Uon with nitric oxide doriors (such as amyl nitrite) or nitrates in any form is therefore cootraindicated. The ecradminislralioo of PDE5 inhibitors, including siklenafil, with guanylate cyclase slimulators, such as riociguat, is contraindicated as it may potentially lead ti symptomaHc hypotension (see Interactions with other medicinal produd:s arid other forms of interaction). Agerits for the treatmer.t of erectile dysfunction, ilcluding sildenafil, should not be used in men for whom seX\lal activity is inadvisable {e.g. patients with severe caroiovascular disorders such as uristable angina or severe cardiac failure) Sildenafil is contraindicated ii patients who have loss al vision in one eye OOcaLJSe of non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neLWOpalhy (NAION), regardless of whether this episode was in connection or no1 with previous PDE5 inhibitor exposure (see Special warnirgs and pre<:autions for use). The safety of sildenaf~ has not tleen studied in lhe following sub-groups of patients and its use is therefore contrail'ldicaled: severe hepatic illl)0irmerrt, hypotension (blood pressure <90/50 mm Hg), recent history of stroke or myocardial infarction and krxJwn hereditary degerierative retinal disorders such as retinitis pigmentosa (a minority of hese patients have genetic disorders of retinal phosphodiesterases). Sp11cial warnings and pracautions for UH A medical history arid physical e.xaminalion should be undertaken to diagnose erectile dysfunction and detelTlline potential underlying cooses, before pharmacological treatment is consiclemd Cardiovascular risk factors Prior to initiating any treatment for erec1ile dysfunction, physicians sMUkl consider the cardiovascular status ol thei"" patierits, since there is a degree of cardiac risk associated with sexual activity. Sildenafil has vasmllalor properties, resulting in mild end trarisienl decreases in blood pressure (see Pharmacodynamic properties). Prior to prescribing sildenafil, physiciaris should carefully oonsider whether !heir patients with certain uOOertying conditions could De adversely atrected by such vasodiletory effects, especially in combination with sexual activity. Patients with increased susceptibility lo vasodilators include those with left ventricular outflow obstructiori (e.g. aortic stenos is, hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy), or those with the rare syndrome of multiple system atrophy mariifasting as severely impaired autonomic control of tllood pressure. Sildenaffl potentiates the hypctenslve effect of ni1rates (see Contrairidicaijons). Serious cardiovasc!Aar events, includir19 myocardial infarction, urislable oogiria, sudden cardiac death, veribicular arrhyttvnia, cerebrovascu1ar haemorrhage, transient ischaemic atlaek, hypertension and hypotension have been reported post-marketing in temporal association with the use of sildenafil. Most, but not all, of these patients had pra--existing cardiovascular risk factors. Many events were reported to occur during or shortly after sexual intercourse and a few were reported to occur shortly after the use of sUdenafil wilhout sexual activity. It is not possible to determine whether these events are related directly to these factors or to other factors. Priapism Agerils for the treatment of erectile dysfunction, including sildenafil, should be used witti caution in palients with anatomical detonnalion of the peris [such as arigulation, cavemosal fibrosis or Peyroriie's disease), or in padents who have conditions which may predispose them to priapism (S41ch as sickle cell anaemia, multiple myeloma or leukaemia). Prolonged erectioris and priapism have been reported wilh sildel'\8.lil in posl-mart:eting experiem:e. In the event of an erection that persists longer than 4 hours, the patient should seek immediate medical asslstance. If priapism is not treated immediately, penile tissue damage and pennanent loss of potency could result. Cor,camitsnt use with other PDES illhibitrn or other treetml?nts for erectile dysfunction The safety arid effJCacy of combiMtions of sildenafil with other PDE5 inhibitors, or other pulmonary arterial hypertensioo (PAH) treatments containing sildenafil, or other treatments for erectile dysfunction have not been studied. Therefore the use of such combinations is not recommended. Effects on vision Cases of visual defects have bean reported spontaneoosly in corviection with the intake of sildenafil 81W other PDE5 inhibitors (see UOOesirable effects). Cases of non--arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropethy, a rare condition, have been reported spontaneously end in en observational study in connection wijh the intake of sildenafil and other PDE5 irihibitors (see Undesirable effects). Patients sliould be advised that in the event of any sudden visual defect, they should stop takirig sildenafil and cons[j! a physician immediately (see Contraindicatior.s). Corn:omitant use with ntonevir Co-administration of sildenafil with ritonavir is not advised (see Interactions with oiler medicinal products and other forms of interaction). Cor,comJtard use with oJfpha-blodcers Caution is advised when sildenafil is administered to palierits taking an alpha-blocker. as the co-administration may lead to symptomatic hypoterision in a few susceptible individuals (see lriteractions with other medicinal products and olh8f' forms of iriteraction}. This is most likely to occur withiri 4 hours post sildenafil dosing. In order to miriimise the potential for developing postural hypotensiori, patients shoo Id 00 haemodynamicalty stable on alpha-blocker therapy prior lo initiating sildenafil treatment. Initiation of sildenafil at a dose of 25 mg sliould be considered (see Dosage and mode/route of OOrrinistration). In addition, p~ysicians should advise patients what to do in the event of postural hypotensive symptoms Effect on b/9eding Studies with human platelets indicate that sildenatll potentiates the anli-aggregatcxy effect of sodium nitmprusside in vitro. There is no safety information on the administration of slldenafil to patierits with t>leeclng disorders or active peptic ulceration. There1ore sildenafll should be administered to ltlese patients only after careful beneftt.-risk assessmert. """""""""""" Sildenafil tablet is not indicated for use by women. Interaction with other medicinal products end other forms of interaction Effects of other medicinal products on sildenafil In vitro studfes Sildenafil metabolism is principally mediated by the cytochrome P450 (GYP) isororms 3A4 (major route) and 2C9 (minor route). Therefore, inhitlik>rs of these isoenzymes may reduce sildena.fll cleararice and inducers of these isoenzymes may ilcrease sldenaf1I clearance. In vivo studies Population pharmacokinetic analysis of clinical trial data iridicated a reduction in sildenaf~ clearance when c;o.administerad with CYP3A4 inhibitors (such as ketoconazole, erylhromyciri, cimetidine). Althotql no increased iricidence of adverse events was observed in these patients, when sildenafil is administered concomitantly with CYP3A4 inhibitors, a startirig dose of 25 mg sho[jd be considered. Co-administration of the HIV protease inhibitor ritonavir, which ls a highly potent P450 inhibitor, at steacty state (500 mg twice daily)with sildenafil (1 00 mg sini;,le dose) resulted in a 300 % (4-fold) increase in sildenafil c ..... and a 1,000 % (11-fold) increase in silderiall plasmaAUC. At 24 hours, the plasma levels of sildenafd were sliD approximately 200 ngtml, oompared to approximEKely 5 ng{ml wheri silderialil was administered alone. This is coosistent with ritonavi""'5 marlwd effects on a broad range of P450 autlstrates. Sildenafil had no effect oo ritonavir pharmacokinetics. Based ori these pharmacokinetic results, co-admir'listralion of sildenafil with ritonavir is not advised (sea Special warnings and precautions for use) and in any event the maxillll.lm dose of sildenafil should under no circumstances exceed 25 mg within 48 hours. Co-administration of the HIV protease inhi>itor saquinevir, a CYP3M inhibitor, at steady state (1.200 mg three times a day~with sildenafil (100 mg single dose) resulted 11 a 140 % increase in sildellafil C.,.. and a 210 % increase in sildenafilAUC. Sildena1il had no effect on saquinavir pharmacokinelics (see Dosage and mode/route of administration). Stronger CYP3A4 irihitlitors such as kelooonazole and ilraconazole WflUld be expected to have greater effects. 'When a single 100 mg dose of sildenafil was administered with erythromycin, a moderate CYP3A4 inhibitor, at steady state (500 mg twice daly for 5 days), there was a 162 % increase in sildenafil systemic exposure (ALIC). In normal healthy male mt.f'llaers, there was no evidence of an affect of azittromycin (500 mg daily for 3 days) on the Al.IC, Cns, t,,.... elimiriatian rale constarit, or sul>sequent half-lifB of siklenafil or its principal circulalirig metabol~e. Cimetidine (800 mg), a cytochrome P450 irilllltor arid non-specific CYP3A4 inhibitor, caused a 56 % iricrease in plasma sildenefil conceotratians Ylhen co­administered with sildenafil (50 mg) to healthy \lolunteers. Grapefnit juice is a weak irihibilor of CYP3A4 gut wall metabolism and may give rise to Rl:.'ldesl increases in plasma levels of sildenafil. Single doses of antacid (magnesium hydroxide/aluminium hydroxide) did not alfecl the bioavailability of sildenafil. Although specific interaction studies were not conducted for all medicinal products, population pharmacokinetic analysis showed no effect of concomitant treatmerit on sildenafil pharmacokinetics when grouped as CYP2C9 il""lhibttors {such as tolbutamide, warfariri, pheriytoin), CYP2D6 if1hibitors (such as selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, lricyciic antidepressants), thiazi09 and related diuretics, loop and potassh.m sparing diuretics, angiotensiri oonverting enzyme inhibitors, calcium chamel blockers, beta-adrenoreceptor antagooists or induoors of CYP450 metabolism (such as rifampiciri, barbiturates). In a study of hsallhy male volunteer&, co-administration of the andothelin antagonist, bosootan, (an inducer of CYP3A4 [moderate], CYP2C9 and possibly of CYP2C19) at steady state (1 25 mg twire a day) with sitderiafil at steady state (80 mg three times a day) resulted in 62.6o/, and 55.4% decrease in sildenafil AUC and C..,,. respedively. Therefore, conoornit.ant admil'lislration of strong CYP3A4 inducers, such as rifampin, is expected to cause greater decreases in plasma aincenlraHons of sildenafil. Nicorandil is a hybrid of potassium d'lannel activator and nm-ate. Due to lhe riitrate comporient, ii has the potential to result in a serious interaction with sildenam. Effecls of sildenafil oo other medicinal products In vitrostucf,es Sildenafll is a weak inhibitor of the cytochrome P450 isofonns 1A2, 2C9, 2C19, 206, 2E1 and 3A4 (ICSO >150 µM). Given sildenafil peak plasma concaritrations of approximately 1 µM after recommend ad doses, it is unlikely that sildenafil will altar the cleararice of substrates of these isoenzymes. There are no data on the interaction of sildenafil end non-specific phosphodiesterase inhil>itcrs such as lheophylline or dipyridamole. In vivos1udies Consistent with its knowri effects on the nitric oxide/cGMP pathway (see Pharmacodynamic properties), sildenafil was showrl to poterltiate the hypotensive effects of nitrates, and its oo-admiistra1br1 with nitric oxide donors or riitrates in any form is therefore contraindicated (see Contrairidications). Riocigua! Preclinical studies showed addiive systemic blood pressure lowering effect when PDES iooibiton; were combined with riociguat. In clinical studies, riociguat has been shown to augment the hypotensive effects of POE5 inhibitors, There was no evidence of favourable clinical effect c:Atie ccmbinaHon in the p::,pulalion studied. Concomitant use of riocii;iuat with POE5 inhibitors, including siltrenafil, is contraindicated (see Contraindications) Col'ICOmitant administration of sildenaf1l to patients taking alpha-blocker tt,erepy may lead lo symptomatic hypotension in a feN susceptible individuals. This is most Hkely to occur within 4 hours post-sUdenafd dosing (see Dosage and mode/route of a<miriistration aoo Sped al warnings and precautions for use). In tlv'ee specific drug-drug interaction studies, the alpha•tllocker doxazosin (4 mg and B mg) and silderiafil (25 mg, 50 mg or 100 mg) were administered simultaneously to patients with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) stabilised ori doxazosin lt1erapy. lri these study populations, mean additional reductions of supine blood pre55ure of 7f7 mmHg, 9/5 rrmHg, and 814 mmHg, and mean additional reductions of standing blood pressure of 616 mmHg, 11/4 mmHg, and 415 mm Hg, respectively, were ot>served. When sildenafil an<I doxazosin were admilistered simultaneously to patients stabilised on doxaz.osin therapy, there were infreq..ient reports of patioots who experienced symptomatic postural hypotension. These reports included dizziness and light..tleadedness, but not syncope. No sigriificanl interactions were shown wnen s~denar• (50 mg) was co-aOOlinistered with tolbutamide (250 mg) or warfarin (40 mg), both of which am metabollsed tly CYP2C9. Sildenafll (50 mg) did not potentiate the i'lcrease in tlleeding lime caused by acetyl salicylic acid (150 mg}. Sildenafil (50 mg) did not potenliate the hyJX)lensive effects of alcotiol in healthy volunteers with mean maximum blood aloohol levets of 80 mg/dl. Pooling of the following classes of anti hypertensive medication: diuretics, tlela•blockars, ACE inhitlitors, angiotoosin II antagonists, anti hypertensive medicinal products (vasodilator and centrally-acting), adrenergic neLKOn blockers, calcium ctianool blockers and alpha-adrenocepla blockers. showed no difference in the side effect profie in patients taking ddenafll compared to placebo treatment. In a specific iriteraction study, where sildenafil (100 mg) was co-admiriistered wi1h amlodipine in hypertensive paUents, there was an additional reductioo on supine systolic blood pressure of 8 mmHg. The corresponding additional reduction in supine diastolic blood pressure was 7 mm Hg. These additional blood pressure reductions were of a similar magnitude kl 1hose seen when sildenaf• was admillistered alone to healthy volooteers (see Pharmacodynamic properties). Sildenafll (1 DO mg) did not affect the steady state pharmaookinetics of the HIV protease inhi:litors, saciuinavir and rttonavir, both of which are CYP3M substrates. In healthy male volunteers. sildenafil at steady state {80 mg t.i.d.) restAted if1 a 49.6% ir1crease iri bosentariAUC and a 42% increase in tlosentari c ... (125 mg b.i.d.). Pregnam:y and lactatlon Sildenaffl Is not indicated for use by women. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant or breastfeedilg women. No relevant advflf'Sa effects were foood in reproduction studies in rats and rabbits following oral administratiori of sildenafi1. There was rio effect on sperm motility or morphology after sif1gle 100 mg oral doses of sildenafi in healthy volunteers (see Pharmacodynamic properties). Effects on al>ilitytodrive and use machines No studies on the efl'eci::l on the abiily to d~ve and use machines have been performed. As dizziness and altered vision were reported ln clinical trials with sildenafil, patients should be aware of how they react to sildenafil, before driving or operating machinery. Undeslrable effects Summart of the safety proflle The safety profile of sildenafil is based ori 9,570 patierils in 74 double-1:lliOO plaoebo-oontrolled clinical studies. The most oorrrnonly reported adverse reactions in clinical studies among sildenafil treated paUenls were headadle, flushing, dyspepsia, nasal congestion, dizziness, nausea, hot flush, visual disturtarice, cyanopsia and vision blurred. Adverse reactions from post-mcJ""keling surveillance has been gathered covering an estimated period >10 years. Because net all adveroo reactions are reported to ltle Marketing AiJhorlzation Holder and included in the safety database, the frequericies of these reactions cannot be re6ably determined. [Page 2] Lisi of adverse reactions In the list below all medically i'nportant adverse reactions, which occurred in dinicaJ trials at an incidence greater than placebo are listed by system organ dass and frequency (very common {~/10), common (.!:1/100 to <1/10), unconYnOO (.!:1/1,000 to <1/100), rare (.?1/10,000 to <1/1,000). Within &aCh frequency grouping, undesirable effects are presented in order of decreasing seriousness. Medically important adverse reactions reported at an incidence greater than placel>o in controlled clinical studies and medically important adverse reactions reported th re ugh post-marketing surveillanr.e ln~ctlonI and lnftatatlons Uncommon: Rhriitis Immune system disorders Uncommon: Hypersensitivity Nervous system disarders Ve,y common: Headache Common· Diuiness Uncommo1r Somnolence, hypoaesttiesia Rm: Cerebrovasculer accident, transient ischaemic attack, seizure•, seirure recvrrence•, syncope Eye dlaOrdel""$ Common; /Jflcommon: ViSlJal colour distortions••, visual disturbance, vision bl\Jrred Lacrination disorders ... , eye pain, photophobia, photopsia, ocular hyperaemia, visual brightness, conjunctiViUs Rar&: Non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic net..-opathy (NAION)*, retil'lal vaSOJli:l""occlusicn•, ratinal haemorri1aga, arteriosclarotic retinopathy, retinal disorder, glaucoma, visual field defect, diplopia, visual acuity reclJced, myopia, asthenopia, vitreous floaters, iris disorder, mydriasis. halo vision, eye oedema, eye swelling, eye disorder, oonjunctival hyperaemia, eye irrlation, abnormal sensation in eye, eyelid oedema, scleral discololJ'ation Ear and labyrlnth disorder$ Uncommon: Vertigo, tinnitus Rsre: Deamess Cardi at: disorders Uncommon: Tachycardia, palpitations Rare: Sl.ldden cardiac daath•, myocardial irifarction, ventricular arrhythmia•, atrial Ebrillalion, oostable angina Vascular dlson:lers Common: Flushing, hot flush Uncommon· Hypertension, hypotension R11plratory, thoracic and mtdlaIUna1 disorders Common: Nasal congestion Uncommon· Epislaxis, sirius congestion Rar&: Throat tightness, nasal oedema, nasal dryness Gastroint.est.inal disorders Common: Nausea, dyspepsia Uncommon: Gastroesophegeal reflux cisease, vomiting, abdomilal pain ~er. dry mouth Rare: Hypoaesthesia oral Skin and MJbcutaneou& tissue disorders Uncommon: Rash Rare: Steven Johnson Syndrome (SJS)•, Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)• Musculoskelatal and connective tissue disorders Uncommon: Myalgia, pain in extremity Renal and urinary disorden Uncommon· Heematuria Reproductive system and breutdisordel'1 Rare: Penile haemorltiage, priapism*, haematospermia, erection increased General disorder8 and administration site condition• Uncommon: Chest pain, fatigue, feeling hot Rara: lrrits.bDity Investigations Uncommon· Heart rate increased Overdose Reported during IX)Sl-rnarketing uveillance only ViS1Jal colour distortions: chloropsia, chromatopsia, cyanopsia, e,yttw-opsia and xarthopsia Lacrim1tion disorders: dry eye, lacrimal disorder and lacrimation increased ln single dose volunteer studies of doses up to 800 mg, adverse reactions were similar to those seell at lower doses, but the Incidence rates and severities were increased. Doses ci 200 mg did not result in increased efficacy but the incidence of adverse reactiolls (headache, flusiling, dizziness, dyspepsia, nasal congestion, altered vision) was increased. In cases of overdose, standard supportive measures should be adopted as required. Renal dialysis is not expected to acoolerale clearance as sildenafil is highly boood lo plasma proteins and not elilTinaled in the urine. PfiARMACOLOGICAL PROPERTIES Pharmacodynamic properties Pharmacotherapeutic group: Drugs used in erectile dysfunction Sildenaf~ is all oral therapy for erectile dysfuncoon. In the nab.Jral setlillg, I.e. with se,cual stimulaUon, It restores Impaired erectile function by locraasing blood flow kl the penis. The physiological mechanism responsible for erection of the pell is involves ttie relea!',8 of nitric oxide (NO) ir1 the corpus cavernosum during serual stimulation. Nitric oxide then activates the enzyme guanylate cyclase, which resuns in inaeased levels of cyclic guanosine mono phosphate (cGMP), producing smooth muscle relaxation in tie corpus csvernosum and allowing inflow of blood Sildenafil is a potent and seleciive inhit>itor of cGMP specific phosphodieslerase type 5 (PDE5) in the oorpus c:aveimsum, 'Mlere PDE5 is responsible for degradation of cGMP. Sildenafil has a peripheral site of action on erections. Sildenafil llas no direct relaxant effect on isolated human corpus cavemosum but potenHy enhances the relaxant effect of NO on this tissue. When ttie NO/cGMP pathway is activated, as occurs with sexual stimulation, inhibition of POE5 by sildenafil results in increased cixpus cavernosum levels of cGMP. Therefore sexual sti100lation is required in order for sildena~I to produce ~s intended beneicial phamiacological effects. Studies in ""1tro have showii that siklenafil is selective for PDES, which is involved in tile erection process. Its effect is more potent on PDE5 than on other known phosphodiesterases. There is a 10-fold selectivity over PDE6 which is involved in the phototransductioo pathway in the retina. Al maximum recommended doses, there is al'I 80-fold selectivity over PDE1, and over 700-fold over PDE2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 11. In partio.llar, sildenafi1 has greater than 4,00D-fold selectivity for PDES over PDE3, the cAMP•specific phosphodiesterase isoform involved in the control of cardiac contractility. Two dinical studies were specifically designed to assess tile time window after dosing during which sildeoail could produce an erection in resporise to sexual stimulation. In a ?£)flie plelhysmography (RigiScan) study of fasted patients, the median time to onset for those wllo obtained erections of 60 % rigidly (sufficient for sexual intercourse) was 25 minutes (range 12-37 minutes) on sildenafil. In a separate RigiScan study, sildenafil was still able to produce an erection in response to sexual stim!Jadon 4-5 hours post-dose. Sildenafil causes mnd arid transient decreases in blood pressure which, in the majority of cases, clo not translate into clinical effects. The mean maximum decreases in supine systolic blood pressure following 100 mg oral dosing of sldenaf1I was 8.4 mm Hg. The corresponding chan~e in supine diastolic blood pressure was 5.5 mmHg. These decreases in bk:lod pressure are oonsistent with the vasodilatory effects of sildenafil, probat>ly due to increased cGMP levels in vascular sroooth muscle. Single oral doses of sildenafil up to 100 mg in healthy volL11teers produced no dinically relevant effects on ECG. In a study oftha haemodynamic effects of a single oral 100 mg dose of sildenafil in 14 patients with severe coronary artarydisaase (CAO) (>70 % stenosis of at least one coronary artefy), tile mean resting systolic and diastolic blood pressures decreased by 7 % and 6 % respectively compared to baseline. Mean pulmonary systolic blood pressure decreased by 9 %. Sildenafil showed no effect on cardiac ootplJ~ end did not impair blood flaw through the stenosed coronary arteries No dinical relevant diflerenoes were demonstraled in time to limiting angina for sildenafil when compared with placebo in a double-blind, placebo-controlled exercise stress trial evaluated 144 patients with erectile dysfunction and chronic stable a11gina, who were taking on a regular bas~ anti-anginal medications (except n~rates}. Mild and transient differences in colOll"" discrinination (blue/green) were detected in some subjects using the Famsworth-Munsell 100 hue test at 1 hour following a 100 mg dose, with no effects evidant after 2 hcx.n post-dose. The postulated mechanism fortiis change in cololl"" discrimination is related to inhibition of PDE6, wllich is involved in the phototransduction cascade of the retina, Sildenafi llas no effect on visual aC1.1ity or contrast sensitivity. In a small size placebo-conlrnled study of patients with documented early age-related moteular degeneration {n=9), siklenafil (single dose, 100 mg) demonstrated no significant change1> in tile visual tests conducted (visual acuity, Amsler grid. colour discrimination simulated traffic ligllt Humpllrey perimeter and pt,otostress). There was no effect on sperm motility ar morphology after single 100 mg oral doses ofsildenafil in healthy vnlmteers. Further klformatkm on cliDir.al 1rials In dinical trials sildeoafil was administered to more than 8,000 patients aged 19-87. Thefolowing patient groups were represented: elderly (19.9%), patients with hyperlertSion {30.9%), diabetes mellilus (20.3%), ischaemic heart disease (5.8%), hyperlipidaemia (19.8%), spinal cord inj~ (0.6""/ci}, depression (5.2%), transurethral resection of the prostate (3.7%), radical prostatectomy (3.3%). The following '10~ were not well represellle<I or exckJded from dinical trials: patients wit!, pelvic Sll""Q8fY, patients post-raci~herapy, patierrts with 58V8f8 renal or hepatic i""l)flirmant and patients with certain cardiova~c!Jar conditions {see Contraindications). ln fixed dose studies, the proportions of patients reporting that treatment improved their erections were 62% (25 mg), 74% (50 mg) and 82% (100 mg) compared to 25% on placebo. In con1rolled clinical trials, tt,e discontinuation rate due to sildenafil w.as low and similar to plaoebo. Across all trials, the proportion of patients reporting improvement on sildenafil were as follows psychogenic erectile dysfunction (84%), mixed erectile dysfunction ff7%), organic erectile dysfunction (68%). elder1y (67%), diabetes mellitus (5S%), ischaemic heart disease (69o/,), hypertension (68%), TURP (61%), radical prostal:ecklmy {43%), spinal cord injury {83%), depression (75%). The safety and efficacy of sildenafil was maintained in long-term studies. Pharmacokinetic properties &mo!! Sildenafil is rapidly absorbed. Maximum observed plasma conceotrations are reached wijhin 30 to 120 minutes (median 60 mintJ!es] of oral dosirlg in the fasted state. The mean absolute oral bloavaHability ls41 % (range 25-63 %).After oral dosf'lg ofsildenafll AVC and c.,., Increase In proportion witt, dose over the reco1M1ended dose range {25-100 mg). When sildenafil is taken with food, the rate of absorpUon is reduced wilh a mean delay in I..._ of 60 mllules and a mean reducion in C,,.. of 29 %. DistribtJticn The mean steactyst.ata volume of distriDUtion (Vd)1orsJldenafil is 105 L, indicating distribution into the tissues.After a single oral dosa of 100 mg, the mean maximum total plasma concentration of sUdenafil is approximately 440 ng/ml (CV 40%). Since sildenefil (and its major cira.ileting N-desmethyl metat>olite) is gs% bound to plasma proteins, this results in the mean maximum free plasma concentration for sildenaf• of 18 rig/ml (38 nM). Protein binding ~ independent of total drug ooncentrations. In healthy volunteers receivl'lg sildenam (100 mg sir1gle dose), less than 0.0002% (average 1SB ng) of the administered dose was present in ejaculate 90 minutes after dosing. ~ Sildenafil is cleared predominantly by the CYP3A4 {major route) and CYP2C9 (miriar route) hepatic microsomal isoenzymes. The major circulating metabolite results from N-demethylalion of sildenafll. This motabolite has a phosphodiesterase selectivity profile similar to sildenafil and an in vitro potency for POE.5 approximately 50% that of the pa root drug. Plasma concentrations of this metabolite are approximately 40% of lhosa seen for sildenafil. The N-<tesmethyl metabolite is further metabolised, with a terminal half-life of approximately 4 h. ~ The total body clearance of s~denafi is 41 IJl1 with a resultant terminal phase half-life of 3-5 h. After either oral or Intravenous administration, sildenafil is excreted as metabolltes predominantly in the faeces (approximately 80 % or adminiis1ered oral dose) and to a lesser extent In the urine {approximately 13% of administered oral dose). Pha,rmacokinetics in special oatient groups Elderly Healthy ekler1y vollllteer.. (65 years or over) had a reduced clearance of sildenafil, res!Jting in approximat-aly 90% higher l)lasma concentrations of sildenafil and the active N-desmetllyl metabor.te compare<! to those seen in ilealtly younger volunteer.. (1&-45 years). Due to age-differences in plasma protein binding, Iha corresponding increase in frae sildanail plasma concentration was appro,cimately 40%. Renal JnsulfJciency ln volunteers with mild to rrKlderate renal impairment (creatl'line clearance =30-80 mUmin), the pharmacokl'letics of sildenafil were not altered after receiving a 50 mg singk} oral dose. The mean Al.IC and c .... of the N-desmethyl metabolite inc:reased up to 126% and up to 73%, respectively, corr1)ared lo age-matclled volunteers witll no renal impairment However, due lo high inter-subject variabillty, these differences were not statistically signiflcart. In volunteers with severe renal impairment (creatinine dearance <30 mUmin), sildenafil clearance was reduced. rasulting in mean increases in AUC and c.,. of 100% and 88% respectively compared to age-matched volunteers with no renal impairment. In addition, N-desmethyl metabolita AUG and c,... values were sglificantly incraaseo by 200% and 79%, respectively. Hepatic insufffciency In volunteers with mild to moderate hepatic cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A and BJ s~denaf1I clearance was reduced, resulHng in increases in ALIC (84%) and c.,u (47o/.) compared to age-matched voluntlers with no hepatic i~airmenl. The pharmacokinetics of sildenafil in patients with severely impaired hepatic function has not been studied. Preclinical safety data Non-clinical data revealed no special hazard for humans based on conventional studies of safety pharmacology, repeated dose toxicity, genotoxicity, carcinogenic potential, and toxicity to reproduction a11d development. PHARMACEUTICAL PARTICULARS lncompattbllltles NotAp~icab~ storage S1ore at temperatures not exceeding 30 °C. CAUTION Foods, Drugs, Devices, a11d Cosmetics Act ~hi bits dispensillg without prescription. AVAILABILITY Alu/Aclar blister pack of 4's (box of 4's) ADVERSE DR\/G REACTION REPORTING STATEMENT For suspected adverse ctug reaction, seek medical attention immediately arw::t report to the FDA al www.fda.gov.ph AND Rite MED at (-+-632) 8-726-0835 or e-mail productsaf-ety@rilemed.com.ph. By reporting undesirable effects, you can help provide more information on the sarety of this mecicine. DATE OF AUTHORIZATION: October2014 DRP-3290-01 DATE OF REVISION; February 2023 CDS'°4 Manufactured by Sandoz Private Limited MIOC, Plot No. 8-AJ2, 8-8; T.T.C. Ind. Area, Kalwe Block, 400708 Navi Mumbai, India Imported by Sandoz Phlllpplnes Corporation 5th and 6th Floor Ayala North Exchange Tower 1 (HO), Ayala Ave. cor. Salcedo and Amorsolo Sts., Brgy. San Lorenzo, Ma Kati, Metro Manila Distributed by RlteMED Phlls., Inc. se Epifania Delos Santos Ave., Mandaluyong, Metro Manila RTM7013851N01 Reg.lPOPHIL xxxxxxxx [Page 3] PRINTED PACKAGING MATERIAL SPECIFICATIONS (SPC) FILE NAME VERSION PREPARED §i DRAWN BY RM SILDENAFIL_50 mg Tab_PI v4.4 Kurt Santos DIMENSIONS I COLORS USED MATERIAL NO. PHARMA CODE 120x520 mm I ■ PANTONE 287 C xxxxxxxx xxxx [Page 4]",1,3,PHARMACEUTICAL FORM Tablet,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T14:40:12.691232+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3290-02,Sildenadil (As Citrate),None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1f1XUtQARtiE4NS5CNs1Uz-9jnLbt18wQ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3290-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1f1XUtQARtiE4NS5CNs1Uz-9jnLbt18wQ_view_usp_drivesdk_5b3486c073.pdf,page_text/DRP-3290-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1f1XUtQARtiE4NS5CNs1Uz-9jnLbt18wQ_view_usp_drivesdk_5b3486c073.json,3c5945160021fa82d207d2b7e029860ae8a914ecf9b603dc1f265294bfb1fcd3,9621928,2,ocr,"Tablet Light blue round slightly dotted tablets, with cross breaking notch on one side and embossment “50” on the other side. The tablet can be divided into equal quarters. CLINICAL PARTICULARS Therapeutic indications Sildenafil is indicated in adult men with erectile dysfunction, which is the inability to achieve or maintain a penile erection sufficient for satisfactory sexual performance. In order for sildenafil to be effective, sexual stimulation is required. Dosage and administration Dosage Use in adults The recommended dose is 50 mg taken as needed approximately one hour before sexual activity. Based on efficacy and tolerability, the dose may be increased to 100 or decreased to 25 mg. The maximum recommended dose is 100 mg. The maximum recommended dosing frequency is once per day. If sildenafil is taken with food, the onset of activity may be delayed compared to the fasted state (see Pharmacokinetic properties). Special populations Elderly patients Dosage adjustments are not required in elderly patients. Patients with renal impairment The dosing recommendations described in “Use in adults” apply to patients with mild to moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance = 30-80 mL/min). Since sildenafil clearance is reduced in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance <30 mL/min), a 25 mg dose should be considered. Based on efficacy and tolerability, the dose may be increased stepwise to 50 mg, 75 mg, and 100 mg. Patients with hepatic impairment Since sildenafil clearance is reduced in patients with hepatic impairment (e.g. cirrhosis), a 25 mg dose should be considered. Based on efficacy and toleration, the dose may be increased to 50 mg, 75 mg, and 100 mg. Pediatric population Sildenafil is not indicated for individuals below 18 years of age. Use in patients using other medicines With the exception of ritonavir for which co-administration with sildenafil is not advised (see Special warnings and precautions for use), a starting dose of 25 mg should be considered in patients receiving concomitant treatment with CYP3A4 inhibitors (see Interactions with other medicinal products and other forms of interaction). In order to minimise the potential of developing postural, patients should be stabile on alpha-blocker therapy prior to initiating sildenafil treatment. In addition, initiation of sildenafil at a dose of 25 mg should be considered (see Special warnings and precautions for use and Interactions with other medicinal products and other forms of interaction). Method of administration For oral use",1,1,PHARMACEUTICAL FORM,"Sildenafil is indicated in adult men with erectile dysfunction, which is the inability to achieve or maintain a penile erection sufficient for satisfactory sexual performance. In order for sildenafil to be effective, sexual stimulation is required.",1,1,Therapeutic indications,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T14:40:49.729219+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3291,Clozapine,Ziproc-25,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1YSyUkGqtNPEDhLiv1adngIAl-X0p3Zz0/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3291_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1YSyUkGqtNPEDhLiv1adngIAl-X0p3Zz0_view_usp_drivesdk_f1d415df9b.pdf,page_text/DRP-3291_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1YSyUkGqtNPEDhLiv1adngIAl-X0p3Zz0_view_usp_drivesdk_f1d415df9b.json,e471ed9bedc87909fad6d775aadea9bae1c00249fb988a6ca1683503b8dacb0e,294457,1,digital_text,"Clozapine (Ziproc-25) 25 mg Tablet and Clozapine (Ziproc-100) 100 mg Tablet are light yellow coloured, round, binconvex, uncoated tablet with bisecting line on one side. CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY Clozapine is classified as an atypical antipsychotic agent, which differs from conventional antipsychotics. It has a weak dopamine receptor blocking activity at D2 receptors, but has a D1 anti-adrenergic, anticholinergic, antihistaminergic and arousal reaction inhibiting effects. It has also been shown to possess antiserotonergic property. Clozapine produces rapid and marked sedation and exerts strong antipsychotic effects. It is effective in patients exhibiting both positive and negative schizophrenic symptoms. PHARMACOKINETICS Although clozapine is well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, its bioavailability is limited to about 50% by fisrt-pass metabolism. Peak plasma concentrations are achieved an average of about 2.5 hours after oral administration. Clozapine is about 95% bound to plasma proteins and has mean terminal elimination half-life of about 12 hours at a steady state. It is almost completely metabolized and routes of metabolism include N-demethylation, hydroxylation, and N-oxidation, the desmethyl metabolite (norclozapine) has limited activity. The metabolism of clozapine is mediated mainly by the cytochrome P450 isoenzyme CYP1A2. Metabolites and trace amounts of unchanged drug are excreted mainly in the urine and also in the feces. There is wide interindividual variation in plasma concentrations of clozapine and no simple correlation has been found between plasma concentrations and therapeutic effect.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Used for the treatment of schizophrenia; reserved for patients who fail to respond to, or who experience severe extrapyramidal side-effects with classical antipsychotics. Used for reducing the risk of recurrent suicidal behavior in those with schizophrenia or schizoaffective disorder who is at risk of suicidal behavior. Also used in the management of treatment-resistant psychoses associated with Parkinson's disease.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:40:52.619813+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3295,Piperacillin (as Sodium) + Tazobactam (as Sodium),Perataz,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/12RG0FLxzcK7Dcq8rc7DIpUVqdmxi-C1i/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3295_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12RG0FLxzcK7Dcq8rc7DIpUVqdmxi-C1i_view_usp_drivesdk_310165aa57.pdf,page_text/DRP-3295_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12RG0FLxzcK7Dcq8rc7DIpUVqdmxi-C1i_view_usp_drivesdk_310165aa57.json,ec343ccdd02a95d1524801968746e5d86686ddd7e2551c758e506216cf83619f,120220,1,digital_text,,,,,"In adults and children over 12 years of age: Bacterial infections of the lungs and airways (chest or lower respiratory tract infections) Bacterial infections within the abdomen Bacterial infections of the urinary tract Bacterial infections of the skin Bacterial infections of the blood (septicemia or blood poisoning) Bacterial infections in people with low white blood cells counts (neutropenia) and hence decreased ability to fight infection (used in combination with an aminoglycosides antibiotic for th is purpose) In children under 12 years of age: Appendicitis complicated by a burst appendix, peritonitis and/or abscess formation in children aged 2 to 12 years. Bacterial infections in children with low white blood cell counts (neutropenia) and hence decreased ability to fight infection (used in combination with an aminoglycoside antibiotic forth is purpose). PHARMACODYNAMICS Piperacillin is an antibiotic that has the ability to kill a wide variety of bacteria. It works by interfering with the formation of bacterial cell walls. It does this by preventing the bacteria forming vital cross-links within their cell walls. These cross-links strengthen the cell walls and allow them to protect the bacteria from their environment. By interfering with the cross-linking meshworkin the cell walls, Piperacillin weakens them. The cell walls of bacteria are essential for their survival. They protect the bacteria from the environment and keep unwanted substances from entering the cells.As Piperacillin weakens the cell walls, it allows unwanted substances to enter the bacterial cells. This causes the cells to swell and eventually rupture, and kills the bacteria. Certain bacteria are resistant to penicillin-type antibiotics, because they have developed the ability to produce defensive chemicals. These chemicals are called beta­ lactamases. They interfere with the structure of penicillin-type antibiotics and stop them from working. Tazobactam is a type of medicine known as a beta-lactamase inhibitor. It is included in this medicine because it inhibits the action of the beta-lactamases produced by in defense by certain bacteria. This prevents the bacteria form inactivating the Piperacillin, and leaves them susceptible to attack. Tazobactam therefore increases the range of bacteria that Piperacillin can kill. PHARMACOKINETICS After the administration of single doses of Piperacillin/Tazobactam to subjects with renal impairment, the half-life of Piperacillin and of Tazobactam increases with decreasing creatinine clearance. At creatinine clearance below 20 mUmin, the increase in half-life is twofold for Piperacillin and fourfold forTazobactam compared to subjects with normal renal function. Piperacillin and Tazobactam are widely distributed into tissues and body fluids including intestinal mucosa, gallbladder, lung, female reproductive tissues (uterus, ovary, and fallopian tube), interstitial fluid, and bile. Mean tissue concentrations are generally 50% to 100% of those in plasma. Distribution of Piperacillin and Tazobactam into cerebrospinal fluid is low in subjects with non-inflamed meninges, as with other Penicillins. Piperacillin is metabolized to a minor microbiologically active desethyl metabolite. Tazobactam is metabolized to a single metabolite that lacks pharmacological and antibacterial activities. Both Piperacillin and Tazobactam are eliminated via the kidney by glomerular filtration and tubular secretion. Piperacillin is excreted rapidly as unchanged drug with 68% of the administered dose excreted in the urine. Tazobactam and its metabolite are eliminated primarily by renal excretion with 80% of the administered dose excreted as unchanged drug and the remainder as the single metabolite. Piperacillin, Tazobactam and desethyl Piperacillin are also secreted into the bile.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T14:40:56.382237+00:00
