PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3350,Hyoscine-N-Butylbromide,Aduscine,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/10B61ad9FbQ29KPMpwMopk90YSjMcYaOP/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3350_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10B61ad9FbQ29KPMpwMopk90YSjMcYaOP_view_usp_drivesdk_e21d941a4f.pdf,page_text/DRP-3350_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10B61ad9FbQ29KPMpwMopk90YSjMcYaOP_view_usp_drivesdk_e21d941a4f.json,17389d76063627464977b6be1bcf0237e7af3b849b443b69c1abd1b6cc936a2d,619483,2,ocr,diluted with Dextrose or with Sodium Chloride 0.9 % Injection Solutions. Hyoscine-N-Butylbromide (Aduscine) 20 mg/mL Solution,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"The anticholinergic effect of drugs such as tri- and tetracyclic HYOSCINE-N-BUTYLBROMIDE antidepressants, antihistamines, quinidine, amantadine, Used for symptomatic relief of gastro- intestinal or genito- urinary disorders characterized by smooth muscle spasm. antipsychotics (e.g. phenothiazines, butyrophenones), ADUSCINE disopyramide and other anticholinergics (e.g. tiotropium, 20 mg/ mL Solution for Injection (1LM./LV.) ipratropium, atropine-like compounds) may be intensified ANTICHOLINERGIC",1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T14:42:29.238790+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3357-02,Rifacare,Rifampicin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1dnmga-IjFw1hOLj8ekano1tb9fqt5jEE/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3357-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1dnmga-IjFw1hOLj8ekano1tb9fqt5jEE_view_usp_drivesdk_8bf10e9d7e.pdf,page_text/DRP-3357-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1dnmga-IjFw1hOLj8ekano1tb9fqt5jEE_view_usp_drivesdk_8bf10e9d7e.json,f5b54e93e93f70272e9567b7e40ffdec140cf97f7ec9ceaa6db61a29ec683209,178924,1,digital_text,"Red orange to brick red colored suspension with sweet strawberry taste containing 200 mg of Rifampicin in each 5 mL. DESCRIPTION A red-brown crystalline powder. Very slightly soluble in water; freely soluble in chloroform; soluble in ethyl acetate and in methyl alcohol. A 1% suspension in water has a pH of 4.5 to 6.5. Store at a temperature not exceeding 40° in airtight containers. Protect from light. ANTIMICROBIAL ACTION Rifampicin is bactericidal against a wide range of microorganisms & interferes with their synthesis of nucleic acids by inhibiting DNA-dependent RNA polymerase. It has the ability to kill intracellular organisms. It is active against mycobacteria, including Mycobacterium tuberculosis & M. leprae, also active against Gram-positive bacteria & less active against Gram-negative bacteria. PHARMACOKINETICS Rifampicin is readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and peak plasma concentrations varying from 4 to 32 micrograms/mL (average 7 micrograms/mL) have been reported after a dose of 600 mg. Food may reduce and delay absorption. Rifampicin is about 80% bound to plasma proteins. It is widely distributed in body tissues and fluids and diffusion into the CSF is increased when the meninges are inflamed. Rifampicin is distributed into breast milk and crosses the placenta. Half-lives for rifampicin have been reported to range initially from 2 to 5 hours, the longest elimination times occurring after the largest doses. However, as rifampicin induces its own metabolism, elimination time may decrease by up to 40% during the first 2 weeks, resulting in half-lives of about 2 to 3 hours. The half-life is prolonged in patients with severe hepatic impairment. Rifampicin is rapidly metabolised in the liver mainly to active 25-O-deacetylrifampicin and excreted in the bile. Deacetylation diminishes intestinal reabsorption and increases faecal excretion, although significant enterohepatic circulation still takes place. About 60% of a dose eventually appears in the faeces. The amount excreted in the urine increases with increasing doses and up to 30% of a dose may be excreted in the urine, about half of it being unchanged drug. The metabolite formylrifampicin is also excreted in the urine. In patients with renal impairment the half-life of rifampicin is not prolonged at doses of 600 mg or less.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,For the treatment of tuberculosis and other infections due to mycobacteria and other susceptible organisms.,1,1,INDICATION,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:42:32.500413+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3360,Domperidone,Dompenyl,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/11hTQKaOkyFaAtQbq8L3VJIFocFDpB0vT/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3360_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_11hTQKaOkyFaAtQbq8L3VJIFocFDpB0vT_view_usp_drivesdk_751cf64d30.pdf,page_text/DRP-3360_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_11hTQKaOkyFaAtQbq8L3VJIFocFDpB0vT_view_usp_drivesdk_751cf64d30.json,0531f53ad9dbdf4d0f5ae05726b59e50d067fed493359ba014ae1d5c43301614,1531526,1,digital_text,,,,,,,,,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-06-30T14:42:45.060282+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3360,Domperidone,Dompenyl,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ArzFOFYWJtAX6dAriico5kJRrDQq7sTZ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3360_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ArzFOFYWJtAX6dAriico5kJRrDQq7sTZ_view_usp_drivesdk_7cfdd5207e.pdf,page_text/DRP-3360_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ArzFOFYWJtAX6dAriico5kJRrDQq7sTZ_view_usp_drivesdk_7cfdd5207e.json,6a940e87ab526b5cef491323d286bd7e5d75ad323f083c892c878491fb5250b8,470581,1,digital_text,Pediatric population Domperidone BP ……….10 mg,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Urinary and faecal excretions amount to 31 and 66% of the oral dose respectively. The proportion of the drug excreted unchanged is small (10% of faecal excretion and approximately 1% of urinary excretion). The plasma half- life after a single oral dose is 7-9 hours in healthy subjects but is prolonged in patients with severe renal insufficiency. Domperidone is a dopamine antagonist with anti-emetic properties, Domperidone does not readily cross the blood-brain barrier. In Domperidone users, especially in adults, extrapyramidal side effects are very rare, but Domperidone promotes the release of prolactin from the pituitary. Its anti-emetic effect may be due to a combination of peripheral (gastrokinetic) effects and antagonism of dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier in the area postrema. Animal studies, together with the low concentrations found in the brain, indicate a predominantly peripheral effect of Domperidone on dopamine receptors. No pharmacokinetic data are available in the Pharmacokinetic properties. Clinical study in infants and children 12 years of age and younger Domperidone is indicated for the relief of the symptoms of nausea and vomiting. In subjects with moderate hepatic impairment (Pugh score 7 to 9, Child-Pugh rating B), the AUC and C of domperidone is 2.9- and 1.5- fold higher, respectively, than in healthy subjects. The unbound max fraction is increased by 25%, and the terminal elimination half-life is prolonged from 15 to 23 hours. Subjects with mild hepatic impairment have a somewhat lower systemic exposure than healthy subjects based on C and AUC, with no change in protein binding or terminal half-life. Subjects with severe hepatic impairment were not studied. Domperidone is contraindicated in patients with max moderate or severe hepatic impairment . In accordance with ICH-E14 guidelines, a thorough QT study was performed. This study included a placebo, an active comparator and a positive control and was conducted in healthy subjects with up to 80 mg per day 10 or 20 mg administered 4 times a day of Domperidone. This study found a maximal difference of QTc between Domperidone and placebo in LS-means in the change from baseline of 3.4 msec for 20 mg Domperidone administered 4 times a day on Day 4. The 2-sided 90% CI (1.0 to 5.9 msec) did not exceed 10 msec. No clinically relevant QTc effect were observed in this study when Domperidone was administered at up to 80 mg/day (i.e., more than twice the maximum recommended dosing). White, circular, flat beveled edge uncoated tablet having “medley” emblem debossed on one side and break line on the other side. Mechanism of action",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:42:48.351816+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3360-01,Domperidone,Domilium,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1mVZb-05d0o_6DmDi2NfkxDcJpdzEIOXQ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3360-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1mVZb-05d0o_6DmDi2NfkxDcJpdzEIOXQ_view_usp_drivesdk_72f93bcf4e.pdf,page_text/DRP-3360-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1mVZb-05d0o_6DmDi2NfkxDcJpdzEIOXQ_view_usp_drivesdk_72f93bcf4e.json,a346f243b35c6110b0e4a8773d3504076ccc1b9bb2eb7faeceb2bd3833744dfc,1488622,1,digital_text,"In accordance with ICH-E14 guidelines, a thorough QT study was performed. This study included a placebo, an active comparator and a positive control and was conducted in healthy subjects with up to 80 mg per day 10 or 20 mg administered 4 times a day of Domperidone. This study found a maximal difference of QTc between Domperidone and placebo in LS-means in the change from baseline of 3.4 msec for 20 mg Domperidone administered 4 times a day on Day 4. The 2-sided 90% CI (1.0 to 5.9 msec) did not exceed 10 msec. No clinically relevant QTc effect were observed in this study when Domperidone was administered at up to 80 mg/day (i.e., more than twice the maximum recommended dosing). A multicentre, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, prospective study was conducted to evaluate the safety and efficacy of Domperidone in 292 children with acute gastroenteritis aged 6 months to 12 years (median age 7 years). In addition to oral rehydration treatment (ORT), randomised subjects received Domperidone oral suspension at 0.25 mg/kg (up to a maximum of 30 mg Domperidone/day), or placebo, 3 times a day, for up to 7 days. This study did not achieve the primary objective, which was to demonstrate that Domperidone suspension plus ORT is more effective than placebo plus ORT at reducing vomiting episodes during the first 48 hours after the first treatment administration. Each uncoated tablet contains:",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Patients should try to take each dose at scheduled time. If a scheduled dose is missed, the missed dose should be omitted and the usual dosing schedule resumed. The dose should not be doubled to make up for a missed dose. Usually, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed one week. One 10mg tablet up to three times per day with maximum dose of 30 mg per day. Domperidone is contraindicated in moderate or severe hepatic impairment. Dose modification in mild hepatic impairment is however not needed. Pediatric population Domperidone should be used at the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration necessary to control nausea and vomiting. It is recommended to take oral Domperidone tablets before meals. If taken after meals, absorption of the drug is somewhat delayed. Since the elimination half-life of Domperidone is prolonged in severe renal impairment, on repeated administration, the dosing frequency of Domperidone tablets should be reduced to once or twice daily depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be reduced. Such patients on prolonged therapy should be reviewed regularly. Domperidone is indicated for the relief of the symptoms of nausea and vomiting. Hepatic Impairment Adults and adolescents (12 years of age and older and weighing 35 kg or more)",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:42:36.597969+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3361-02,Atorvastatin (as calcium),None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1pur5UCVOkBQ2wgLT3GOnExyOkcuZIV4d/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3361-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1pur5UCVOkBQ2wgLT3GOnExyOkcuZIV4d_view_usp_drivesdk_6911523160.pdf,page_text/DRP-3361-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1pur5UCVOkBQ2wgLT3GOnExyOkcuZIV4d_view_usp_drivesdk_6911523160.json,5eeba58390a8dfab6dec9745c271266e686f9155f2b121a83044dc62f283446d,279471,21,digital_text,"Atorvastatin (as calcium) tablet is a white, round film-coated tablet containing atorvastatin calcium. Atorvastatin (as calcium) is a synthetic lipid-lowering agent, which is an inhibitor of 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-coenzyme A (HMG-CoA) reductase. This enzyme catalyzes the conversion of HMG-CoA to mevalonate, an early and rate-limiting step in cholesterol biosynthesis. The empirical formula of atorvastatin (as calcium) is (C33H34FN2O5)2Ca3H2O and its molecular weight is 1209.42. Its structural formula is: Atorvastatin (as calcium) is a white to off-white crystalline powder, practically insoluble in aqueous solutions of pH 4 and below. It i s very slightly soluble in distilled water, pH 7.4 phosphate buffer, and acetonitrile; slightly soluble in ethanol; and freely soluble in methanol.",1,1,2.0 DESCRIPTION,"Atorvastatin is indicated as an adjunct to diet for the treatment of patients with elevated total cholesterol (total-C), low density lipoprotein, cholesterol (LDL-C), apolipoprotein B (apo B), and triglycerides (TG) and to increase high density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C)in patients with primary hypercholesterolemia (heterozygous familial and non-familial hypercholesterolemia), combined (mixed) hyperlipidemia (Fredrickson Types IIa and IIb), elevated serum TG levels (Fredrickson Type IV), and for patients with dysbetalipoproteinemia (Fredrickson Type III) who do not respond adequately to diet. Atorvastatin is also indicated for the reduction of total-C and LDL-C in patients with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. Prevention of Cardiovascular Complications In patients without clinically evident cardiovascular disease (CVD), and with or without dyslipidemia, but with multiple risk factors for coronary heart disease (CHD), such as smoking, hypertension, diabetes, low HDL-C, or a family history of early CHD, atorvastatin is indicated to: - Reduce the risk of fatal CHD and non-fatal myocardial infarction (MI) - Reduce the risk of stroke - Reduce the risk of revascularization procedures and angina pectoris In patients with clinically evident CHD, atorvastatin is indicated to - Reduce the risk of non-fatal MI, - Reduce the risk of fatal and non-fatal stroke, - Reduce the risk for revascularization procedures, - Reduce the risk of hospitalization for congestive heart failure (CHF), - Reduce the risk of angina. As adjunct to lifestyle changes, such as proper diet and exercise, intensive atorvastatin treatment has been shown to halt the progression of atherosclerosis (total atheroma or plaque volume) in patients with coronary artery disease and other individuals who are at high risk for cardiovascular disease. Chronic Kidney Disease In patients with diabetes with moderately decreased eGFR, Atorvastatin is indicated to reduce the risk for cardiovascular disease. In patients with clinically evident coronary heart disease and CKD not requiring dialysis, atorvastatin is indicated to reduce the risk of major cardiovascular events including stroke. In patients with clinically evident coronary heart disease and/or diabetes with microalbuminuria, atorvastatin is indicated to reduce the rate of GFR decline and progression of CKD. [Page 3] 3 Pediatric Patients (10-17 years of age) Atorvastatin is indicated as an adjunct to diet to reduce total-C, LDL-C, and apo B levels in boys and post-menarchal girls, 10 to 17 years of age, with heterozygous familial hyperchlolesterolemia if after an adequate trial of diet therapy, the following findings are present: LDL-C remains ≥190 mg/dL or LDL-C remains ≥160 mg/dL and;  There is a positive family history of premature CVD or  Two or more other CVD risk factors are present in the pediatric patient",2,3,4.1 Therapeutic Indications,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:42:51.818048+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3362-02,Atorvastatin (as calcium),None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/12QQkr1nFCSxMZwlXw8SQu7LdFEbEm8eS/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3362-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12QQkr1nFCSxMZwlXw8SQu7LdFEbEm8eS_view_usp_drivesdk_80e62931ba.pdf,page_text/DRP-3362-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12QQkr1nFCSxMZwlXw8SQu7LdFEbEm8eS_view_usp_drivesdk_80e62931ba.json,5eeba58390a8dfab6dec9745c271266e686f9155f2b121a83044dc62f283446d,279471,21,digital_text,"Atorvastatin (as calcium) tablet is a white, round film-coated tablet containing atorvastatin calcium. Atorvastatin (as calcium) is a synthetic lipid-lowering agent, which is an inhibitor of 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-coenzyme A (HMG-CoA) reductase. This enzyme catalyzes the conversion of HMG-CoA to mevalonate, an early and rate-limiting step in cholesterol biosynthesis. The empirical formula of atorvastatin (as calcium) is (C33H34FN2O5)2Ca3H2O and its molecular weight is 1209.42. Its structural formula is: Atorvastatin (as calcium) is a white to off-white crystalline powder, practically insoluble in aqueous solutions of pH 4 and below. It i s very slightly soluble in distilled water, pH 7.4 phosphate buffer, and acetonitrile; slightly soluble in ethanol; and freely soluble in methanol.",1,1,2.0 DESCRIPTION,"Atorvastatin is indicated as an adjunct to diet for the treatment of patients with elevated total cholesterol (total-C), low density lipoprotein, cholesterol (LDL-C), apolipoprotein B (apo B), and triglycerides (TG) and to increase high density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C)in patients with primary hypercholesterolemia (heterozygous familial and non-familial hypercholesterolemia), combined (mixed) hyperlipidemia (Fredrickson Types IIa and IIb), elevated serum TG levels (Fredrickson Type IV), and for patients with dysbetalipoproteinemia (Fredrickson Type III) who do not respond adequately to diet. Atorvastatin is also indicated for the reduction of total-C and LDL-C in patients with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. Prevention of Cardiovascular Complications In patients without clinically evident cardiovascular disease (CVD), and with or without dyslipidemia, but with multiple risk factors for coronary heart disease (CHD), such as smoking, hypertension, diabetes, low HDL-C, or a family history of early CHD, atorvastatin is indicated to: - Reduce the risk of fatal CHD and non-fatal myocardial infarction (MI) - Reduce the risk of stroke - Reduce the risk of revascularization procedures and angina pectoris In patients with clinically evident CHD, atorvastatin is indicated to - Reduce the risk of non-fatal MI, - Reduce the risk of fatal and non-fatal stroke, - Reduce the risk for revascularization procedures, - Reduce the risk of hospitalization for congestive heart failure (CHF), - Reduce the risk of angina. As adjunct to lifestyle changes, such as proper diet and exercise, intensive atorvastatin treatment has been shown to halt the progression of atherosclerosis (total atheroma or plaque volume) in patients with coronary artery disease and other individuals who are at high risk for cardiovascular disease. Chronic Kidney Disease In patients with diabetes with moderately decreased eGFR, Atorvastatin is indicated to reduce the risk for cardiovascular disease. In patients with clinically evident coronary heart disease and CKD not requiring dialysis, atorvastatin is indicated to reduce the risk of major cardiovascular events including stroke. In patients with clinically evident coronary heart disease and/or diabetes with microalbuminuria, atorvastatin is indicated to reduce the rate of GFR decline and progression of CKD. [Page 3] 3 Pediatric Patients (10-17 years of age) Atorvastatin is indicated as an adjunct to diet to reduce total-C, LDL-C, and apo B levels in boys and post-menarchal girls, 10 to 17 years of age, with heterozygous familial hyperchlolesterolemia if after an adequate trial of diet therapy, the following findings are present: LDL-C remains ≥190 mg/dL or LDL-C remains ≥160 mg/dL and;  There is a positive family history of premature CVD or  Two or more other CVD risk factors are present in the pediatric patient",2,3,4.1 Therapeutic Indications,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:42:55.354266+00:00
