PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3365,"""Rifampicin /
Ethambutol Hydrochloride/
Isoniazid""",Myrin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1z2YAdf3v5KEgMDejPqZ8cRpQ9W_9Lrpn/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3365_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1z2YAdf3v5KEgMDejPqZ8cRpQ9W_9Lrpn_view_usp_drivesdk_61f27421c4.pdf,page_text/DRP-3365_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1z2YAdf3v5KEgMDejPqZ8cRpQ9W_9Lrpn_view_usp_drivesdk_61f27421c4.json,32409e5ba19ae49bcfec30995139f83c1fa029b363e8711d8b3d0f797fe4f58c,269818,15,digital_text,"Ethambutol has a chemical name (S,S)-N,N′-Ethylenebis (2-aminobutan-1-ol) dihydrochloride. It is a white or almost white, hygroscopic, crystalline powder. Freely soluble in water; soluble in alcohol. A 2% solution in water has a pH of 3.7 to 4.0. Rifampicin has a chemical name 3-(4-Methylpiperazine-1-yliminomethyl) rifamycin SV; (12Z,14E,24E)-(2S,16S,17S,18R,19R,20R,21S,22R,23S)-1,2-Dihydro-5,6,9,17,19- pentahydroxy-23-methoxy-2,4,12,16,18,20,22-heptamethyl-8-(4-methylpiperazin-1- yliminomethyl)-1,11-dioxo-2,7-(epoxypentadeca[1,11,13]trienimino) naphtho[2,1-b] furan-21- yl acetate. It is a reddish-brown or brownish-red, crystalline powder. Slightly soluble in water, in alcohol, and in acetone; soluble in methyl alcohol. A 1% suspension has a pH of 4.5 to 6.5. Isoniazid has a chemical name Isonicotinohydrazide. It is a white or almost white, crystalline powder or colorless crystals. Freely soluble in water; sparingly soluble in alcohol. A 5% solution in water has a pH of 6.0 to 8.0.",1,1,2.0 DESCRIPTION,"Ethambutol HCl + Rifampicin + Isoniazid (Myrin) is indicated for the continuation/ maintenance phase treatment of new and re-treatment cases of pulmonary and extrapulmonary tuberculosis caused by susceptible strains of mycobacteria. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), in populations with known or suspected high levels of isoniazid resistance, giving Ethambutol HCl + Rifampicin + Isoniazid (Myrin) in the continuation phase of treatment of new TB patients is an acceptable alternative to Rifampicin+ Isoniazid. The recommended duration of treatment is 4 months. Ethambutol HCl + Rifampicin + Isoniazid Viewed on: Saturday, June 8, 2024 - 9:47:44 PM Eastern Time [Page 2] For previously treated patients, (returning after defaulting/relapsing from their first treatment), Ethambutol HCl + Rifampicin + Isoniazid (Myrin) may be used during the maintenance or continuation phase of therapy. The recommended duration of treatment for this phase is 5 months. * Regimen should be adjusted appropriately as soon as Drug Susceptibility Testing (DST) results become available.",1,2,4.1. Therapeutic Indications,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:43:04.994317+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3367,Azithromycin (as dihydrate),Zithromax,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Qi3hQKCbT8kvkQCvOMdySSnbbYmY0VXJ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3367_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Qi3hQKCbT8kvkQCvOMdySSnbbYmY0VXJ_view_usp_drivesdk_12c8679c55.pdf,page_text/DRP-3367_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1Qi3hQKCbT8kvkQCvOMdySSnbbYmY0VXJ_view_usp_drivesdk_12c8679c55.json,550654da7c46b0812208d92c1dcb58c50a745d340819ad4fed9824371385d71a,344391,21,digital_text,"Azithromycin has the chemical name (2R,3S,4R,5R,8R,10R,11R,12S,13S,14R)- 13-[(2,6-dideoxy-3-C-methyl-3-O -methyl-L-ribo- hexopyranosyl) oxy]-2-ethyl-3,4,10-trihydroxy -3,5,6,8,10,12,14-hepta- methyl-11-[[3,4,6-trideoxy-3-(dimethylamino)--D-xylo-hexopyranosyl] oxy]-1-oxa- 6-azacyclopentadecan-15-one. Azithromycin is derived from erythromycin; however, it differs chemically from erythromycin in that a methyl-substituted nitrogen atom is incorporated into the lactone ring. Its molecular formula is C38H72N2O12, and its molecular weight is 749.00. Azithromycin has the following structural formula: Azithromycin, as the dihydrate, is a white crystalline powder with a molecular formula of C38H72N2O12·2H2O and a molecular weight of 785.0. O N CH 3 OH O H 3 C HO H 3 C H 3 C HO O CH 3 O CH 3 CH 3 O O CH 3 C H3 N H 3 C CH 3 CH 3 OH O CH 3 HO CH 3 AZITHROMYCIN Viewed on: Tuesday, June 11, 2024 - 2:52:47 AM Eastern Time [Page 2]",1,1,2. DESCRIPTION,"Azithromycin is indicated for infections caused by susceptible organisms; in lower respiratory tract infections including bronchitis and pneumonia, in skin and soft tissue infections, in acute otitis media and in upper respiratory tract infections including sinusitis and pharyngitis/tonsillitis. (Penicillin is the usual drug of choice in the treatment of Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis, including the prophylaxis of rheumatic fever. Azithromycin is generally effective in the eradication of streptococci from the oropharynx; however, data establishing the efficacy of azithromycin and the subsequent prevention of rheumatic fever are not available at present.) In sexually transmitted diseases in men and women, azithromycin is indicated for the treatment of uncomplicated genital infections due to Chlamydia trachomatis. It is also indicated for the treatment of chancroid due to Hemophilus ducreyi and uncomplicated genital infections due to non-multiresistant Neisseria gonorrhoeae; concurrent infection with Treponema pallidum should be excluded. Azithromycin is indicated, either alone or in combination with rifabutin, for prophylaxis against Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare complex (MAC) infection, an opportunistic infection prevalent in patients with advanced human immunodeficiency virus (HIV). Azithromycin is indicated in combination with ethambutol for the treatment of disseminated MAC (DMAC) infection in patients with advanced HIV infection. Azithromycin IV is indicated for the treatment of community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) caused by susceptible organisms, including Legionella pneumophila, in patients who require initial IV therapy. Azithromycin IV is indicated for the treatment of pelvic inflammatory diseases (PID) caused by susceptible organisms (Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoea, Mycoplasma hominis) in patients who require initial IV therapy. Viewed on: Tuesday, June 11, 2024 - 2:52:47 AM Eastern Time [Page 3]",2,2,4.1 Therapeutic Indications,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T14:43:08.467298+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-3367,Azithromycin (as dihydrate),Zithromax,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1SEAvkL2EFH5GM3wO3RkfSG7slgXbWwZW/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-3367_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1SEAvkL2EFH5GM3wO3RkfSG7slgXbWwZW_view_usp_drivesdk_cf4ef251fa.pdf,page_text/DRP-3367_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1SEAvkL2EFH5GM3wO3RkfSG7slgXbWwZW_view_usp_drivesdk_cf4ef251fa.json,6f2497e74a5b5a433090e9ab25762827f02f21babaf1aa5672fbbbe81a043def,173202,6,digital_text,"This medicine contains azithromycin, which is one of a group of antibiotics called macrolides. It is used to treat infections caused by certain bacteria and other micro-organisms. 3.0 WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE? Powder for Oral Suspension: Azithromycin powder for oral suspension is presented as a white to off-white dry powder which yields, on reconstitution with water, off-white to orange to brown suspension containing the equivalent of 200 mg azithromycin per 5 mL. The powder for oral suspension is packed in 15 mL and 22.5 mL in high density polyethylene bottles. Film-coated Tablets 250 mg: Azithromycin film-coated tablets are capsular shaped and contain azithromycin dihydrate equivalent to 250 mg of azithromycin and engraved with “Pfizer” on one side and “ZTM 250”on the other side, blister pack (box of 6’s). Film-coated Tablets 500 mg: Azithromycin film-coated tablets are capsular shaped and contain azithromycin dihydrate equivalent to 500 mg of azithromycin and engraved with “Pfizer” on one side and “ZTM 500” on the other side, blister pack (box of 3’s). 4.0 STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE Powder for Oral Suspension:When reconstituted as directed, each teaspoonful (5 mL) contains azithromycin dihydrate equivalent to 200 mg of azithromycin. Film coated Tablet 250 mg:Each tablet contains azithromycin dihydrate equivalent to 250 mg azithromycin. Film coated Tablet 500 mg:Each tablet contains azithromycin dihydrate equivalent to 500 mg azithromycin. 5.0 WHAT IS THE MEDICINE USED FOR? This medicine contains azithromycin, which is one of a group of antibiotics called macrolides. It is used to treat infections caused by certain bacteria and other micro-organisms which include: • chest, throat or nasal infections (such as bronchitis, pneumonia, tonsillitis (pharyngitis and sinusitis) • ear infections • skin and soft tissue infections (such as an abscess or boil) • sexually-transmitted diseases caused by organisms called Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoea. AZITHROMYCIN Viewed on: Tuesday, June 11, 2024 - 2:55:43 AM Eastern Time [Page 2]  prophylaxis against Mycobacterium avium intracellulare complex (MAC) infection, either alone or in combination with rifabutin.  disseminated MAC (DMAC) infection in patients with advanced HIV infection either alone or in combination with ethambutol. You must talk to a doctor if you do not feel better or if you feel worse. 6.0 WHAT DOES THIS MEDICINE DO TO THE BODY? Azithromycin is the first of a subclass of macrolide antibiotics, known as azalides. Antibiotics are used to treat infections caused by bacteria. They work by killing or stopping the growth of the bacteria causing the infection. 7.0 HOW MUCH AND HOW OFTEN SHOULD YOU USE THIS MEDICINE? This medicine will be given to you by your doctor or healthcare professional. In Adults: For sexually transmitted diseases (caused by Chlamydia trachomatis, Haemophilus ducreyi or susceptible Neisseria gonorrhoeae), the dose is 1 g daily taken as a single oral dose. If you are allergic to penicillin and/or cephalosporins (drugs which treat bacterial infection), please consult local treatment guidelines. For prevention of against MAC infections in patients infected with the HIV, the dose is 1200 mg once per week. For the treatment of DMAC infections in patients with advanced HIV infection, the recommended dose is 600 mg once a day For all other infections for which Azithromycin (ZITHROMAX) Powder for Oral Suspension/Film- Coated Tablet is used, the usual dose in adults is 500 mg daily for 3 days. The length of treatment may be as long as 5-7 days, depending on the disease and how your doctor prescribes the medicine. In Children: Film-Coated Tablet - Refer to adult dosage. Powder for Oral Suspension - The maximum recommended total dose for any treatment is 1500 mg for children. In general, the total dose in children is 30 mg/kg. The total dose of 30 mg/kg is given as a single daily dose of 10 mg/kg daily for 3 days, or given over 5 days with a single daily dose of 10 mg/kg on Day 1, then 5 mg/kg on Days 2-5. For pharyngitis: Azithromycin (ZITHROMAX) Powder for Oral Suspension is given as a single dose of 10 mg/kg or 20 mg/kg for 3 days. For otitis media (ear infections): Azithromycin (ZITHROMAX) can be given as a single dose of 30 mg/kg. If your child weighs less than 15 kg, you should measure the dose as closely as possible. For children weighing 15 kg or more, Azithromycin (ZITHROMAX) Powder for Oral Suspension should be administered using an appropriate measuring device according to the guide provided below: Viewed on: Tuesday, June 11, 2024 - 2:55:43 AM Eastern Time [Page 3] AZITHROMYCIN SUSPENSION 30 mg/kg Total Treatment Dose Weight (kg) 3-Day Regimen 5-Day Regimen <15 10 mg/kg once daily on Days 1-3. 10 mg/kg on Day 1, then 5 mg/kg once daily on Days 2-5. 15-25 200 mg (5 mL) once daily on Days 1-3. 200 mg (5 mL) on Day 1, then 100 mg (2.5 mL) once daily on Days 2-5. 26-35 300 mg (7.5 mL) once daily on Days 1-3. 300 mg (7.5 mL) on Day 1, then 150 mg (3.75 mL) once daily on Days 2-5. 36-45 400 mg (10 mL) once daily on Days 1-3. 400 mg (10 mL) on Day 1, then 200 mg (5 mL) once daily on Days 2-5. >45 Dose as per adults. Dose as per adults. You should tell your doctor if you/your child has kidney or liver problems as your doctor may need to alter the normal dose. 8.0 WHEN SHOULD YOU NOT TAKE THIS MEDICINE? If you/your child are allergic to azithromycin or any other macrolide antibiotic such as erythromycin or macrolide or ketolide antibiotic clarithromycin or any of the excipients. An allergic reaction may cause skin rash or wheezing. 9.0 CARE THAT SHOULD BE TAKEN WHEN TAKING THIS MEDICINE? Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before taking Azithromycin (Zithromax) if you/your child have or have had any of the following:  kidney problems  heart conditions  diabetes  liver problems: your doctor may need to monitor your liver function or stop the treatment  myasthenia gravis (a condition that causes certain muscles to become weak)  or if you are taking any ergot derivatives such as ergotamine (used to treat migraine) as these medicines should not be taken together with Azithromycin (Zithromax). Tell your doctor immediately if you feel your heart beating in your chest or have an abnormal heartbeat, or get dizzy or faint or suffer from any muscle weakness when taking Azithromycin (Zithromax). Tell your doctor immediately if you experience any secondary infection including fungi infection. If you develop diarrhea or loose stools during or after treatment, tell your doctor at once. Do not take any medicine to treat your diarrhea without first checking with your doctor. If your diarrhea continues, please inform your doctor. 10.0 UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS Like all medicines, this medicine can cause side effects although not everybody gets them. Tell your doctor immediately if you experience any of the following symptoms after taking this medicine as the symptoms can be severe. Viewed on: Tuesday, June 11, 2024 - 2:55:43 AM Eastern Time [Page 4] The following side effects have been reported in clinical trials: - low levels of neutrophils, a type of white blood cells - hearing loss, deafness and ringing or buzzing in the ears - nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, loose stools, abdominal discomfort (pain/cramps), and flatulence - abnormal liver function - rash, swelling of the skin and mucous membranes - pain and inflammation at the side of infusion. Side effects reported during post-marketing experience: - yeast infection of the mouth and throat, infection of the vagina - low platelet count - serious allergic reactions - eating disorder (anorexia) - aggressive reaction, nervousness, agitation, and anxiety - dizziness, convulsions, headache, hyperactivity, reduced or abnormal sense of touch, drowsiness, and loss of consciousness - deafness, impaired hearing or ringing in the ears, vertigo (spinning sensation) - rapid and abnormal heartbeat - low blood pressure - vomiting/diarrhea, indigestion, constipation, inflammation of the colon and pancreas, tongue discoloration - inflammation of the liver and yellowish discoloration of the skin and eyes (jaundice), liver failure - itching, rash, light sensitivity, swelling (edema), hives, swelling of skin and mucous membranes - joint pains - inflammation of the kidneys and kidney failure - weakness, fatigue, discomfort 11.0 WHAT OTHER MEDICINE OR FOOD SHOULD BE AVOIDED WHILST TAKING THIS MEDICINE? Tell your doctor or pharmacist if you/your child are taking, have recently taken or might take any other medicines. In particular, Azithromycin (Zithromax) may interact with the medicines listed below:  ergot  warfarin or any similar medicine to prevent blood clots  cyclosporin (used to suppress the immune system to prevent and treat rejection of a transplanted organ or bone marrow)  antacids (for indigestion)  digoxin (used to treat heart failure)  colchicine (used for gout and familial Mediterranean fever)  terfenadine (for hay fever or a skin allergy)  zidovudine (used to treat HIV infections)  statins (cholesterol lowering medicines)  antidepressants (medicines used to treat depression) (antidepressants);  antipsychotic medicines (used to treat mental health problems);  fluoroquinolone antibiotics (to treat bacterial infections). Azithromycin film-coated tablet and powder for oral suspension can be taken with or without food. Viewed on: Tuesday, June 11, 2024 - 2:55:43 AM Eastern Time [Page 5] Pregnancy, breast-feeding and fertility If you are pregnant or breast-feeding, think you may be pregnant or are planning to have a baby, ask your doctor or pharmacist for advice before taking this medicine. Driving and using machines Azithromycin (Zithromax) is not expected to affect your ability to drive or use machines. Azithromycin (Zithromax) suspension contains sucrose, a type of sugar (3.87 g in 5 mL). If you have been told by your doctor that you have an intolerance to some sugars contact your doctor before taking this medicine. If you are diabetic, you may need to take this into account in your diet. May be harmful to the teeth. 12.0 WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU MISS A DOSE? If you forget to take Azithromycin (Zithromax) take it as soon as you can. Take your next dose at the right time. Do not take a double dose to make up for a forgotten dose. 13.0 SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS OF OVERDOSAGE Overdose treatment is symptomatic and supportive. 14.0 WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU HAVE TAKEN MORE THAN THE RECOMMENDED DOSAGE? If you/your child take too much Azithromycin (Zithromax) they may feel unwell. Tell your doctor or contact your nearest hospital casualty department immediately. Take any remaining medicine with you. 15.0 HOW SHOULD YOU KEEP THIS MEDICINE? Powder for Oral Suspension: Store at temperatures not exceeding 25°C for the powder and the reconstituted solution. Film-coated Tablets: Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. 16.0 WHEN SHOULD YOU CONSULT YOUR DOCTOR? Tell your doctor if you experience any of these: • If you observe any fungi infection during the course of the treatment. • If you experience diarrhea which may be related to Clostridium difficile which is known as Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea (CDAD) • If you experience the worsening of existing weakness and rapid fatigue of muscles which are under your control 17.0 FDA REGISTRATION NUMBER 200 mg/5 mL Powder for Oral Suspension: DRP-3367 250 mg Film-coated Tablet: DRP-8703 500 mg Film-coated Tablet: DRP-8702 Viewed on: Tuesday, June 11, 2024 - 2:55:43 AM Eastern Time [Page 6] 18.0 DATE OF FIRST AUTHORIZATION/RENEWAL OF AUTHORIZATION 200 mg/5 mL Powder for Oral Suspension: 01 Sep 1993 250 mg Film-coated Tablet: 07 Feb 2020 500 mg Film-coated Tablet: 07 Feb 2020 Keep out of reach of children. For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. CAUTION: Foods, Drugs, Devices and Cosmetics Act prohibits dispensing without prescription. Manufactured by: Powder for Oral Suspension: Haupt Pharma Latina, S.R.L Strada Statale 156, Km 47,600 04100 Borgo San Michele Latina, Italy 250 mg and 500 mg Tablets: Pfizer Pharmaceuticals LLC Km 1.9 Road 689, Vega Baja, Puerto Rico 00693, USA Packed by: 250 mg and 500 mg Tablets: Pfizer Manufacturing Deutschland GmbH Betriebsstätte Freiburg, Mooswaldallee 1 79090 Freiburg, Germany Marketing Authorization Holder: Pfizer, Inc. 19F – 20F, 8 Rockwell Building, Hidalgo Drive, Rockwell Center, Poblacion, Makati City, 1210 Metro Manila, Philippines Under Authority of PFIZER INC., New York, NY, USA Revision No.: PIL revision number 0.1 Revision Date: 09 Aug 2022 Reference: Zithromax LPD revision no: 12.3 Reference date: 09 Aug 2022 Viewed on: Tuesday, June 11, 2024 - 2:55:43 AM Eastern Time [Page 7]",1,6,2.0 DESCRIPTION OF PRODUCT,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T14:43:11.359507+00:00
