PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4135-03,Co-Amoxiclav,Comoxitab,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZRn6jsGaShUjgiHU-ueh_glnAO1NyGrk/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4135-03_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ZRn6jsGaShUjgiHU-ueh_glnAO1NyGrk_view_usp_drivesdk_d880dc21a6.pdf,page_text/DRP-4135-03_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1ZRn6jsGaShUjgiHU-ueh_glnAO1NyGrk_view_usp_drivesdk_d880dc21a6.json,a01b06f6a4e4af4053ee2c847f7f2df6776c78c237d2e30e5b55a2372abc00a5,424279,1,digital_text,,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Amoxicillin with Clavulanic acid is generally well tolerated. The majority of side effects observed in clinical trials were of a mild transient nature. The most frequently reported adverse effects were diarrhoea/loose stools, nausea, skin rashes, urticaria, vomiting and vaginitis (1%). The overall incidence of side effects and in particular diarrhoea, increased with the higher recommended dose. Other less frequently reported reactions include abdominal discomfort, flatulence and headache. Erythema multiforme, Stevens Johnson Syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T15:04:37.519307+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4145-01,Betahistine Dihydrochloride,Vertasanz,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xqJQsYugwslfbQ8p6fvAbIoV6eIPtud1/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4145-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1xqJQsYugwslfbQ8p6fvAbIoV6eIPtud1_view_usp_drivesdk_9435773afa.pdf,page_text/DRP-4145-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1xqJQsYugwslfbQ8p6fvAbIoV6eIPtud1_view_usp_drivesdk_9435773afa.json,3a6cefdf52bd36668384de3b42d96c8c4f0d3ca48c25c35a96058b559feb59cc,1438500,1,digital_text,"Light yellow colored, round, flat, scored on one side uncoated tablets having beveled edges. The surface of the tablets may have impregnation",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Betahistine Dihydrochloride is used to reduce the symptoms of vertigo, tinnitus, and hearing loss associated with Ménière’s disease. Distributions Absorption Betahistine is completely absorbed after oral administration, and for fasting subjects, peak plasma concentrations of 14C-labelled betahistine are attained approximately one hour after oral administration. After oral administration of Betahistine, its levels in plasma are very low. Therefore, the pharmacokinetic assessment of this drug is based on plasma concentration data of the only known metabolite, 2-pyridylacetic acid. Little or binding occurs with human plasma proteins. Metabolism and Elimination",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:04:44.927797+00:00
