PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4454-01,Colchicine,None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1A2Su9JzAnX8cFH6S_BYIgQGUfuYHpWyb/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4454-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1A2Su9JzAnX8cFH6S_BYIgQGUfuYHpWyb_view_usp_drivesdk_686133e9d5.pdf,page_text/DRP-4454-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1A2Su9JzAnX8cFH6S_BYIgQGUfuYHpWyb_view_usp_drivesdk_686133e9d5.json,673f44aafd1358f8073b4c2e3bb9b8acac416005f09c71119f38c5a6ea957932,438819,1,digital_text,Colchicine is readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and peak concentrations occur in plasma by half an hour to two hours.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Adults only: The leukocytes, kidney, liver and the spleen also contain high concentrations of Colchicine. Most of the drug is excreted in the faeces but 10 to 20% is excreted in the urine and this proportion rises in patients with liver disorder. Colchicine is distributed into breast milk. Gout: 1 mg initially, followed by 500 micrograms every two to three hours until relief of pain is obtained or vomiting or diarrhea occurs. A total dose of 6 mg should not be exceeded. The course should not be repeated within three days. Or as prescribed by the physician.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:31:56.480276+00:00
