PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4473,Epirubicin (as hydrochloride),Epichlor-50,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/18RpB__o7mRWCXkOAycnJVnwXHfIcaisK/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4473_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18RpB__o7mRWCXkOAycnJVnwXHfIcaisK_view_usp_drivesdk_7ce4b08ebd.pdf,page_text/DRP-4473_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18RpB__o7mRWCXkOAycnJVnwXHfIcaisK_view_usp_drivesdk_7ce4b08ebd.json,2e63a248ec8846d46cedc236f2e02a925140510b55f914d2f25101486d35ecd3,517225,1,digital_text,,,,,"Breast cancer: Epirubicin HCI injection is indicated as a component of adjuvant therapy in patients with evidence of axillary node tumor involvement following resection of primary breast cancer. It is also used alone or with other anitineoplastic in acute leukemias, lymphomas, multiple myeloma. Epirubicin is given by intravesical instillation for the treatment and prophylaxis of certain form of superficial bladder cancer. E230089 Epirubicin a Mean Pharmacokinetic Parameters in Patients with solid tumors b c d e f g Epirubicin Dose C AUC t Cl Vmax 1/2 ss 2 (mg/m ) (mcg/mL) (mcg.hr/mL) (hours) (L/hour) (L/kg) 60 5.7 ± 1.6 1.6 ± 0.2 35.3 ± 9 65 ± 8 21 ± 2 75 5.3 ± 1.5 1.7± 0.3 32.1 ± 5 83 ± 14 27 ± 11 120 9.0 ± 3.5 3.4 ± 0.7 33.7 ± 4 65 ± 13 23 ± 7 150 9.3 ± 2.9 4.2 ± 0.8 31.1 ± 6 69 ± 13 21 ± 7 Khandelwal Laboratories Pvt. Ltd. Manufactured by: Plot B-1, Wagle Industrial Estate, Thane-400 604, India Imported & Distributed by: 6/F RFM Corporate Center, Pioneer St. Mandaluyong, Metro Manila MedEthix I N C O R P TO R A E EPIRUBICIN HYDROCHLORIDE ® [Page 2]",1,1,Indications:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T15:33:25.897338+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4474,Ceftriaxone (as sodium),Cetrilife,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1F22Y_4-huJESifLrda5tcd39s-X1-tbb/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4474_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1F22Y_4-huJESifLrda5tcd39s-X1-tbb_view_usp_drivesdk_29f4317723.pdf,page_text/DRP-4474_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1F22Y_4-huJESifLrda5tcd39s-X1-tbb_view_usp_drivesdk_29f4317723.json,7f23c3f0fb2c04275b67f53171500bc0777c70a992fc19fe00a73bd0417858c3,524221,1,digital_text,White to yellowish powder in clear glass sealed vials.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Used for the treatment of severe infections (e.g., sepsis, meningitis) due to susceptible bacteria and for the treatment of penicillin-resistant N. gonorrhea infection and multiple-drug resistant Salmonella typhi.",1,1,INDICATION :S,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:33:56.103999+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4474-01,Ceftriaxone (as sodium),New Ambiceph,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/12xQwrZdkuFRU5TF8Uq2pTLiuSesUUG04/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4474-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12xQwrZdkuFRU5TF8Uq2pTLiuSesUUG04_view_usp_drivesdk_366f0770ac.pdf,page_text/DRP-4474-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12xQwrZdkuFRU5TF8Uq2pTLiuSesUUG04_view_usp_drivesdk_366f0770ac.json,48971f90b1a2606252621a2ab0b50d6f5b23c76a4ca0bd1edfdaf5f2d3369ec0,1845833,1,digital_text,"White powder in Clear Glass Sealed Vials. Mechanism of action Ceftriaxone inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis following attachment to penicillin binding proteins (PBPs). This results in the interruption of cell wall (peptidoglycan) biosynthesis, which leads to bacterial cell lysis and death. Resistance Bacterial resistance to ceftriaxone may be due to one or more of the following mechanisms: -reduced affinity of penicillin-binding proteins for ceftriaxone. -outer membrane impermeability in Gram-negative organisms. -bacterial efflux pumps. Susceptibility testing Breakpoints Minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) breakpoints established by the European Committee on Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing (EUCAST) are as follows: c. Isolates with a ceftriaxone MIC above the susceptible breakpoint are rare and, if found, should be re- tested and, if confirmed, should be sent to a reference laboratory. COMMONLY SUSCEPTIBLE SPECIES Clinical efficacy against specific pathogens Gram-positive aerobes, Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible)£, Staphylococci coagulase- negative (methicillin-susceptible)£, Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A), Streptococcus agalactiae (Group B), Streptococcus pneumonia, Viridans Group Streptococci, Gram-negative aerobes, Borrelia burgdorferi, Haemophilus influenza, Haemophilus parainfluenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, Neisseria gonorrhoea, Neisseria meningitides, Proteus mirabilis, Providencia spp, Treponema pallidum. INHERENTLY RESISTANT ORGANISMS The prevalence of acquired resistance may vary geographically and with time for selected species and local information on resistance is desirable, particularly when treating severe infections. As necessary, expert advice should be sought when the local prevalence of resistance is such that the utility of ceftriaxone in at least some types of infections is questionable. d. Breakpoints apply to a daily intravenous dose of 1 g x 1 and a high dose of at least 2 g x 1. a. Susceptibility inferred from cefoxitin susceptibility. Chlamydia spp., Chlamydophila spp., Mycoplasma spp., Legionella spp., Ureaplasma urealyticum Gram-positive aerobes, Enterococcus spp., Listeria monocytogenes, Gram-negative aerobes, Acinetobacter baumannii, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, Anaerobes, Clostridium difficile Gram-positive aerobes, Staphylococcus epidermidis+, Staphylococcus haemolyticus+, Staphylococcus hominis+, Gram-negative aerobes, Citrobacter freundii, Enterobacter aerogenes, Enterobacter cloacae, Escherichia coli%, Klebsiella pneumoniae%, Klebsiella oxytoca%, Morganella morganii, Proteus vulgaris, Serratia marcescens, Anaerobes, Bacteroides spp., Fusobacterium spp., Peptostreptococcus spp., Clostridium perfringens b. Susceptibility inferred from penicillin susceptibility. SPECIES FOR WHICH ACQUIRED RESISTANCE MAY BE A PROBLEM Others: Pathogen Dilution Test (MIC, mg/L) Susceptible Resistant Enterobacteriaceae ≤1 > 2 Staphylococcus spp a. a. Streptococcus spp. (Groups A, B, C and G) b. b. Streptococcus pneumoniae ≤0.5c. > 2 Viridans group Streptococci ≤0.5 >0.5 Haemophilus influenzae ≤0.12 c. > 0.12 Moraxella catarrhalis ≤1 > 2 Neisseria gonorrhoeae ≤0.12 > 0.12 Neisseria meningitidis ≤0.12 c. > 0.12 Non-species related ≤1d. > 2 Hypersensitivity to the active substance, to any other cephalosporin or to any of the excipients. History of severe hypersensitivity (e.g. anaphylactic reaction) to any other type of beta-lactam Antibacterial agent (penicillins, monobactams and carbapenems). Ceftriaxone is highly protein-bound and is able to displace bilirium from albumin binding sites, causing hyperbilirubinemia; its use should be avoided in jaundiced neonates. Neutropenia has been reported with most cephalosporins; a complex mechanism has been attributed to that associated with Ceftriaxone. There have been rare reports of fatal haemolysis associated with Ceftriaxone. Ceftriaxone is contraindicated in: Premature neonates up to a postmenstrual age of 41 weeks (gestational age + chronological age)* - with hyperbilirubinaemia, jaundice, or who are hypoalbuminaemic or acidotic because these are conditions in which bilirubin binding is likely to be impaired*",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"The bactericidal activity of Ceftriaxone results from the inhibition of cell wall synthesis. Ceftriaxone demonstrates a high degree of stability in the presence of beta-lactamases both penicillinases and cephalosporinase of gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria. Ceftriaxone is usually active against following micro-organisms in-vitro. Used for the treatment of severe infections (e.g., sepsis, meningitis) due to susceptible bacteria and for the treatment of penicillin resistant gonorrhea infection and multiple-drug resistant Salmonella typhi. Bacteriodes fragilis, Clostridium spp., Peptostreptococcus spp.",1,1,INDICATION :,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:33:29.782383+00:00
