PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4494,Ranitidine (As Hydrochloride),Alcera,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_RYUnRW-YSca-pXJSyJeDUW4gaxdqxBY/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4494_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1_RYUnRW-YSca-pXJSyJeDUW4gaxdqxBY_view_usp_drivesdk_5a1fd53ac9.pdf,page_text/DRP-4494_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1_RYUnRW-YSca-pXJSyJeDUW4gaxdqxBY_view_usp_drivesdk_5a1fd53ac9.json,95d04a6c0c700c057d748415825c50e0214873077b9de104588e6cae97a87dd7,2775527,1,ocr,"Ranitidine (as hydrochloride) 150 mg Tabletis pale red, mottled round, biconvex, film-coated tablet Ranitidine (as hydrochloride) 300 mg Tablet is orange-red to pale red, oval, biconvex, film-coated tablet",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Ranitidine is a histamine H,-receptor antagonist. It inhibits gastric acid secretion and reduces pepsin output in conditions such as duodenal and gastric ulcers, gastro- esophageal reflux disease, selected cases of persistent dyspepsia and pathological hypersecretory states such as Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. Ranitidine may be used for the prophylaxis of gastrointestinal hemorrhage as a consequence of stress ulceration and in patients at risk of acid aspiration during general anesthesia or child birth. Itis also used to reduce malabsorption and fluid loss in patients with short bowel syndrome and to reduce the degradation of enzyme supplements given to patients with pancreatic insufficiency.",1,1,INDICATIONS,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T15:35:08.297269+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4501-01,Levocetirizine Dihydrochloride,Veltir,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/12OP5YrfksUh1bBTbzbsxjI5y8kr59lJ8/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4501-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12OP5YrfksUh1bBTbzbsxjI5y8kr59lJ8_view_usp_drivesdk_7982dd6afa.pdf,page_text/DRP-4501-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12OP5YrfksUh1bBTbzbsxjI5y8kr59lJ8_view_usp_drivesdk_7982dd6afa.json,68257807d2802227334ac30b6ccef6acb483df6bd91468e2b030eab4796343bb,454108,1,digital_text,"White to off white coloured, round shaped, biconvex plain on both sides film-coated tablet. PHARMACOLOGY Mechanism of Action Levocetirizine, the active enantiomer of cetirizine, is an antihistamine; its principal effects are mediated via selective inhibition of H1 receptors. The antihistaminic activity of Levocetirizine has been documented in a variety of animal and human models. In vitro binding studies revealed that Levocetirizine has an affinity for the human H -receptor 2-fold higher than that of cetirizine (Ki = 3 nmol/L vs. 6 nmol/L, respectively). The clinical 1 relevance of this finding is unknown. PHARMACODYNAMICS Studies in adult healthy subjects showed that Levocetirizine at doses of 2.5 mg and 5 mg inhibited the skin wheal and flare caused by the intradermal injection of histamine. In contrast, dextrocetirizine exhibited no clear change in the inhibition of the wheal and flare reaction. Levocetirizine at a dose of 5 mg inhibited the wheal and flare caused by intradermal injection of histamine in 14 pediatric subjects (aged 6 to 11 years) and the activity persisted for at least 24 hours. The clinical relevance of histamine wheal skin testing is unknown. A QT/QTc study using a single dose of 30 mg of Levocetirizine did not demonstrate an effect on the QTc interval. While a single dose of Levocetirizine had no effect, the effects of Levocetirizine may not be at steady state following single dose. The effect of Levocetirizine on the QTc interval following multiple dose administration is unknown. Levocetirizine is not expected to have QT/QTc effects because of the results of QTc studies with Cetirizine and the long post-marketing history of cetirizine without reports of QT prolongation.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,For the symptomatic relief of allergic conditions including rhinitis and chronic urticaria.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:35:11.544546+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4501-02,Levocetirizine Dihydrochloride,None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-pNYr5bcBiEWVBR1sjjpJ9wtjG-wSv4u/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4501-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1-pNYr5bcBiEWVBR1sjjpJ9wtjG-wSv4u_view_usp_drivesdk_83759b2ab3.pdf,page_text/DRP-4501-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1-pNYr5bcBiEWVBR1sjjpJ9wtjG-wSv4u_view_usp_drivesdk_83759b2ab3.json,42891a7c53573998b543004e9a889f7cbc7622ff5118ab000f8d8309b50c3613,455136,1,digital_text,"Children 6 to 11 years of age: 2.5 mg (1/2 tablet) once daily in the evening. Excretion via feces accounts for only 12.9% of the dose. Levocetirizine is excreted both by glomerular filtration and active tubular secretion. Levocetirizine is 90% bound to plasma proteins. The distribution of Levocetirizine is restrictive, as the volume of distribution is 0.4 L/kg. Levocetirizine is contraindicated in the patients with history of hypersensitivity to Levocetirizine or any of the other constituents of the formulation. Distribution: No tissue distribution data are available in humans, neither concerning the passage of Levocetirizine through the blood-brain-barrier. In rats and dogs, the highest tissue levels are found in liver and kidneys, the lowest in the CNS compartment.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,,1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T15:35:15.122970+00:00
