PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4804-01,Celecoxib,None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1y3cQv5Uhg5iUz3LebiD8cJW3AfC43_oV/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4804-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1y3cQv5Uhg5iUz3LebiD8cJW3AfC43_oV_view_usp_drivesdk_ebc992adf3.pdf,page_text/DRP-4804-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1y3cQv5Uhg5iUz3LebiD8cJW3AfC43_oV_view_usp_drivesdk_ebc992adf3.json,e3c92ea8aaf2a80619c30f00441267c9431b522231247e06706f70f87a4a60aa,1484588,1,digital_text,White/White hard gelatin capsule size '0' containing white granular powder.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Used in the symptomatic relief of rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, and in the adjunctive treatment of adenomatous colorectal polyps; Also used in the management of acute pain and dysmenorrhea.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:49:22.192625+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-481,Multivitamins,Multilem,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KtXSZmBheUq3dskGmSibuR8J-x6BAmOJ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-481_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KtXSZmBheUq3dskGmSibuR8J-x6BAmOJ_view_usp_drivesdk_edc525962d.pdf,page_text/DRP-481_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KtXSZmBheUq3dskGmSibuR8J-x6BAmOJ_view_usp_drivesdk_edc525962d.json,fc16eb0c76c15796e41ba4953ef02f1cbe0b7bc04d948ff606d0998a8b3eec20,1228350,1,ocr,"A yellow to brown colored powder contained in a scarlet/brown capsule size #1. Blister pack by 10’s, box of 30’s and 100’s capsule What is in the medicine? Multilem Capsule contains vitamins which are organic substances required for various metabolic processes. Strength of the medicine: Each capsule contains: Retinol Acetate (Vitamin A), USP seseeeeeee- 1526 IU Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) .- 400 IU Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C).... Nicotinic Acid ... 80 mg 20 mg d-a-Tocopheryl Acid Succinate (Vitamin E)... 10 mg Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) . 2.0 mg Thiamine Mononitrate (Vitamin B1) -. 1.3 mg Riboflavin (Vitamin B2) .. 1.3 mg Folic Acid 170 mcg Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 5 mcg What is this medicine used for? General nutritional supplementation of daily diet for prevention of vitamin deficiency especially in case of inadequate nutrition, growth, strenuous physical activity, convalescence and for maintenance of body resistance. These are also used to provide nutritional balance necessary for proper physical growth and mental development. How much and how often should you use this medicine? Take one capsule daily, or as prescribed by the physician. When should you not take this medicine? If you are allergic to any of the components in the product. - Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: = Do not take more than the recommended dose. Do not use after the expiry date stated on the label. Undesirable effects of this medicine: Generally, if taken with recommended dose, this medicine is well tolerated but in some cases the following may be seen: Retinol Acetate (Vitamin A)- Hypervitaminosis A due to intake of very high doses can cause fatigue, irritability, anorexia, loss of weight, vomiting, sedation, dizziness, confusion and diarrhea. Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3)- Excessive intake may lead to hypercalcemia or hyperphosphatemia. d-a-Alpha-Tocopheryl Acid Succinate (Vitamin E)- A higher incidence of necrotizing enterocolitis has been noted in premature infants weighing less than 1.5 kg treated with Vitamin E. Riboflavin (Vitamin B2)- Large doses may result to bright yellow discoloration in urine. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12)- Allergic hypersensitivity reactions have occurred rarely that includes rash, itching and anaphylaxis. Nicotinic Acid- It has a vasodilating action that causes flushing, sensation of heat, faintness and pounding in the head. What other medicine or food should be avoided while taking this medicine? Retinol Acetate (Vitamin A)- Neomycin, Cholestyramine or Liquid paraffin may reduce the absorption of Vitamin A. Increase risk of hypervitaminosis A if given with retinoids such as isotretinoin and tretinoin. Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3)- Increased risk of hypercalcaemia if Vitamin D is given with Thiazide diuretics, Calcium, Phosphate. d-a-Tocopheryl Acid Succinate (Vitamin E)- Cholestyramine, Colestipol and Orlistat may interfere with the absorption of Vitamin E. High doses of Vitamin E may increase the effects of anti-coagulants. Pyridoxine HCI (Vitamin B6)- Pyridoxine reduces the effects of Levodopa and Altretamine. Vitamin B6 decreases concentration of Phenobarbital and Phenytoin. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12)- Neomycin, Aminosalicylic Acid, Histamine H2 Antagonist, Omeprazole and Colchicine reduce the absorption of Vitamin B12. Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C)- Omeprazole. What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Signs and symptoms of overdose: The following symptoms might be seen in large doses: Retinol Acetate (Vitamin A)- Intake in excess of 10,000 IU daily or weekly intakes in excess of 25,000 IU have resulted to hypervitaminosis A (fatigue, irritability, anorexia, loss of weight, vomiting, sedation, dizziness, confusion and diarrhea). Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3)- Anorexia, lassitude, nausea, vomiting, constipation, diarrhea, polyuria, nocturia, sweating, headache, thirst, somnolence and vertigo. d-a-Tocopheryl Acid Succinate (Vitamin E)- Large doses may cause diarrhea, abdominal pain and disturbances, blurred vision, dizziness, fatigue and weakness. Pyridoxine HCI (Vitamin B6)- Long term use of large doses is associated with the development of severe peripheral neuropathies. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12)- Gastrointestinal disturbances, fever, chills, hot flushing, dizziness, malaise, acneform, eruptions and tremor. Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C)- Large doses may cause diarrhea, abdominal pain and disturbances. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Protect from direct sunlight. When should you consult your doctor? If any undesirable effect occurs. “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph.” Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction Registration number: DRP-481 Date of Renewal Authorization: March 13, 2023 Date of Revision of Patient Information Leaflet: September 11, 2023 Manufactured by the Marketin Authorization Holder: Distributed by:",1,1,Description of the product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T15:49:43.316242+00:00
