re that should be taken when taking this medicine: - Do not take more than the recommended dose. - Do not use after the expiry date stated on the label. Undesirable effects of this medicine: Generally, if taken with recommended dose, this medicine is well tolerated but in some cases the following may be seen: Retinol cetate (Vitamin A)- Hypervitaminosis A due to intake of very high doses can cause fatigue, irritability, anorexia, loss of weight, vomiting sedation, dizziness, confusion, and diarrhea. Cholecalciferol Vitamin D3)- Excessive intake may lead to hypercalcemia or hyperphosphatemia d- &-Tocophery Acid Succinate (Vitamin E)- A higher incidence of necrotizing enterocolitis has been noted in premature infants weighing less than 1.5 kg treated with Vitamin E. Riboflavin (Vitamin B2)- Large doses may result to bright yellow discoloration in urine. anocobalamin (Vitamin B12)- Allergic persensitivity reactions have occurred rarely that includes rash,itching, and anaphylaxis. Nk c otinic Acid- It has a vasodilating action t hi at causes flushing, sensation of heat, faintness, pounding in the head. What other medicine or food should be avoided while taking this medicine? Retinol Acetate (Vitamin A)- Neomycin, Cholestyramine or paraffin may reduce the absorption of Vitamin A. Increase risk of hyper- vitaminosis A if given with retinoids such as isotretinoin and tretinoin. Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3 d-a«-Toco t - Increased risk of hypercalcaemia if Vitamin D is given with Thiazide diuretics, Calcium and Phosphate. of Vitamin Em a ryl Acid Succinate (Vitamin E)- Cholestyramine, Colestipol, Orlistat may interfere with the absorption of Vitamin E increase the effects of anti-coagulants High doses Pyridoxine HC r( Vitamin B6)- Pyridoxine reduces the effects of Levodopa and Altretamine. Vitamin B6 decreases concentration of Phenobarbital and Phynetoin. Vitamin Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12)- Neomycin, Aminosalicylic Acid, Histamine H2 Antagonist, Omeprazole, Colchicine redu