PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-01,Ceftriaxone (as sodium),Kairoxon,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jRUNs6L3WIsothnlKNp6Vl1n2w6_ki-c/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1jRUNs6L3WIsothnlKNp6Vl1n2w6_ki-c_view_usp_drivesdk_e11b990ce1.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1jRUNs6L3WIsothnlKNp6Vl1n2w6_ki-c_view_usp_drivesdk_e11b990ce1.json,b0e949153e0b9b77c162e301b70a957940bc9ffbd1feb142468d8951fd1aad3b,542115,1,digital_text,White crystalline powder. Ceftriaxone sodium USP,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Doses for infants and children (under 50 kg) are 20 to 50 mg/kg once daily; for severe infections, up to 80 mg/kg daily may be given. In neonates, the maximum dose should not exceed 50 mg/kg daily;",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:59:44.244773+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-02,Ceftriaxone (as sodium),Cefftry,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oFcia7G1A1qlBuld2eRBtaLwHqg_w3I9/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1oFcia7G1A1qlBuld2eRBtaLwHqg_w3I9_view_usp_drivesdk_be7147adb6.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1oFcia7G1A1qlBuld2eRBtaLwHqg_w3I9_view_usp_drivesdk_be7147adb6.json,1d1c805ea5b0abf5943ec14d3e3379b0fbab8f2c518bb925a8bae37a73349d4e,1049344,1,digital_text,"White crystalline powder. Ceftriaxone sodium USP equivalent to Ceftriaxone .................... 1 g Ceftriaxone inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis following attachment to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs). This results in the interruption of cell wall (peptidoglycan) biosynthesis, which leads to bacterial cell lysis and death. Bacterial resistance to Ceftriaxone may be due to one or more of the following mechanisms: • Hydrolysis by beta-lactamases, including extended-spectr um beta-lactamases (ESBLs), carbapenemases and Amp C enzymes that may be induced or stably derepressed in certain aerobic Gram-negative bacterial species, • Outer membrane impermeability in Gram-negative organisms, and • Bacterial efflux pumps. Susceptibility testing breakpoints • Reduced affinity of penicillin-binding proteins for Ceftriaxone, Doses for infants and children (under 50 kg) are 20 to 50 mg/kg once daily; for severe infections, up to 80 mg/kg daily may be given. In neonates, the maximum dose should not exceed 50 mg/kg daily; intravenous doses in neonates should be given over 60 minutes. Doses above 50 mg/kg should be given IV infusion only. Or as prescribed by the physician. Studies have shown that Ceftriaxone, like some other cephalosporins, can displace bilirubin from serum albumin. As with all beta-lactam antibacterial (Cephalosporin) agents, serious and occasionally fatal hypersensitivity reactions have been reported. In case of severe hypersensitivity reactions, treatment with Ceftriaxone must be discontinued immediately and adequate emergency measures must be initiated. Before beginning treatment, it should be established whether the patient has a history of severe hypersensitivity reactions to Ceftriaxone, to other cephalosporins or to any other type of beta- lactam agent. Caution should be used if Ceftriaxone is given to patients with a history of non-severe hypersensitivity to other beta-lactam agents. During prolonged treatment, complete blood count should be performed at regular intervals. It is indicated for the treatment of susceptible infections. They include chancroid, endocarditis, gastroenteritis (invasive salmonellosis; shigellosis), gonorrhea, Lyme disease, meningitis (including meningococcal meningitis prophylaxis), pneumonia, septicemia, syphilis, typhoid fever, and Whipple’s disease. It is also used for surgical infection prophylaxis. Ceftriaxone is widely distributed in body tissues and fluids. It crosses both inflamed and non-inflamed meninges, generally achieving therapeutic concentrations in the Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF). It crosses the placenta and low concentrations have been detected in breast milk. High concentrations are achieved in bile.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"In patients of any age, Ceftriaxone must not be mixed or administered simultaneously with any calcium containing intravenous solutions, even via different infusion lines or at different infusion sites. However, in patients older than 28 days of age, Ceftriaxone and calcium-containing solutions may be administered sequentially one after another if infusion lines at different sites are used or if the infusion lines are replaced or thoroughly flushed between infusions with physiological salt-solution to avoid precipitation. In patients requiring continuous infusion with calcium-containing total parenteral nutrition (TPN) solutions, healthcare professionals may wish to consider the use of alternative antibacterial (Cephalosporin) treatments which do not carry a similar risk of precipitation. If the use of Ceftriaxone is considered necessary in patients requiring continuous nutrition, TPN solutions and Ceftriaxone can be administered simultaneously, albeit via different infusion lines at different sites. Alternatively, infusion of TPN solution could be stopped for the period of Ceftriaxone infusion and the infusion lines flushed between solutions. Pediatric population Ceftriaxone is contraindicated in premature and full-term neonates at risk of developing bilirubin encephalopathy. Immune-mediated hemolytic anaemia An immune-mediated hemolytic anaemia has been observed in patients receiving cephalosporin class antibacterials including Ceftriaxone. Severe cases of hemolytic anemia, including fatalities, have been reported during Ceftriaxone treatment in both adults and children. If a patient develops anaemia while on Ceftriaxone, the diagnosis of a cephalosporin-associated anaemia should be considered and Ceftriaxone discontinued until the aetiology is determined. Long-term treatment Colitis/Overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms Antibacterial (Cephalosporin) agent-associated colitis and pseudomembranous colitis have been reported with nearly all antibacterial (Cephalosporin) agents, including Ceftriaxone, and may range in severity from mild to life-threatening. Therefore, it is important to consider this diagnosis in patients who are present with diarrhoea during or subsequent to the administration of Ceftriaxone. Discontinuation of therapy with Ceftriaxone and the administration of specific treatment for Clostridium difficile should be considered. Medicinal products that inhibit peristalsis should not be given. Severe renal and hepatic insufficiency In severe renal and hepatic insufficiency, close clinical monitoring for safety and efficacy is advised. Interference with Coombs tests may occur, as Ceftriaxone may lead to false-positive test results. Ceftriaxone can also lead to false-positive test results for galactosaemia. Non-enzymatic methods for the glucose determination in urine may give false-positive results. Urine glucose determination during therapy with Ceftriaxone should be done enzymatically. The presence of Ceftriaxone may falsely lower estimated blood glucose values obtained with some blood glucose monitoring systems. Alternative testing methods should be used if necessary. Sodium Superinfections with non-susceptible microorganisms may occur as with other Antibacterial agents. Interference with serological testing Each gram of Ceftriaxone contains 3.6 mmol sodium. This should be taken into consideration in patients on a controlled sodium diet. Cases of pancreatitis, possibly of biliary obstruction aetiology, have been reported in patients treated with Ceftriaxone. Most patients presented with risk factors for biliary stasis and biliary sludge (e.g. preceeding major therapy, severe illness and total parenteral nutrition). A trigger or cofactor of Ceftriaxone-related biliary precipitation cannot be ruled out. Calcium-containing diluents, such as Ringer's solution or Hartmann's solution, should not be used to reconstitute Ceftriaxone vials or to further dilute a reconstituted vial for intravenous administration because a precipitate can form. Precipitation of Ceftriaxone-calcium can also occur when Ceftriaxone is mixed with calcium-containing solutions in the same intravenous administration line. Ceftriaxone must not be administered simultaneously with calcium-containing intravenous solutions, including continuous calcium-containing infusions such as parenteral nutrition via a Y-site. However, in patients other than neonates, Ceftriaxone and calcium-containing solutions may be administered sequentially of one another if the infusion lines are thoroughly flushed between infusions with a compatible fluid. In vitro studies using adult and neonatal plasma from umbilical cord blood demonstrated that neonates have an increased risk of precipitation of Ceftriaxone-calcium. Antibacterial (Cephalosporin) spectrum When shadows are observed on sonograms, consideration should be given to the possibility of precipitates of calcium Ceftriaxone. Shadows, which have been mistaken for gallstones, have been detected on sonograms of the gallbladder and have been observed more frequently at Ceftriaxone doses of 1 g per day and above. Caution should be particularly considered in the paediatric population. Fertility: No impairment of renal function has been observed after concurrent administration of large doses of Ceftriaxone and potent diuretics (e.g. furosemide).",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:59:48.111402+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-03,Ceftriaxone (As Sodium),Airefix,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XvcxvgFzeEyWRtqQZ4fNsn3AuI5NUJva/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-03_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1XvcxvgFzeEyWRtqQZ4fNsn3AuI5NUJva_view_usp_drivesdk_0260339626.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-03_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1XvcxvgFzeEyWRtqQZ4fNsn3AuI5NUJva_view_usp_drivesdk_0260339626.json,1e67131e443067f9dc86055c36e618c07e903b43367044a94b6c97242e5585c9,1173955,1,digital_text,"Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (Stevens-Johnson syndrome or Lyell's syndrome/Toxic epidermal necrolysis) have been reported; however, the frequency of these events is not known. Colitis/Overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms In less frequent cases, findings have been associated with symptoms. In symptomatic cases, conservative non-surgical management is recommended. Discontinuation of Ceftriaxone treatment in symptomatic cases should be at discretion of the clinician. In patients of any age, Ceftriaxone must not be mixed or administered simultaneously with any calcium containing intravenous solutions, even via different infusion lines or at different infusion sites. However, in patients older than 28 days of age, Ceftriaxone and calcium-containing solutions may be administered sequentially one after another if infusion lines at different sites are used or if the infusion lines are replaced or thoroughly flushed between infusions with physiological salt-solution to avoid precipitation. In patients requiring continuous infusion with calcium-containing total parenteral nutrition (TPN) solutions, healthcare professionals may wish to consider the use of alternative antibacterial (Cephalosporin) treatments which do not carry a similar risk of precipitation. If the use of Ceftriaxone is considered necessary in patients requiring continuous nutrition, TPN solutions and Ceftriaxone can be administered simultaneously, albeit via different infusion lines at different sites. Alternatively, infusion of TPN solution could be stopped for the period of Ceftriaxone infusion and the infusion lines flushed between solutions.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"It is indicated for the treatment of susceptible infections. They include chancroid, endocarditis, gastroenteritis (invasive salmonellosis; shigellosis), gonorrhea, Lyme disease, meningitis (including meningococcal meningitis prophylaxis), pneumonia, septicemia, syphilis, typhoid fever, and Whipple’s disease. It is also used for surgical infection prophylaxis.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:59:51.736079+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-04,Ceftriaxone (As Sodium),Midtrix,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1faEXtxphU_RG4hPPA8jEtZP0DOmpeDZ8/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-04_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1faEXtxphU_RG4hPPA8jEtZP0DOmpeDZ8_view_usp_drivesdk_981d2cdfc5.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-04_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1faEXtxphU_RG4hPPA8jEtZP0DOmpeDZ8_view_usp_drivesdk_981d2cdfc5.json,523cff12604f6e1c59b90c78a21530a88679a030b4773b2dea2d72573b3b24e7,536404,1,digital_text,"White crystalline powder. • Reduced affinity of penicillin-binding proteins for Ceftriaxone, Resistance Minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) breakpoints established by the European Committee on Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing (EUCAST) are as follows: Each vial contains: Mechanism of Action Ceftriaxone inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis following attachment to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs). This results in the interruption of cell wall (peptidoglycan) biosynthesis, which leads to bacterial cell lysis and death. • Bacterial efflux pumps. Bacterial resistance to Ceftriaxone may be due to one or more of the following mechanisms:",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Ceftriaxone demonstrates non-linear dose-dependent pharmacokinetics because of its protein binding; about 85% to 95% is bound to plasma proteins depending on the concentration of Ceftriaxone. Interaction with calcium-containing products Studies have shown that Ceftriaxone, like some other cephalosporins, can displace bilirubin from serum albumin. Immune-mediated hemolytic anaemia An immune-mediated hemolytic anaemia has been observed in patients receiving cephalosporin class antibacterials including Ceftriaxone. Severe cases of hemolytic anemia, including fatalities, have been reported during Ceftriaxone treatment in both adults and children. If a patient develops anaemia while on Ceftriaxone, the diagnosis of a cephalosporin-associated anaemia should be considered and Ceftriaxone discontinued until the aetiology is determined. Antibacterial (Cephalosporin) agent-associated colitis and pseudomembranous colitis have been reported with nearly all antibacterial (Cephalosporin) agents, including Ceftriaxone, and may range in severity from mild to life-threatening. Therefore, it is important to consider this diagnosis in patients who are present with diarrhoea during or subsequent to the administration of Ceftriaxone. Discontinuation of therapy with Ceftriaxone and the administration of specific treatment for Clostridium During prolonged treatment, complete blood count should be performed at regular intervals. Colitis/Overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms difficile should be considered. Medicinal products that inhibit peristalsis should not be given. Superinfections with non-susceptible microorganisms may occur as with other Antibacterial agents. Severe renal and hepatic insufficiency Interference with Coombs tests may occur, as Ceftriaxone may lead to false-positive test results. Ceftriaxone can also lead to false-positive test results for galactosaemia. Non-enzymatic methods for the glucose determination in urine may give false-positive results. Urine glucose determination during therapy with Ceftriaxone should be done enzymatically. The presence of Ceftriaxone may falsely lower estimated blood glucose values obtained with some blood glucose monitoring systems. Alternative testing methods should be used if necessary. Sodium Interference with serological testing Each gram of Ceftriaxone contains 3.6 mmol sodium. This should be taken into consideration in patients on a controlled sodium diet. In severe renal and hepatic insufficiency, close clinical monitoring for safety and efficacy is advised. Ceftriaxone has a limited spectrum of antibacterial (Cephalosporin) activity and may not be suitable for use as a single agent for the treatment of some types of infections unless the pathogen has already been confirmed. In polymicrobial infections, where suspected pathogens include organisms resistant to Ceftriaxone, administration of an additional antibiotic should be considered. In case a lidocaine solution is used as a solvent, Ceftriaxone solutions must only be used for intramuscular injection. Contraindications to lidocaine, warnings and other relevant information of lidocaine must be considered before use. The lidocaine solution should never be administered intravenously. Such precipitates disappear after discontinuation of Ceftriaxone therapy. Rarely precipitates of calcium Ceftriaxone have been associated with symptoms. In symptomatic cases, conservative non-surgical management is recommended and discontinuation of Ceftriaxone treatment should be considered by the physician based on specific benefit risk assessment. When shadows are observed on sonograms, consideration should be given to the possibility of precipitates of calcium Ceftriaxone. Shadows, which have been mistaken for gallstones, have been detected on sonograms of the gallbladder and have been observed more frequently at Ceftriaxone doses of 1 g per day and above. Caution should be particularly considered in the paediatric population. Biliary lithiasis Antibacterial (Cephalosporin) spectrum Use of lidocaine",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:59:55.537382+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-05,Ceftriaxone (As Sodium),Gotriaxone,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/18R_GqfkoIJLx5WRh9bVEVKfG4rw9jn9_/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-05_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18R_GqfkoIJLx5WRh9bVEVKfG4rw9jn9__view_usp_drivesdk_08deec64fa.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-05_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18R_GqfkoIJLx5WRh9bVEVKfG4rw9jn9__view_usp_drivesdk_08deec64fa.json,38d05eea5d9ebb04bdfaa69318a70cff3975a754a3db1c9801c0aa07dbc9358f,572593,1,digital_text,Ceftriaxone sodium USP White crystalline powder.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"It is indicated for the treatment of susceptible infections. They include chancroid, endocarditis, gastroenteritis (invasive salmonellosis; shigellosis), gonorrhea, Lyme disease, meningitis (including meningococcal meningitis prophylaxis), pneumonia, septicemia, syphilis, typhoid fever, and Whipple’s disease. It is also used for surgical infection prophylaxis.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T15:59:58.697086+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-06,Ceftriaxone (as sodium),Lifetrixone,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1uUwstCO-GjxuRUjEG3oDQwFfvHpRUaTB/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-06_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1uUwstCO-GjxuRUjEG3oDQwFfvHpRUaTB_view_usp_drivesdk_2cdd05f046.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-06_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1uUwstCO-GjxuRUjEG3oDQwFfvHpRUaTB_view_usp_drivesdk_2cdd05f046.json,d5e836d5e416e7ae8488e33fae0ae9d33f9547cf097b86d766bbc380c17c23b2,445000,1,digital_text,,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"The usual adult dose is 1 to 2 g daily as a single dose or in two divided doses; in severe infections, up to 4 g daily may be given. Ceftriaxone is given as the sodium salt by slow intravenous injection over at least 2 to 4 minutes, by intermittent intravenous infusion over at least 30 minutes, or by deep intramuscular injection. If more than 1 g is to be injected intramuscularly, then the dose should be divided between more than one site. It is indicated for the treatment of susceptible infections. They include chancroid, endocarditis, gastroenteritis (invasive salmonellosis; shigellosis), gonorrhea, Lyme disease, meningitis (including meningococcal meningitis prophylaxis), pneumonia, septicemia, syphilis, typhoid fever, and Whipple’s disease. It is also used for surgical infection prophylaxis. Doses for infants and children (under 50 kg) are 20 to 50 mg/kg once daily; for severe infections, up to 80 mg/kg daily may be given. In neonates, the maximum dose should not exceed 50 mg/kg daily; intravenous doses in neonates should be given over 60 minutes. Doses above 50 mg/kg should be given IV infusion only. Or as prescribed by the physician.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T16:00:01.704115+00:00
