PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-08,Ceftriaxone (As Sodium),Puxenon,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1VM4kruFikNW2erblXGlsZ4WqJ5DwDYSO/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-08_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1VM4kruFikNW2erblXGlsZ4WqJ5DwDYSO_view_usp_drivesdk_79b879ce1b.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-08_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1VM4kruFikNW2erblXGlsZ4WqJ5DwDYSO_view_usp_drivesdk_79b879ce1b.json,5e2fe51ea2fa3260469789b7dfd52778aa4f6a6a2cb14ad811640211d401d343,491985,1,digital_text,,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"It is indicated for the treatment of susceptible infections. They include chancroid, endocarditis, gastroenteritis (invasive salmonellosis; shigellosis), gonorrhea, Lyme disease, meningitis (including meningococcal meningitis prophylaxis), pneumonia, septicemia, syphilis, typhoid fever, and Whipple’s disease. It is also used for surgical infection prophylaxis.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T16:00:28.041380+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-09,Ceftriaxone (as sodium),Fenadef,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1YltkWvfb6_cqaBcRlxO1JPXeD4ylMuTB/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-09_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1YltkWvfb6_cqaBcRlxO1JPXeD4ylMuTB_view_usp_drivesdk_d3872c6bd0.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-09_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1YltkWvfb6_cqaBcRlxO1JPXeD4ylMuTB_view_usp_drivesdk_d3872c6bd0.json,077b8ee0449f51878b086e3e51bb85ec5cc64b01cf734518ab4215ea1fb7a975,491415,1,digital_text,"Susceptibility testing breakpoints Sterile Water for Injection BP ........... 10 mL Mechanism of Action Each ampoule contains: •Hydrolysis by beta-lactamases, including extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs), carbapenemases and Amp C enzymes that may be induced or stably derepressed in certain aerobic Gram-negative bacterial species, Bacterial resistance to Ceftriaxone may be due to one or more of the following mechanisms: Ceftriaxone inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis following attachment to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs). This results in the interruption of cell wall (peptidoglycan) biosynthesis, which leads to bacterial cell lysis and death. Resistance • Reduced affinity of penicillin-binding proteins for Ceftriaxone, • Bacterial efflux pumps. Minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) breakpoints established by the European Committee on Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing (EUCAST) are as follows:",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Pediatric population Ceftriaxone is contraindicated in premature and full-term neonates at risk of developing bilirubin encephalopathy. Immune-mediated hemolytic anaemia Hypersensitivity reactions Ceftriaxone is given as the sodium salt by slow intravenous injection over at least 2 to 4 minutes, by intermittent intravenous infusion over at least 30 minutes, or by deep intramuscular injection. If more than 1 g is to be injected intramuscularly, then the dose should be divided between more than one site. During prolonged treatment, complete blood count should be performed at regular intervals. Colitis/Overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms Superinfections with non-susceptible microorganisms may occur as with other Antibacterial agents. Severe renal and hepatic insufficiency Antibacterial (Cephalosporin) spectrum Ceftriaxone has a limited spectrum of antibacterial (Cephalosporin) activity and may not be suitable for use as a single agent for the treatment of some types of infections unless the pathogen has already been confirmed. In polymicrobial infections, where suspected pathogens include organisms resistant to Ceftriaxone, administration of an additional antibiotic should be considered. Interference with Coombs tests may occur, as Ceftriaxone may lead to false-positive test results. Ceftriaxone can also lead to false-positive test results for galactosaemia. The presence of Ceftriaxone may falsely lower estimated blood glucose values obtained with some blood glucose monitoring systems. Alternative testing methods should be used if necessary. Non-enzymatic methods for the glucose determination in urine may give false-positive results. Urine glucose determination during therapy with Ceftriaxone should be done enzymatically. Interference with serological testing Sodium In severe renal and hepatic insufficiency, close clinical monitoring for safety and efficacy is advised. Each gram of Ceftriaxone contains 3.6 mmol sodium. This should be taken into consideration in patients on a controlled sodium diet. Use of lidocaine In case a lidocaine solution is used as a solvent, Ceftriaxone solutions must only be used for intramuscular injection. Contraindications to lidocaine, warnings and other relevant information of lidocaine must be considered before use. The lidocaine solution should never be administered intravenously. When shadows are observed on sonograms, consideration should be given to the possibility of precipitates of calcium Ceftriaxone. Shadows, which have been mistaken for gallstones, have been detected on sonograms of the gallbladder and have been observed more frequently at Ceftriaxone doses of 1 g per day and above. Caution should be particularly considered in the paediatric population. Such precipitates disappear after discontinuation of Ceftriaxone therapy. Rarely precipitates of calcium Ceftriaxone have been associated with symptoms. In symptomatic cases, conservative non-surgical management is recommended and discontinuation of Ceftriaxone treatment should be considered by the physician based on specific benefit risk assessment. Biliary lithiasis Biliary stasis Cases of pancreatitis, possibly of biliary obstruction aetiology, have been reported in patients treated with Ceftriaxone. Most patients presented with risk factors for biliary stasis and biliary sludge (e.g. preceeding major therapy, severe illness and total parenteral nutrition). A trigger or cofactor of Ceftriaxone-related biliary precipitation cannot be ruled out. During treatment with Ceftriaxone, undesirable effects may occur (e.g. dizziness), which may influence the ability to drive and use machines. Patients should be cautious when driving or operating machinery. Reproductive studies have shown no evidence of adverse effects on male or female fertility. Breastfeeding: Foods, Drugs, Devices, and Cosmetics Act prohibits dispensing without prescription. No impairment of renal function has been observed after concurrent administration of large doses of Ceftriaxone and potent diuretics (e.g. furosemide). Renal lithiasis In patients treated with Ceftriaxone, the Coombs test may lead to false-positive test results. Pregnancy: Concomitant use with oral anticoagulants may increase the anti-vitamin K effect and the risk of bleeding. It is recommended that the International Normalised Ratio (INR) is monitored frequently and the posology of the anti-vitamin K drug adjusted accordingly, both during and after treatment with Ceftriaxone. For this reason, glucose level determination in urine during therapy with Ceftriaxone should be carried out enzymatically.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:00:31.213756+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-10,Ceftriaxone (as sodium),Forneumo,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KRvFO_fj66zl2-65Kq2fW9WQFgUAqUD6/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-10_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KRvFO_fj66zl2-65Kq2fW9WQFgUAqUD6_view_usp_drivesdk_3d4174b2d3.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-10_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KRvFO_fj66zl2-65Kq2fW9WQFgUAqUD6_view_usp_drivesdk_3d4174b2d3.json,176b37ce0dac285736d0ca87522fc1d4ef11fd6214eb9a2101485c809b01adef,499987,1,digital_text,White crystalline powder.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Ceftriaxone is widely distributed in body tissues and fluids. It crosses both inflamed and non-inflamed meninges, generally achieving therapeutic concentrations in the Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF). It crosses the placenta and low concentrations have been detected in breast milk. High concentrations are achieved in bile.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:00:34.607602+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-11,Ceftriaxone (As Sodium),Rainoxone,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KBQ-wdxufr9Avp9xkxzdYo24QEcrbaya/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-11_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KBQ-wdxufr9Avp9xkxzdYo24QEcrbaya_view_usp_drivesdk_cb5a040069.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-11_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KBQ-wdxufr9Avp9xkxzdYo24QEcrbaya_view_usp_drivesdk_cb5a040069.json,cfa6c840f7761239431437991da5443aae56d6f7d5f7ddcb83dd5aeaf91b845a,74744,1,digital_text,White crystalline powder.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"It is indicated for the treatment of susceptible infections. They include chancroid, endocarditis, gastroenteritis (invasive salmonellosis; shigellosis), gonorrhea, Lyme disease, meningitis (including meningococcal meningitis prophylaxis), pneumonia, septicemia, syphilis, typhoid fever, and Whipple’s disease. It is also used for surgical infection prophylaxis.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:00:37.672170+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-12,Ceftriaxone (As Sodium),Emaxxone,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1sCAbx7YJj_lwfDbnt19ydkbmz9y7c-m6/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-12_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1sCAbx7YJj_lwfDbnt19ydkbmz9y7c-m6_view_usp_drivesdk_693e963a6c.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-12_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1sCAbx7YJj_lwfDbnt19ydkbmz9y7c-m6_view_usp_drivesdk_693e963a6c.json,197ff285222aee19872ceccc3eec70d4ef72426aeb1c87c81fc7e351aa5f2455,494588,1,digital_text,,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (Stevens-Johnson syndrome or Lyell's syndrome/Toxic epidermal necrolysis) have been reported; however, the frequency of these events is not known. Foods, Drugs, Devices, and Cosmetics Act prohibits dispensing without prescription. Likewise, non-enzymatic methods for glucose determination in urine may yield false-positive results. For this reason, glucose level determination in urine during therapy with Ceftriaxone should be carried out enzymatically. In patients treated with Ceftriaxone, the Coombs test may lead to false-positive test results. Simultaneous administration of probenecid does not reduce the elimination of Ceftriaxone. Changes in bowel flora may be because of the greater biliary excretion of Ceftriaxone; diarrhea may occur more often, especially in children. Biliary sludge or pseudolithiasis due to precipitate of calcium Ceftriaxone has been seen occasionally in patients receiving Ceftriaxone. Similarly, deposition of the calcium salt has occurred rarely in the urine. Ceftriaxone is highly protein bound and is able to displace bilirubin from albumin binding sites, causing hyperbilirubinemia; its use should be avoided in jaundiced neonates. In overdose, the symptoms of nausea, vomiting and diarrhea can occur. Ceftriaxone concentrations cannot be reduced by hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis. There is no specific antidote. T reatment of overdose should be symptomatic.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T16:00:40.573473+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-4940-13,Ceftriaxone (as sodium),Amtecxone,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1zt62Cs5A-ZlQLUnhsU2sPUCmt0S5Fcm1/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-4940-13_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1zt62Cs5A-ZlQLUnhsU2sPUCmt0S5Fcm1_view_usp_drivesdk_54c7bbcbf1.pdf,page_text/DRP-4940-13_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1zt62Cs5A-ZlQLUnhsU2sPUCmt0S5Fcm1_view_usp_drivesdk_54c7bbcbf1.json,d4c4d28dafe695e0e4ba1efda399bacb95cf28364a862b2909951745d27fb50b,450723,1,digital_text,White crystalline powder.,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Doses for infants and children (under 50 kg) are 20 to 50 mg/kg once daily; for severe infections up to 80 mg/kg daily may be given. In neonates, the maximum dose should not exceed 50 mg/kg daily; It is indicated for the treatment of susceptible infections. They include chancroid, endocarditis, gastroenteritis (invasive salmonellosis; shigellosis), gonorrhea, Lyme disease, meningitis (including meningococcal meningitis prophylaxis), pneumonia, septicemia, syphilis, typhoid fever, and Whipple’s disease. It is also used for surgical infection prophylaxis. Colitis/Overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (Stevens-Johnson syndrome or Lyell's syndrome/Toxic epidermal necrolysis) have been reported; however, the frequency of these events is not known. Interaction with calcium-containing products Cases of fatal reactions with calcium-Ceftriaxone precipitates in lungs and kidneys in premature and full-term neonates aged less than 1 month have been described. At least one of them had received Ceftriaxone and calcium at different times and through different intravenous lines. In the available scientific data, there are no reports of confirmed intravascular precipitations in patients, other than neonates, treated with Ceftriaxone and calcium-containing solutions or any other calcium-containing products. In vitro studies demonstrated that neonates have an increased risk of precipitation of Ceftriaxone-calcium compared to other age groups. If a patient develops anaemia while on Ceftriaxone, the diagnosis of a cephalosporin-associated anaemia should be considered and Ceftriaxone discontinued until the aetiology is determined. During prolonged treatment, complete blood count should be performed at regular intervals. Hypersensitivity reactions Immune-mediated hemolytic anaemia In less frequent cases, findings have been associated with symptoms. In symptomatic cases, conservative non-surgical management is recommended. Discontinuation of Ceftriaxone treatment in symptomatic cases should be at discretion of the clinician. Studies have shown that Ceftriaxone, like some other cephalosporins, can displace bilirubin from serum albumin. In patients of any age, Ceftriaxone must not be mixed or administered simultaneously with any calcium containing intravenous solutions, even via different infusion lines or at different infusion sites. However, in patients older than 28 days of age, Ceftriaxone and calcium-containing solutions may be administered sequentially one after another if infusion lines at different sites are used or if the infusion lines are replaced or thoroughly flushed between infusions with physiological salt-solution to avoid precipitation. In patients requiring continuous infusion with calcium-containing total parenteral nutrition (TPN) solutions, healthcare professionals may wish to consider the use of alternative antibacterial (Cephalosporin) treatments which do not carry a similar risk of precipitation. If the use of Ceftriaxone is considered necessary in patients requiring continuous nutrition, TPN solutions and Ceftriaxone can be administered simultaneously, albeit via different infusion lines at different sites. Alternatively, infusion of TPN solution could be stopped for the period of Ceftriaxone infusion and the infusion lines flushed between solutions. Pediatric population Long-term treatment Antibacterial (Cephalosporin) agent-associated colitis and pseudomembranous colitis have been reported with nearly all antibacterial (Cephalosporin) agents, including Ceftriaxone, and may range in severity from mild to life-threatening. Therefore, it is important to consider this diagnosis in patients who are present with diarrhoea during or subsequent to the administration of Ceftriaxone. Discontinuation of therapy with Ceftriaxone and the administration of specific treatment for Clostridium Ceftriaxone is contraindicated in premature and full-term neonates at risk of developing bilirubin encephalopathy.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:00:43.331796+00:00
