PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5335-02,Cyclophosphamide,Cytoace,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KGVew8ZpryGbAO5XoT1cX-tKa6iNyu1e/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5335-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KGVew8ZpryGbAO5XoT1cX-tKa6iNyu1e_view_usp_drivesdk_043a6f1f10.pdf,page_text/DRP-5335-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1KGVew8ZpryGbAO5XoT1cX-tKa6iNyu1e_view_usp_drivesdk_043a6f1f10.json,f2a8293b77b970ac02ec088225de4e4a608e407bd24951f2adcdcbde674f6d89,442013,1,digital_text,Cyclophosphamide is administered as an inactive prodrug that is activated in the liver. PHARMACODYNAMICS,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Used in the treatment of various forms of malignancies such as Burkitt’s and other non-Hodgkins lymphomas, multiple myeloma and mycosis fungoides. It is also used in gestational trophoblastic tumours and malignancies of the brain, breast, endometrium, lung, and ovary; in childhood malignancies such as neuroblastoma, retinoblastoma, Wilms’ tumour and in sarcomas and some leukemias. It is also indicated for the treatment of immune-mediated conditions such as severe rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythmatosus, vasculitis, dermatomyositis, and interstitial lung disease.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:19:46.272036+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5335-04,Cyclophosphamide,Cyclixine 500,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1uU2P9BT2m97oDTzRrHBbKq1ZrCgTi-V9/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5335-04_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1uU2P9BT2m97oDTzRrHBbKq1ZrCgTi-V9_view_usp_drivesdk_f2d23cb729.pdf,page_text/DRP-5335-04_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1uU2P9BT2m97oDTzRrHBbKq1ZrCgTi-V9_view_usp_drivesdk_f2d23cb729.json,ce345fefd12b7bd8280ba93caa53cc4b72475b0e678929523d984f96402e5d88,835509,1,digital_text,"It remains to be unknown whether the cytostatic effect that is entirely dependent on the alkylation of DNA or other mechanisms, such as inhibition of chromatin transformation processes or inhibition of DNA polymerases, play a role. The metabolite acrolein has no antineoplastic activity, but is responsible for the adverse urotoxic effect. The immunosuppressive effect of Cyclophosphamide is based on the fact that Cyclophosphamide has an inhibitory effect on B- cells, CD4 + T-cells and to a lesser extent on CD8 +-T-cells. In addition, it is assumed that Cyclophosphamide has an inhibitory effect on the suppressor that regulate the IgG2 class of antibodies. Cyclophosphamide.........................500 mg Cyclophosphamide Cross-resistance, especially with structurally related cytotoxic agents, e.g. ifosfamide, as well as other alkylating agents, cannot be excluded.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Distribution: Cyclophosphamide is found in the cerebrospinal fluid and the mother's milk. Cyclophosphamide and metabolites can pass through the placenta. Cyclophosphamide and its metabolites are primarily excreted by the kidneys. Absorption: Elimination: The plasma half-life of Cyclophosphamide is about 4 to 8 hours in adults and children. The plasma half-lives of the active metabolites are not known. Use During Pregnancy: Cyclophosphamide can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman. Therefore, if this drug is used during pregnancy, or if the patient becomes pregnant while taking this drug, the patient should be apprised of the potential hazard to the fetus. Women of childbearing potential should be advised to avoid pregnancy during Cyclophosphamide therapy. Initial treatment is given by intravenous injection. After satisfactory remission has occurred, maintenance therapy with tablets is recommended. Individual dosage should take into account the general state of the patient and the White Blood Count.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:19:49.554703+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5335-05,Cyclophosphamide,Cyphocare,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1o3CL0zkftmk0dQnhRJv5JpihC6qhkHSt/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5335-05_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1o3CL0zkftmk0dQnhRJv5JpihC6qhkHSt_view_usp_drivesdk_68856098a9.pdf,page_text/DRP-5335-05_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1o3CL0zkftmk0dQnhRJv5JpihC6qhkHSt_view_usp_drivesdk_68856098a9.json,d0b0e32a3cea39dc115ca8f965c4153cc5503450283edffa64a81ce7b481bbfb,446772,1,digital_text,"It remains to be whether the cytostatic effect is entirely dependent on the alkylation of DNA or other mechanisms unknown that , such as inhibition of chromatin transformation processes or inhibition of DNA polymerases play a role. The metabolite acrolein , has no antineoplastic activity, but is responsible for the adverse urotoxic effect. Pharmacodynami :cs Used in the treatment of various forms of malignancies such as Burkitt’s and other non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas, multiple myeloma and mycosis fungoides. It is also used in gestational trophoblastic tumours and malignancies of the brain, breast, endometrium, lung, and ovary; in childhood malignancies such as neuroblastoma, retinoblastoma, Wilms’ tumour and in sarcomas and some leukemias. It is also indicated for the treatment of immune-mediated conditions such as severe rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupu erythematosus, vasculitis, dermatomyositis, and lung disease.s interstitial Cyclophosphamide is activated in the liver to the active metabolites 4-hydroxy-cyclophosphamide and aldofosfamide (tautomeric form of 4-hydroxy-cyclophosphamide) through phase I metabolism by cytochrome P450 (CYP) enzymes. Different CYP isozymes contribute to the bioactivation of Cyclophosphamide, including CYP2A6, 2B6, 2C9, 2C19 and 3A4, 2B6 in which it exhibits highest 4-hydroxylase activity. Detoxification is done mainly through glutathione-S-transferases (GSTA1, GSTP1) and alcohol dehydrogenase (ALDH1, ALDH3). Two to four hours after administration of yclophosphamide, the plasma C concentrations of the active metabolites are maximal, after which a rapid decrease of plasma concentrations takes place. Cyclophosphamide has been demonstrated to have a cytostatic effect in many tumour types. Cyclophosphamide is quickly and almost completely absorbed from parenteral sites. Distribution: The plasma half-life of yclophosphamide is about 4 to 8 hours in adults and children. The plasma half-lives of the active C metabolites are not known. Cross-resistance, especially with structurally related cytotoxic agents, e.g., ifosfamide, as well as other alkylating agents, cannot be excluded. Absorption: Cyclophosphamide engages probably to the S-or G2-phase of the cell cycle. Metabolism: Following high-dose IV administration within the framework of allogeneic bone marrow transplantation, the plasma concentration of pure yclophosphamide follows linear first-order kinetics. Compared with conventional yclophosphamide C C therapy, there is an increase in inactive metabolites, indicating saturation of activating enzyme systems, but not of the stages of metabolism leading to inactive metabolites. During the course of high-dose yclophosphamide therapy over several days, C there is a decrease in the areas under the plasma concentration-time curve of the parent compound, probably due to auto- induction of microsomal metabolism activity.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Initial treatment is given by intravenous injection. After satisfactory remission has occurred, maintenance therapy with tablets is recommended. Individual dosage should take into account the general state of the patient and the white blood count. PHARMACOKINETIC :S Less than 20% of yclophosphamide is bound to plasma proteins. The protein binding of the metabolites of yclophosphamide C C is higher but less than 70%. To what extent the active metabolites protein bound, is not known. Use During Lactation: Cyclophosphamide is excreted in breast milk. Therefore, nursing should be discontinued while the patient is on Cyclophosphamide therapy. Cyclophosphamide is administered as an inactive prodrug that is activated in the liver. Use During Pregnancy: Cyclophosphamide can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman. Therefore, if this drug is used during pregnancy, or if the patient becomes pregnant while taking this drug, the patient should be apprised of the potential hazard to the fetus. Women of child bearing potential should be advised to avoid pregnancy during Cyclophosphamide therapy.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:19:52.587001+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5335-06,Cyclophosphamide,Cyclophos,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vpaxYEgbFzvt2x5bQ9nCreQo9MLFQ3x3/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5335-06_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1vpaxYEgbFzvt2x5bQ9nCreQo9MLFQ3x3_view_usp_drivesdk_a635d93e93.pdf,page_text/DRP-5335-06_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1vpaxYEgbFzvt2x5bQ9nCreQo9MLFQ3x3_view_usp_drivesdk_a635d93e93.json,a3124350407a525da55c62b9a371e84d70ecba0a828e2a07e9c048d1457bcbb2,437881,1,digital_text,"A white crystalline powder. Cyclophosphamide has been demonstrated to have a cytostatic effect in many tumour types. It remains to be shown whether the cytostatic effect is entirely dependent on the alkylation of DNA or other mechanisms such as inhibition of chromatin transformation processes or inhibition of DNA polymerases play a role. The metabolite acrolein has no antineoplastic activity, but is responsible for the adverse urotoxic effect. Cyclophosphamide is quickly and almost completely absorbed from parenteral sites. Metabolism: Cyclophosphamide is administered as an inactive prodrug that is activated in the liver. Elimination: Following high-dose IV administration within the framework of allogeneic bone marrow transplantation, the plasma concentration of pure Cyclophosphamide follows linear first- order kinetics. Compared with conventional Cyclophosphamide therapy, there is an increase in inactive metabolites, indicating saturation of activating enzyme systems, but not of the stages of metabolism leading to inactive metabolites. During the course of high- dose Cyclophosphamide therapy over several days, there is a decrease in the areas under the plasma concentration-time curve of the parent compound, probably due to auto-induction of microsomal metabolism activity. Cyclophosphamide and its metabolites are primarily excreted by the kidneys. Less than 20% of Cyclophosphamide is bound to plasma proteins. The protein binding of the metabolites of cyclophosphamide is higher but less than 70%. To what extent the active metabolites protein bound, is not known. Distribution: Cyclophosphamide is activated in the liver to the active metabolites 4-hydroxy-cyclophosphamide and aldofosfamide (tautomeric form of 4-hydroxy-cyclophosphamide) through phase I metabolism by cytochrome P450 (CYP) enzymes. Different CYP isozymes contribute to the bioactivation of Cyclophosphamide, including CYP2A6, 2B6, 2C9, 2C19 and 3A4, 2B6 in which it exhibits highest 4-hydroxylase activity. Detoxification is done mainly through glutathione-S-transferases (GSTA1, GSTP1) and alcohol dehydrogenase (ALDH1, ALDH3). Two to four hours after administration of Cyclophosphamide, the plasma concentrations of the active metabolites are maximal, after which a rapid decrease of plasma concentrations takes place. Cyclophosphamide is found in the cerebrospinal fluid and the mother's milk. Cyclophosphamide and metabolites can pass through the placenta. The plasma half-life of Cyclophosphamide is about 4 to 8 hours in adults and children. The plasma half-lives of the active metabolites are not known. Absorption:",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Used in the treatment of various forms of malignancies such as Burkitt’s and other non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas, multiple myeloma and mycosis fungoides. It is also used in gestational trophoblastic tumours and malignancies of the brain, breast, endometrium, lung, and ovary; in childhood malignancies such as neuroblastoma, retinoblastoma, Wilms’ tumour and in sarcomas and some leukemias. It is also indicated for the treatment of immune-mediated conditions such as severe rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus, vasculitis, dermatomyositis, and intestitular lung disease.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:19:55.624681+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5335-07,Cyclophosphamide,Phosphaxil,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1NCJ_6lXrUCohErduPn5DtBIyW21OF0iP/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5335-07_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1NCJ_6lXrUCohErduPn5DtBIyW21OF0iP_view_usp_drivesdk_24e090df7f.pdf,page_text/DRP-5335-07_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1NCJ_6lXrUCohErduPn5DtBIyW21OF0iP_view_usp_drivesdk_24e090df7f.json,8f790a510d1149c7ea114d86acf21836ee3a31087bbbdb72c3e156eb0cbe8376,453683,1,digital_text,"The plasma half-life of Cyclophosphamide is about 4 to 8 hours in adults and children. The plasma half-lives of the active metabolites are not known. The immunosuppressive effect of Cyclophosphamide is based on the fact that Cyclophosphamide has an inhibitory effect on B-cells, CD4 + T-cells and to a lesser extent on CD8 +-T-cells. In addition, it is assumed that Cyclophosphamide has an inhibitory effect on the suppressor that regulate the IgG2 class of antibodies. Pharmacodynamics:",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,Cyclophosphamide should not be taken by patients with known hypersensitivity to oxazaphosphorine and in cases of severe bone marrow depression.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:19:58.769227+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5335-08,Cyclophosphamide,Sykloget,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1qAOT7EokJ_z6gaQY-_wDVwu0AyhPAkr1/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5335-08_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1qAOT7EokJ_z6gaQY-_wDVwu0AyhPAkr1_view_usp_drivesdk_988f172635.pdf,page_text/DRP-5335-08_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1qAOT7EokJ_z6gaQY-_wDVwu0AyhPAkr1_view_usp_drivesdk_988f172635.json,ace0cad68d7916f2b6261eb3ab0a558e47f0f59949e32c5e12f6714337c16718,889635,1,digital_text,Cyclophosphamide engages probably to the S-or G2-phase of the cell cycle. equivalent to anhydrous Pharmacodynamics:,1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Used in the treatment of various forms of malignancies such as Burkitt’s and other non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas, multiple myeloma and mycosis fungoides. It is also used in gestational trophoblastic tumours and malignancies of the brain, breast, endometrium, lung, and ovary; in childhood malignancies such as neuroblastoma, retinoblastoma, Wilms’ tumour and in sarcomas and some leukemias. It is also indicated for the treatment of immune-mediated conditions such as severe rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus, vasculitis, dermatomyositis, and interstitial lung disease.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T16:20:02.308294+00:00
