PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5518-08,Cefixime (as trihydrate),Typhofix-Ds,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WwBJXDvFPXIMEIkGJdCBfOk64p7W-kBa/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5518-08_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1WwBJXDvFPXIMEIkGJdCBfOk64p7W-kBa_view_usp_drivesdk_072d592c00.pdf,page_text/DRP-5518-08_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1WwBJXDvFPXIMEIkGJdCBfOk64p7W-kBa_view_usp_drivesdk_072d592c00.json,163d74f669a5e394476bcccf989d3c7dc00bacf28dc9a37e9e6dad00543c6e4d,2260451,1,ocr,"White to light yellow/orange coloured, orange flavored, free flowing powder when reconstituted it gives yellow to orange coloured",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Foods, Drugs, Devices, and Cosmetics Act prohibits dispensing without prescription. For the treatment of infections due to sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph. Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction.",1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T17:09:40.198181+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5518-09,Cefixime (as trihydrate),Acecef,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1a1PiJHD2wk9wV9B9gDPKjxq-F_ud8k_k/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5518-09_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1a1PiJHD2wk9wV9B9gDPKjxq-F_ud8k_k_view_usp_drivesdk_19b48ebcb5.pdf,page_text/DRP-5518-09_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1a1PiJHD2wk9wV9B9gDPKjxq-F_ud8k_k_view_usp_drivesdk_19b48ebcb5.json,fad8030a3087e0fa4fceb8a38ca568c277e78b303aa0b8028654aa4e2db511f9,797865,1,digital_text,"Each 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) of the reconstituted suspension contains: Cefixime (as Trihydrate) USP ................ 100 mg Cefixime is a third generation cephalosporin with antibacterial activity similar to penicillins, carbacephems and cephamycins. Cefixime exerts its bactericidal activity by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. It binds to specific penicillin-binding proteins responsible for the synthesis of peptidoglycan, a heteropolymeric structure that gives the cell wall its mechanical stability. The final stage of peptidoglycan synthesis involves completion of the cross-linking of the terminal glycine residue of the pentaglycine bridge to the fourth residue of the pentapeptide. The transpeptidase that catalyzes this step is inhibited by cephalosporins. Thus, inhibition of the transpeptidase interrupts peptidoglycan synthesis, causing formation of defective cell walls and osmotically unstable spheroplasts and lysis of the bacteria. Only 40 to 50% of an oral dose of Cefixime is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, whether taken before or after meals, although the rate of absorption may be decreased in the presence of food. Cefixime is better absorbed from oral suspension than from tablets. Absorption is fairly slow; peak plasma concentrations of 2 to 3 micrograms/mL and 3.7 to 4.6 micrograms/mL have been reported between 2 and 6 hours after single doses of 200 and 400 mg, respectively. The plasma half-life is usually about 3 to 4 hours and may be prolonged when there is renal impairment. About 65% of Cefixime is bound to plasma proteins. Information on the distribution of Cefixime in body tissues and fluids is limited. It crosses the placenta. Relatively high concentrations may be achieved in bile and urine. About 20% of an oral dose (or 50% of an absorbed dose) is excreted unchanged in the urine within 24 hours. Up to 60% may be eliminated by non renal mechanism; there is no evidence of metabolism but some is probably excreted in the feces from bile. It is not substantially removed by dialysis.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,6 months up to 1 year: 7.5 mL daily. Children 1 to 4 years: 10 mL daily. The usual course of treatment is 7 days. This may be continued for up to 14 days if required. Or as prescribed by the physician. Children over 6 months: 8 mg/kg daily in 1 to 2 divided doses; or Children 5 to10 years: 20 mL daily.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T17:09:43.751780+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5518-10,Cefixime (as trihydrate),Soniceph,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UlxDjmnHQ6TvaQx11MH6kQNK73_ZBLVP/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5518-10_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1UlxDjmnHQ6TvaQx11MH6kQNK73_ZBLVP_view_usp_drivesdk_d8139f40ee.pdf,page_text/DRP-5518-10_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1UlxDjmnHQ6TvaQx11MH6kQNK73_ZBLVP_view_usp_drivesdk_d8139f40ee.json,70765e9d00db8c6cb7afd7ce4409910d9eef816a88c93cf4bdc98e6ee95bbd35,1048772,1,digital_text,"Cefixime for oral suspension is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to Cefixime or other cephalosporins. Only 40 to 50% of an oral dose of Cefixime is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, whether taken before or af ter meals, although the rate of absorption may be decreased in the presence of food. Cefixime is better absorbed from oral suspension than from tablets. Absorption is fairly slow; peak plasma concentrations of 2 to 3 micrograms/mL and 3.7 to 4.6 micrograms/mL have been reported between 2 and 6 hours after single doses of 200 and 400 mg, respectively. The plasma half-life is usually about 3 to 4 hours and may be prolonged when there is renal impairment. About 65% of Cefixime is bound to plasma proteins. Information on the distribution of Cefixime in body tissues and fluids is limited. It crosses the placenta. Relatively high concentrations may be achieved in bile and urine. About 20% of an oral dose (or 50% of an absorbed dose) is excreted unchanged in the urine within 24 hours. Up to 60% may be eliminated by non renal mechanism; there is no evidence of metabolism but some is probably excreted in the feces from bile. It is not substantially removed by dialysis.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,,1,1,INDICATIONS:,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T17:09:47.211039+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5518-11,Cefixime (as trihydrate),Dyruxim-100,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HKjcdzSA45mRP1-9p-khN-S6lpPktKXZ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5518-11_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1HKjcdzSA45mRP1-9p-khN-S6lpPktKXZ_view_usp_drivesdk_9b7a2b4bfc.pdf,page_text/DRP-5518-11_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1HKjcdzSA45mRP1-9p-khN-S6lpPktKXZ_view_usp_drivesdk_9b7a2b4bfc.json,8f4e70f877e243874618e413fec4da37e28a82fbbfa9cc1c3e951a94868b8f58,1693728,1,digital_text,"Cefixime (as Trihydrate) USP ................ 100 mg White to light yellow/orange coloured, orange flavored, free flowing powder when reconstituted it gives yellow to orange coloured suspension. Only 40 to 50% of an oral dose of Cefixime is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, whether taken before or af ter meals, although the rate of absorption may be decreased in the presence of food. Cefixime is better absorbed from oral suspension than from tablets. Absorption is fairly slow; peak plasma concentrations of 2 to 3 micrograms/mL and 3.7 to 4.6 micrograms/mL have been reported between 2 and 6 hours after single doses of 200 and 400 mg, respectively. The plasma half-life is usually about 3 to 4 hours and may be prolonged when there is renal impairment. About 65% of Cefixime is bound to plasma proteins. Information on the distribution of Cefixime in body tissues and fluids is limited. It crosses the placenta. Relatively high concentrations may be achieved in bile and urine. About 20% of an oral dose (or 50% of an absorbed dose) is excreted unchanged in the urine within 24 hours. Up to 60% may be eliminated by non renal mechanism; there is no evidence of metabolism but some is probably excreted in the feces from bile. It is not substantially removed by dialysis. Cefixime is a third generation cephalosporin with antibacterial activity similar to penicillins, carbacephems and cephamycins. Cefixime exerts its bactericidal activity by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. It binds to specific penicillin-binding proteins responsible for the synthesis of peptidoglycan, a heteropolymeric structure that gives the cell wall its mechanical stability. The final stage of peptidoglycan synthesis involves completion of the cross-linking of the terminal glycine residue of the pentaglycine bridge to the fourth residue of the pentapeptide. The transpeptidase that catalyzes this step is inhibited by cephalosporins. Thus, inhibition of the transpeptidase interrupts peptidoglycan synthesis, causing formation of defective cell walls and osmotically unstable spheroplasts and lysis of the bacteria.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,The usual course of treatment is 7 days. This may be continued for up to 14 days if required. Or as prescribed by the physician.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T17:09:51.106448+00:00
