PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-5518-19,Cefixime (as trihydrate),Cefitrene 100,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1w7kJm4ntFo27LNAiLRjTTZ6fYjxBE7WR/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-5518-19_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1w7kJm4ntFo27LNAiLRjTTZ6fYjxBE7WR_view_usp_drivesdk_525f5fc545.pdf,page_text/DRP-5518-19_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1w7kJm4ntFo27LNAiLRjTTZ6fYjxBE7WR_view_usp_drivesdk_525f5fc545.json,60288ad878ab1978d010c7b1a21e8380b1e924f8470f6b3f513b025a880c97e3,1236673,1,digital_text,"The dose of Cefixime Suspension should be adjusted in patients with renal impairment as well as those undergoing continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) and hemodialysis (HD). Patients on dialysis should be monitored carefully. Cefixime should be prescribed with caution in individuals with a history of gastrointestinal disease, particularly colitis. Cephalosporins may be associated with a fall in prothrombin activity. Those at risk include patients with renal or hepatic impairment, or poor nutritional state, as well as patients receiving a protracted course of antimicrobial therapy, and patients previously stabilized on anticoagulant therapy. Prothrombin time should be monitored in patients at risk and exogenous vitamin K administered as indicated. use of Cefixime and other broadspectrum antibiotics (including macrolides, semisynthetic penicillins, and cephalosporins); therefore, it is important to consider this diagnosis in patients who develop diarrhea in association with the use of antibiotics. Store at temperatures not exceeding 30ºC.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,6 months up to 1 year: 7.5 mL daily. Children 1 to 4 years: 10 mL daily. The usual course of treatment is 7 days. This may be continued for up to 14 days if required. Children 5 to10 years: 20 mL daily.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T17:10:23.619384+00:00
