PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-6743-02,Rosuvastatin (as calcium),Rovastin,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1bc20RqOjj7Tp-W2POr8EZrSRfftCUa9a/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-6743-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1bc20RqOjj7Tp-W2POr8EZrSRfftCUa9a_view_usp_drivesdk_0124b81e1a.pdf,page_text/DRP-6743-02_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1bc20RqOjj7Tp-W2POr8EZrSRfftCUa9a_view_usp_drivesdk_0124b81e1a.json,5fa5480131b16c6bf09ef161d7f239a9eaa3c520a98705cfb4b8994866ae9585,868115,2,ocr,"Rosuvastatin 10 mg film-coated tablet is an old rose, round, biconvex and plain on both sides. PHARMACOKINETICS Peak plasma concentrations of rosuvastatin were reached 5 hours following oral dosing. The absolute bioavailability of rosuvastatin is approximately 20%. Rosuvastatin is 90% bound to plasma proteins, mostly albumin. This binding is reversible and independent of plasma concentrations. Mean volume of distribution at steady-state of rosuvastatin is approximately 134 liters. Rosuvastatin is not extensively metabolized; approximately 10% of radiolabeled dose is recovered as metabolite. The major metabolite is N-desmethyl rosuvastatin, which is formed principally by cytochrome P450 isoenzyme CYP2C9. Overall, greater than 90% of active plasma HMG-CoA diict: if inhibitory ity is accounted for rosuvastatin in the parent compound. Following oral administration, rosuvastatin and its metabolites are primarily excreted in feces (90%). The elimination half-life of rosuvastatin is approximately 19 hours.",1,1,DESCRIPTION:,"For the of management hyperlipidaemias, including — primary hypercholesterolaemia (Type lla), mixed dyslipidaemia (Type Hb) hypertriglyceridaemia (Type IV). It may also be used in the patients with homozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia",1,1,INDICATIONS:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T18:35:58.641417+00:00
