ute iron poisoning may occur within 10 to 60 minutes or be delayed for several hours. Initial clinical manifestations may encompass acute gastrointestinal irritation, including Manufactured by: epigastric pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea of green and subsequently tarry stools, melena and PT. ABBOTT INDONESIA hematemesis which may be associated with drowsiness, pallor, cyanosis, lassitude, seizures, shock and JI. Raya Jakarta Bogor Km. 37 coma. Hepatic necrosis and hepatic failure may develop. Because of potential toxic effects of overdosage, Cimanggis, Depok, 16415, Indonesia immediate medical attention is warranted. Iron poisoning should be treated by emptying the stomach via ipecac-induced vomiting or preferably, by Manufactured for: ABBOTT LABORATORIES gastric lavage with a large bore tube. If the patient has experienced multiple episodes of vomiting, especially if the vomitus contains blood, ipecac syrup should not be administered. Venice Corporate Center, 8 Turin St. Vomitus should be examined for returned Gradumet tablets. If sufficient tablets are not returned, the Mckinley Town Center, Fort Bonifacio possibility of whole gut lavage with 0.9% sodium chloride solution plus a saline cathartic should be Taguig City, Philippines considered. Surgical removal of iron tablets, which are visible in abdominal radiographs, may be required if other means of removing the drug are unsuccessful. CCDS-0046 v2.0 The best method for assessing the severity of an iron ingestion is to measure the serum iron and the total Date of Revision of Patient Information Leaflet iron binding capacity (TIBC). If the serum iron level is greater than TIBC, the potential for systemic toxicity exists. Serum iron and total iron-binding capacity levels may be used as guidelines for use of 23 Aug 2012 deferoxamine, an agent used to chelate elemental iron. Chelation therapy with deferoxamine should be considered when the following conditions exist: 1. A potentially lethal dose (180 to 300 mg/kg or more) of elemental iron has been ingested; 2. Serum iron concentrations are greater than 400 to 500 yg/dL. 3. Serum iron concentration exceed total iron binding capacity; and/or 4. Patients have severe symptoms of iron intoxication such as coma, shock or seizure. Hemodialysis is of little value in the treatment of iron intoxication. Supportive treatment, including suction and maintenance of airway; correction of acidosis and control of shock and dehydration with intravenous fluids or blood, oxygen and vasopressors, should be administered as required. Cc) Abbott [Page 2]",1,1,Description of the Product,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T18:47:28.123830+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-6992,Amlodipine(As Besilate),Diadipine,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/12MUiw15R5mitRYAEsq9F-T9lWHz0rZyS/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-6992_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12MUiw15R5mitRYAEsq9F-T9lWHz0rZyS_view_usp_drivesdk_8a9aa92b40.pdf,page_text/DRP-6992_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12MUiw15R5mitRYAEsq9F-T9lWHz0rZyS_view_usp_drivesdk_8a9aa92b40.json,f9da89e6de295e2cb0a7fc8dc1237636e72fef1935000bc7ddd119a77a82317b,1980390,1,ocr,"animals. No adequate or well-controlled studies have been done in humans. Amlodipine should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk of the fetus. It is not known whether Amlodipine (DIADIPINE) is a white round, biconvex tablet with score on one side and company logo on the other side. amlodipine is excreted into human milk, so clinicians should consider risk/benefit ratio for breastfeeding women.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Amlodipine (DIADIPINE) 10 mg Tablet Blister pack by 20’s (Box of 100’s)— DRP — 6992 Treatment of hypertension and prophylaxis of angina. DATE OF RENEWAL AUTHORIZATION: March 23, 2022",1,1,INDICATION:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T18:47:42.179863+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-6993,Guaifenesin + Phenylpropanolamine Hydrochloride,Ritusin Plus,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/18Xz1GrTEdS4_-4XjOEkiw38YZ667zvwq/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-6993_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18Xz1GrTEdS4_-4XjOEkiw38YZ667zvwq_view_usp_drivesdk_ac4c1e0644.pdf,page_text/DRP-6993_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18Xz1GrTEdS4_-4XjOEkiw38YZ667zvwq_view_usp_drivesdk_ac4c1e0644.json,2d66aa5c2f54fae24908e4b6567dafa3f8fd34cee70b5e1320daf53157d9aac2,982078,1,ocr,"Ritusin Plus is clear, red syrup with sweet cherry odor and taste contains ina 60mL/120mL amber bottle. Whatis in the medicine? s loosen congestion in your chest and throat, making it easier to cough out through your mouth. Phenylpropanolamine HCl is a decongestant, it works Ritusin Plus syrup contains Guaifenesin and Phenylpropanolamine HCI. Guaifenesin is an expectorant. It hel b y constricting (shrinking) blood vessels (veins and arteries) in your body. Constriction of blood vessels in your sinuses, nose, and chest allows drainage of those areas, which decreases congestion. Strength of the medicine: Each 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) contains: Guaifenesin 100 mg Phenylpropanolamine Hydrochloride ............... 6.25 mg \ Ethyl Alcohol 2.3% = Whatis this usedtTor sr = Combination of Guaifenesin and Phenylpropanolamine HCl is used for the relief of nasal congestion and dry non-productive cough associated with the common cold and other respiratory allergies, for persistent cough due to influenza, bronchitis, and pharyngitis. How much and how often should you use this medicine? Infant: 2.5mL (1/2 teaspoon) Children: 5.0mL (1 teaspoonful) Adult: 15mL (1 tablespoonful) Every 4 hours, or as prescribed by the physician. When should you not take this medicine? + Ifyou are allergic to any ingredients in the medicine. + Patients with diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular diseases, hyperthyroidism, hepatic impairment and asthma unless recommended by a doctor. + Ifyou are pregnant or breastfeeding. Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: + This medication may impair your thinking or reactions. Use caution when driving, operating machinery, or performing other hazardous activities. + Donottake Phenylpropanolamine HCl for longer than 7 days if your condition does not improve or if your symptoms are accompanied by a high — fever. + Phenylpropanolamine HCI must use with caution in patients with high blood pressure, toxic goiter, benign prostatic hypertrophy, heart rate irregularity, glaucoma and in those taking antidepressants. + Donottake more than the recommended dose. + Donotuse after the expiry date stated. Undesirable effects of this medicine: — _ + Common side effects may include dizziness, weakness, mild headache; diarrhea, upset stomach; dry mouth, nose, or throat; feeling nervous, restless, irritable, or anxious; or sleep problem (insomnia). + More serious side effects may encounter, consult your doctor immediately if you are experiencing an allergic reaction (difficulty breathing; closing of your throat; swelling of your lips, tongue, or face; or hives); seizures; unusual behavior or hallucinations; or an irregular or fast heartbeat. What other medicine or food should be avoided? - Taking certain MAO inhibitors with this medication may cause a serious possibly fatal) drug interaction. Avoid taking isocarboxazid, methylene blue, moclobemide, phenelzine, procarbazine, rasagiline, selegiline, or tranylcypromine during treatment with this medication. A very dangerous drug interaction could occur, leading to serious hypertensive crisis(sudden, severe increase in blood pressure that can lead to stroke). - Other medicines that may also interact with Phenylpropanolamine HCl includes furazolidone: guanethidine; indomethacin; methyldopa; bromocriptine; caffeine in cola, tea, coffee, chocolate, and other products; theophylline; tricyclic antidepressants such as amitriptyline, doxepin, and nortriptyline; other commonly used tricyclic antidepresants, including amoxapine, clomipramine, desipramine, imipramine, protriptyline, and trimipramine; phenothiazines such as chlorpromazine, thioridazine, and prochlorperazine; and other commonly used phenothiazines, including fluphenazine, perphenazine, mesoridazine, and trifluoperazine. What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Do not double the dose. Signs and symptoms of overdose: Dextromethorphan HBr overdose symptoms: + Excitation, confusion, and respiratory depression may occur after overdosage. Phenylpropanolamine HCI overdose symptoms: + Extreme tiredness, sweating, dizzines, a slow heart beat, and acoma. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? art s - x notexceeding 30°C. Protect from direct sunlight. pt se When should you consult your doctor? -If symptoms persist and new symptoms occur, consult your doctor. -Tell your doctor if you have the following before taking this medication: diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular diseases, hyperthyroidism, hepatic impairment and asthma. Ps | diatel “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph ”. Seek at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. DRP-6993 Date of Authorization: February 09, 2018 Patient Information Leaflet Date of Revision: December 03, 2018 Manufactured by the Distributed by: Marketing Authorization Holder:",1,1,Description of the product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T18:47:52.595194+00:00
