pairment and asthma unless recommended by a doctor. + Ifyou are pregnant or breastfeeding. Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: + This medication may impair your thinking or reactions. Use caution when driving, operating machinery, or performing other hazardous activities. + Donottake Phenylpropanolamine HCl for longer than 7 days if your condition does not improve or if your symptoms are accompanied by a high — fever. + Phenylpropanolamine HCI must use with caution in patients with high blood pressure, toxic goiter, benign prostatic hypertrophy, heart rate irregularity, glaucoma and in those taking antidepressants. + Donottake more than the recommended dose. + Donotuse after the expiry date stated. Undesirable effects of this medicine: — _ + Common side effects may include dizziness, weakness, mild headache; diarrhea, upset stomach; dry mouth, nose, or throat; feeling nervous, restless, irritable, or anxious; or sleep problem (insomnia). + More serious side effects may encounter, consult your doctor immediately if you are experiencing an allergic reaction (difficulty breathing; closing of your throat; swelling of your lips, tongue, or face; or hives); seizures; unusual behavior or hallucinations; or an irregular or fast heartbeat. What other medicine or food should be avoided? - Taking certain MAO inhibitors with this medication may cause a serious possibly fatal) drug interaction. Avoid taking isocarboxazid, methylene blue, moclobemide, phenelzine, procarbazine, rasagiline, selegiline, or tranylcypromine during treatment with this medication. A very dangerous drug interaction could occur, leading to serious hypertensive crisis(sudden, severe increase in blood pressure that can lead to stroke). - Other medicines that may also interact with Phenylpropanolamine HCl includes furazolidone: guanethidine; indomethacin; methyldopa; bromocriptine; caffeine in cola, tea, coffee, chocolate, and other products; theophylline; tricyclic antidepressants such as amitriptyline, doxepin, and nortriptyline; other commonly used tricyclic antidepresants, including amoxapine, clomipramine, desipramine, imipramine, protriptyline, and trimipramine; phenothiazines such as chlorpromazine, thioridazine, and prochlorperazine; and other commonly used phenothiazines, including fluphenazine, perphenazine, mesoridazine, and trifluoperazine. What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Do not double the dose. Signs and symptoms of overdose: Dextromethorphan HBr overdose symptoms: + Excitation, confusion, and respiratory depression may occur after overdosage. Phenylpropanolamine HCI overdose symptoms: + Extreme tiredness, sweating, dizzines, a slow heart beat, and acoma. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? art s - x notexceeding 30°C. Protect from direct sunlight. pt se When should you consult your doctor? -If symptoms persist and new symptoms occur, consult your doctor. -Tell your doctor if you have the following before taking this medication: diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular diseases, hyperthyroidism, hepatic impairment and asthma. Ps | diatel “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph ”. Seek at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. DRP-6993 Date of Authorization: February 09, 2018 Patient Information Leaflet Date of Revision: December 03, 2018 Manufactured by the Distributed by: Marketing Authorization Holder:",1,1,Description of the product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T18:48:01.728297+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-6994,Amlodipine(As Besilate),Diadipine,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/17EPHXi5vah2HjUN0TLy_DO_6b2p0o6dq/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-6994_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_17EPHXi5vah2HjUN0TLy_DO_6b2p0o6dq_view_usp_drivesdk_49262ec44b.pdf,page_text/DRP-6994_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_17EPHXi5vah2HjUN0TLy_DO_6b2p0o6dq_view_usp_drivesdk_49262ec44b.json,be5d77e32adb2717e40e5a7f6eb1e7b3021ac05f8d5f607ab91bc2aa813808de,1980390,1,ocr,"animals. No adequate or well-controlled studies have been done in humans. Amlodipine should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk of the fetus. It is not known whether Amlodipine (DIADIPINE) is a white round, biconvex tablet with score on one side and company logo on the other side. amlodipine is excreted into human milk, so clinicians should consider risk/benefit ratio for breastfeeding women.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,"Amlodipine (DIADIPINE) 10 mg Tablet Blister pack by 20’s (Box of 100’s)— DRP — 6992 Treatment of hypertension and prophylaxis of angina. DATE OF RENEWAL AUTHORIZATION: March 23, 2022",1,1,INDICATION:,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T18:48:16.019645+00:00
