ss advised by a physician. -Severe skin lesions such as SJS (Stevens-Johnson Syndrome) and Lyell's syndrome have been reported rarely with the administration of mucolytics such as Ambroxol. These may be due to the severity of the underlying disease or concomitant medication. If new skin or mucosal lesions appear, discontinue Ambroxol and consult a physician immediately. -Do not use after the expiry date stated on the label. Undesirable effects of this medicine: Ambroxol is generally well tolerated. Hypersensitivity reaction such as mild stomach disturbance such as_ heartburn, dyspepsia, diarrhea, nausea and vomiting may occur. Rash/urticaria, angioedema (rapid swelling of the skin), anaphylactic reactions and other allergic reactions have been reported. What other medicine or food should be avoided while taking this medicine? There were no reports of adverse drug interactions with other medications. Administration of Ambroxol with antibiotics (Amoxicillin, Cefuroxime, Erithromycin) leads to higher antibiotic concentration in bronchopulmonary secretions and in sputum. Tell your doctor about other medicines you are taking, especially other medicines for cough and cold or allergy. What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Signs and symptoms of overdose: Overdose symptoms of Ambroxol leads to extension of undesirable effects such as heartburn, dyspepsia, diarrhea, nausea and vomiting, and other anaphylactic reactions. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Protect from direct sunlight. When should you consult your doctor? If symptoms persist, consult your doctor “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.g