PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7067,Paracetamol+Phenylpropanolamine Hydrochloride+Chlorphenamine Maleate,Diaflu,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/18QAULeDJQP2aL-jcQdL_1IEjNQmycdCQ/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7067_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18QAULeDJQP2aL-jcQdL_1IEjNQmycdCQ_view_usp_drivesdk_b3acf8cef6.pdf,page_text/DRP-7067_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_18QAULeDJQP2aL-jcQdL_1IEjNQmycdCQ_view_usp_drivesdk_b3acf8cef6.json,0552c73215c5ac1848c08e842ab14ffea839b786a6028029def9ffab29a817d8,2468302,1,ocr,"The medicine is an orange colored syrup with orange flavor and aroma contained in a 60 mL amber bottle. What is in the medicine? Symeril is a combination of Paracetamol, Phenylpropanolamine Hydrochloride and Chlorphenamine Maleate. Paracetamol has analgesic and anti-pyretic properties and weak anti-inflammatory activity. Chlorphenamine Maleate is an anti-histamine used for the symptomatic relief of allergic conditions. Phenylpropanolamine HCl is a decongestant. It works by constricting (shrinking) blood vessels (veins and arteries) in your body. Constriction of blood vessels in your sinuses, nose, and chest allows drainage of those areas, which decreases congestion. Strength of the medicine: Each 5 mL ( ar 4 teaspoontul) contains: 125 mg Phenylpropanolamine Hydrochloride .......... Chlorphenamine h 0.5 mg Ethyl Alcohol 2.1% What is this medicine used for? Paracetamol + Pher m pro} pan jolamine HCI+ Chlo enamine Maleate (SYMERIL) is used for relief of symptoms associated with common colds or fu Vialealte ee zt flere aan = such as headache, a ih lergic rhinitis, smusitis, hay fe fever or other upper respiratory allérgiés, nasal corigestion, sneezing, runny nose, fever and minor aches and pains. How much and how often should you use this medicine? Children: 2-6 years old: 5 mL (1 teaspoonful) 7-12 years old: 10 mL (2 teaspoonfuls) 12 years old: 15 mL (1 tablespoonful) Every 6 hours, or as prescribed by the physician When should you not take this medicine? You may not be able to take this medicine, or you may require a dosage adjustment or special monitoring during treatment if you have any of the following: -kidney disease -liver disease -diabetes -glaucoma -any type of heart disease or high blood pressure -thyroid disease -emphysema or chronic bronchitis, or -difficulty urinating or an enlarged prostate Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: -When taking this medicine use caution when driving, operating machinery, or performing other hazardous activities. Combination of Paracetamol+ Phenylpropanolamine HCl+ Chlorphenamine Maleate may cause dizziness or drowsiness. If you experience dizziness or drowsiness, avoid these activities. Use alcohol cautiously. Alcohol may increase drowsiness and dizziness while taking Paracetamol/Chlorphenamine/Phenylpropanolamine HCI. -Alcohol may also cause damage to the liver when it is taken with Paracetamol -Do not take this medicine for longer than 7 to 10 days in a row. - -Itis not known whether Paracetamol+ Pheny| wee opanolamine HCl+ Chlorphenamine Maleate will harm an unborn baby. Also, this medication passes aviomsane eee mente ‘nto bréast milk and can harm’a nursing Baby"" 5 0 not take this medication without first falking f6 your décor F you'aré breast teeding or prégnant. Undesirable effects of this medicine: Less serious side effects may be more likely to occur that includes dryness of the eyes, nose, and mouth; drowsiness or dizziness; blurred vision; difficulty urinating; or excitation in children. If you experience any of the following rare but serious side effects, stop taking the medicine and seek emergency medical attention or notify your doctor immediately: an allergic reaction (difficulty breathing; closing of your throat; swelling of your lips, tongue, or face; or hives); liver damage (yellowing of the skin or eyes, nausea, abdominal pain or discomfort, unusual bleeding or bruising, or severe fatigue); blood problems (easy or unusual bleeding or bruising); or low blood sugar (fatigue, increased hunger or thirst, dizziness, or fainting). Phenylpropanolamine HCl, has been associated with an increased risk of hemorrhagic stroke (bleeding into the brain or into tissue surrounding the brain) in women. Men may also be at risk. What other medicine or food should be avoided? Combination of Paracetamol+ Phenylpropanolamine HCI+ Chlorphenamine Maleate may increase the effects of other drugs that causes drowsiness, including antidepressants, alcohol, other antihistamines, pain relievers, anxiety medicines, seizure medicines, and muscle relaxants. Dangerous sedation, dizziness, or drowsiness may occur if Paracetamol+ Phenylpropanolamine HCl+ Chlorphenamine Maleate is taken with any of these",1,1,Description of the product:,"Do not take this medicine if you have taken a monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI) such as isocarboxazid, phenelzine, or tranylcypromine in the last 14 days. A very dangerous drug interaction could occur, leading to serious hypertensive crisis (sudden, severe increase in blood pressure that can lead to stroke). What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Do not take a double dose of this medication. Signs and symptoms of overdose: Symptoms of Paracetamol+ Phenylpropanolamine HCI+ Chlorphenamine Maleate overdose include a dry mouth, large pupils, flushing, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhea, seizures, confusion, sweating, and an irregular heartbeat. neawer ai = Soc eee a rata a S i ert Senet ‘What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Protect from direct sunlight. When should you consult your doctor? If symptoms persist and any undesirable effect occurs, consult your doctor. “For suspected adve! d 7 rse drug r ‘eaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph”. I See! | attention ly at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. DRP-7067 Date of Renewal Authorization: July 21, 2022 Patient Information Leaflet Date of Revision: December 13, 2022 Manufactured by the Marketing Authorization Holder: Distributed by:",1,1,medications.,medium,needs_human_review,2026-06-30T18:52:43.087033+00:00
