PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7137,Sodium Citrate + Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide + Maleate,Menthodex-DM,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-Rrl-IU6Yf94nFe-VlB_tjFqpiCvyzy7/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7137_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1-Rrl-IU6Yf94nFe-VlB_tjFqpiCvyzy7_view_usp_drivesdk_1a1939feb7.pdf,page_text/DRP-7137_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1-Rrl-IU6Yf94nFe-VlB_tjFqpiCvyzy7_view_usp_drivesdk_1a1939feb7.json,2468bece0498ea2f550d232da965b613816101968480f8a36f8d7cd458b75e85,2273872,1,ocr,"The medicine is a clear red syrup with sweet cherry flavor, aroma and taste contained in a 60 mL and 120 mL amber round bottle. What is in the medicine? Menthodex-DM contains: - Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide used as control of cough associated with common colds, influenza, allergy and other respiratory infections, inhalation of irritating substance and psychogenic cough. - Phenylpropanolamine HCI which is a decongestant. It works by constricting (shrinking) blood vessels (veins and arteries) in your body. Constriction of blood vessels in your sinuses, nose and chest allows drainage of those areas, which decreases congestion - Chlorphenamine Maleate that is an antihistamine used for the symptomatic relief of allergic conditions. Strength of the medicine: Each 5 mL contains: Sodium Citrate, USP 180 mg Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide 15 mg Phenylpropanolamine HCl . 15 mg Chlorphenamine Maleate 2m Ethyl Alcohol 0.51% What is this medicine used for? Suppresses dry cough and promotes expectoration in productive cough associated with asthma, pneumonia, bronchitis, pertussis and other respiratory diseases allergic or non-allergic in nature. Also used as antitussive, decongestant and antihistamine. How much and how often should you use this medicine? Children: 1-9 years old. 2.5 - 5 mL (1/2-1 teaspoonful) Adults. ...5 - 10 mL (1-2 teaspoonfuls) Every 4 hours or as prescribed by the physici an. When should you not take this medicine? Use with caution in patients with a history of liver disorder, bronchial asthma and cardiac disorders. Increasing dosage can lead to psychotic reactions, cardiovascular changes and precipitation of anginal attacks. Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: Antihistamines may cause drowsiness and dulling of mental alertness. Patients undergoing treatment with these drugs should not take charge of vehicles, other means of transportation or machinery where loss of attention may lead to accidents. Undesirable effects of this medicine: It appears to be rare and may include dizziness and gastrointestinal disturbances, CNS depression, drowsiness, headache, psychomotor impairment, and antimuscarinic effects such as dry mouth, thickened respiratory tract secretions, blurred vision, urinary difficulty or retention, constipation, and increased gastric reflux. It also may cause nervousness, restlessness, insomnia/sleep disturbances, feeling of uneasiness (anxiety), confusion, high blood pressure, rapid heartbeat (palpitation), chest tightness, muscle shaking (tremor), agitation, irritability, aggressiveness (particularly in young children) and nausea. What other medicine or food should be avoided while taking this medicine? _——— Dextromethorphan HBr - Amiodarone, Haloperidol, Propafenone, Quinidine, SSRI’s and Thioridazine Phenylpropanolamine HCI: - Do not take Phenylpropanolamine if you have taken a monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI) such as isocarboxazid, phenelzine or tranylcypromine in the last 14 days. A very dangerous drug interaction could occur, leading to serious hypertensive crisis (sudden, severe increase in blood pressure that can lead to stroke). - It may also enhance the sedative effects of Alcohol, Barbiturates, Hypnotics, Opioid Analgesics, Anxiolytic Sedatives, and Antipsychotics. - Amantadine with Phenylpropanolamine is reportedly causes severe psychosis. - Other medicines that may also interact with Phenylpropanolamine includes furazolidone; guanethidine; indomethacin; methyldopa; bromocriptine; caffeine in cola, tea, coffee, chocolate, and other products; theophylline ; tricyclic antidepressants such as amitriptyline, doxepin and nortriptyline; other commonly used tricyclic antidepressants, including amoxapine, clomipramine, desipramine, imipramine, protriptyline and trimipramine; phenothiazines such as chlorpromazine, thioridazine and prochlorperazine; and other commonly used phenothiazines, including fluphenazine, perphenazine, mesoridazine, and trifluoperazine. Chlorphenamine Maleate - may enhance the sedative effects of Alcohol, Barbiturates, Hypnotics, Opioid Analgesics, Anxiolytic, Sedative and Antipsychotics. What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose, skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Do not double the dose. Signs and symptoms of overdose: Dextromethorphan HBr - excitation, confusion and respiratory depression may occur after overdosage. Phenylpropanolamine HCI - extreme tiredness, sweating, dizziness, a slow heart beat and a coma. Chlorphenamine Maleate - Ataxia, excitement, tremors, psychoses, hallucinations and convulsions; hyperpyrexia may also occur. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C Protect from direct sunlight. When should you consult your doctor? If symptoms persist, consult your doctor. Eee “For suspected adverse dru: eaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph”. e! d | attention i ; ig ly at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. DRP-7137 Date of Renewal Authorization: December 11, 2018 Patient Information Leaflet Date of Revision: April 03, 2023 Manufactured by the @ Distributed by: Marketing Authorization Holder: = § REJ DIAMOND",1,1,Description of the product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T18:58:45.528573+00:00
