PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7145,Paracetamol+Phenylephrine Hydrochloride+Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide,Mucolem Plus,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1YMnQBmo01xRPvAZOrQjivJ40yMyBBuBp/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7145_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1YMnQBmo01xRPvAZOrQjivJ40yMyBBuBp_view_usp_drivesdk_a54672b2b9.pdf,page_text/DRP-7145_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1YMnQBmo01xRPvAZOrQjivJ40yMyBBuBp_view_usp_drivesdk_a54672b2b9.json,23fb6c256a7e35dbccfeb310dbb213a6d0e6cfcd82ca40d987c8d162b8db0ef6,1201736,1,ocr,"Moclobemide and others may result in hypertensive crisis. Awhite colored powder contained in a dark green (cap)/light green (body) capsule size # 0 in by 10’s blister pack, box of 100’s. What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose, skip the missed dose and What is in the medicine? continue with the scheduled dosing. Do not double the dose. Mucolem Plus contains Paracetamol, Phenylephrine Hydrochloride and Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide. Paracetamol is a pain reliever and fever reducer. Phenylephrine Hydrochloride is a decongestant that shrinks Signs and symptoms of overdose: blood vessels in the nasal passages. Dilated blood vessels can cause nasal congestion (stuffy nose). Common signs and symptoms happen during each stage of a Paracetamol overdose. Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide is a cough suppressant; it affects the signals in the brain that trigger cough + First 24 hours: Nausea, vomiting, stomach pain, and loss of appetite, paleness, tiredness, and sweating. reflex. * 24 to 72 hours after the overdose: Pain in your upper right side, dark urine, urinating less often than usual, skin and the whites of your eyes turn yellow. Strength of the medicine: * 72 to 96 hours after the overdose: Blood in your urine, fever, lightheadedness, or fainting, fast breathing or trouble Each capsule contains: breathing, extreme weakness or tiredness, feeling very hungry, or shaking, blurred vision, a fast heartbeat, or Paracetamol 325 mg headache that will not go away, trouble staying awake, confusion, coma. Phenylephrine Hydrochloride... 25 mg Phenylephrine HCI overdose symptoms: Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide. 10mg + Anxiety, nervousness, or insomnia, nausea and vomiting, very high blood pressure, seizures, fever or headache, rapid or slow heart rate, bleeding in the brain (cerebral hemorrhage), psychosis (such as having hallucinations), What is this medicine used for? kidney failure, muscle damage, high blood sugar (hyperglycemia). Paracetamol, Phenylephrine HCI, and Dextromethorphan HBr combination is used for the treatment of cough due Dextromethorphan HBr overdose symptoms: + Excitation, confusion, and respiratory depression may occur after overdosage. to the common cold, upper respiratory infection or irritation including allergic rhinitis, headache, runny nose, fever, nasal congestion and minor aches and pain. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How much and how often should you use this medicine? Adult: 1 capsule every 6 hours, or as prescribed by the physician. How should you keep this medicine? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. When should you not take this medicine? + Patients with diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular diseases, hyperthyroidism, hepatic impairment and asthma Protect from direct sunlight. unless recommended by a doctor. + If you are pregnant or breastfeeding. When should you consult your doctor? + If you are allergic to any ingredients in the medicine. -If symptoms persist and new symptoms occur, consult your doctor. -Tell your doctor if you have the following before taking this medication: diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: diseases, hyperthyroidism, hepatic impairment and asthma. * This medication may impair your thinking or reactions. Be careful if you drive or do anything that requires you to be alert. “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph”. Seek medical attention + Avoid drinking alcohol. It may increase your risk of liver damage while you are taking Paracetamol. immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. + Ask a doctor before using any other cold, allergy, pain, or sleep medication. Paracetamol is contained in many combination medicines. Taking certain products together can cause you to get too much. Paracetamol which can DRP-7145 lead to a fatal overdose. Do not take more than 4g of Paracetamol in 24hours which is the maximum daily dose since it may cause liver damage. DATE OF AUTHORIZATION: December 13, 2017 * Do not take for longer than 7 days in a row. Stop taking the medicine and call your doctor if you still have a fever after 3 days of use, you still have pain after 7 days, if your symptoms get worse, or if you have a skin rash, ongoing Patient Information Leaflet Date of Revision: November 15, 2018 headache, or any redness or swelling. + Do not take more than the recommended dose. * Do not use after the expiry date stated. Manufactured by the Distributed by: Undesirable effects of this medicine: Marketing Authorization Holder:",1,1,Description of the product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T18:59:29.540175+00:00
