PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7317-05,Cefuroxime (as Axetil),Mediroxime,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1takSDr7EmdwvlZcFahHCF4S6WcHZuQs8/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7317-05_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1takSDr7EmdwvlZcFahHCF4S6WcHZuQs8_view_usp_drivesdk_258621b381.pdf,page_text/DRP-7317-05_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1takSDr7EmdwvlZcFahHCF4S6WcHZuQs8_view_usp_drivesdk_258621b381.json,958edc294927263de91780d8e642b81d4e954a8de82f16d2ef3f0aad51db7bbe,965989,1,digital_text,"Cefuroxime Axetil is a prodrug of Cefuroxime. Cefuroxime is a semi-synthetic, broad-spectrum cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. Cefuroxime Axetil is resistant to beta-Iactamase. PHARMACOLOGY MECHANISM OF ACTION Cefuroxime, like the penicillins, is a beta-Iactam antibiotic. By binding to speciﬁc penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs) located inside the bacterial cell wall, it inhibits the third and last stage of bacterial cell wall synthesis. Cell lysis is then mediated by bacterial cell wall autolytic enzymes such as autolysins; it is possible that cefuroxime interferes with an autolysin inhibitor. PHARMACOKINETICS Cefuroxime axetil is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and is rapidly hydrolysed in the intestinal mucosa and blood to cefuroxime. Approximately 50% of serum cefuroxime is bound to protein. Absorption of the tablet is greater when taken after food (absolute bioavailability of Cefuroxime Axetil 250 mg Tablets increases from 37% to 52%). Despite this diﬀerence in absorption, the clinical and bacteriologic responses of patients were independent of food intake at the time of tablet administration in 2 studies where this was assessed. Cefuroxime is excreted unchanged in the urine; in adults, approximately 50% of the administered dose is recovered in the urine within 12 hours.",1,1,DESCRIPTION,Cefuroxime Axetil Tablets are indicated for the treatment of patients with mild to moderate infections caused by susceptible strains of the designated microorganisms in the conditions listed below: - Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis - Acute Bacterial Otitis Media - Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis - Acute Bacterial Exacerbations of Chronic Bronchitis and Secondary Bacterial Infections of Acute Bronchitis - Uncomplicated Skin and Skin-Structure Infections - Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections - Uncomplicated Gonorrhea - Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans),1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-06-30T19:13:08.724310+00:00
