ves or nicotinic acid. Rarely, 2-4982 rhabdomyolysis with acute renal failure may develop. RTM700810INO02 Reg. IPOPHIL",1,1,INDICATIONS,medium,needs_human_review,2026-07-01T16:33:29.591322+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7842,VALSARTAN + HYDROCHLOROTHIAZIDE,DUOVAL,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1lR6UTSab_XWuGd-MlAAw5CzXulNGcU9R/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7842_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1lR6UTSab_XWuGd-MlAAw5CzXulNGcU9R_view_usp_drivesdk_c8a1889a15.pdf,page_text/DRP-7842_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1lR6UTSab_XWuGd-MlAAw5CzXulNGcU9R_view_usp_drivesdk_c8a1889a15.json,c696549d7be6c8da6afc39fd3bf75233e5b5a49c051e792ddd66f3ec3f6715f8,1932987,2,digital_text,"Valsartan is chemically described as N-(1-oxopenty)-N-1[[2-(1H-tetrazol-5-yl) [1, 1- biphenyl]-4-yl] methyl] L-valine. Its empirical formula is C24 H29 N5O3 and its molecular weight is 435.5. Valsartan is a white to partially white ﬁne powder, it is soluble in ethanol and slightly soluble in water. Hydrochlorothiazide is a white, or partially white, practically odourless, crystalline powder. It is slightly soluble in water; freely soluble in sodium hydroxide solution, in n-butylamine, and in methylformamide; sparingly soluble in methanol and insoluble in ether, in chloroform, and in dilute mineral acids. Hydrochlorothiazide is chemically described as 6-chloro-3, 4-hydrochlorothiazide. Its empirical formula is C7H8CIN3O4S2 and its molecular weight is 297.73. Valsartan is non-peptide orally active speciﬁc angiotensin II antagonist acting on the AT1 receptor subtype and hydrochlorothiazide is a thiazide diuretic.",1,1,DESCRIPTION:,(Valsartan + hydrochlorothiazide) Duoval ﬁlm-coated tablets are indicated for the treatment of hypertension.,1,1,INDICATIONS AND USAGE:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-07-01T16:33:33.543319+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7844-05,Iron (As Iron Sucrose),Ferriscript,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/12xN3PJpj8TMCSV8oKdcbsJ7fxRhChXpk/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7844-05_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12xN3PJpj8TMCSV8oKdcbsJ7fxRhChXpk_view_usp_drivesdk_9d73443e7a.pdf,page_text/DRP-7844-05_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_12xN3PJpj8TMCSV8oKdcbsJ7fxRhChXpk_view_usp_drivesdk_9d73443e7a.json,e2839bdeea7a2d3db38c9c3307dd3b475cf892d4b276b7ee72850f93c158f629,1477769,1,digital_text,"Iron Sucrose Injection is a sterile, dark brown slightly viscous solution of ferric hydroxide in complex with Sucrose in Water for Injection filled in a 5 mL amber coloured glass ampoule containing Ferric Hydroxide in complex with Sucrose as its active ingredient. Its chemical name is (2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-2-[(2S,3S,4S,5R)-3,4-dihydroxy-2,5-bis(hydroxymethyl)oxolan-2-yl]oxy-6(hydroxymethyl)oxane-3,4,5- triol;iron(3+);oxygen(2-). It has a molecular weight of 34,000 to 60,000 daltons, and its molecular formula is Na Fe O (OH)·3(H O)]n·m(C H O ). The structural formula is:2 5 8 2 12 22 11",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION:,Indicated in the treatment of iron deficiency anemia in patients undergoing chronic hemodialysis who are receiving supplemental erythropoietin therapy.,1,1,INDICATIONS:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-07-01T16:33:36.895779+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7847,Enalapril Maleate,None,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/10ubcGr_Jlm2saVvDSyZDN4tS7lnyaSvy/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7847_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10ubcGr_Jlm2saVvDSyZDN4tS7lnyaSvy_view_usp_drivesdk_59d556e20a.pdf,page_text/DRP-7847_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_10ubcGr_Jlm2saVvDSyZDN4tS7lnyaSvy_view_usp_drivesdk_59d556e20a.json,03de849d6be87beb8fc4168e89a3d8f498996fc7fcc214b3d79b28daf7f316e8,191339,1,digital_text,"RiteMED® Enalapril maleate 20 mg Tablet: Peach colored, round- shaped tablet bisected on one side. PHARMACODYNAMICS Enalapril maleate is a prodrug that is rapidly metabolized by the liver esterases to enalaprilat, its active metabolite, following oral administration. Enalapril maleate itself has little pharmacologic activity. Enalapril maleate lowers blood pressure by antagonizing the effect of the renin-angioten - sin-aldosterone system (RAAS). The RAAS is a homeostatic mechanism for regulating hemodynamics, water and electrolyte balance. During sympathetic stimulation or when renal blood pressure or blood flow is reduced, renin is released from granular cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus in the kidneys. In the blood stream, renin cleaves circulating angiotensinogen to angiotensin I (AT I), which is subsequently cleaved to angiotensin II (AT II) by angiotensin- converting enzyme (ACE). AT II increases blood pressure using a number of mechanisms. First, it stimulates the secretion of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex. Aldosterone travels to the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and collecting tubules of nephrons where it increases sodium and water reabsorption by increasing the number of sodium channels and sodium-potassium ATPases on cell membranes. Second, AT II stimulates the secretion of vasopressin (also known as antidiuretic hormone or ADH) from the posterior pituitary gland. ADH stimulates further water reabsorption from the kidneys via insertion of aquaporin-2 channels on the apical surface of cells of the DCT and collecting tubules. Third, AT II increases blood pressure through direct arterial vasoconstriction. Stimulation of the Type 1 AT II receptor on vascular smooth muscle cells leads to a cascade of events resulting in myocyte contraction and vasoconstriction. In addition to these major effects, AT II induces the thirst response via stimulation of the hypothalamic neurons. ACE inhibitors inhibit the rapid conversion of AT I to AT II and antagonize RAAS-induced increases in blood pressure. ACE (also known as kininase II) is also involved in the enzymatic deactivation of bradykinin, a vasodilator. Inhibiting the deactivation of bradykinin increases bradykinin levels and may sustain the effects of enalaprilat, the active metabolite of enalapril, by causing increased vasodilation and decreased blood pressure. PHARMACOKINETICS Enalapril acts as a prodrug of the diacid enalaprilat, its active form, which is poorly absorbed by mouth. Following oral administration, about 60% of a dose of enalapril is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and peak plasma concentrations are achieved 3 to 4 hours after an oral dose of enalapril. Following an oral dose, enalapril is excreted in the urine and faeces as enalaprilat and unchanged drug. Enalapril is 50 to 60% bound to plasma proteins; its elimination is multiphasic but its effective half-life for accumulation following multiple doses of enalapril is reported to be about 11 hours in patients with normal renal function. Enalaprilat is removed by haemodialysis and by peritoneal dialysis.",1,1,PRODUCT DESCRIPTION,"Used in the treatment of hypertension and heart failure. It may also be given prophylactically to patients with asymptomatic left ventricular dysfunction to delay the onset of symptomatic heart failure and to those with left ventricular dysfunction to reduce the incidence of coronary ischaemic events, including myocardial infarction.",1,1,INDICATIONS,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-07-01T16:33:40.512830+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7859-03,Meropenem (As Trihydrate),Carmepem,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1QE5G2FXTWDJ_-UUNNGgRUj5cV1YnQ1Qs/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7859-03_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1QE5G2FXTWDJ_-UUNNGgRUj5cV1YnQ1Qs_view_usp_drivesdk_7443d2a0dc.pdf,page_text/DRP-7859-03_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1QE5G2FXTWDJ_-UUNNGgRUj5cV1YnQ1Qs_view_usp_drivesdk_7443d2a0dc.json,b38e48c5254a527fa5234c47644bfada5082aab8607ba7c00a593eaef3266566,7821336,1,digital_text,,1,1,DESCRIPTION,,1,1,INDICATION,low,needs_human_review_no_sections_found,2026-07-01T16:33:48.672232+00:00
PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-7860,Paracetamol,Alagesic,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1bBnsds_MFs0_piIUIEs4tOav7GHXhBHm/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-7860_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1bBnsds_MFs0_piIUIEs4tOav7GHXhBHm_view_usp_drivesdk_9b8e0b44d0.pdf,page_text/DRP-7860_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1bBnsds_MFs0_piIUIEs4tOav7GHXhBHm_view_usp_drivesdk_9b8e0b44d0.json,42772a20052ea8c811d6051525a8ce2229900af65129c9870cf0d74a3bea5cca,793534,1,ocr,"The medicine is a milky orange colored suspension with orange flavor and aroma contained in a 15mL amber bottle. What is in the medicine? Paracetamol has analgesic and anti-pyretic properties and weak anti-inflammatory activity. Paracetamol is often the analgesic or anti-pyretic of choice, especially in the elderly and in patients in whom salicylates or other NSAIDs are contraindicated. Such patients include asthmatics, those with a history of peptic ulcer, and children. Strength of the medicine: Each milliliter of suspension contains 100mg Paracetamol. What is this medicine used for? Paracetamol (ALAGESIC) is used for relief of painful conditions including headache, toothache, and other minor aches and pains. Also indicated for reduction of fever. How much and how often should you use this medicine? Infants: Below 6 months old . 0.3 to 0.6 mL 6 months to 1 year old . 0.6 to 1.2 mL 1 year to 2 years old .... - 1,2 to 1.8 mL To be taken 3 to 4 times daily, or as prescribed by the physician When should you not take this medicine? Do not use this medicine if you are allergic to acetaminophen or paracetamol. Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: Paracetamol should be given with care to patients with impaired kidney or liver functions. Chronic use should be avoided. Undesirable effects of this medicine: Adverse effects of Paracetamol are rare and usually mild, although haematological reactions including thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis have been reported. Skin rashes and other hypersensitivity reaction occurs occasionally. What other medicine or food should be avoided? The risk of Paracetamol toxicity may be increased in patients receiving other potentially hepatotoxic drugs or drugs that induce liver microsomal enzymes. The absorption of paracetamol may be accelerated by drugs such as metoclopramide. Excretion may be affected and plasma concentrations altered when given with probenecid. Colestyramine reduces the absorption of paracetamol if given within 1 hour of paracetamol. What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose, skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Signs and symptoms of overdose: The toxicity of Paracetamol overdose has long been recognized. Potentially toxic doses are those that are greater than 150mg/kg/dose in children and greater than 7-10 g/dose in adults may develop severe liver damage and less often, renal tubular necrosis. Early signs of overdosage with paracetamol are nausea, vomiting, and may also include lethargy and sweating usually settles within 24 hours. Abdominal pain may be the first indication of liver damage which is not usually apparent for 24 to 48 hours and sometimes may be delayed for up to 4 to 6 days after ingestion. Activated charcoal may be used to reduce gastrointestinal absorption, if it can be given within 1 hour of the overdose, and if more than 150mg/kg or 12g of paracetamol has been ingested. There is little evidence that gastric lavage is of benefit in those who have overdosed solely in paracetamol. If there is any doubt about the timing or the need to treat, then a patient should be treated with an antidote of choice (Acetyicysteine). What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Protect from direct sunlight. Keep out of reach of children. When should you consult your doctor? If symptoms persist and any undesirable effect occurs. “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph”. Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. DRP-7860 Date of Authorization: July 17, 2021 Patient Information Leaflet date of revision: June 10, 2022 Manufactured by the Distributed by: Marketing Authorization Holder:",1,1,Description of the product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-07-01T16:33:56.872696+00:00
