PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-8176,Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) + Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) + Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12),Diabee,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1M7kGixbx6edf2bXUB6uSP959UInAo85o/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-8176_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1M7kGixbx6edf2bXUB6uSP959UInAo85o_view_usp_drivesdk_5ea61824e3.pdf,page_text/DRP-8176_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1M7kGixbx6edf2bXUB6uSP959UInAo85o_view_usp_drivesdk_5ea61824e3.json,b149bdecb780fbca99f37fcea4cc688777459b13883e1ccc6cd4fa629b8e35fe,499592,1,ocr,"The medicine is a white to off-white colored, round biconvex tablet with score on one side and company logo on the other side. Blister pack by 20's, box of 100 tablets. What is in the medicine? This tablet contains Vitamin B1, B6, and B12. B-Complex vitamins are required for the normal function of the nervous system and are used as adjunct in the management of various neuromuscular disorders. Strength of the medicine: Each tablet contains: Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) eee eee eee eee eee eee eee ee ee ee ee ee ee ee 100 mg Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) SeTTrrr rr rCrrerrr errr rrr eee eee ere ee eee ee eee ee ee ee ee 5 mg eee eee eee ee ee eee eee eee eee eee eee ee eee ee eee ee eee ee ee ees Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 50 mcg What is this medicine used for? Diabee tablet is used for prevention and treatment of Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 deficiencies manifested as abnormal touch sensation (numbness, glove and stocking sensation, burning and pricking), muscle pain, loss of reflexes in the hands, knees, ankles, calves and feet. How much and how often should you use this medicine? One tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician. When should you not take this medicine? You should not take this if you are allergic to any components of this medicine. Care that should be taken when taking this medicine: on the label. Do not use more than the recommended dose unless recommended by a doctor and do not use after the expiry date Undesirable effects of this medicine: Adverse effect with these vitamins are rare. Long term use of large doses of pyridoxine is associated with the development of severe peripheral neuropathies. Other side effects reported with cyanocobalamin includes gastrointestinal disturbances, fever, chill,hot flushing, dizziness, malaise, acneform and bullous eruptions, and tremor What other medicine or food should be avoided while taking this medicine? Pyridoxine has different interactions with levodopa, altretamine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, hydralazine, isoniazid, penicillamine, and oral contraceptives. Absorption of Vitamin B12 from the gastrointestinal tract may be reduced by neomycin, aminosalicylic acid, histamine H,-antagonists, omeprazole, and colchicines. What should you do if you miss a dose? Take the dose as soon as you remember but if the missed dose is near the next dose, skip the missed dose and continue with the scheduled dosing. Signs and symptoms of overdose: Overdose with the following specific vitamins include the following: Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) - Skin rashes, allergic reactions, agitation, insomnia, and heart palpitations. Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6)- Long term use of large doses of pyridoxine is associated with the development of severe peripheral neuropathies, numbness on the extremities, tingling sensation, muscle cramps, restlessness, fatigue, mood swings, and insomnia. Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12)- Unilateral tingling sensation, panic attacks, insomnia, and palpitations. What to do when you have taken more than the recommended dosage? If you ingested more than the recommended dose, contact the nearest hospital or your doctor immediately. How should you keep this medicine? Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Protect from direct sunlight When should you consult your doctor? If symptoms persist and any undesirable effect occurs. “For suspected adverse drug reaction, report to the FDA: www.fda.gov.ph”- Seek medical attention immediately at the first sign of any adverse drug reaction. DRP-8176 Date of Renewal Authorization: June 17, 2021 Patient Information Leaflet Date of Revision: July 18, 2021 Manufactured by the Marketing Authorization Holder: =, ) Distributed by:",1,1,Description of the product:,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T21:30:37.484828+00:00
