PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-8842-01,Ascorbic acid,Unbranded,PIL_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WwA-9rdoswgoYao6K9AT96ggxBOxUZ73/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-8842-01_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1WwA-9rdoswgoYao6K9AT96ggxBOxUZ73_view_usp_drivesdk_800ee84d5a.pdf,page_text/DRP-8842-01_PIL_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1WwA-9rdoswgoYao6K9AT96ggxBOxUZ73_view_usp_drivesdk_800ee84d5a.json,63116be50f55d9d44cc1c64be7ac33fab48b07a623321d4f57b853118b8f64c1,93824,2,digital_text,"Rose Pharmacy Ascorbic Acid (as Sodium Ascorbate) Capsule is a white to beige crystalline powder enclosed in brown capsule. WHAT IS IN THE MEDICINE? This medicine contains Sodium Ascorbate or Vitamin C. Vitamin C is a water-soluble vitamin that is necessary for normal growth and development. This vitamin is required for the growth and repair of tissues in all parts of the body. It is necessary to form collagen- an important protein used to make skin, scar tissue, tendons, ligaments, and blood vessels. Vitamin C is essential for wound healing and for the repair and maintenance of cartilages, bones and teeth. Vitamin C is also one of the many antioxidants- nutrients that block some of the damage caused by free radicals. Antioxidants also help reduce the damage to the body caused by toxic chemicals and pollutants such as cigarrete smoke, food preservatives, etc. Vitamin C is also a co-factor in many biological processes especially in iron metabolism, resistance to infection, and in cellular respiration. STRENGTH OF THE MEDICINE Each capsule contains 562.50mg of Sodium Ascorbate. This is equivalent to 500mg of Ascorbic acid. WHAT IS THE MEDICINE USED FOR? Ascorbic Acid as Sodium Ascorbate is used for the prevention and treatment of Vitamin C deficiency. HOW MUCH AND HOW OFTEN SHOULD YOU USE THE MEDICINE? You may take this medicine following the doses below: For regular supplementation: Take 1 capsule daily. For increased resistance against cold and flu: Take 2-3 capsules daily. WHEN SHOULD YOU NOT TAKE THIS MEDICINE? You should not take this medicine if you are allergic to Sodium Ascorbate or to any of the product’s components. CARE THAT SHOULD BE TAKEN WHEN TAKING THIS MEDICINE You should consult your doctor before taking this medicine if you have hyperoxaluria (an excessive excretion of oxalate in your urine) or a condition known as glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency. UNDESIRABLE EFFECTS OF THIS MEDICINE Vitamin C toxicity is very rare, because the body cannot store the vitamin and immediately excretes it in urine. However, if you take amounts greater than 2,000mg/day you may experience stomach upset and diarrhea. The daily use of high doses may lead to the formation of oxalate stones in the kidneys. You may also experience disturbed water and electrolyte balance in your body, increased hemolysis (breaking-up of red blood cells), increased calcium in the kidneys and suppression of cobalamine (Vitamin B 12) activity. If you have a condition called glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency, taking too much Vitamin C can cause hemolysis which may cause you to feel tired, or dizzy, or even cause you to be anemic. In some rare cases, you may experience painful urination or pink/bloody urine. If any of these effects persist, tell your doctor immediately. For women who take high doses of Vitamin C during pregnancy, your babies may develop a condition known as ‘rebound scurvy’ in which upon delivery, your baby’s intake of Vitamin C drops leading to symptoms of scurvy and requiring immediate Vitamin C supplementation. 12.5 x 17.5 cm 17.5cm 12.5 cm FRONT VERSION1 - 6 - 2020 PATIENT INFORMATION LEAFLET [Page 2] WHAT OTHER MEDICINE OR FOOD SHOULD BE AVOIDED WHILE TAKING THIS MEDICINE? Concurrent intake of more than 200mg of Ascorbic acid per 300mg of elemental iron would increase absorption of iron from your intestines. Increased urinary excretion of Ascorbic acid and decreased excretion of aspirin occur when you take these drugs together. Taking Ascorbic acid with paracetamol can prolong the presence of paracetamol in your blood. Ascorbic acid is not compatible in solution with drugs like aminophylline, bleomycin, erythromycin, lactobionate, nafcillin, sodium nitrofurantoin, conjugated estrogens, sodium bicarbonate, sulfafurazole, diethanolamine, chloramphenicol sodium succinate, chlorothiazide sodium and hydrocortisone sodium succinate. Ascorbic acid has also been reported to interfere with blood thinning treatments. You should consult your doctor for advice before taking Ascorbic acid if you are taking any of the above-mentioned medications. WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU MISS A DOSE? If you miss a dose, you may take it as soon as you remember. However, if you remember it at a time that is already close to the next dose, just skip the missed dose and take the next dose as scheduled. Do not take two doses of the medicine at the same time. SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS OF OVERDOSE If you take too much Sodium Ascorbate, you may nausea, vomiting, indigestion, diarrhea, flushing of the face, headache, fatigue and disturbed sleep. WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU HAVE TAKEN MORE THAN THE RECOMMENDED DOSAGE? If you have taken more than the recommended dose of this medicine and you are experiencing adverse symptoms, it would be best to seek medical attention immediately. HOW SHOULD YOU KEEP THIS MEDICINE? You should keep this medicine at temperatures not exceeding 30°C and protect it from heat and direct sunlight. Keep it out of reach and sight of children. WHEN SHOULD YOU CONSULT A DOCTOR? If your symptoms persist or worsen after a few days of taking this medicine, or if you believe you are experiencing an adverse reaction to this medicine, it would be best to consult your doctor as soon as possible. 12.5 x 17.5 cm 17.5cm 12.5 cm BACK VERSION2 - 6 - 2020",1,2,DESCRIPTION OF THE PRODUCT,,,,,low,needs_human_review_missing_section,2026-06-30T21:57:47.970806+00:00
