PH_FDA_VERIFICATION_PORTAL,DRP-9351-01,,,PI_01,"Package Insert, Patient Information Leaflet, PI/PIL, Indication, SmPC, SPC",https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XXkEuD56Ub-bF4MUbOTJRn7CGlllyxU5/view?usp=drivesdk,pdf/DRP-9351-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1XXkEuD56Ub-bF4MUbOTJRn7CGlllyxU5_view_usp_drivesdk_94a0ac992a.pdf,page_text/DRP-9351-01_PI_01_https_drive.google.com_file_d_1XXkEuD56Ub-bF4MUbOTJRn7CGlllyxU5_view_usp_drivesdk_94a0ac992a.json,e81f3da73b3fc4af0af7b25caf0470adb935dcd4531d12acfc60de989f7d4e0b,514194,1,digital_text,"Colorless or slightly yellow, clear solution. Granisetron hydrochloride Injection is an antinauseant and antiemetic agent. Chemically it is endo-N-(9- methyl-9-azabicyclo [3.3.1] non-3-yl)-1-methyl-1H-indazole-3-carboxamide hydrochloride with a molecular weight of 348.9 (312.4 free base). Its empirical formula is C H N OHCl, while its chemical 18 24 4 structure is:",1,1,DESCRIPTION:,"Granisetron Injection is indicated for: The prevention of nausea and/or vomiting associated with initial and repeat courses of emetogenic cancer therapy, including high-dose cisplatin. The prevention and treatment of postoperative nausea and vomiting in adults. As with other antiemetics, routine prophylaxis is not recommended in patients in whom there is little expectation that nausea and/or vomiting will occur postoperatively. In patients where nausea and/or vomiting must be avoided during the postoperative period, Granisetron Injection is recommended even where the incidence of postoperative nausea and/or vomiting is low.",1,1,INDICATIONS AND USAGE:,high,machine_extracted_review_recommended,2026-07-01T16:44:40.357403+00:00
